(3455, 'Lentil elite lines and new varieties targeted to Sudan and similar agroecologies that have high-yield potential', 'Genetic innovations at the levels of target product profiles for breeding, elite lines in development, and newly released varieties that have been prioritised by national stakeholders for fast-tracked development and release in their country and similar agroecologies. This record is for lentil in Sudan and similar agroecologies that have high-yield potential.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3455?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(6843, 'Lentil elite lines and new varieties targeted to Uzbekistan and similar agroecologies that have high-yield potential', 'Genetic innovations at the levels of target product profiles for breeding, elite lines in development, and newly released varieties that have been prioritised by national stakeholders for fast-tracked development and release in their country and similar agroecologies. This record is for lentil in Uzbekistan and similar agroecologies that have high yield potential.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6843?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3449, 'Sweetpotato elite lines and new varieties targeted to Uzbekistan and similar agroecologies that are drought- and heat-tolerant, early-maturing, and high-yield potential', 'Genetic innovations have been prioritized by national stakeholders for fast-tracked development and release, focusing on target product profiles, elite lines in development, and newly released varieties. This record is for sweetpotato in Uzbekistan and similar agroecologies that are drought- and heat-tolerant, early-maturing, and high yield potential. In 2023, two already locally adopted clones ''Sochakinur'' and ''Toyloki'' were tested. In 2024, in-vitro material of 6 new sweetpotato clones were approved for entry into Uzbekistan and are now being prepared for testing in 2025.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3449?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3448, 'Potato elite lines and new varieties targeted to Uzbekistan and similar agroecologies that are drought-, heat- and salinity-tolerant, have a high dry matter content, and high-yield potential', 'Genetic innovations have been prioritized by national stakeholders for fast-tracked development and release, focusing on target product profiles, elite lines in development, and newly released varieties. This record specifically addresses potatoes in Uzbekistan and similar agroecologies, with traits like drought, heat, and salinity tolerance, high dry matter content, and high yield potential. In 2023, the CIP clone ''Pskem'', already registered as a variety in Uzbekistan, was tested. In 2024, in-vitro material of 18 new potato clones and 1 older clone were approved for entry into Uzbekistan and are now being prepared for testing in 2025.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3448?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(2221, 'Faster growing genetically improved silver carp (generation 2) produced in Bangladesh', 'Genetically superior candidate parents from G2 were selected and mated to produce G3 families in 2024.  These included 124 families selected for rapid growth, 31 control line (i.e. unimproved), 15 selected for negative growth, 38 selected x DoF/China strain and 19 DoF/China strain families.  Crosses with the DoF/China strain were made to allow direct comparison of genetic quality and possible future introgression of additional genetic diversity into the WorldFish silver carp strain.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2221?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(18168, 'Faster growing genetically improved silver carp (generation 3 families) produced in Bangladesh', 'Genetically superior candidate parents from G2 were selected and mated to produce G3 families in 2024.  These included 124 families selected for rapid growth, 31 control line (i.e. unimproved), 15 selected for negative growth, 38 selected x DoF/China strain and 19 DoF/China strain families.  Crosses with the DoF/China strain were made to allow direct comparison of genetic quality and possible future introgression of additional genetic diversity into the WorldFish silver carp strain.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18168?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(18331, 'Faster growing genetically improved farmed tilapia (GIFT) generation 19 in Malaysia', 'Genetically superior G18 parents were selected and mated to produce Generation 19 (G19) of the Genetically Enhanced Farmed Tilapia (GIFT) in Penang (Malaysia) in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18331?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(13484, 'Improving Internal Nitrogen Accumulation and Utilization through Manipulating Catabolic Pathways for Increased Yield', 'Genome editing was employed to target a gene involved in nucleoside catabolism, resulting in the accumulation of low molecular weight nitrogenous compounds in plants. The aim was to enhance nitrogen remobilization, leading to a subsequent increase in yield without compromising grain quality in rice. Previously designed gene constructs were used to transform elite puddled transplanted rice (PTR) and direct-seeded rice (DSR) varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13484?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(3202, 'Mapping recommendation domains for agricultural technologies', 'Geospatial analysis generated spatial maps of the recommendation domains for sustainable intensification technologies. The analytics results will guide extension and development agencies in targeting the most suitable zones to promote the efficient allocation of resources and reduce the risk of technology failure.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3202?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(12581, 'Guidance for conducting better evaluations of food system interventions to improve planetary and human health published to inform effective actions for food systems transformation', 'Global interest in the need to transform food systems to protect human and planetary health provides a major opportunity for improving nutrition in tandem with achieving other societal goals. Ensuring that food systems transformation truly leads to sustainable healthy diets for all requires a much stronger evidence base than what is currently available. Policymakers, funders, implementing partners, and researchers are looking for credible evidence about what works to guide food systems transformation in better directions. The guidance draws on six program evaluations from Africa and South Asia, experts, including one from Sustainable Healthy Diets, and provides six recommendations for designing better evaluations of food system interventions. Better evaluation methodologies for such complex studies can generate the evidence that is urgently needed to support the ambitious goals of food systems transformation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12581?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(3010, 'Global Product Repository to assist researchers in submitting and curating a database of seed product market segments, target product profiles, and products', 'Global Product Repository developed  to have a query-able online platform that  can be used  by CGIAR scientists, NARES, and other stakeholders to  submit and search for evidence in the development of target product profiles (TPPs). The Global Product Repository (GPR) also aims to visualize  rice market segments through an  interactive map.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3010?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(6298, 'Introduction and co-evaluation of ILRI gene bank forage resources at different ecologies', 'Grass and legume forage accessions have been accessed from ILRI gene bank and evaluated in three MFS sites in the Ethiopian highlands.  Best performing forage accessions will be used for further comprehensive studies at bigger plots and diverse ecologies. This study will help to identify forage options useful for system intensification and diversification', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6298?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(55, 'Grasspea breeding network accelerates genetic gains for more productive, low-neurotoxin varieties for low-tillage rice systems in South Asia, and for food and fodder use in the Middle East ', 'Grasspea is a multi-purpose, and hardy legume which can sustain drought, waterlogging and salinity, and can be grown under minimal external inputs. In collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, ICARDA breeding efforts are underway to reduce the neurotoxin content in grasspea, while improving its seed yield and biomass, to provide a remunerative crop for safe consumption by humans and animals. The two breeding pipelines pursue 1. Grasspea as a relay no-till crop in rice fields in South Asia, reducing its cost of production. 2. Grasspea as a multipurpose crop for grain, feed, vegetables, and straw, for dry regions in Africa and the Middle East, where its split seeds, flour, and twigs are used for human consumption and fodder, and the plant residues for nutritious animal feed. The current breeding cycle time is 4 years. In 2024, seventy-two advanced lines of were evaluated for yield in diverse environments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/55?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(49, 'Groundnut breeding network in eastern and southern Africa accelerates genetic gains for improved varieties that serve three newly defined market segments and deploy modern optimization, cycling and phenotyping practices.', 'Groundnut breeding targeted at eastern and southern Africa continues the work that ICRISAT has been doing under CIMMYT leadership, and in collaboration with Kenya, Malawi, Mozambique, Sudan, Tanzania, Uganda, and Zambia.  The network has three pipelines and target product profiles (TPPs): 1. Short duration tan groundnuts for food and food processing; 2/3. Medium duration resilient red or tan groundnuts for food processing, confectionery flour, and snacks. During 2023, the network jointly reviewed trait priorities to be pursued through breeding. Country product design teams were formed; 30% of members are women. Breeding program assessment were conducted in Tanzania, Sudan, Malawi and Mozambique, to assess how to best include NARES  strengths and capabilities in collaborative germplasm development. The key elements of effective breeding pipelines are being  implemented including: high-throughput phenotyping, rapid cycling of early generation germplasm, simulations to optimize NARES and CG breeding schemas, and joint evaluations and variety identification.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/49?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(343, 'The Breeding Portal helps CGIAR crops to optimize pipeline structure and leverage breeding gains within a particular crop, complementing diversity studies and quantitative genetics analytics', 'Having adequate genetic variance is necessary to make genetic gain. Making genetic gain also means depleting genetic variance. Strategies are needed to augment trait variance in the elite germplasm pool. This variance can come from trait discovery and deployment (TD&D) pipelines, other crop breeding pipelines, or less-related genetic resources. An effective germplasm use strategy aligns breeding pipelines with target product profiles (TPP), understands trait availability viz priorities, and strategically leverages investments across pipelines and organizations. Since 2023, all CGIAR programs use the Breeding Portal to document the Breeding Pipeline - TPP relationships and define the essential traits that need to be improved. During deep-dive sessions in 2024, 74% of programs stated that they introgress between 1 to 11 critical traits into the main breeding pipelines (see Result 1241). There is need to better document and rationalize the TD&D - Breeding Pipeline relationships in the Breeding Portal.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/343?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7265, 'Plant-based nuggets to diversify the sustainable and healthy food offer', 'Healthy Nuggets made with vegetable proteins and sugarcane bagasse fiber (usable waste), with competitive prices for the market, which contributes to the use of industry waste. It seeks to reduce the environmental impact by using less water in its production. All of this with technology that allows us to produce varieties of products and presentations for the target audience. This product is good for consumption for all people regardless of age and gender', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7265?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(15660, 'New rice varieties released in South Asia', 'Here''s a revised paragraph, sorted by year and simplified for better clarity:

Between 2021 and 2024, a total of 117 rice varieties were released across India (95), Nepal (9), and Bangladesh (13). 
India:
- 2021: 41 varieties
- 2022: 1 variety
- 2023: 24 varieties
- 2024: 29 varieties

Out of the 95 varieties, 62 were approved by the Central Varietal Release Committee (CVRC), which included 31 direct and 31 one-parent IRRI varieties. The State Varietal Release Committee (SVRC) approved 32 varieties, with 14 direct and 18 one-parent IRRI varieties.

Nepal:
- 2021: 2 varieties
- 2022: 7 varieties
- 2023: 0
- 2024: 0

Bangladesh:
- 2021: 3 varieties
- 2022: 6 varieties
- 2023: 2 varieties
- 2024: 2 varieties', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15660?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9183, 'Satellite-based static and dynamic crop host distribution maps for improving spore dispersal and environmental suitability forecasting models, advisory to farmers, and the surveillance of susceptible host and green bridging areas', 'High-resolution (10 m) static and dynamic (greenness) host wheat distribution maps generated using Random Forest and Sentinel-2 satellite time series; Pre-processed with state-of-the-art methods;
Classification based on EthCT2020 dataset;
Greenness is expressed through NDVI thresholding (all NDVI values between 0.15-0.8 are considered as green wheat)', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9183?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(12732, 'Improved fodder (Napier) for mid-hill smallholder mixed farming systems to improve dairy livestock productivity for milk, reduce erosion and restore degraded lands', 'High-value fruit trees for Nepal''s mid-hill smallholder mixed farming systems to improve household nutrition and income diversity.
Report of first year''s growth and performance details of the tropical and sub-tropical fruit trees cultivated by the farmers of Khotang and Surkhet. Preliminary report is prepared and further insights will be reported at and end of year 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12732?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6057, 'An data aggregation and analytics platform for researchers and decision makers to benchmark and prioritise farming systems for development support', 'his innovation consists of a database schema to integrate findings from multiple high-profile farm household surveys, including the World Bank''s LSMS-ISA and the CGIAR''s RHoMIS surveys, and others. The combined data are available for exploration and anlaysis through dashboards, which permit description of the farming practices and human welfare and livelihoods status. Additionally, there are various options for sub-setting populations according to geographic location, farming system type, asset/wealth category, standardised livelihood typologies, user defined threshold-based typologies, or user defined empirically defined typologies. This innovation akes two significant contributions: first, by providing a common means to integrate data from multiple farm household surveys; and second, by providing an accessible means to conduct farming systems analysis for the planning and prioritisation of research and development activities. It is currently in at a pilot stage, meaning that full functionality has been achieved and it is being tested by potential users.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6057?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17429, 'Drone-assisted agronomic diagnostic for small-scale farming in Guatemala', 'his innovation, leverages drone photogrammetry to provide precise, timely, and actionable data on crop growth and health, particularly focusing on maize due to its prevalence in the region. By systematically capturing high-resolution images and using satellite imagery, both with multispectral data, aims to empower local farmers with the knowledge needed to optimize crop management and yield, thereby contributing to the overarching goals of resilience and sustainability in the agricultural sector. During 2024, drone images were captured in two departments of Guatemala (Zacapa and Totonicapán) and processed for this purpose. Two key farmers organizations have been part of the development of the innovation (CDRO in western Guatemala and ADIPAZ in eastern Guatemala) and seek to apply the learning with their beneficiary farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17429?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7283, 'Holistic Localized Agroecology Performance Assessment (HOLPA) tool for assessing the performance of fields, farms and landscapes transitioning to agroecology', 'HOLPA consists of two parts to permit both context specificity and global comparison of results: (1) a set of global performance indicators (2) the Local Indicator Selection Process (LISP), in which food system actors co-design metrics of agroecological performance in a given context. Together, they constitute a framework for holistic localized performance assessment, or HOLPA, which can be applied across diverse farming contexts and agroecological activities. The evidence will enable communities to see the impact of agroecology on things that matter to them, while also giving investors and other decision-makers a clear picture of agroecology’s performance under a wide variety of conditions.
In 2024, Local performance indicators have been selected in 15 communities across eight countries and incorporated into the HOLPA tool. Data has been collected from more than 1,600 households and has been analyzed. Country reports are under production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7283?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(10024, 'Intensifying Homestead Gardens to Boost Food Security and Empower Women in Bangladesh', 'Homestead gardening is the most complex multi-strata integrated production system that combines all farming components (crop, livestock, tree, and occasionally fish) and provides household food security, employment, and income generation opportunities. Homestead gardens are mainly managed by women. With this innovation, SI-MFS in collaboration with BARI, aims at intensifying and diversifying homestead gardens, benefitting households by increasing nutritional status and extra income. Ensuring small packages of good quality seeds and seedlings through local service providers or local seed companies will increase year-round vegetable and fruit production. The cowdung from livestock production are used to fertilize homestead gardens and the crop residues from homestead gardens are used as fodder to livestock. In addition, this innovation will also increase women''s empowerment.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10024?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(14352, 'Low-Carbon Footprint Rice Hybrid (IR139216H)', 'Hybrid rice cultivar, IR139216H, was identified with significantly  lesser methane emission in comparison to NSIC Rc 222 (inbred check). 

Several new two-line hybrids mature in 110 days and yield higher than 10 tons/ha with reduced methane emissions per unit yield per day in comparison to best  inbred varieties like NSIC Rc222.
 
Higher yielding and shorter duration hybrids result in lower methane emissions on a per day basis. This type of materials are classified as low-carbon footprint hybrids.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14352?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(54, 'Barley breeding networks accelerate genetic gains for climate-resilient and nutritious dual-purpose varieties that meet farmers'' and markets'' demands in semi-arid and arid regions', 'ICARDA develops new barley varieties in collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, through the following breeding pipelines: 1. Feed Barley for arid and semi-arid regions: high grain and straw productivity and stability in mild to severe drought conditions. 2. Food and Fodder Barley: advanced drought tolerance and mostly hull-less genotypes combine high grain size and yield with enhanced quality for human nutrition (β-glucan, iron, and zinc content) and straw production. 3. Feed and Forage Barley for favorable environments: combining high grain and biomass production and disease resistance, with traits such as early vigor and regeneration capacity for grazing. 4. Malt and Fodder Barley: combines malting quality to meet industrial standards with disease and lodging resistance and high straw production. The current cycle time is 4 years. Between 2022-2024, partners in 5 countries released 11 varieties, 10 of them deriving from pipelines improved for drought or salinity tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/54?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(53, 'Faba bean breeding networks in northern and eastern Africa and Asia accelerate genetic gains for more productive, heat- and disease-resistant varieties', 'ICARDA is improving the productivity and yield stability of faba bean by aiming to make sure responses to biotic and abiotic stress factors, such as climatic conditions and diseases, are effectively controlled and inherent yield potential is improved. ICARDA breeding efforts are underway to develop faba bean with greater tolerance to drought and heat, that can keep producing at 35°C during the flowering stage. In addition, ICARDA is developing lines with reduced cooking time and lower levels of anti-nutritional glyosides, vicine and covicine. New candidate varieties are developed in collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, through the following breeding pipelines: 1. Low rainfed cool, 2. Irrigated (Nile Valley), 3. High Rainfed (Africa Highlands). The current breeding cycle time is 4 years. Between 2022-2024, partners in Egypt and Lebanon released 4 varieties, three of them improved for drought and heat tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/53?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(56, 'Lentil breeding network accelerates genetic gains for more climate resilient, multi-disease resistant varieties that meet the diverse demands of farmers in Eastern and North Africa, West and South Asia', 'ICARDA is seeking ways to take advantage of lentil''s high nutritional value and low water use that makes it well adapted to cereal-based cropping in dry regions. Focus is on developing improved varieties with heat and drought tolerance, greater productivity and with the ability to mechanize production. Winter hardiness is important for cereal systems. Water-logging tolerance is important in the highlands.  Collaborative breeding activities with public and private sector partners and farmers, pursue the following breeding pipelines: 1. Herbicide-tolerant and machine-harvestable varieties for the rainfed cereal systems in West Asia and North Africa. 2. Drought- and heat-tolerant varieties with resistance against multiple diseases found in the rainfed highlands. 3. Adaptation to rice-based systems in South Asia. The current breeding cycle time is 4 years. Between 2022-2024, partners in 5 countries released 13 varieties selected for drought and heat tolerance, two of them in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/56?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16982, 'ICARDA’s Genebank Seed Request Application helps in managing requests properly, developing time-tracking Key Performance Indicators and getting users feedback', 'ICARDA’s Genebank Seed Request Application allows users to register, select and request ICARDA genebank material. It allows the ICARDA genebank managers to manage requests, users more efficiently.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16982?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(10509, 'Diagnostic tools validated by ICARDA’s Germplasm Health Unit (GHU) for detection of cereal seed-borne pests', 'ICARDA’s Germplasm Health unit (GHU) is responsible for the monitoring, clearance and documentation of safe germplasm movement at the Center''s locations in Lebanon and Morocco. All incoming and outgoing genetic resources and breeding germplasm must go through a strict quarantine monitoring system, including seed health testing. To confirm the health status of seed samples, the GHU conducts different types of tests based on the nature of the pest to be identified. In this manual, we listed and described the procedures of diagnostic tools validated by ICARDA’s GHU for detection of cereal seed-borne pests.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10509?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(3119, 'Diagnostic tools validated by ICARDA’s Germplasm Health Unit (GHU) for detection of legume seed-borne pests', 'ICARDA’s Germplasm Health unit (GHU) is responsible for the monitoring, clearance and documentation of safe germplasm movement at the Center''s locations in Lebanon and Morocco. All incoming and outgoing genetic resources and breeding germplasm must go through a strict quarantine monitoring system, including seed health testing. To confirm the health status of seed samples, the GHU conducts different types of tests based on the nature of the pest to be identified. In this manual, we listed and described the procedures of diagnostic tools validated by ICARDA’s GHU for detection of legume seed-borne pests:', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3119?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(16778, 'Accelerating genetic gains to augment food, feed and bioethanol productivity of sorghum varieties to improve food security and incomes of farmers in Asia, Africa and Latin America', 'ICRISAT aims to develop high-yielding, climate-resilient, and nutritionally enhanced sorghum varieties that cater to the specific needs of farmers and consumers in Asia and Africa, ensuring that sorghum remains a sustainable and dependable crop for the millions who rely on it for sustenance and livelihoods. Food yields, fodder value, and grain mold, Anthracnose and leaf blight resistance are being improved in three normal sorghum and one sweet sorghum pipelines. Current cycle times are at 48 months. Genomic selection is being established. High-throughput phenotyping approaches and breeding schemes are continuously being improved to access greater genetic variance, increase selection intensity and accelerate genetic gains. Multi-environment trials cover up to 44 environments per subregion. Between 2022 and 2024, partners in India released 13 varieties, four of them improved for post-flowering drought tolerance. Three others are sweet sorghums which can potentially be used as a raw material for bio-ethanol production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16778?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16808, 'Accelerating genetic gains to augment the productivity, disease-resistance and micronutrient content of finger millet varieties to benefit farmers in South Asia and Africa', 'ICRISAT engages in research and breeding efforts to develop improved varieties with higher yields, increased resilience to climatic stresses, and further enriching its nutritional value. It collaborates with local stakeholders and partners to ensure that finger millet''s potential is fully harnessed in combating malnutrition, supporting livelihoods, and enhancing traditional food systems. Yield, zinc, iron and calcium content, and blast resistance are being improved in two pipelines targeting different market segments in Asia. They are also being tested in Africa. Current cycle time is 60 months with the intent to reduce to 48 months. Genomic selection is being established. High-throughput phenotyping approaches and breeding schemes are being improved to access greater genetic variance, increase selection intensity and accelerate genetic gains. Multi-environment trials cover up to 13 environments in each subregion.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16808?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16838, 'Accelerating genetic gains to augment the productivity and oil content of groundnut varieties to benefit farmers in South Asia and Africa', 'ICRISAT is advancing groundnut breeding efforts to enhance groundnut''s potential impact on food security, nutrition and farmers'' incomes.  Yield, drought tolerance or oleic acid content are being improved in two pipelines targeting different market segments in each subregion. Current cycle time is 48 months with the intent to accelerate through speed-breeding. Genomic selection is being established using multi-environment calibration data. High-throughput phenotyping approaches and breeding schemes are being improved to access greater genetic variance, increase selection intensity and accelerate genetic gains. Multi-environment trials cover up to 48 environments. Genetic resources are being evaluated for donors that can provide stem rot resistance, seed dormancy, late leaf spot resistance and rust resistance. 1987-2017 genetic gain analysis show a steady increase of between 35 to 41 kg per year and hectare. Between 2022-2024, partners in Bangladesh, India, and Tanzania released 5 varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16838?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16842, 'Accelerating genetic gains to augment the productivity, end use quality, climate and disease resilience of chickpea varieties to benefit farmers in eastern and southern Africa and Asia', 'ICRISAT''s commitment to chickpea breeding has led to significant breakthroughs, focusing on developing varieties that are high-yielding and resilient to environmental challenges, especially drought. Currently, yield, end use quality, drought and heat tolerance, and resistant to Fusarium wilt and Ascochyta blight resistance are being improved in four pipelines targeting different Desi and Kabuli market segments in each subregion. Current cycle time is between 36 and 48 months with the intent to accelerate through speed-breeding. Genomic selection is being established for 100-seed weight, days to maturity, plant height, and pods per plant. High-throughput phenotyping approaches and breeding schemes are being improved to access greater genetic variance, increase selection intensity and accelerate genetic gains. Multi-environment trials cover up to 20 environments. Genetic resources are being explored for resistance to Ascochyta blight diseases and Fusarium wilt, and higher protein contents. Between 2022 and 2023, partners in India registered three chickpea varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16842?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16801, 'Accelerating genetic gains to augment the productivity and resilience of biofortified or dual-purpose pearl millet hybrids and varieties to benefit farmers in Africa and South Asia', 'ICRISAT''s pearl millet research and breeding is aimed at further enhancing the crop''s resilience, productivity, and nutritional quality to help farmers overcome challenges posed by climate change and scarce resources, ultimately promoting food security and uplifting livelihoods in arid and semi-arid regions across the globe. Grain yield, biofortification with zinc and iron, Striga, mildew and blast resistance are being improved in nine pipelines differing in maturity, targeted ecologies (better endowed,  drought or heat affected), and purpose (forage, dual-use, food). Current cycle times are at 36 months. Genomic selection is being established. High-throughput phenotyping approaches and breeding schemes are continuously being improved to access greater genetic variance, increase selection intensity and accelerate genetic gains. Multi-environment trials cover up to 33 environments per subregion. On-farm trialing with 563 growers established 20% higher yields of candidate hybrids. Between 2022 and 2024, partners in India and NIgeria released 11 varieties, nine of them biofortified.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16801?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3387, 'Decision support tools for site specific fertilizer recommendation in Burundi', 'ICT-support tools for site specific fertilizer recommendation are being developed for cassava farmers in Burundi in collaboration with the International Fertilizer Development Center, Fertilisants Organo-Minéraux Industries (the national agency in charge of fertilizer production in Burundi), and Institut des Sciences Agronomiques du Burundi (the national agricultural research institute). This is based on data from two-year experiments on cassava fertilization conducted by the project ‘Consortium for Improving Agriculture-based Livelihoods in Central Africa’ in the country using the three main fertilizers that Fertilisants Organo-Minéraux Industries produces (TOTAHAZA, IMBURA and BAGARA). The recommended rate of each fertilizer per agro-ecological zone and planting month (August to January) and the expected yield increase has been mapped.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3387?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(7372, 'Planning for voice: A novel approach to help women develop the skills they need to exercise voice and agency in their communities', 'IFPRI researchers developed the curriculum, ‘Planning for Voice,’ to equip women with key knowledge and skills to help them navigate the process of demanding assets through the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act 2005 (MGNREGA). It addresses both informational and skills gaps, designed to work with women in small groups within their existing social networks, so that each woman receives intensive focus and opportunities for practicing skills during a condensed time period. By the end of the course, participants should be able to: • Understand the basic process for requesting a MGNREGA asset and the key local stakeholders involved • Describe a  MGNREGA asset that could improve their individual lives or livelihoods or improve the community more broadly • Articulate different ways to play a role in influencing what their community decides to build • Develop a strategy to overcome foreseen barriers to exercising their voice and agency.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7372?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(9692, 'Spatial Production Allocation Model (SPAM) 2020 for country studies of crop production systems', 'IFPRI’s global cropping system model – Spatial Production Allocation Model (SPAM) comprises the estimates of crop area, yield and production for 40+ major crops under four rainfed and irrigated production systems across a global 5 arc minute grid. This is done through a downscaling approach that accounts for spatial variation in the biophysical conditions influencing the productivity of individual crops within the cropland extent, and that uses crop prices to weigh the gross revenue potential of alternate crops when considering how to prioritize the allocation of specific crops to individual gridcells. Based on the SPAM global model, we further developed the SPAM country model, so it is more suitable for regional and country studies. The most recent global SPAM products are outdated (global: 2010, Sub-Saharan Africa: 2017). Under the ClimBeR initiative, we update the input data to 2020 to capture the recent changes of crop production to develop climate-risk profiles.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9692?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7870, 'Released yam varieties identified for vulgarization', 'IITA''s yam breeding program achieved significant success, releasing 25 varieties in West Africa over the last five years. In Nigeria, four water yam and five white yam varieties were introduced. To deliver genetic gains, a pilot Demand Creation Trials (DCT) network tested six varieties with farmers across eight states on over 300 on-farms. Individual farmers received a new variety, comparing it with their local cultivar, providing feedback. The top three preferred yam cultivars were identified and demonstrated in community-based DCT plots across 11 states, engaging over 1700 farmers. Additionally, SAH plantlets of newly released varieties were multiplied to enhance quality planting material for trials. The collaboration with NARS under the BMGF-funded yam project played a pivotal role in these achievements.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7870?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(7309, 'A suite of digital tools to map the morphology of rivers to enable monitoring of changes due to river flow management', 'Implementation of e-flows or environmental flows is a requirement of water resource managers but is seldom adequately done. The Digital Innovation Initiative is researching application of digital tools to enable better management of rivers and implementation of e-flows. These tools include drone (UAV) mounted Lidar, Sonar, Ground Penetrating Radar and other sensors that deliver high resolution 3D data describing a river channel and allowing calculation of the river hydraulics. Generated data can be used not only to assess the discharge required to implement e-flows, but can also be used to monitor changes in river morphology over time.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7309?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4994, 'Cost Benefits of on-farm Anti-Microbial Use reducing interventions in Semi-Intensive poultry farming systems', 'Improve evidence-based on Anti-Microbial Use practices, farm characteristics, and production costs, as well as cost-benefits of farm-level Anti-Microbial Use reduction efforts by 30% in semi-intensive poultry farming systems. This is in accordance with the recommendation from the 2022 Muscat Declaration.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4994?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(12716, 'improved nappier fodder for Mixed Farming Systems of Mid-hills Nepal', 'Improved fodder (Napier) for mid-hill smallholder mixed farming systems to improve dairy livestock productivity for milk, reduce erosion and restore degraded lands.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12716?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(9911, 'Evaluating Banana Bunchy Top Disease [BBTD] Integrated Pest and Disease Management (IPDM) practices in Benin.', 'In 2023, data collection continued to determine the activity range of soap and botanical product formulations, alone and in mixtures (soap and botanical products) to control banana aphids. A dose dependent relationship was found for a pyrethrin preparation and a neem-based formulation (crude and commercial). However, mortalities were low, except when mixed with soap. These tests will continue in 2024 and will be used to determine potential formulations to test in the field. Field tests of soap and botanicals initiated in 2022/2023 were suspended due to the prolonged rainy season between March and December 2023. These tests will be resumed in 2024. 10 candidate generalist potential aphid predators have been indentified and will be studied in the field in 2024. Two potential parasitoid species are under functional study on banana aphid and have been sent for molecular sequence evaluation using barcoding gene sequences. These data will be available in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9911?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(7829, 'Assessing Farmer Preferences for Silvopastoral Production System Sustainable Intensification Interventions: Preliminary Results from Northern. Laos', 'In 2023, farmer preferences for silvopastoral sustainable intensification interventions involving both private and public good benefits assessed through mixed mode surveys based on Best-Worst Scaling (BWS) workshops, a Choice Experiment (CE) and a payments for ecosystem services conservation tender approach in two districts of Northern Laos. In 2024, the PES conservation tender approach was implemented and 534 farmer bid offers (involving ≈320 individual farmers) across 7 interventions in 17 communities in 2 Provinces were received. Of these xx bids were selected for implementation, considering cost-benefit ratio (e.g. cost per tree planted), conservation value (e.g. native species), and equity considerations (e.g. household''s land size, gender, number of households that could be supported). Selected bids included (1) 135 households participating in forage trials, (2) 118 households establishing living fences with over 40 tree species, (3) 62 households protecting trees on pastures, and (4) 17 households producing tree seedlings for inclusion in silvopastures', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7829?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(2220, '90 standard genebank operating procedures (SOPs) were drafted or updated to incorporate new improved protocols, procedures or technologies at AfricaRice, CIAT, CIMMYT, CIP, ICRISAT, IITA, ILRI, IRRI', 'In 2024, 90 standard genebank operating procedures (SOPs) were drafted or updated to incorporate new improved protocols, procedures or technologies at AfricaRice, CIAT, CIMMYT, CIP, ICRISAT, IITA, ILRI, IRRI. In 2023, 21 Standard operating procedures (SOPs) were drafted, 96 were updated and 76 were audited in ten genebanks.  In 2022, 49 SOPs have been newly drafted or modified with improved methods in all nine CGIAR genebanks. The new SOPs are the result of trialing new techniques and piloting of new technologies that have been found to improve existing seed germination, conservation, disease testing or other genebank procedures that are documented and implemented in CGIAR genebanks and germplasm health units. In 2022, examples of new SOPs include a protocol for high-throughput DNA extraction and a procedure for analysing genotypic data developed by CIAT and a dormancy-breaking protocol for African rice species in AfricaRice. Genebanks and GHUs are continuously attempting to improve genebank and phytosanitary procedures as a means to become more efficient.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2220?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8458, 'The Agroecology Initiative started implementing three agroecological business incubation and acceleration programs, one self-supported and two in collaboration with partners.', 'In 2024, the Agroecology Initiative implemented the following business incubation and acceleration programs, designed to encourage agribusinesses in embracing sustainable and eco-friendly agricultural practices:

1.  The Agroecological Business Incubator/Accelerator, developed by the Agroecology Initiative, provided funding for testing new agroecological business model ideas in Burkina Faso, Peru, Tunisia and Senegal.
2. The CGIAR Food Systems Accelerator (CFSA) 2024 cohort, a partnership with the Ukama Ustawi Initiative, boosted agroecological business models in Kenya and Zimbabwe. 
3. The Neycha Accelerator and Fund Cohort 2 Bootcamp, in partnership with Biovision, provided a transformative platform for agroecological entrepreneurs from Kenya to gain practical skills, insights, and mentorship to drive sustainable business growth.
These programs aim to foster new or redesigned agroecological business models, supporting the transition of food systems towards more sustainable and equitable production and consumption.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8458?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(8935, 'Breakthrough discovery on plant odour sources to mimic attractant pheromones', 'In Africa, food legumes like cowpeas and beans are vital protein sources but suffer yield losses of up to 80% due to insect pests. Smallholder farmers rely on insecticides, contributing to resistance and food safety concerns. This study explores a sustainable alternative using semiochemicals for pest management. Using coupled gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC–MS) and coupled GC-electroantennographic detection (EAD) analyses, this study identified 2-methylbutyl 2-methylbutanoate as the aggregation pheromone of Clavigralla elongata, a key legume pest. Field trials in East and West Africa showed this pheromone and its aanalog isopentyl butanoate, attracted multiple pests, including Maruca vitrata, Megalurothrips sjostedti, Bemisia tabaci, and the natural enemy Gryon fulviventris. Optimized pheromone traps significantly reduced pests and increased natural enemy density sixfold. Legume yields in pheromone-treated fields matched insecticide-treated ones, exceeding controls by 320–590%. These findings establish coreid bug pheromones as effective, sustainable tools for legume pest management without insecticides.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8935?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8869, 'Promotion of natural biological control of African rice gall midge (AfRGM) through habitat manipulation in Nigeria', 'In Africa, lowland rice is severely impacted by the African rice gall midge (AfRGM), Orseolia oryzivora. It has become a significant problem in Nigeria in recent years, with reports of its increased pest status. The larvae of AfRGM infect rice tillers during the vegetative growth stage and produce tubular galls called onion leaves or silver shoots, which causes crop damage. Infestation causes irreversible damage to the tillers, which inhibits their capacity to produce more leaves or a panicle. Recent interest in IPM has focused renewed attention on other approaches among which is the use of habitat manipulation for the conservation of natural enemies. Paspalum gall midge, O. bonzii infests Paspalum scrobiculatum, a common weed in rice fields. Paspalum gall midge cannot attack rice but is an alternative host for the two main parasitoids of AfRGM (Platygaster diplosisae – larval parasitoid and Aprostocetus procerae – pupal parasitoid).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8869?phase=3', NULL, NULL),
(8987, 'The Open Learning Portal: Centralized Education on Sustainable Agriculture Practices through Moodle-Based Platform featuring Microlearning Courses and Collaborative Tools', 'In alignment with EiA’s vision, the EiA LMS platform is developed on Moodle, this platform stands as a central repository for a diverse array of educational resources, specifically tailored to meet the needs of purpose-driven and demand-led partnerships within various Use Cases. The platform''s rich features include an extensive series of microlearning courses, interactive discussion forums, and timely announcements, all aimed at fostering a dynamic learning environment. It hosts a wealth of educational resources, complemented by engaging assessments and quizzes to enhance the learning experience. Progress tracking and user analytics are integral components, providing valuable insights for continuous improvement. Through the Open Learning Portal, the platform notably advances team capabilities. It offers a structured process for course development, starting from needs assessment, moving through content curation and storyline development, to comprehensive course review, user testing, and ultimately, the launch.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8987?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2161, 'A standardised quantitative methodology to locate data gaps in FishBase databases to prioritise Aquatic Foods Systems transformation', 'In Aquatic Food Systems, the adage "we don''t know what we don''t know" is particularly relevant. To truly understand the potential for aquatic foods system transformation, we must know where the largest data gaps are, and the effort vs impact trade-offs that exist in filling them.
This is a method to identify gaps in data and knowledge using the FishBase databases of aquatic biodiversity and traits for 11 countries of interest according to 4 key areas: topic, time, taxonomy, and territory.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2161?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(8208, 'Development of 100 days orange fleshed sweet potato variety/es to fit in yearly 3 crops cropping system of farmers in Bangladesh', 'In Bangladesh, it is primarily cultivated by smallholder farmers, with a growing focus on biofortified and early bulking varieties to combat malnutrition and improve yields. This study evaluated the yield performance and adaptability of early bulking sweet potato genotypes across different environments in Bangladesh using advanced yield trials. The AMMI (Additive Main Effects and Multiplicative Interaction) and joint regression analyses were conducted to assess genotype-by-environment interactions. The results indicated that genotype and environment significantly influenced sweet potato performance, with check variety SP12 exhibiting the highest yield. However, the assessment of viral disease incidence revealed that all the studied genotypes were susceptible to significant viral infections, which could undermine their overall viability in production systems. Consequently, while genotypes P4-22 and P2-551 demonstrated adaptability across environments, their severe susceptibility to virus diseases highlights the need to replicate the trials with clean material in the next season for a definitive conclusion.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8208?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(15718, 'Cooperative forage seed production and sharing system among dairy farmers of Bobo-Dioulasso (Burkina Faso)', 'In Bobo-Dioulasso (Burkina Faso), dairy farmers have a growing need for quality fodder. Faced with an inadequate local market for forage seeds, dairy industry stakeholders operating under a Dairy Innovation Platform (DIP) have been experimenting with a forage seed production and sharing system based on cooperation between dairy farmers. Mother dairy farmers set up a Fodder Demo-Plot on their farm consisting of 4 forage crops (maize, sorghum, cowpea and mucuna). For each crop, 2/3 of the FDP''s area was dedicated to forage production and the remaining 1/3 to seed production. Seeds produced are divided into three equal parts between the ''mother farmer'' and two ''babies farmers'' with a view to introducing three FDPs in year n+1.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15718?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3487, 'Introduction of legume vegetables in cereals production areas to improve soil health of smallholder farmers'' land', 'In collaboration with FRESH Initiative partner World Vegetable Center, this innovation development includes the sustainable intensification and diversification of maize based production systems, including maize intercropping with vegetable. This production method is currently testing in Allada municipality in Benin. Legume vegetables, such as Yard Long Bean and Mungbean, are intercropped with maize during the production season. In order to test the benefits of this approach, FRESH researchers will monitor soil health, maize crop yield, relative change in farmer income level, and availability of legume vegetables for consumers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3487?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1032, 'Cactus pear a drought-tolerant crop, provides food and livestock feed and thereby improves the livelihood of poor farmers in the dry areas', 'In collaboration with the FAO-ICARDA cactus network and NARS partners to promote and scale up cactus pear as a crop well-adapted to agricultural farming in the dry areas, that can be utilized by millions of poor farmers for important nutritional and income-creating purposes. Being a versatile crassulacean acid metabolism (CAM) with an extraordinary water use efficiency makes it a viable resilient important agricultural resource that enable farmers getting valuable products, including fruits for human consumption and fodder for livestock while helping to reduce the need for watering livestock due to its high-water content. This collaboration led to a substantial increase in demand for planting materials, resulting in providing improved planting materials, that can serve different agro-ecological zones. ICARDA alongside its NARS partners, has also carried out extensive awareness campaigns and capacity development activities to train farmers on promoting cactus pear as a multi-functional crop.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1032?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(10471, 'Digital Dojo: A suite of datasets and modeling tools that facilitate analysis of food, land, and water systems to inform policy and investment decisions', 'In collaboration with the World Modelers program, CGIAR Initiative on Digital Innovation soft-launched the Digital Dojo online platform that enables system dynamics modeling researchers in CGIAR and partner institutions to test what-if scenarios and facilitates informed policy and investment decisions at multiple scales (from a location-specific local-level to national and regional-scale). Built on an open-source modeling framework, Dojo, the Digital Dojo provides a suite of features for digital twin and system modeling researchers to register and transform datasets and operate containerized modeling tools. Additionally, its artificial intelligence-based AI Assistant tool allows users to conduct literature search and review  in natural languages, leveraging Large Language Models. As of December 2023, the platform hosts 27 models and 264 datasets, all of which have been contributed by partners throughout the development process. The platform is at the beta stage for the public testing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10471?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(9596, 'Digital Agriculture Plot: An in-situ farm monitoring system in the Dry Corridor of Guatemala', 'In early 2023, following the graduation of 26 professionals from the "Digital Agriculture" diploma at CUNORI in Chiquimula, Guatemala, the Digital Agricultural Plot was established. This concept, birthed from the diploma program, addresses the need for a real-world space, a plot, equipped with the latest digital monitoring technology. Here, innovative tools like sensors and Internet of Things (IoT) technologies are tested and applied to the unique challenges of Guatemala''s dry corridor. The aim is to improve agricultural practices in the region, focusing on being socially, environmentally, and economically effective, and directly benefiting the local community.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9596?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(12189, 'Optimized indigenous forages of Ethiopia for smallholder farmer small ruminant production', 'In Ethiopia, livestock primarily consume indigenous forages, which are traditionally provided either fresh or after undergoing various processing methods. Farmers'' preferences for using these forages is being assessed,  the nutritional value evaluated and the potential for further development and commercialization of different varieties of these forages explored.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12189?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(2182, 'Fish cage production in small reservoirs in Ghana to support the livelihoods and empowerment of empowerment of youth, women, and small-scale farmers', 'In Ghana’s North-East Region, an innovative aquaculture pilot initiative is transforming local communities by creating new livelihood opportunities. The completion of the first cycle of the aquaculture pilot marks a significant milestone for the participating communities.  2024 saw the completion of the harvest for the first cycle of production of fish in the reservoirs of four communities in Northern Ghana(Langbinsi, Nalerigu, Nansoni, and Tombu). The second cycle of production is now on-going and was partly financed from the earnings from the first round.
The success of the pilot has caught the attention of various stakeholders, including the Ministry of Fisheries and Aquaculture Development, which is now planning to expand the initiative across all 5 regions of Northern Ghana. 
The introduction of aquaculture is turning local dams into sources of growth and development. By utilizing aquaculture, the initiative not only boosts economic prospects but also fosters community resilience and pride.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2182?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(1099, 'Integrated-approach with Public Private Partnerships and community-based engagement through stakeholder platforms for vaccine delivery, monitoring and evaluation, and improved biosecurity for control of diseases in smallholder livestock systems', 'In Mali, vaccination campaigns against major endemic livestock diseases are conducted every year by the Direction Nationale des Services Vétérinaires (DNSV). Some of these diseases include contagious bovine pleuropneumoniae (CBPP), peste des petits ruminants (PPR) and bovine and ovine pasteurellosis. However, adequate vaccination coverage that will lead to sufficient herd immunity is often hard to achieve. Some of the challenges to vaccination are logistical, socio-economical, institutional and organisational. To address the various vaccination challenges, a multi-stakeholder approach through Innovation Platforms (IPs) was applied to create a sphere of learning, information sharing and interaction among vaccine supply chain stakeholders. The IP approach focused on three pillars: Communication and collaboration, Knowledge sharing through collective action and Capacity building. This IP approach has been successful in increasing uptake of vaccines by farmers and raising the health of livestock, which makes the livestock more productive."', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1099?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7745, 'Management of food legume pod borer and aphids through intercropping in Morocco', 'In Morocco, faba bean and kabuli chickpea are the most important legume crops for their high protein content and incomes for farmers. Farmers practice different cropping systems but their ecological services like pest management are not investigated. In this study, we investigated the roles of coriander intercropping as alternative management of pod borers in chickpea and faba bean aphids.  Results showed that Coriander intercropping with chickpea reduced the mean number of pod damage (28%) as compared with sole cropping (49%). In faba bean, the mean number of aphids was decreased in faba bean intercropped with Coriander as opposed to sole cropping.  Intercropping increased the number and diversity of natural enemies that affect the two pests. This innovation will be further demonstrated under farmer field conditions in 2023/24 cropping season.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7745?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(10041, 'Cassava peel as a nutritious animal feed for livestock feed suppliers and livestock-keepers', 'In Nigeria, processing cassava for food and industry yields around 15 million tons of wet peels annually. These peels are usually dumped near processing centres to rot or dry enough to be burned. Rotting heaps release methane into the air and a stinking effluent that pollutes nearby streams and underground water, while burning produces clouds of acrid smoke. However, when properly dried, peels can be an ingredient in animal feed. Previous attempts over two decades to use peels in animal feed failed to yield profitable options for drying wet peels at commercial scale, but recent research suggests that cassava peels can be processed into high-quality cassava peel (HQCP) products to be used as nutritious, low-cost animal feed ingredients by livestock feed suppliers and their customers (livestock-keepers)', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10041?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8536, 'Rapid multiplication technologies (semi-autotrophic hydroponics) to produce quality planting material of cassava improved varieties for early generation seed production by seed companies/producers', 'In Nigeria, the cassava seed system is successfully integrating newly released varieties into the value chain. The breeding program continues SAH plantlets propagation for late-stage multilocation trials, producing 14 varieties for various product profiles. A total of 123,567 plantlets were multiplied at the SAH facility, contributing to breeder seed production. Modifications to SAH technology, such as nodal procedures and innovative screen house systems, were well-received. While poundable and fine face varieties were downscaled due to low demand, poundable will be promoted in the Northern part of Nigeria. Additionally, 386 plantlets were produced for a potential candidate line in the RTB breeding program, showing superiority in trials. The IITA breeding program ensured nucleus seed stock materials for EGS companies, delivering pencil stems of high-demand and new varieties for breeder seed production. IITA-GoSeed established 60 ha breeder seed fields with varieties like game changer and baba 70 leading production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8536?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(2696, 'Seed policies and laws in Nature-Positive Solutions'' countries: understanding legal and policy context for strengthening farmer-managed seed systems', 'In order to improve farmer-managed seed systems in NATURE+ five countries, it''s essential to understand the current existing seed (related) policies and laws in the implementing countries to address and remove potential obstacles for effective support to farmer-managed seed systems. In several countries, right now seed policies and laws are hindering or preventing farmers from fully benefiting from minor crops and local varieties. This report highlights the policy and legal elements required to fully promote farmer-managed based seed systems as a critical innovation to develop a NATURE+ production system. The Initiative already developed a number of innovations related to seed system development, which can only be implemented at scale when a supportive policy and legal context exists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2696?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(17185, 'Self-paced online training modules on project level Women''s Empowerment in Agriculture Index (pro-WEAI)', 'In response to the growing demand for WEAI surveys, the CGIAR GENDER Impact Platform''s Methods Module developed a distance learning course on pro-WEAI. This online course teaches specific skills related to using pro-WEAI to meet the needs of various users: quantitative analysts, monitoring & evaluation specialists, donors, and field supervisors. Participants will receive digital certification upon successful completion of the course. The Pro-WEAI Foundations Module is available in English, French, and Arabic. This module introduces researchers and practitioners to the pro-WEAI tool, covering its background and practical application within project contexts. This module provides conceptual frameworks, resources, and approaches to integrating the index tools into projects, based on real-world experiences from the team that developed it. Completion of the Foundations Module is a prerequisite for all subsequent modules. The subsequent modules build on the Foundations Module and include:
Survey Fieldwork: Learn how to administer pro-WEAI surveys, from planning enumerator training to pilot testing and data collection. This module covers survey questions, response options, and fieldwork strategies.
Qualitative Analysis: Explore the qualitative methods used in pro-WEAI, including the rationale behind including qualitative methods, detailed protocols for different types of respondents, best practices for planning qualitative studies, and guidance on conducting analysis and disseminating results from qualitative pro-WEAI protocols.
Calculating the Index: Learn how to calculate pro-WEAI indicators and the pro-WEAI score. This module covers how to use the Stata do- and ado-files, read output tables and figures, and apply the results to your projects.
Interpreting and Presenting Pro-WEAI Results: Learn how to interpret both the qualitative and quantitative findings from pro-WEAI surveys. This module focuses on deriving insights for future programming and project management, building on the previous modules.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17185?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5227, 'Brachiarea integration into maize-based cropping system for smallholder farmers multi-benefit, resilient and climate smart agriculture', 'In Ruzizi plain of DRC, research activities on the integration of Brachiarea into maize-based system to mitigate the the major agricultural constrains in the region (animal feed lack, soil fertility depletion and climate change) are ongoing. So far, the most performing Brachiarea varieties were identified, maize and Brachiarea productivity, carbon incorporation into the soil and soil coverage when Brachiarea is integrated into the system were assessed. The feasibility and productivity of maize resowed in Brachiarea plot established during the last cropping season were evaluated. The feasibility and productivity of replicating Brachiarea suckers to overcome the issue of no seed availability and high cost were assessed and farmers’ impression and opinions on Brachiarea-maize intercropping system were received and integrated in next step of the technology development. During last season, we carried out experiment and identified the best and cost-efficient way to control weeds at early growth stage, in the Brachiarea field.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5227?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(17046, 'Agroecology business model for the reduction of imports: production of composite bread incorporating 30% millet as a substitute for wheat', 'In Senegal, approximately 10,000,000 baguettes of bread are consumed every day. Almost all of this bread is produced from wheat, a cereal imported and subsidized by the State of Senegal. This generates expenses of several billion CFA francs per year, representing a considerable loss of income for local sectors and small producers. However, agri-food research has shown that it is possible to produce compound bread, incorporating a high percentage of local cereals, such as millet. Although naturally gluten-free, millet can be used effectively in bread making. The Senegalese Institute of Agricultural Research (ISRA) has developed short-cycle millet varieties, adapted to climate change and producing large grains, ideal for flour production, thus meeting the needs of millers and bakers. It is in this context that we are developing compound bread, with an incorporation rate of 30% millet, making it possible to reduce wheat imports by the same proportion. In the past, millet was mainly sold raw in markets, without adding value. Thanks to this innovation, millet is now processed locally, contributing to wealth creation, reducing imports and generating jobs in local communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17046?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4726, 'Training package on animal health, welfare and biosecurity for cattle and pig livestock keepers and animal health workers in Vietnam, for increased livestock productivity and profitability', 'In Son La province, north-west Vietnam, a tailored training package has been introduced for smallholder farmers and animal health professionals under the Li Chan project (CRP Livestock). In Sustainable Animal Productivity (SAPLING) we''ve adapted and enhanced these materials to better address the needs of the Son La community.  This innovation emphasizes animal health biosecurity and recent advancements in animal health. For the farmers, the training package includes a detailed training guideline, complemented by training materials, focusing on three key pillars: biosecurity, animal health, and animal welfare. Animal health professional training package is more specialized for those who already have basic knowledge of animal health, which covers biosecurity, animal diseases, vaccines and drugs, and outbreak control. Through this training material, our goal is to strengthen animal health and productivity in the targeted area. The innovation is already being adapted for a Mobile application (app.) in a bilateral project', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4726?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(19741, 'Group certificates of customary rights of occupancy for securing shared grazing lands in Tanzania', 'In Tanzania policy and legislation provides for the provision of certificates of customary rights of occupancy (CCROs). However commonly these are issued on an individual basis and challenges arise when trying to secure tenure for a group that shares resources. The Livestock and Climate Initiative has been working with the Government of Tanzania to pilot the provision of group CCROs to livestock keeper associations. In 2022 the first CCROs were issued to four villages in a shared grazing land cluster called OLENGAPA securing around 85,000 hectares of grazing land. In late 2024 another group CCRO was issued to the Amei village livestock keepers association to secure 50,588 hectares of shared grazing land benefiting 15,407 livestock keepers predominantly pastoralists. Amei is part of the four village cluster of villages making up the ALOLLE shared grazing land.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19741?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(9470, 'Development of education and training materials for agricultural practices based on farmers'' needs provided through digtal application (Zalo) for farmers in Vietnam', 'In the context of AMD-WP1 and aligning with the ongoing work on Agro-climatic Bulletin (ACB) development under AMD-WP3 in Vietnam, the project team from the Alliance of Bioversity International and CIAT, in collaboration with the Dragon Institute at Can Tho University and the Department of Crop Production, developed online training materials for rice farmers in the Mekong River Delta. The goal is to provide high-quality, user-friendly e-learning materials. These will be disseminated through the Zalo messaging app, along with climate/practice advisory cards of the ACBs. To ensure the materials meet farmers'' needs, six gender-segregated focus group discussions (FGDs) were conducted across three communes in Hau Giang and Soc Trang. These discussions identified the top ten agricultural practices that farmers find important yet challenging. The team reviewed existing training materials and created ten new technical resources, including blogs with images and videos, to offer practical guidance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9470?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3242, 'Appropriate and promising diversification options in rice-based systems with vegetable and legume in farmers’ fields in Cote d’Ivoire', 'In the integrated rice-cucumber and rice-tomato systems, cucumber or tomato is cultivated during the dry season (March–May), and rice serves as the rotation crop during the wet season (June – October). The residue of cucumber or tomato is incorporated into the soil before rice cultivation. These integrated cropping systems prove to be well-suited for lowlands with water availability either through surface water (river or dam) or shallow wells during the dry season. As compared to existing farming practices where rice is solely cultivated in the wet season, these systems substantially increased farmers'' net income by 13,651 to 22,357 USD/ha in an on-farm participatory experiment at two rainfed lowland sites, Mbe and Lokapkli, in the Bouake region of Cote d’Ivoire. This suggests that these systems could be promoted in rainfed lowland systems to increase farmers’ income and provide opportunities for raising household dietary diversity through the consumption of nutritious vegetables.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3242?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(4646, 'Feeding strategies based on locally available feeds, preferred improved forages and associated farmer-friendly training materials targeting women and men cattle and pig producers', 'In the Northwest Highlands of Vietnam, cattle and pigs are characterized by low productivity and mainly fed with low quality forages, crop residues and food waste. Feed and forage quality is generally of low quality resulting in low livestock output. Assessments conducted in the region have identified the main feed-related challenges including winter feed shortage, poor diets, and low yield of available forages. Farmers also have limited knowledge on suitable forage types and management, feed processing and preservation, which greatly affects livestock productivity. Gender-responsive feed intervention strategies that can address these context-specific challenges, mainly winter feed shortage, can greatly improve livestock productivity and efficiency. Such interventions include capacity building of women and men farmers, extension & vet staff on utilization of locally available feed resources and purchased feeds, feed conservation, diet formulation, and feeding regimes, and promoting improved forage varieties (establishment, management, and utilization), for cattle and pigs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4646?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(16681, ' Development of forage mixtures of vetch, triticale and oats to improve biomass production, soil health and forage quality for small ruminants in Tunisia', 'In Tunisia, some of the main constraints in crop-livestock production systems are the monoculture of cereals, which leads to poor soil health, and lack of nutrient-rich forages to keep small ruminants in good health. 
Some forage mixtures (Vetch-Oat, Vetch-Triticale, Vetch-Oat - Triticale), are being pre-defined and recommended by the research team composed of INRAT (Institut National de Recherche Agronomique de Tunis), Office of livestock and pasture (OEP) and ICARDA. Different combinations for forage seed mixture are tested in different climatic  and soil contexts, and recommendations are provided to a private seed company COTUGRAIN, to tailor its seed mixtures products for commercialization.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16681?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7359, 'Public Private Partnership to Foster Availability of Forage Seed Mixtures in Tunisia', 'In Tunisia, some of the major constraints in crop-livestock production systems are monoculture of cereals leading to poor soil health and lack of nutrient rich forages for the key small ruminant activity. Forage seeds were insufficient, and no national seed company had been engaged in forage seed production as this wasn''t considered beneficial to them.
In 2019, ICARDA and the National Institute of Agronomic Research of Tunisia – INRAT started a facilitation process for a Public-private partnerships through cooperation and engaging with COTUGRAIN, a private seed production and commercialization company. The partnership is set around the commercialization of some forage crop seeds, in addition to some forage mixtures (Vetch-Oat, Vetch-Triticale, Vetch-Oat - Triticale), pre-defined and advised by the research team composed of INRAT. Different formula for forage seed mixture were tested, recommendations were provided to the private company COTUGRAIN, to tailor its seed mixtures products for commercialization. 
Recently, Cotugrain acknowledged the integration of the co-design methodology into their projects, which has made it possible to create seed solutions adapted to new agroecological conditions. Cooperation with partners such as ICARDA has made it possible to increase the area cultivated with vetch and mixture seeds (ID 26 it doubles each year for mixture seeds it has gone from 10T to 30T to 80T), particularly for adaptation to changing climatic conditions. The enterprise has also strengthened its interactions with research institutions (INRAT, ICARDA) and has set up a tripartite agreement for the dissemination of adapted seeds and the application of agroecological practices on a larger scale"', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7359?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4623, 'App for use by smallholder pig keepers in Uganda to determine the profit or loss of their household pig enteprises (Pig Profit $ app)', 'In Uganda a key objective for keeping pigs is income, yet most smallholder pig keepers do not track the profit of their household pig enterprise.  This app is designed to address this gap.  Farmers enter all revenue, as well as all costs, associated with pig keeping, and the app reports profit (or loss) over a time-period set by the user. This information will aid the farmers to better understand the economic performane of their pig enterprice, for more informed decision making.  The app is farmer friendly, and in the national language of Luganda as well as English.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4623?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(1818, 'An innovative model for collaborating across CGIAR centers to facilitate a meta-analysis of research on how to increase adoption of new varieties', 'In Work Package 3, which focuses on behavioral intelligence, we seek to pinpoint interventions that induce faster uptake of new varieties. Specifically, we consider varieties which we believe will have effects in the 5 CGIAR impact areas. We run parallel experiments in Ethiopia, India, Kenya, Nigeria, and Uganda to allow us to generate externally applicable knowledge about the general constraints, as well as unique local barriers or opportunities, to adoption of new varieties that farmers face. By coordinating closely on what interventions to implement, and implementing joint survey modules, we will be able to pool the data and conduct a meta-analysis to study what interventions effectively increase adoption of varieties, and the extent to which their effectiveness depends on the traits of the promoted varieties. The evidence attached to this innovation provides an overview of registered trials and design for meta-analysis research, which will evaluate common intervention across different contexts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1818?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(1103, 'Financial tool targeted to help pastoralists cope with drought related risks in the drylands of Africa', 'Index-based drought risk financing and insurance (IBDRFI) instruments have been designed to protect pastoralists in the face of drought. In all Index-based livestock insurance (IBLI) products,Normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) time series are elaborated to obtain an area-aggregated index of relative seasonal forage availability. When the index falls below a pre-defined threshold, payouts are triggered and increase proportionally to the severity of estimated forage scarcity. This assumes that, when forage is scarce, grazing resources are depleted quickly, leading to deteriorating livestock conditions and increased livestock mortality. Pastoralists could use the payouts to make production decisions that reduce their herd losses during the drought including, purchasing animal fodder, water or veterinary services. IBDRFI initiatives have been so far implemented in Africa with different modalities, including retail microinsurance products, macrolevel insurance schemes for social livelihood protection, or scalability mechanisms of social safety net programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1103?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5097, 'Individualized extension approach (Dairy Farmer Advisor) that integrates advisory services with delivery of proven dairy technologies to smallholder dairy farmers for enhanced profitability of dairy farm enterprises.', 'Individualized advisory services for smallholder dary farmers, delivered by shared farm managers (dairy farm advisors -DFAs) who leverage advisory services to catalyze demand for complementary dairy inputs and services. The DFAs provide dedicated and targeted enterprise management services to farmers, visiting farms regularly to provide husbandry and production decision support with the aim of realizing a jointly agreed profitability targets.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5097?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8837, 'Elite yam clones selected for release nomination and on-farm testing to benefit farmers and NARS breeders', 'Innovative yam breeding trials have identified elite clones and selected superior candidates for variety nomination, including two regional variety trials spanning two years across West Africa. Five top candidates from each trial are undergoing seed increase for on-farm testing in 2024, with two additional nominees also slated for field evaluation. These elite clones offer promising varieties for release, benefitting farmers who will grow them and NARS breeders who can utilize them in cross-breeding programs to enhance yam breeding efforts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8837?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(4075, 'Efficacy of eco-friendly biopesticides for control of African Rice Gall Midge and rice stem borers', 'Insect pests such as the rice stem borers (African rice gall midge (AfRGM) Orseolia oryzivora, and the stalk-eyed flies – Diopsis spp. are among the economically most important pests of rainfed upland/lowland and irrigated rice in West Africa. The larvae of stem borers cause significantly yield loss during the vegetative (seedlings to panicle initiation) and reproductive stages by producing silver shoots/deadhearts and whiteheads, respectively, which prevent panicle development. The extensive use of synthetic insecticides to control these pests has raised concerns regarding environmental and human health. Consequently, this has necessitated the need for alternative control measures such as the use of biopesticides: Entomopathogenic fungi and botanicals. In the present study, two Entomopathogenic fungi (Metarhizium anisopliae(MET 31); Beauveria bassiana (Bb11) and two botanical biopesticides (Azadirachta indica (Raktin); Eucalyptus sp. (Lyptol)) were assessed against (AfRGM) and Diopsis species in Nigeria.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4075?phase=3', 1, NULL),
(1943, 'Increase maize and legume yields in Malawi through cropping arrangement and soil fertility management', 'Integrate sustainable cropping systems i.e. cropping arrangements (for example, two rows of maize and two rows of soybean or cowpea) and integrated soil fertility management i.e. inoculants, site-specific fertilizer recommendation, and manure to enhance livestock feed, soil fertility and crop yields.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1943?phase=1', 6, NULL),
(10108, 'Integrated Agriculture-Aquaculture (IAA) systems that produce aquatic and terrestrial foods on the same land using the resources from one to benefit the other.', 'Integrated Agriculture-Aquaculture (IAA) systems are diversified farming systems where aquatic and terrestrial foods are grown together on the same plot using the resources from one to benefit the other. In Egypt, for example, the production of fish such as Nile tilapia, mullet, and African catfish creates nutrient-rich water that can then be used to irrigate crops such as date palm, olives, figs, pomegranates, guava, onions, sugar beets, and tomato. IAA includes a range of technologies and methods that can be put together and adapted to each farming situation as needed. IAA is one of the most promising and fastest-growing food production systems in Egypt since it enhances production diversity, land productivity, water efficiency and nutrient cycling on farms.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10108?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(12043, 'Intelligent Agricultural Systems Advisory Tool-A Digital Platform for Scalable Contextual Agro-Advisories', 'Intelligent Agricultural Systems Advisory Tool (iSAT) is a digital platform ICRISAT and its partners developed to deliver contextual, weather-based agro-advisories at scale. To address the growing need for precise and timely agricultural guidance, iSAT integrates AI, local climate data, and crop-specific information to support farmers'' decision-making and risk management. Piloted successfully in Ananthapur,  India the platform is currently being scaled in Odisha, India, reaching over 6000 farmers. With ongoing AI testing, iSAT aims to enhance advisory accuracy and personalization, empowering farmers and agribusinesses to improve productivity and climate resilience.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12043?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(16614, 'IWMI and African Group of Negotiators Expert Support (AGNES) have developed an Nationally Determined Contributions (NDC) Capacity Scorecard to meet countries'' NDC targets and identify potential areas for capacity building in government and national institutions', 'International Water Management Institute (IWMI) and the Africa Group of Negotiators Expert Support (AGNES) identified that countries were not aligning their capacity for implementation with their response to the UNFCCC. In response, IWMI and AGNES have developed an NDC Capacity Scorecard to provide an objective measure for countries to ascertain whether they have the capability to meet their NDC targets and identify potential areas for capacity building in government and national institutions. It is important for countries to have this understanding of their capability to deliver, given that they  will be held to account for the targets that are set. The consequence of only focusing on ever increasing targets for emissions reduction is that most countries in the Global South don’t have the capacity to achieve the targets, which sets them up for failure. A critical consideration is how to achieve a “whole of government” approach that links the business and private sector to develop a collaborative, sustainable approach. In a session held at the recent COP29 in Baku, Azerbaijan, this question was explored with inputs from South Africa, Kenya, Uganda and the National Business Initiative of South Africa.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16614?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8779, 'Artificial intelligence tool developed to identify rice accessions in genebanks', 'IRRI genebank staff with private sector partners, have imaged thousands of rice seeds and panicles and generated data to train AI tool to discern seed morphological characteristics and identify rice accessions. The AI tool has been trained on 100,000 accessions and successfully identifying accessions with morphological distinctions. The tool is improving the efficiency in managing 130K rice collections and can also contribute to high-throughput phenotyping approaches. As a result of the AI tool development, the IRRI genebank won an award from Google in 2023 to refine the tool and use it to carry out a large-scale evaluation for tolerance to flooding, salinity and drought. Using the tool, over 60k IRG accessions were screened for abiotic stresses (drought, submergence, salinity).  Best accessions for these traits identified. By the end of 2024, >125,000 accessions imaged will be used for retraining the model to select the next batch of accessions for  abiotic screening.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8779?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7262, 'NUTRIPIZZA PIZZA', 'It is a pizza made from broccoli stem flour, malt bran (byproducts of the food industry) and other cereal flours as chickpea, tarwi and almond flour. The target audience is young people and adults who are looking for a quick, novel and healthy alternative to conventional pizzas and want to have a rich, nutritious experience in a fast food with high protein and functional content.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7262?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(7720, 'Software based in Machine Learning to classify the severity of Late blight in potato crops', 'It is a Random Forest algorithm developed in Python to classify 21 categories of Late blight severity in potato cops, where the class 0 is soil and from 1 to 21 were symptom score measured by experts in field. It use as inputs aerial multispectral images acquired of two potato trials.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7720?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5084, 'Development and Application of a Quantitative Tool for Measuring Knowledge, Attitudes, and Practices Among Animal Health Professionals', 'It is important to understand how and why antibiotics are used. Usage of such can be categorized into three key areas:
    Knowledge: This involves assessing what veterinarians, farmers, and other stakeholders know about antibiotics and how resistance develops.
    Attitudes: Understanding the attitudes of individuals within the target population towards antibiotic use.
    Practices: Examining how antibiotics are prescribed and used in practice.
Collecting this information can be accomplished through Knowledge, Attitude and Practice surveys. While many studies use the Knowledge, Attitude and Practice surveys, they often fail to collect data comprehensively or use unvalidated instruments. To address these shortcomings, we aim to develop a Knowledge Attitude Practice survey, tailored for small-holder dairy farmers and veterinarians in Kenya. The project also explores the application of Item Response Theory (IRT) modeling in evaluating measurement scales.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5084?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(3236, 'Towards the establishment of Agroecological Living Landscapes (ALLs) in eight countries: Considerations for stakeholder engagement for sustainable agroecological transitions', 'Key activities implemented towards the establishment of Agroecological Living Landscpaes included stakeholder (STH) mapping, the assessment of existing multistakeholder initiatives, and, in some cases, implementation of the early phases of a visioning exercise.  National and local multi-stakeholder events, review of existing documentation, semi-structured interviews and focus group discussions were used to varying degrees by each country. Agreements are now being negotiated with key national or local stakeholders in some countries (e.g. Tunisia, Kenya) to foster and clarify their active participation in the Initiative and in the decision-making regarding ALL identification and establishment. A rather large number of stakeholders were identified in each country during stakeholder mapping and a total of 1.346 individual Food System Actors (FSA) have been engaged to date across the 7 countries', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3236?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5643, 'Assessment on improving water use efficiency to increase agriculture productivity in mid-hills of Nepal through micro irrigation practices by International Water Management Institute', 'Khotang and Surkhet lie in the mid-hills of Nepal and are drought-prone areas. Insufficient water availability in both areas has impacted agricultural productivity. During the participatory innovation selection process for various components of Mixed Farming System, farmers ranked micro-irrigation as their priority innovation to increase agriculture productivity. Hence, interested farmers were given training on drip irrigation practices, along with other micro-irrigation techniques such as  multiple water use systems. 70 drip irrigation kits and 113 sprinkler heads are distributed to the farmers for the pilot study. Farmers are selected based on inclusive criteria to ensure that women and marginalized farmers are not left behind.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5643?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(2232, 'A landrace accession at the Bioversity genebank was evaluated to have promising characteristics for cultivation in Kerala', 'Kluai Namwa Khom, a dwarf variety from the ABB Pisang Awak subgroup was provided by the Bioversity genebank for evaluation in Kerala, India. The study has indicated that the landrace is a promising candidate for introduction as a table banana for cultivation in areas prone to high winds.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2232?phase=1', 5, NULL),
(7071, 'Genetic diversity of two poleroviruses affecting faba bean and chickpea crops in Tunisia', 'Knowledge of genetic variation and the exact identity of viruses affecting legume crops is essential for breeding for resistance and effective crop management.  Based on the serological results, representative chickpea and faba bean isolates of Chickpea chlorotic stunt virus (CpCSV) and Beet western yellows virus (BWYV) were selected for further molecular analysis with RT-PCR and sequencing of the partial coat protein gene. Molecular analyses recognized that Turnip yellows virus (TuYV) and Brassica yellows virus (BrYV) are newly identified viruses closely related to, but significantly different from BWYV. Phylogenetic analysis showed that Tunisian isolates of BWYV are clustered with similar isolates from Cyprus and Türkiye, where CpCSV Tunisian isolates are clustered with reference accessions under Serogroup-II. The outcome of this study will provide Tunisian scientists and breeders with a clearer understanding of viruses that may pose a threat to legume crops, their putative inoculum sources and the required crop management options.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7071?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(9357, 'Rice-Fish Suitability Decision Support System Modelling to inform the sustainable planning and management of rice-fish systems leading to improved food security, nutrition, incomes, and employment in Myanmar and Cambodia', 'Known as the rice-fish decision support system (DSS), it is a bundle of digital and analytical tools used to inform the planning and management of rice-fish systems. Suitability maps derived from the DSS have shown the potential for the transformation of 15,700 km2 across the Ayeyarwady region, supporting improved local food security, nutritional security, incomes, and employment. As well as helping identify areas most suitable to the introduction of rice-fish polyculture, the tools help ensure that the needs of stakeholders are taken into consideration. A similar Decision Support System (DSS) for rice-fish suitability is now being developed for Cambodia following the same approach and method as the one in place for Myanmar. In addition, the DSS for Myanmar has now been refined at a lower scale to support decisions at the local level.

2024 the innovation is still relevant and active but no update.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9357?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8227, 'Small farm machinery to empower women and youth in the Mixed Farming Systems', 'Labor scarcity, rising wage rate, drudgery are major problems in the Mixed Farming Systems in Bangladesh. Mechanization of agricultural production system is very limited. The increased mechanization of crop-livestock system is crucial to increase productivity, profitability, and sustainability of the Mixed Farming Systems. Given the dominance of smallholder farmers, small farm machines have huge scope in the Mixed Farming Systems of Bangladesh. The Initiative is testing small farm machines (fodder chopper, mini tiller, PTOS, reaper, mini rice mill, and oil extractor) as mechanization service provision by women and youth. In the model villages women and youth entrepreneurs have been supported with different types of small-scale agri-machinery. This report describes the profiles of the women benefciaries, the type of agri-machinery, mechanization services in the village, and initial income generation by entrepreneurs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8227?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(14933, 'Restoration Co-Design (ReCoDe): fusing global with local knowledge for cost-effective, scalable, sustainable restoration', 'Land restoration can increase agricultural and ecological productivity where restoration helps producers achieve their livelihood objectives cost-effectively and sustainably. Restoration Co-Design (ReCoDe) is a structured process for fitting restoration techniques based on global and/or local knowledge into the contexts of local management systems. Producers (pastoralists, farmers), practitioners (NGO, government) and researchers (ILRI and partners) co-design restoration protocols, producers test them, and the results are scaled up in producers'' restoration plans. ReCoDe is an efficient approach for designing restoration of rangelands, drylands, pastoralist and other communal grazing lands, and other challenging environments for restoration.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14933?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8692, 'Development of a high-throughput genotyping platform to monitor rice blast pathogens from field samples', 'Leaf blast in rice, caused by Magnaporthe oryzae, is challenging to manage due to diverse avirulence (AVR) genes. Effective resistance deployment requires detecting AVR genes specific to M. oryzae strains. The pathogen has four populations based on genomic characteristics (genome-wide SNP data), each with distinct pathogenicity patterns. We developed and validated 8 SNP markers to distinguish these populations and 39 markers targeting AVR genes, including AVR-Pizt, AVR-Pii, AVR-Pia, AVR-Pik, AVR-Pib, AVR-Pi9, and AVR-Pita. Using PACE, 26 markers showed ≥80% sensitivity, 50% specificity, or 81% accuracy for monitoring. Additional markers are under development, with successful ones integrated into the PathoTracer platform and validated with isolates from other countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8692?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(9865, 'Simulation modelling of leaf blast, neck blast, and bacterial blight epidemics to identify risk factors, develop management recommendations, and reduce yield losses under different climate change scenarios.', 'Leaf blast, neck blast and bacterial blight are the most important rice diseases. Simulation modelling will facilitate the identification of  risk factors, develop  management recommendations, and reduce yield losses under different climate change scenarios. Simulation models for leaf blast and bacterial blight were improved and neck blast was developed based on EPIRICE, a generic model for rice diseases. Infection rates were mainly driven by relative humidity, temperature, rainfall, and crop age. The values of parameters were based on field experiments, pest surveys and published literature. The coupling point for the leaf blast and neck blast models was a linear regression on the effect of leaf blast severity on neck blast incidence. Simulated levels of leaf blast severity, neck blast incidence and bacterial blight incidence were generally higher in the wet season than in the dry season. Additional research activities are necessary to improve the performance of the models.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9865?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(5372, 'CGIAR Gender Equality Initiative''s Learning labs to promote learning among stakeholders including development agencies, farmers and researchers on the gender-responsive socio-technical innovation bundling approach to enhance resilience of women farmers', 'Learning labs can be understood as multisectoral, multistakeholder and transdisciplinary spaces where practice and research interact and learning occurs through the design, implementation and monitoring of Socio-technical  innovation bundles (STIB) to build resilience and empowerment of men , women and youth farmers across Africa and Asia. We have co-developed and designed learning labs with all relevant value chain actors. We intend to measure what combinations work where and why and, provide this learning to next users who could scale these bundles.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5372?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16685, 'Rhizobial inoculation of forage legume seeds in Tunisia to enhance symbiotic nitrogen fixation, increase biomass production and promote sustainable agroecological practices in forage systems', 'Leguminous forage crops, including sulla, faba beans, and vetch, establish symbiotic relationships with specific Rhizobia bacteria in their root nodules, enabling biological nitrogen fixation. This innovation involves inoculating forage legume seeds with carefully selected rhizobia strains tailored to crop species and local environmental conditions. The specific Rhizobia bacteria enhanced nitrogen fixation, leading to improved biomass yield, superior forage quality ( higher protein content), and increased crop resilience. Post-harvest, residual fixed nitrogen enriches the soil, benefiting subsequent crops like cereals by reducing the need for synthetic nitrogen fertilizers. This reduces production costs for farmers, improves soil fertility, and minimizes the ecological impact of agriculture. By integrating rhizobial inoculation into forage crop systems,  this innovation promotes sustainable agricultural practices while supporting soil health, crop productivity, and long-term agroecological balance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16685?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8107, 'Integrated management of chickpea and lentil viruses and their vectors in North Shewa, Ethiopia', 'Lentil and chickpea are the most important legume crops grown in the mid highlands of central Ethiopia. Productivity of both crops is affected by several biotic factors such as pod borers, aphids, wilt/root rot, foliar diseases, and viruses. Most of the plant viruses are transmitted by many aphid species and thus management of insect vectors is one of the main options for controlling viral diseases. On-farm trials were conducted at Enewary, North Shewa in 2022/23 main cropping season to identify best options for the management of emerging lentil and chickpea virus diseases and their aphid vectors. Spraying insecticides significantly minimized aphid populations and reduced the incidence of virus symptoms. The highest lentil biomass and grain yield was recorded for the early sowing date combined with insecticide sprays. The results obtained indicated that management of viruses and aphid vectors can be achieved through adjusting sowing date combined with limited insecticide sprays.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8107?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(8029, 'An Artificial Intelligence-based scientific data query tool for user-friendly access and analysis of the African Dairy Genetic Gains (ADGG) database', 'Leveraging recent developments in Large Language Models (LLMs), a new Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based data query tool was developed to enable user-friendly access to scientific data collected and managed by the African Dairy Genetic Gains (ADGG) program. Currently intended users are extension workers who visit the farm to collect data for ADGG program and progressive farmers who wish to query the data collected about their farms and animals using Natural Language Inputs. The natural language inputs given by the users automatically generate Structured Query Language (SQL) queries and return responses in a user-friendly format. This approach bypasses the need for technical knowledge, allowing non-specialist users like farmers to interact with complex databases. Importantly, the system maintains context, allowing for intuitive exchanges. Additionally, it stores queries, SQL, and responses, aiding researchers to refine the process. Standard security protocols protect the database since the AI doesn''t directly interact with it.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8029?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(7703, 'Guide on flexible varietal licensing approaches and options for national partners', 'Licensing agreements are an important tool that public research institutions can use to increase dissemination of publicly-bred varieties. Licensing can formalize the relationship between public and private actors in the seed sector, increase the availability and affordability of quality seed, and improve adoption of technology and, ultimately, farmers’ access to seed. While some NARES have not yet implemented licensing programs, others have, with varied licensing strategies across NARES in Africa. This Guide is designed as a tool for public research institutions and their partners, including CGIAR Centers, to guide the transfer of rights and authorization at the national level in order to use developed material and registered varieties for commercial purposes, in exchange for a predetermined payment (e.g., royalties), or performance of particular conditions. New Markets Lab, a NGO specializing in legal and regulatory analysis, has been responsible for this work which is in collaboration with Seed Equal WP5.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7703?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(4197, 'Developing phenology models to optimize biological control against the fall armyworm in Africa', 'Life table data for three native natural enemies of the fall armyworm, namely Cotesia icipe, Chelonus bifoveolatus and Charops diversipes were completed at the five temperature levels 20, 25, 28, 30 and 32 C. In addition, similar studies were concluded with the noctuid pest together with Cotesia marginiventris and Chelonus insularis, two exotic parasitoids newly introduced to Africa for use in biological control campaigns against the fall armyworm. These data are currently used to develop phenology-based simulation models to produce a decision tool to optimize biological control efficiency against the fall armyworm under different climatic and ecological scenarios.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4197?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(12287, 'Augmentation workflows for scaling of major genes in mainstream breeding programs', 'Line augmentation aims to create a critical mass of elite material containing valuable new genes to enable simple and easy scaling through mainstream breeding. Augmentation workflows have been implemented in rice. At IRRI, a pyramid of 3 blast resistance genes (Pi9+Pi35+Pikh) has been completed into 40 elite backgrounds nominated by breeding programs. At AfricaRice augmentation has been initiated into 15 elite breeding lines for blast and rice yellow mottle virus resistance. Evaluation of breeding value of completed lines shows these match or exceed the original recipient breeding value.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12287?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4636, 'Livestock Champions for delivery of gender-sensitive Social Behavior Change Communication (SBCC) package to small ruminant producers in Mali', 'Livestock champions models will be created and mentored by the project. They will be trained on the behavioural change communication package to improve their knowledge on best practices on herd management, to increase farmer-level capacity on best-practices in small-ruminant management.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4636?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(2694, 'Dynamic evolutionary populations of productivity of mixtures of different of barley in Ethiopian Highlands', 'Maintaining inter-varietal crop diversity, created through evolutionary plant breeding (EPB), on farms is proven to improve farmers productivity, resilience to climate change and enhance dietary diversity. We have tested evolutionary populations created through Evolutionary Plant Breeding in Ethiopia, Bhutan, Iran, Jordan, Nepal and Uganda for four crops: Barley, beans, wheat and rice and identified superior performing populations under different farming systems. Integration of evolutionary plant breeding to Sustainable Intensification of Mixed farming System (SI-MFS) in target countries is important to sustain crop production sector of target farming systems. At least one composite or mixture population is developed for each country targeted by the SI-MFS initiative.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2694?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(19185, 'Maize Seed Field and Maize Lethal Necrosis (MLN) Tracker: A Check, Detect, and Reporting System for maize clean seed production and prevention of maize lethal necrosis spread', 'Maize Seed Field and MLN Tracker: A Check, Detect, and Reporting WebApp for maize clean seed production and prevention of maize lethal necrosis (MLN) spread. The App, known as Maize Clean Seed Production Tracker, is an Android-based Open Data Kit (ODK) software enabled through a web-based toolbox to support data management and decisions for conducting MLN surveillance in a time series and to implement Standard Operating Procedures for MLN pathogen-free commercial seed production. From the phytosanitary perspective, it is critical to follow rigorous procedures to produce and exchange MLN pathogen-free commercial maize seed within and between countries to prevent the spread and negative impact of the disease on maize production. The tool was developed and piloted for validation in Kenya. The tool helps monitor proactive MLN risk mitigation in real time. Together with the MLN surveillance data portal, it guides the establishment of seed fields in low or no-disease-pressure sites.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19185?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(8551, 'Sweetpotato genotypes selected for advanced yield trials for white, orange and purple fleshed sweetpotato pipelines', 'Many released varieties are not adopted by farmers and other end users because of lack of important traits desired by end-users. To focus breeding programs, target product profiles (TPPs) are required. End users including gender related traits were captured in the TPP that were developed for each market segment/breeding pipeline. These traits are considered during selection of genotypes for advancement at each breeding stage. Pesek-Baker and DISIRE programs are used to develop indices that are used for selection of genotypes that combine all desirable traits on TPPs. The lists provided indicate the number of clones that were selected in each breeding pipeline.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8551?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8504, 'Use of Doctor Vida device for virus testing in sweetpotato by Loop-mediated Isothermal Amplification (LAMP) method', 'Many virus detection methods are mostly performed in a laboratory but also are time-consuming, and expensive. The Loop-mediated Isothermal Amplification (LAMP) method has been adapted and/ or adopted for using on site (i.e., greenhouse, field, etc.) and it is cheaper and rapid in detecting viruses. Doctor Vida is a portable device originally developed LAMP detection of SARS-CoV-2 during the COVID-19 pandemic and is now being adapted for testing plant pathogens as a cost-effective alternative for virus detection. Comparative tests using the same reagents and virus-infected samples showed 100% concordance and similar sensitivity between Doctor Vida and Genie (the gold standard) for detecting sweetpotato viruses (SPFMV, SPCSV, and begomoviruses). Besides, Doctor Vida operates with portable power sources like cell phone power banks, making it suitable for on-site virus detection. These findings confirm the reliability of LAMP tests on Doctor Vida device for detecting plant pathogens.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8504?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4988, 'Manual seeder for maize and beans in Honduras', 'Mechanical deceive to save labor in the planting of maize and beans for medium to large smallholder farmers. Alternatively, cooperatives buy the seeder and provide the seeding service / or seeder for rent.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4988?phase=3', NULL, NULL),
(9573, 'Development of Version 1 of model to project water table using limited satellite data', 'Mechanistic modeling of the water table using satellite data enhances traditional agricultural models by incorporating 2D/3D water movement, including lateral seepage, and optimizing parameters via satellite imagery. This is crucial for researchers and users needing accurate irrigation status information (e.g., irrigation frequency). Rice cultivation is a major source of non-CO₂ agricultural emissions due to methane release from flooded fields, yet it holds significant potential for emission reduction. However, investments in methane reduction primarily focus on waste management and livestock, with limited funding directed toward rice farming. A key challenge, particularly in the Mekong Delta, is the lack of atmospheric Earth observation (EO) data, which limits effective irrigation management and emission monitoring.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9573?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4032, 'Meghdoot v2: An enhanced agrometeorological advisory app for Indian farmers', 'Meghdoot is an Android mobile application that provides India''s official agrometeorological advisories to farmers. Meghdoot was developed by International Crops Research Institute for the Semi-Arid Tropics (ICRISAT) to support the weather data dissemination effort by the India Meteorological Department (IMD). Since its release in 2021, Meghdoot has been downloaded more than 400,000 times. To improve the usability and performance of the application, CGIAR Research Initiative on Digital Innovation will enhance the user interfaces and the overall user experience of the Meghdoot app by applying human-centered design approaches and improved hyper-local weather source data from IMD. In 2024, new features are added to provide more user-friendly, actionably advisories using AI and the Large Language Model approaches.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4032?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(1242, 'Availing digital advisory content through an easily searchable content hub for farmer support organizations (Nigeria)', 'Mercy Corps is collaborating with the Excellence in Agronomy initiative to develop agronomic advisory solutions aimed at benefiting over 300,000 smallholder farmers. This initiative focuses on creating gender- and youth-responsive agronomic solutions, covering soil health, crop productivity, climate change adaptation, and precision cropping system management. The collaboration involves digitizing specific value chain agronomic content for Nigeria, which will be disseminated through the MercyCorps Sprout platform. This digital content is designed to reach farmers via organizations and various communication channels. The first year of the project was dedicated to establishing partnerships, developing the Minimum Viable Product (MVP) tool, and outlining data and tool requirements along with prototype development. The second year focuses on validating and piloting the MVP to ensure its effectiveness and impact. This innovative approach seeks to sustain agricultural productivity and ecosystem services, adapt to climate change, and refine cropping system management solutions for smallholder farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1242?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(20226, 'Microsite (Krishi kund) for restoration of degraded land for improved production and ecosystem services', 'Microsite (Krishi Kund) offers a resource-efficient alternative for restoring degraded lands by concentrating natural amendments within specific areas for improving agricultural diversity, soil health, and overall ecosystem services. It also helps alleviate the pressure of intensive farming on other lands. Moreover, this practice holds promise for reversing migration among tribal communities.
This practice focuses on improving micro-sites rather than entire parcels of land using organic amendments. In this method, small cylindrical pits (did: 60cm, depth 60cm) are excavated at 1.2m intervals, filled with locally available organic materials. This approach improves approximately 20% (6450 pits per hectare) of the land area, resulting in a similar yield of fully restored land.
Drought tolerant crops viz pigeon pea, caster for the poor water availability sites and other cash crops including vegetables at sites of water availability are being grown. A two-year recovery period was estimated for the cost incurred on land restoration.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20226?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8730, 'Biological treatments for the prevention of moniliasis in aromatic cocoa and cocoa CCN51 in Ucayali, Peru', 'Mineral broth are a promising agroecological input to prevent moniliasis in cacao. In this innovation process, the team assesses 
1) the frequency of the application of inputs and biofertilizers produced by the cooperative (biol + Sulfocalcic Broth + Broth bisose + Trichoderma), for the control of moniliasis in aromatic cocoa. Experiments are being conducted in 4 plots in the following districts: Neshuya/Campo Verde/ Huipoca/ Von Humbolt/ with Colpa de Loros'' cooperative; 
2) the application of sulfocalcic broth, bordales broth, bisose broth and ozonized oil to control moniliasis in CCN51 cocoa with Banaqui Curimaná Curimaná.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8730?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4223, 'Developing a safe and efficient biopesticide against Fall armyworm (FAW) through genomic selection and testing of nucleopolyhedroviruses native to Africa', 'More than 150 caterpillars of the Fall armyworm (FAW) showing symptoms of virus attack were field-sampled in Benin and Nigeria. Screening for the presence of native nucleopolyhedroviruses (SfMNPV) was conducted in the framework of a collaboration with the University Wageningen, the Netherlands. Promising isolates were identified to be tested under laboratory conditions prior to further work under semi-field and field conditions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4223?phase=1', 4, NULL),
(9847, 'Nature-Positive Aggregated Permaculture Farms: Enhancing Ecosystem Services, Livelihoods, and Sustainable Farming Practices for Smallholder Farmers in Nyando and Vihiga, Kenya', 'More than 165 farmers in Nyando in Kenya have aggregated their land to form two large farms. One farm is 32 hectares in Agoro East sub-location aggregated by 83 farmers and the other in Jimo East sub-location we have a 10 hectare farm aggregated by 80 farmers. Farms will be design to embody permaculture principles in which farmers jointly designed various aspects of the farm including cereal reduction, vegetable production, fish ponds, irrigation, agro-forestry, apiculture and a section for livestock production. farmers jointly share resources, participate in joint production and value addition activities with the aim of achieving economies of scales while at the same time contributing to eco-system services and livelihoods. In Vihiga, where special aggregation is not possible, we have aggregated production at landscapes level with 52 farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9847?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8186, 'Yellow Jacaranda (Schizolobium Parahybum) as a potential coffee and banana shade tree', 'Most coffee and banana shade trees are slow growing – taking multiple years to provide needed shade levels. We are evaluating a novel tree – yellow jacaranda as a potential source of shade for coffee and possibly banana to improve the adaption to climate chnage. The tree is fast growing, nitrogen fixing and will thus improve nutrient recycling while offering shade for the crops. Its fast-growing nature [up to 5 meters in the first year] could allow for the needed shade levels for coffee to be obtained in a shorter time duration. The trees can produce branches at heights of over 10 meters if not pruned. Studies will also focus on their management [pruning and spacing] for attainment of the optimal branches and shade levels. As a global good, the tree will also improve carbon sequestration of the coffee-banana systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8186?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(12299, 'Integration of multipurpose forages with improved agronomic practices, cereal-legume intercropping, for enhanced productivity and climate resilience in mixed crop livestock systems of Malawi', 'Multipurpose forages, integrated with cereal-legume intercropping systems, offer opportunities for crop-livestock integration, diversification, and intensification, particularly in areas with limited land. Agronomic knowledge and practices can be shared on the same plot, delivering multiple benefits: diversified food production, improved biomass for livestock feed, and increased manure for soil fertility enhancement. Additionally, these systems reduce production costs and contribute to ecosystem services, such as soil erosion control through deep rooting, Biological Nitrification Inhibition (BNI), and carbon sequestration. Forage legumes also fix atmospheric nitrogen, enhancing soil fertility and supporting crop production, while promoting climate resilience.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12299?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(13284, 'My Farm Trees: Digital platform with blockchain to incentivize farmer- and community-led tree-based restoration of degraded landscapes: Vietnam country module', 'My Farm Trees is a digital tool for documentation, verification and quality control of tree seed supply networks for land restoration, and connecting and rewarding network actors for ecosystem services. It uses blockchain to create a transparent link of information from seed collection to tree growth – to improve livelihoods, food security and climate mitigation. A module of the tool was developed for Vietnam, including 577 tree species with their scientific, Vietnamese and local names. The tool interface was translated to Vietnamese, to make it accessible for local users.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13284?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(13721, 'My Farm Trees: Digital platform with blockchain to incentivize farmer- and community-led tree-based restoration of degraded landscapes:  Laos country module', 'My Farm Trees is a digital tool for the documentation, verification and quality control of tree seed supply networks for agroforestry and land restoration, and for connecting and rewarding network actors for ecosystem services. It uses blockchain to create a transparent link of information from seed collection to tree growth – to improve livelihoods, food security and climate change mitigation. A module of the tool was developed for Laos, including 188 local tree species with their scientific, Lao and local names. The tool allows farmers to document and report their efforts on integrating trees in silvopastoral and other mixed farming systems, to access payments for ecosystem services', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13721?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(2938, 'Systematic approach for national seed system stakeholders to understand seed demand and inform delivery pathways: a protocol to follow for data collection and systematization..', 'NARES breeding programs and seed multiplication and delivery are two different entities that show little integration. The so called “hand-over point” is of crucial importance and a poorly developed bottleneck in the chain. The NARES departments that are responsible for seed multiplication and delivery are poorly equipped with strategic tools to plan seed delivery and feed information back to breeders. This action-oriented systematic approach aims to be a tool (a guideline) for the NARES to fill this gap. It consists of different components that need to be brought in line with each other - through iteration (if the budget needed for the first draft plan is too high, the number of selected regions/target groups to whom to distribute seed of the (new, cleaned) variety needs to be adjusted, to finally match available budget, etc. etc.).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2938?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(3055, 'Enhancing Early Generation Root Tubers and Banana Seed Production Planning and Business: Introducing a Seed Requirement Estimation (SRE) Tool for Public Sector', 'National Agricultural Research Institutes (NARIs) face challenges in correctly estimating the required amount of sweetpotato early generation seed (EGS) every season due to lack of information on adoption of improved seed and varieties by farmers and the negative effect of farmers’ use of planting materials of unknown health status. This makes it difficult for NARIs to fully sustain their EGS business. Therefore, a user-friendly tool to estimate the requirements for EGS was developed and automated using Excel & web based. We also introduced the tool with other roots, tubers and banana crop experts. Each crop owner has developed basic version of seed requirement tool.  We have identified minimum viability of the product by interacting with potential users/clients by segmenting them across RTB crops. This will enable us to increase the adoption of the tool among potential clients/users.  3 customer segments were identified - seed producers, public sector and NGOs etc', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3055?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(8228, 'Nano-culture technology developed at CIAT is now being used to safety duplicate the cassava collection at a partner institute', 'National partner Agrosavia, La Selva branch, now hosts a partial safety duplicate of part of the cassava collection managed by CIAT. CIAT has purchased and maintains incubators at Agrosavia, which currently safeguards 4,280 accessions in nano culture, a technique developed at CIAT. In 2024, CIAT have been adjusting lightening conditions and long-distance monitoring of environmental conditions in the growth chamber with the in vitro nano cultures to optimize cultures longevity. CIAT will continue using this safety duplicate platform as part of routine operations going forward.
Nano-culture is a plant tissue culture technique in which cassava in vitro plantlets are cultivated under sterile, controlled conditions—regulated light, temperature, and photoperiod—within small containers (2 ml) at a slow growth rate. This technology enables the preservation of a large number of accessions in a minimal space. Additionally, it is a highly efficient method that requires less plant tissue as source material, reduces propagation time, and minimizes culture medium consumption due to the small container size.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8228?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1833, 'Supporting smallholder farmers’ access to diversified seed systems for food and nutrition security and climate change adaptation', 'Nature+ aims at achieving its impact through support for crop and variety diversification and agroforestry (trees on farm), including the use of minor crops and farmer varieties. All of this diversity requires to be available to farmers through well-functioning seed systems. Seeds need to be of good quality and have the traits women and men farmers are interested in. Given that private sector and formal seed systems focus on only a few crops, this innovation is about developing strong and functioning farmers’ led seed systems that will allow farmers to access the required diversity to ensure success in achieving the impact of the initiative, including improving nutrition, biodiversity conservation, and resilience. Key activities include agro-ecological farming, participatory crop selection and improvement, community 
seed banking, value chain development, and seed policy development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1833?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9315, 'Nature Positive Circular Bioeconomy Innovation Hubs: Accelerating collaboration for localized transformative sustainable solutions', 'Nature-Positive Solutions Initiative, in collaboration with national and international partners has set up Circular Bioeconomy Innovation Hubs (CBE-IH) in India and Vietnam. The hub serves as a collaborative space for researchers, innovators, financiers and scaling partners to foster circular bioeconomy ideas into transformative products and services. It promotes collaboration and facilitates the growth of the innovation ecosystem. The innovation hub is established based on the local context. In India, the hub is established in partnership with BAIF Development foundation while in Vietnam Nature+ is collaborating with United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) which has already set up Vietnam Circular Economy Hub.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9315?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(15959, 'Multi-Dimensional Sustainability Assessment Tool (MSAT) for Smallholder Farming Systems to design and promote sustainable intensification strategies', 'Nearly 500 million farmers are smallholders, producing ~80% of food in Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa. 
• Smallholder mixed farming systems operate within complex socio-economic and environmental contexts, presenting both opportunities and challenges. 
• The smallholder farming systems are highly heterogeneous, vulnerable, and face a multitude of challenges, Hence, there is a need for a multidimensional understanding of these farming systems to design and promote sustainable intensification strategies', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15959?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8433, 'Potential of P. ostreatus as a bio-control agent of root-knot nematodes (Meloidogyne spp.) and burrowing nematodes [Radopholus similis].', 'Nematodes are a key pest to bananas and other vegetables. Through in vitro and pot trials, P. ostreatus was observed to respectively kill nematodes, and protect egg plants and banana plantlets. Next step is to evaluate P. ostreatus under field conditions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8433?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(9574, 'Participatory Netmapping workshops to understand and envision Sustainable and Low-Emission Food Systems with different stakeholders', 'Netmapping aims to 1) To understand both the present image and the future vision of the food system with  citizens of different stakeholder groups and in relation to lowering emissions in the food system, and 2) To understand the meaning of low-emission food systems for the stakeholders, and factors impacting low-emission food systems in the area. Participatory approaches in Nandi County amplify diverse voices, foster consensus among stakeholders, and enable system simulations to understand key factors like pricing and supply chains. These methods empower local communities, enhance decision-making, and support the development of a low-emission food system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9574?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(20415, 'High-throughput technologies, such as near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS), unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV), ground penetrating radar (GPR) are promising technology for efficient and accurate phenotyping of key cassava traits', 'NIRS models were developed for several traits or model development is ongoing. Independent data were used to validate the calibration models. Some of the developed models predicted traits with sufficient accuracy for screening purposes, while others did not. For the latter, the calibration models are undergoing reevaluation, retraining, and validation prior to deployment. A model for automate quantification of PPD is being developed, the results are promising. Assessment of vegetation indices, and plant height using UAV imagery were undertaken. The data collected provide information on the trials growth and health and will help build predictive models for efficient and accurate phenotyping of biomass, plant growth, development status, yield-related traits and adaptation. GPR technology is being explored for fast tract selection of high-yielding and early storage root bulking. High-throughput phenotyping will allow screening of early-generation breeding materials and quick data turnaround, decrease phenotyping error, and speed up variety development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20415?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4905, 'AT least 5 digital training materials for 2023', 'No of digital training tools, number participants, number of views, downloads, countries covered', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4905?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(8895, 'Genotypes with high nutritional qualities selected from sweetpotato breeding pipelines', 'Nutritional traits are important in sweet potato breeding. Iron is important to prevent anemia in pregnant mothers and children. High levels of polyphenols inhibit iron absorption and its always important to strike a balance between the two. The list provided shows genotypes that were selected for advancement due to high levels of iron and zinc. After further analysis of nutritional content, eating quality traits and agronomic data, four clones in the orange fleshed pipeline and seven clones in the white fleshed pipeline were selected, handed over to the national agriculture research program for final testing and variety release of at least one clone from each pipeline. Released varieties have a wider reach to communities that suffer most from micronutrient deficiency problems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8895?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(14246, 'Improved Forage for Livestock Nutrition, Soil Fertility, and Sustainable Crop-Livestock Integration for smallholder farmers in Malaw', 'On farm- and on-station trials explore diverse forage types useful for strengthening crop livestock integration, diversification and intensification of smallholder farming systems in Malawi. Sensitization and improvement of quality roughages, can improve both biomass yield and quality, providing better nutrition for animals. Some forages, such as Brachiaria, are adapted to low rainfall and offer additional environmental benefits like  controlling soil erosion, reducing nitrogen loss, and sequestering carbon. Mucuna pruriens, another forage, serves as a cover crop but also has potential as livestock feed. Forage legumes, like Crotalaria, also fix nitrogen in the soil, enhancing soil fertility. Dual purpose maize and groundnut varieties can increase biomass yields and quality on croplands without compromising grain yield. Private sector interest in forages like Brachiaria and Crotalaria is growing, increasing seed availability. Mucuna pruriens is produced locally, further supporting sustainable livestock feed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14246?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(9255, 'Farmer biopesticide for fall armyworm management in Kenya and Zambia - Field testing', 'On station experiments at Kitie and Itumbini, Machakos County and Muguga, Kiambu County in Kenya
On station experiments Ngwerere, Kasisi, Palabana, Honda and Kanakantapa in Zambia', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9255?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8748, 'A framework for incorporating gender considerations in One Health research and development.', 'One Health research and intervention outcomes are strongly influenced by gender dynamics. However, there is very limited evidence and discussion to guide using a gender lens in One Health activities. We propose a framework to identify key gender considerations in One Health research for development with a focus on Low- and Middle Income countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8748?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6911, 'Creation of a learning alliance as a network approach to support learning and alignment across stakeholder groups in scaling innovations in Patuakhali, Bangladesh', 'One of the aims of the AMD project is to improve deltaic production systems by working with farmers and local governments to identify, synthesize, evaluate, and scale interventions that help adapt or mitigate climate change. The International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) convenes and facilitates Learning Alliances (LA) in various areas in the Asian Mega Deltas including the Ganges-Brahmaputra Delta in Bangladesh. In Bangladesh, IRRI organized two workshops in Patuakhali in 2023. 

The aim of this workshop is primarily to form an LA, which is a network of diverse stakeholders within the region that has an interest to address climate-related issues in the Ganges-Brahmaputra Delta. More specifically, the workshop aimed to:
•	Understand the main production constraints due to climate change impacts 
•	Identify opportunities (technology options) to address production constraints 
•	Assess the stakeholders in Patuakhali who are relevant for addressing the problems

Learning visits with stakeholders in key experimental sites were done in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6911?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6909, 'Creation of a learning alliance as a network approach to support learning and alignment across stakeholder groups in scaling innovations in Khulna, Bangladesh', 'One of the aims of the AMD project is to improve deltaic production systems by working with farmers and local governments to identify, synthesize, evaluate, and scale interventions that help adapt or mitigate climate change. The International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) convenes and facilitates Learning Alliances (LA) in various areas in the Asian Mega Deltas including the Ganges-Brahmaputra Delta in Bangladesh.

The aim of this workshop is primarily to form an LA, which is a network of diverse stakeholders within the region that has an interest to address climate-related issues in the Ganges-Brahmaputra Delta. More specifically, the workshop aimed to:
•	Understand the main production constraints due to climate change impacts 
•	Identify opportunities (technology options) to address production constraints 
•	Assess the stakeholders in Khulna who are relevant for addressing the problems

Learning visits with stakeholders in key experimental sites were done in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6909?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8792, 'Decision support tools developed to manage Maize Lethal Necrosis (MLN) during maize seed production', 'One of the important components for the sustainable and efficient management of MLN is to implement a digital tool/app for efficient data management with several partners.  An App, which is known as Maize Clean Seed Production Tracker has been developed in collaboration with IITA. This App is ODK software-based and is enabled through a web-based toolbox to support data management and decision support for conducting MLN surveillance in a time series and to implement SOPs for MLN pathogen-free commercial seed production. The App is being piloted in Kenya in partnership with Kenya Plant Health Inspectorate Service (KEPHIS), and will be further scaled in sub-Saharan Africa in partnership with relevant regulatory agencies. During 2024, a total of 204 staff members (including 175 Male; 29 Female) of KEPHIS were trained at Kitale and Nakuru Counties in Kenya on the use of the App.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8792?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(20297, 'On-farm Genomic Selection (GS) approach using a sparse testing design for rainfed upland rice breeding pipeline in Africa', 'On-farm Genomic Selection (GS) using a Sparse Testing design has been implemented  in rainfed upland rice breeding pipeline covering six countries in Africa. A total of 898 (896 tests and 2 checks) upland breeding lines evaluated in six diverse target population environments (TPE) in Côte d’Ivoire, Mali, Nigeria, DRC, Guinea Conakry, and Sierra Leone using a P-Rep design. Thirty percent of the test lines (282 genotypes) were tested twice and distributed across farms and remaining 616 test lines were identically distributed into six countries . Two checks: 1 regional check (NERICA 4) and 1 local check were used, and each check was replicated twice within a country. The trial is being conducted using a modified  tricot design. Five sites/location have been  chosen, and 10 farmers are involved  on each site/village.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20297?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5737, 'Pre- and post- season farmer knowledge, beliefs, and information survey protocol and instrument.', 'On-farm research is being conducted in multiple sites in Northern Bangladesh. This is the survey tool used to assess the impact of various interventions made on-farm and at the household level at multiple times along the cropping patterns.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5737?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(19064, 'On-Farm Testing and TRICOT Trials: Insights from Recent Past and Future Improvements based on experiences of diverse crops and partners for use in Dryland Crops', 'On-farm testing results help in parental recycling, on-farm verification, and varietal release. TRICOT is a powerful OFVT tool. To further refine, a 3-day workshop was organized December 3–5, 2024 at Nairobi, with the participation of CG, NARES and One Acre fund. The experiences from groundnut, maize, beans, potato and cowpea were shared along with BMGF, 1000 farms, and One-CG, representatives. 

The workshop was divided into five sessions, viz., Sampling and characterization of suitable farmers and sites; Trial design and measurement; Data analysis and decision-making, Operationalization, coordination, and scaling; and Action plan and key takeaways, outlining future directions. 

A focused discussion was organized to address barriers to adoption, seamless seed availability, and efficient data collection and reporting. The discussions resulted in the identification of four cross-cutting themes namely Advancement Process, Process Mapping and Harmonized SOPs, Digital Ecosystem Integration and Cross-Crop Coordination. To operate these cross-cutting themes, working groups were established.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19064?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1754, 'Diversity for Restoration (D4R) tool for Ghana: online catalogue and decision-support tool for selecting multipurpose tree species for mixed farming systems', 'Online tool containing a catalogue of multipurpose tree species to be planted or conserved in the selected target regions of Ghana. All tree species are fully characterized and scored in relation to specific objectives of planting (particularly within silvopastoral systems) and site conditions. The tool also contains indication on where to source tree planting material based on regions of provenance', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1754?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(2855, 'Multiplication of mucuna seed to support initial on-farm validation and subsequent of scaling of innovative mucuna cover cropping within perennial crops in eastern DR Congo', 'On-station studies conducted in eastern DR Congo, have shown cover crops including Mucuna to offer multiple services such as erosion control, soil health regeneration, weed management, soil moisture and temperature moderation within banana and coffee fields. These practices improve overall biomass production and crop yields and offer opportunities for livestock integration. These innovations have been tested and are currently being scaled. Access to seed is limiting and is an important complementary innovation crucial for furthering the uptake of Mucuna and other cover crops.  Thus, efforts to demonstrate and multiply Mucuna seed is ongoing at the Mulungu INERA station. This will identify the best way to multiply the seed and is supporting validation trials and ongoing scaling efforts. Strategies will be explored for a more sustainable production of seed to support farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2855?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(2896, 'On-farm validation of velvet bean (Mucuna pruriens) as a cover crop in banana and coffee systems in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo', 'On-station trials at INERA of shade- and drought-tolerant fast-growing nitrogen-fixing cover crops like Mucuna pruriens and first growing grasses have resulted in increased farm productivity, restoration of degraded lands and soils through improving soil physical, chemical, and biological properties and in banana and coffee systems. Mucuna can also reduce weed burden, improving the quality of life for women and children who provide most of the labor for weeding. Validation trials have also been conducted on farms aligned to large cooperatives/companies. These demonstrations over the year attracted over 800 farmers and partners to learn the soil health enhancement practices and are important demo and learning sites. Lessons from the on-farm testing are informing the innovation finetuning process and on-going scaling process. Studies to capture the agronomic and socio-economic aspects of the innovation, farmers perceptions, innovation uptake and diffusion levels/ process and dis-adoption levels are ongoing to inform the ongoing scaling process.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2896?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7758, 'Demonstration and scaling of integrated management of parasitic weeds on faba bean in Northern Ethiopia and Morocco', 'Parasitic weeds (Orobanache and Phelipanche spp) are food legume crops production constraints in Mediterranean region and East Africa highlands. Integrated management of parasitic weed management (IPWM) on faba bean was demonstrated and scaled in Morocco and Ethiopia in 2022/23 cropping season. Application of two sub-lethal sprays of glyphosate herbicide at flowering stages of faba bean was effective in managing the parasitic weed (O. crenata) in both countries. Based on 2021/22 results (two sprays of sub-lethal herbicide, fungicide seed treatment against faba bean gall and partially resistant cv. Hashengie in Ethiopia, IPWM options were adopted by many farmers in the intervention zones. Partial budget analyses showed high incomes for the innovations. Moreover, alternative non-host crops were identified to be included in the cropping system to reduce the seed bank of O. crenata. The IPWM innovations will be further scaled out in 2023/24 cropping season in both countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7758?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2768, 'Integrated management options against parasitic weeds and gall disease on faba bean for smallholder farmers in highlands of Ethiopia', 'Parasitic weeds are major biotic constraints on food legumes and causing a decline in area and productivity in North and East Africa. May efforts have been made to develop  partially  resistant faba bean cultivars from ICARDA breeding lines.  The level of resistance in released varieties is low and requires additional management options to effectively reduce the economic impacts parasitic weeds. Demonstration and scaling of integrated management of parasitic weeds was implemented in five districts in Amhara region. Male and female farmers selected partially resistant cv. Hashengie + two sprays of sublethal doses of glyphosate at early flowering stages of the crop. The innovation reduced parasitic weeds population and doubled crop productivity. Three field days were organized in 2022 with participation of 103 (10 female) farmers, extension agents and experts. Early generation seeds (0.8 t breeder and 1.5 t C2) of cv. Hashengie  will be used next season.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2768?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(17896, 'Guidance for designing nutrition-sensitive agri-food system interventions in the Mekong Delta in Cambodia', 'Participatory developed in response to stakeholder needs, this guidance was developed based on lessons learned from research studies (AMD and other research), and stakeholder experience and consultations. It is intended as  a practical guide in the design and implementation of effective and relevant nutrition-sensitive agri-food systems interventions in the Mekong Delta region in Cambodia for use by governmental institutions, non-governmental organizations (NGOs), development partners, community-based organizations, and research and academic institutions', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17896?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(1508, 'Installment of subnational innovation system that fosters knowledge exchange for value-adding co-creation and context-specific testing and validations of agronomic and organizational recommendations', 'Participatory framework that fosters knowledge exchange for value-adding co-creation and context-specific testing and validation of climate-resilient, low-emission and nutrition-sensitive agricultural practices and technologies providing options suitable for diverse farming households, agro-ecologies and production systems and bringing novel ways to empower local change agents that facilitate innovation adoption and diffusion. The network includes experiments in Mexico, Honduras, Guatemala and Peru and has collaborations with Colombia. that can be used by local farmers', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1508?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(9510, 'Development of a Participatory Guarantee System (PGS) by Seed Equal partners for Seed Quality Assurance in farmer producer organizations that are producing seed', 'Participatory Guarantee System (PGS) is an integral component of a decentralized, farmer-led seed system that aims to empower farmer groups with the collective authority to validate the standards of truthfully labeled seeds (TLS). Mainstream seed certification procedures require a licensed seed officer to verify the quality in accordance with the Indian Minimum Seed Standards and provide certification. Smallholder and women seed producers might not be able to adhere to or afford the stringent requirements of formal certification procedures. PGS transfers the responsibility of quality assurance and certification from government to Seed Growers’ Associations, that  facilitate the procurement of seed and provide their members advice on processes and practices. They certify the seeds produced by member farmers based on assessment of seed sources and production processes through regular inspection and record keeping. While promoting equity and diversity within the seed chain, the PGS also ensures that seed quality is preserved.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9510?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1035, 'Participatory rangeland management: an inclusive approach for improving governance, tenure security and productivity of rangelands', 'Participatory Rangeland Management (PRM) is a defined step-by-step process for improving local level rangeland management by local pastoralist communities. It follows three stages of investigation (including participatory mapping), negotiation and implementation. It supports establishment and/or strengthening of rangelands management institutions, and supports the production of a rangeland management plan and an agreement with local government to improve access of communities to natural resources and land. M&E and capacity building are also important steps.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1035?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(12579, 'Participatory videomaking: a tool to improve understanding of adolescents'' perceptions of their food environments and inform interventions for sustainable healthy diets in VIet Nam', 'Participatory videomaking is a methodological tool that can be used to promote community engagement in research and develop critical awareness of an issue or topic. Researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets and the National Institute of Nutrition used this methodology with 54 adolescents (15-17 years old) selected from public schools in three districts in Viet Nam. The adolescents documented their perceptions of their food environments using cameras on their mobile phones. Analysis of the videos revealed challenges and opportunities adolescent face in accessing and consuming sustainable healthy diets. The research team are using the videos to inform the next stage of research, the co-design of community action plans to enhance food environments for better diets.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12579?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(8689, 'PathoTracer 2.0: Advanced Rice Disease Management through Dynamic Data Visualization and Analysis', 'PathoTracer, developed by IRRI, is a decision-support platform for rice disease and pathogen management. In 2023, it underwent a transformation from the PathoTracer RShiny to a more advanced QlikSense application, aiming to automate data management and improve visualization of rice pathogen data, especially for Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae (Xoo). The updated PathoTracer2.0 dashboard, powered by QlikSense Enterprise and Amazon Web Services, offers dynamic visualization and analysis, enabling users to track global Xoo incidences and dominant AXoo strains. Incorporating data from 19 partner institutes, it also lists rice varieties with resistance genes, aiding informed decision-making in rice disease management. In 2024, PathoTracer was improved and reverted to using Rshiny and R visualization libraries for mapping Xoo isolate locations. The application currently houses data submitted from 22 partner institutes. List of varieties containing R-genes for specific Axoo populations was also enabled.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8689?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(10460, 'Enhancing PCR-based methods for bacteria detection on rice seeds. Validation and verification of detection procedures for seedborne bacteria', 'PCR (polymerase chain reaction) based methods are proven to be faster, cheaper and still highly effective methods for the detection of presence of bacteria in seeds compared to the traditional methods. In spite of the cons, such as the need of highly specific equipment and technical competence or the higher rate of false-positive results, these methods provide reliable and fast results allowing  the streamlining of end-to-end transboundary movement of seeds, with a lead time of 3-5 days as compared to 10-12 weeks. To attain the validation and verification of these highly specific methods, protocols require thorough evaluation under strict standards. The implementation of such methods and their cross validation with the national plant protection organization, will allow the Seed Health laboratory at IRRI to offer this service to researchers, partners and  external customers in the Philippines to enhance their capacity to import and export rice seeds.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10460?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(3194, 'Reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) assay for detection and characterization of Pea seed-borne mosaic virus in legumes applied by ICARDA’s Germplasm Health Unit', 'Pea seed-borne mosaic virus (PSbMV, genus Potyvirus, family Potyviridae) is the most important virus infecting lentil both in terms of economic importance and geographical distribution. It was reported that PSbMV can even lead up to 61% yield loss. Therefore, developing and implementing effective management strategies for PSbMV is very important to reduce yield and quality loss. However, accurate detection of virus disease is the first essential step for effective management strategies for control of this disease. Molecular is efficient tool to accurately detect and monitor the occurrence and development of crop diseases. This report describes a validated reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) assay for laboratory detection and characterization of PSbMV applied by ICARDA’s GHU.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3194?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(3178, 'Pencil stem technology for rapid propagation of cassava seed for seed companies and seed producers in sub-Saharan Africa', 'Pencil stem technology is expected to provide a susbstitute for bulky planting materials which is a major challenge in cassava seed delivery systems. The pencil stem product also has a similar health status to the source semi-autotrophic hydroponics (SAH) plantlets used in the production. The production of the pencil stem products requires little land, space and agronomic resources to produce breeder seeds when compared to directly producing normal stems for breeder seed. Pencil stems are easy to transport to rural seed producers/farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3178?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5087, 'Assessing the feasibility of Oxford Nanopore Technology (ONT) for 16S rRNA sequencing as a diagnostic tool in detecting bacterial infections in febrile patients within a pastoral setting', 'People routinely get exposed to multiple  zoonotic infections  as they interract with livestock or wildlife, or consume their products. These infections are  caused by  bacterial, viral or parasitic agents that may end up causing  epidemics. Public health care facilities  however do not usually have adequate facilities for detecting many of these infections to enable the implementation of  interventions in good time. This  challenge  needs to be addressed so as to develop capacity for stopping zoonotic spillover. The Oxford Nanopore Technology (ONT) sequencing method provides a high-resolution, real-time analysis of bacterial diversity in clinical samples, uncovering both known and novel pathogens. it uses 16S rRNA sequencing for a comprehensive characterization of bacteria but its application has been limitted to lab settings. We have assessed ONT''s effectiveness in identifying bacterial communities in febrile patients’ samples from northern Kenya. We are currently refining the analysis pipelines and assessing the practicality of using portable ONT sequencers for sequencing in remote healthcare settings. Further, we intend to employ this technology for concurrent pathogen detection in humans, animals, and wildlife samples. This will transform zoonotic bacterial pathogen identification, illuminating cross-species infections and co-infections at human-livestock-wildlife interfaces, leading to precise prevention and management strategies in a one-health approach.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5087?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(8793, 'Dormancy breaking protocol for several crop wild relatives and forage species optimized', 'Physical seed dormancy breaking treatments for O. barthii, O. officinalis and O. alta were optimized.  Different recommendations were made for each of the three species: O. barthii - 50C dry heat method for 14 days and dehulled seeds germination; O. officinalis - 50C dry heat method for 7 days and intact seeds germination; and O. alta - 50C dry heat method for 7 days and dehulled seeds germination.

Dry heat treatment followed by pre-soaking intact seeds in different chemicals (0.0029M Gibberellic acid, 0.2 M Nitric acid ) were also applied to O. barthii and O. officinalis to break seed dormancy.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8793?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4621, 'Social behaviour change communication (SBCC) campaign on best practices in beef and pig production and marketing, targeting women and men livestock keepers in North-west Vietnam.', 'Pig and beef cattle keepers in Son La province of NorthWest Vietnam rarely engage in best practice in pig and beef cattle production and marketing.  There are numerous reasons for this, a key one being lack of access to information.  To address this, we are developing a social behaviour change communication (SBCC) campaign that will share best practice messages on herd health, breeding, animal nutrition and marketing.  The campaign will be designed to reach livestock keepers of different genders (women and men) as well as ethnicities (primarily Thai and H''mong).  Currently the strategy for the campaign is being developed in conjunction with behaviour change specialists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4621?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(11264, 'Eastern and Southern Africa (ESA) Regional Pigeonpea and Chickpea crop improvement working groups established in close CGIAR-National Agricultural Research Systems (NARS) collaborations', 'Pigeonpea and chickpea crop improvement networks for ESA were established through a series of meetings at regional and country levels (Product Design Teams- PDTs) duly involving stakeholders along the whole teams and identified country specific MSs and TPPs, that led to develop 3 and 4 regional market segments (MSs) and target product profiles (TPPs), respectively in pigeonpea and chickpea. Based on the strengths of the NARS and country priorities, agreed on regional breeding pipelines to be led by network NARS member countries. CIMMYT leads over all coordination, trialling, product advancement and on-farm testing strategies. Identified 5 regional priority and 3 pre-breeding priority traits to be included in breeding pipelines that are well designed to capture breeding program optimization plans. Also noticed upcoming niche markets like green vegetable types for MSs refinement. In Tanzania conducted a market survey with VC actors in 7 chickpea potential areas.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/11264?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(4599, 'Gender and Plant Health Global Research Network - an interdisciplinary platform to make a difference in plant health research and interventions for equity, equality and efficiency', 'Plant Health Initiative has established Gender and Plant Health Global Research Network. More than 100 people from the global South (85% biophysical scientists, 15% social scientists) obtained membership by attending either on-line or in-person training held in 2023. The members work on interdisciplinary research through supervision and technical support by a gender expert in the initiative and share their experience and finding through newsletters and online seminars. The global network includes regional groups in Africa, Asia and latin America in which contest-specific problems are discussed in their common languages.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4599?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(5008, 'Plastic crate rental service for fruit and vegetables farmers and retailers to circumvent high post-harvest losses in the transportation of fresh fruits or vegetables', 'Plastic crates drastically reduce food loss while stored and/or transported. The crates should replace the traditional raffia baskets that lead to high post-harvest losses during storage and transportation. The adoption rate is currently low because the price is high, and/or the benefits of using plastic crates must be better understood. The majority of plastic crates are on a rental basis.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5008?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(18763, 'Development of forecasting models for management of pod-borer (Helicoverpa armigera Hübner) in pigeonpea (Cajanus cajan L) and chickpea (Cicer arietinum L)', 'Pod borer (Helicoverpa armigera) is still significant challenge in pulse cultivation in India. We aimed to develop regression model using weather indices for forecasting pod borer outbreaks in pigeonpea and chickpea. The historical adult trap catch data; eggs and larval data, and actual weather data of ICRISAT location from 1991 to 2023 was used.  Models predict crop stage at the first appearance of the pest (Y1), and maximum population of the pest at peak infestation time (Y2). Results showed that temperature, relative humidity, rainfall, and sunshine hours significantly influenced the population dynamics of pod borer. For pigeonpea, model accurately predicted maximum eggs population at 43rd SMW and maximum larval population at the 45th SMW. In chickpea, model predicted the maximum eggs population at 45th SMW and the maximum larval population at 46th SMW. The model''s effectiveness validated in current season for pigeonpea at major growing regions of Karnataka and Telangana.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18763?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(7752, 'Development and evaluation of eco-friendly biopesticides against aphids and pod borer of food legumes in Morocco', 'Pod borer and aphids are the most economically important insect pests in chickpea and faba bean respectively. Besides their economic damage, aphids play a role as virus vectors on food legumes. This study aimed to validate eco-friendly biopesticides (entomopathogenic fungi and essential oils) on pod borers and aphids selected in 2021/22. Laboratory results showed that two entomopathogenic fungi (Verticillium lecanii and Paecilomyces farinosus) were effective against second instar larvae of chickpea pod borer.  Similar results were found under field conditions where the two entomopathogenic fungi reduced larval population. The essential oils extracted from Ocimum basilicum and Origanum vulgare at 5000 ppm were the most effective against aphids under field conditions. Entomopathogenic fungi and essential oils can be integrated in the management of pod borers and aphids as eco-friendly alternative pest management options in Morocco. Efforts will be made to test different formulations in 2023/24 cropping season.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7752?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4983, 'Testing Point of Cow, a penside diagnostic test, for treatment decision making in mastitis', 'Point of Cow is a disposable chip manufactured by Fluimedix (Denmark).  It is a compact and user-friendly diagnostic tool that serves as a crucial component in antibiotic treatment decision making. The chip is engineered to detect both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria within 4-24hrs,  enabling quick decision-making on whether to use antibiotic treatment for mastitis. Point of Cow is primarily designed for dairy farmers, but can also be used by milk processors and quality control professionals in the dairy industry. It is a valuable tool for anyone involved in milk production and processing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4983?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(10454, 'Low-cost solutions to improve tomato transport and storage', 'Postharvest losses are a global challenge, and fragile fruits, such as tomatoes, often suffer significant losses. In response, FRESH researchers are exploring a novel approach to packaging and transporting tomatoes. In the Philippines, a collaboration between he University of Philippines Mindanao and FRESH core partner Applied Horticultural Research aimed to mitigate losses by experimenting with simple yet effective interventions. The focus was on preventing physical damage from tight stacking, a practice compromising nutritional value and increasing susceptibility to diseases. The solution involves double-lining crates with newspaper before packing and implementing a quality grading scale. Trials in diverse storage conditions consistently demonstrated that tomatoes handled with these improved methods outperformed traditionally managed ones, exhibiting higher quality, reduced disease incidence, and enhanced visual appeal. Preliminary trials in Tanzania are underway, anticipating that the potential benefits, including improved quality, reduced wastage, and extended shelf life, will outweigh initial transportation cost increases.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10454?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(47, 'Potato breeding networks in Africa use citizen science to inform selection in a new generation of clones derived from crosses with between elite Latin American and locally adapted materials', 'Potato is a smallholder cash crop in East and Central African highlands. In many of the drier potato-growing regions, more frequent water and heat stress are leading to declining yields. The East Africa Potato Breeding Network was formalized in May 2023. New candidate varieties are to include resilience traits such as heat tolerance, earliness-to-ripen, virus and late blight resistance, good water-use efficiency. Breeding cycle time is 3-4 years. The resulting varieties are being tested in multilocation trials with public and private partner organizations, and through citizen science approaches with farmers. Even though varieties have been released in the past, recent insights point to yield, taste, marketability, and tuber quality as being most important to farmers. These insights help to adjust target product profiles. Their development is being accelerated through genomic selection, faster cycle times, spatial and GxE analyses that optimize variety testing, and more accurate consumer trait assessments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/47?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(892, 'Potato Tracker Georgia web-app to provide a comprehensive digital solution for the development of the Georgian potato value chain', 'Potato Tracker Georgia is a multi-stakeholder, multi-functional web-app that operates via an online cloud and can be accessed from anywhere and by any pre-registered user. Potato Tracker has been developed from Seed Tracker which was developed by IITA for cassava. The tracker seamlessly connects seed producers, seed traders and seed quality certifiers through its very user-friendly interface. Features include crop varieties, production and stock inventory, seed quality certification, seed tracking, plant health decision support, online trade, and more.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/892?phase=1', 8, NULL),
(2637, 'Farmers diversify rice variety production and enter markets with premium quality rice (PRQ) varieties', 'Premium quality rice market linkages to specialized millers to enhance profits for smallholder farmers and small businesses in Northern Bangladesh. During the reporting period, Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia (CSISA) in alignment with Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA) and eight seed companies in Bangladesh produced and sold around 400 tons of Premium Quality Rice (PQR) seeds in the CSISA working areas. Data collected from the Department of Agricultural Extension in 16 districts indicated that PQR varieties were cultivated in 79 of their 107 upazilas and that a total of 934,196 farmers cultivated PQR on 215,145 hectares of land. The research team worked with 12 seed companies in nine districts. 695.35 tons of PQR seed were sold in these nine districts. 115,892 farmers (at an average of five farmers per hectare), and 23,178 hectares of land were involved in these markets.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2637?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(14146, 'Construct design and assembly to target the eIF4E gene for Potyvirus Y (PVY) resistance in potato', 'Prime editing (PE) approach will be used to edit the genome of potato for Potyvirus Y (PVY) resistance. The eIF4E gene(s) that will be targeted for editing in this study the variety Shangi. These genes have natural alleles which are the targeted mutated forms of eIF4E that our gene-editing tools will create. Shangi, a potato variety that is found in Kenya and is highly susceptible to PVY virus but preferred by farmers due to its short dormancy period, versatile use and good taste, will be utilized in this study.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14146?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(2923, 'A seed potato production planning and business decision tool: streamlining production and business decisions for seed potato entrepreneurs in Kenya and Uganda', 'Private (small- and medium-scale) seed potato businesses in Sub-Sahara Africa have challenges in the planning of their production and its related factors (screenhouse capacity, labor, land, cash flow, storage capacity, …) leading to premature sales of seed potato due to lack of suitable land available for further multiplication or unsold stock of starter material, among others. Therefore, this seed potato production planning and business decision tool aims to support them to improve their production planning and its related factors while showing the profitability of their planned production schemes.  The development of a user-friendly online and smartphone application is foreseen. Moreover, this tool shows the business opportunities for small- and medium-scale seed businesses in Uganda and Kenya, but it can be used in other countries as well because the identified challenges cut across many countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2923?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(353, 'Standardized approaches for market segment and target product profile definition empower African and Asian partners to engage in a more transparent and inclusive setting of joint breeding network targets', 'Product Design Teams (PDT) were introduced and further scaled in 2023 among NARES partners. In 2024, 31 PDT meetings were held, bringing the total number of PDTs conducted since 2022 to 95, covering 15 crops in 24 countries. Approximately 400 national Target Product Profiles (TPPs) have been developed through these meetings. They are currently being uploaded to the Breeding Portal which makes the information publicly accessible through GloMIP (https://glomip.cgiar.org). PDTs are NARES-led with participation and support from CGIAR through ABI-TRANSFORM. NARES leadership of PDTs and the involvement of multistakeholder national participants has been key to ensuring client-driven prioritization, national ownership and alignment of CGIAR breeding efforts with national priorities as requested by various NARES consultations.  National TPPs are aggregated in regional network meetings to define regional market segments and regional TPPs that are pursued by CGIAR breeding programs to ensure they are aligned with national needs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/353?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9570, 'Floating chambers for greenhouse gas (GHG) measurements in aquaculture designed and tested', 'Protocol for measuring greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from aquaculture systems. The protocol describes how to build GHG measurement chambers that can float and, thus, be used for GHG sampling from aquaculture ponds. Emissions from aquaculture are a big data gap and become more and more mainstream. We are using the chambers in our own research starting this season and we work with Can Tho University and Cuu Long Delta Rice Research Institute who are building the capacity for building and operating the chambers. Tests showed that the chambers are stable and it''s easy to take samples from them.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9570?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(10568, 'Protocols for the Development of Food Products from Biofortified Rice', 'Protocols for the Development of Food Products from Biofortified Rice with Consumption and Commercialization Potential in the Department of Bolívar, Colombia, conducted in collaboration with SENA (National Learning Service) regional Bolívar. The purpose of these protocols is to generate options for the transformation and commercialization of biofortified rice produced in the region. 
The target audience is rice producers in the department of Bolivar. With these protocols and product prototypes, it is expected that they will be able to access new marketing channels and give added value to their crop.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10568?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(3652, 'Publicly available multi-crop reference genomes database', 'Quality reference genomes are foundational requirement to enable rapid development of genomic resources at scale to support breeders and their germplasm development programs.  While several commodity crops have good reference genomes (due to private funding but with restricted access), many crops in the CGIAR and NARES do not have adequate references. This innovative shared service draws on public-private partnership with Corteva, CGIAR and NARES to develop publicly available reference genomes databases for many non mainstream but critical crops for the global south, i.e. groundnut, cowpea cassava, banana, plantain, yam, common bean, potato, sweet potato and forages.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3652?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(18830, 'Leveraging the drone and satellite imagery to identify crop stresses in the semi-arid regions', 'Quantification of crop stresses for targeted intervention, drone data was collected weekly to capture high-resolution imagery for monitoring crop biotic stresses, including diseases and pest infestations. This imagery helped map spatial disease patterns and assess the progression of stresses across different crops. Various analytical approaches were tested to refine detection techniques and improve early warning systems. Additionally, drone-based observations were compared with satellite imagery to evaluate differences in spatial and temporal resolution, ensuring a comprehensive assessment of crop health. The integration of these data sources enhanced the accuracy of stress detection, enabling timely interventions. The findings contribute to developing advanced biotic stress monitoring frameworks, supporting precision agriculture through early stress diagnosis and targeted management strategies. This research will enable researchers to integrate Earth observation technologies into crop health monitoring, improving resource management and resilience in agricultural systems, particularly in semi-arid regions where timely stress detection is crucial for sustainable farming.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18830?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(10071, 'Rapid Analysis of Country-Level Greenhouse Gas (GHG) Emission and Mitigation Opportunities in Agriculture and land use Sector', 'Quantifying GHG emissions and mitigation potential is important for all sectors of the economy including food systems for achieving the 2°C target outlined by the Paris Agreement. However, actions to reduce emissions from food systems have lagged behind other sectors. Investment and mitigation actions in the food sector have been constrained by the lack of policy-relevant and science-based methods for estimating GHG emissions and mitigation potential that contribute to decision-making. Most developing countries set their NDC (nationally Determined Contributions) targets through top-down aggregated estimates of national technical mitigation potentials, rather than the bottom-up information needed to inform implementation planning.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10071?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6889, 'R functions to classify and target agronomic innovations to be used by data scientists, agriculture economists and modelers for ex-ante analysis at different scales', 'R functions and an example on using discrete choice models in characteristics space to segment, classify and target agronomic innovations. The R codes and functions are developed by the initiative data scientists, agriculture economists and modelers for their use to analyze data that will help explain the potential impact of agronomy innovations that are being developed by the Use Cases of the Excellence in Agronomy Initiative.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6889?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(785, 'Guidance for conducting randomized controlled trials (RCTs) of complex interventions published to improve RCT design and the interpretation of results', 'Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) are increasingly used as one of the tools to build the evidence base on the effectiveness of complex interventions to address food systems. Researchers conducting RCTs of complex interventions face design and analytic challenges that are not fully addressed in existing guidelines. This leads to questions and confusion about how to conduct these trials and interpret their findings. Guidelines, co-authored by a Sustainable Healthy Diets researcher, were published in 2022 to address key topics of confusion including the number of outcomes that can be assessed, the definition and meaning of primary and secondary outcomes and whether to control for multiplicity. The guidance ensures that trials are conducted to the highest scientific standards while maximizing the evidence that can be extracted. In 2024, the teamed continued (but did not complete) efforts to disseminate this innovation and build capacity for use by researchers, funders, policymakers and others.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/785?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3035, 'Portable sequencing technology is implemented for plant disease diagnostics in at least one National Plant Protection Organization in LAC and in SEA', 'Rapid and low-cost genetic identification of pathogens is key for management diseases and identification of resistant plant varieties. Over the last 5 years we have been implementing portable sequencing protocols taking advantage of the rapid development of this technology as a COVID genomic surveillance tool, used worldwide. Applied to plant pests and pathogens we were able to identify and confirm the incursion of Fusarium TR4 in Peru, the identity of Bemisisa tabaci genotypes in SEA associated with cassava and the occurrence of novel viruses in LAC, Africa and Asia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3035?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(4077, 'Promotion of natural biological control of African rice gall midge (AfRGM) through habitat manipulation in Nigeria', 'Recent interest in IPM/organic farming has focused renewed attention on other approaches among which is the use of habitat manipulation for the conservation of natural enemies. Habitat manipulation is using agricultural areas/surrounding environments with the aim of conserving the population of natural enemies. Paspalum gall midge (PGM), O. bonzii infests Paspalum scrobiculatum, a common weed in rice agroecosystems. Paspalum gall midge cannot attack rice but is an alternative host for the two main parasitoids of AfRGM (Platygaster diplosisae – larval parasitoid and Aprostocetus procerae – pupal parasitoid). However, the parasitoid populations build up too late to prevent heavy AfRGM infestation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4077?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(57, 'Chickpea breeding network accelerates genetic gains for heat and disease-resistant varieties, to meet farmer and end-user preferences and crop rotation needs', 'Recognizing Kabuli chickpea''s ability to grow in nutrient-poor, rainfed soils, ICARDA''s breeding program improves the crop by breeding-in heat and drought tolerance traits while introducing the crop into rotation with conventional cereal crops and other pulses. This innovation develops candidate varieties in collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, targeted at the following systems and seasons: 1. Irrigated systems, 2. Rainfed winter season, 3. Rainfed spring. Breeding is a cyclical process with new, further improved varieties forthcoming on a regular basis, pursuing objectives such as better yields, nutritional quality, and Ascochyta blight resistance. The crop can be a significant contributor to incomes at household level. The current breeding cycle time is 4 years. Between 2022-2024, partners five countries released 14 varieties. They were improved for drought and heat tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/57?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6900, 'Facilitating agricultural mechanization options through local service providers (LSP) model to improve the livelihood of resource-poor women and youth in coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'Recognizing the importance of agricultural mechanization to address shortage in agricultural labor, the International Rice Research Institute partnered stakeholders had developed local service providers (LSP) model involving women and youth in harvesting paddy by using a reaper (wife-husband model) The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) continued mentoring the LSPs through trainings and demonstrations on mechanized harvesting of paddy to attract more women and youth in agriculture and to ease the physical burden on women during manual harvesting and increase their contribution to household earnings. 

Training on mechanical harvesting of rice by reapers was organized at 10 water management group areas in polders to create awareness of agricultural mechanization so that women and youth can explore new livelihood opportunities locally. Four pairs’ women and youths (wife-husband) provided mechanical harvesting services to the community harvesting paddy by a reaper. Other mechanization options include fodder chopper, rice huller, and maize sheller.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6900?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9654, 'AquaIndex: a framework of indicators for sustainable aquatic foods.', 'Reliable qualitative and quantitative data on aquatic food systems are essential for ensuring sustainable food production and development. AquaIndex is a structured framework of indicators designed to assess the sustainability of aquatic food systems, covering environmental, social, and economic dimensions.AquaIndex provides a standardized approach for evaluating sustainability outcomes, enabling stakeholders—including researchers, policymakers, industry actors, and development agencies—to measure, compare, and track progress over time. The framework will help synthesize and benchmark the sustainability performance of aquatic food systems at local, national, and international levels. AquaIndex supports evidence-based decision-making to improve aquaculture governance, policy interventions, and investment strategies. It will help guide sustainable practices, ensuring that aquatic food systems contribute to food security, climate resilience, and economic stability while minimizing environmental impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9654?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(3177, 'Diverse and healthy cropping systems on-station trial protocol at ICAR-CSSRI, Karnal​-Haryana (India)', 'Research Platform Trial at ICAR-CSSRI (Central Soil Salinity Research Institute), Karnal, Haryana is part of the first impact pathway that focuses on farm diversification and nutrition-sensitive landscapes and will contribute to the outcome “farmers are exposed to innovations and improves management recommendations. Northwest Indo Gangetic plains (IGP) challenged by soil health deterioration, water table declined and air pollution due to crop residue burning as cropping system is dominating by the monotonous rice-wheat system. This system also cereal-cereal based crops and not offering the diversified healthy diets in the region. To address all challenges TAFSSA designed a diversified cropping systems combined with regenerative management strategies to offers better productivity, profitability, sustainability and resiliency.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3177?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3519, 'Research Protocol for linking farm diversification, markets and nutrition through on-farm agronomic trials and trainings.', 'Research Protocol describing the Participatory Socio-Agronomic Research carried out in Northern Bangladesh, which includes on-farm agronomic trials and trainings but also nutrition and marketing trainings. In the agronomic trials  different cropping patterns are compared. These cropping patterns are more diverse and include crops with higher nutritional value that the conventional ones, which often are based 100% on cereal and/or carbohydrate rich cops. Agronomic data as well as parameters to assess production costs and benefits is collected. Agronomic trainings and marketing trainings are linked to these agronomic trials and further in some location farmer HH are given nutrition trainings. This will allow assessing the contribution of improved access to knowledge about nutrition on diversification in crop production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3519?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(3521, 'Agronomic research platform trial comparing diversified cropping patterns in Dinajpur, Bangladesh', 'Research Protocol for agronomic trial in controlled conditions (on-station). Comparing 9 different cropping patterns relevant to the production system of northern Bangladesh. Agronomic data, parameters to assess production costs and benefits, incl. labor requirements, as well as some environmental parameters, are collected. The results from this trial will inform the community of practice, from farmers to scientists and policymakers about the benefits and constraints, advantages and disadvantages of different alternatives to the most common current cropping system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3521?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(936, 'Comparing cropping patterns on-farm in Bangladesh', 'Research Protocol for on-farm agronomic trials. Comparing different cropping patterns relevant to the production system of the respective district in Northern Bangladesh. These cropping patterns are more diverse and include crops with higher nutritional value than the conventional ones, which often are based 100% on cereal and/or carbohydrate-rich crops. Agronomic data as well as parameters to assess production costs and benefits is collected.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/936?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(17187, 'Combined market- and consumer-oriented intervention to improve food safety, livelihoods, and diets among urban households in Bangladesh', 'Researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets and Resilient Cities collaborated on the design of an innovation in 2024, which will be implemented and tested starting in 2025 in collaboration with the Food Systems for Nutrition Innovation Lab. The intervention will work at the interface between market provision and consumer demand to support markets to deliver safe food and promote healthier diets. The team will design and measure the effectiveness and costs of a synergistic vendor- and consumer-oriented strategy to improve food safety and the livelihoods of food vendors and to improve the diets of adult women of child-bearing age and children under 5 years from low- and middle-income families in Dhaka. Two synergistic approaches will be used: a market-oriented strategy that strengthens wet markets and the associated informal food vendors to deliver safe and nutritious diets and a consumer-oriented intervention that empowers and encourages consumers to demand safer and higher-quality foods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17187?phase=4', 0, NULL),
(8916, 'Integrated rice-fish aquaculture systems for climate resilient rice based communities in the Cambodian Mekong Delta', 'Rice and fish are the main commodities produced and consumed in Cambodia. They provide the most contribution to caloric intake, but are affected by climate change. Aquaculture within rice fields is proposed as a climate resilient practice to manage the production for both rice and fish. This innovation ensures rice management is suitable for integrating the fish, and also that selection of fish species and aquaculture management are suitable for rice-based systems. Integrated rice-fish culture entails growing the rice while fish is able to enter the fields at specific periods, maintaining fish shelter through small pond or canal, and integrated management suitable for both rice and fish.

The Initiative is currently co-designing the climate smart aspects of integrated rice-fish culture. It has completed one season of experiments in semi-controlled conditions and is now incorporating the lessons drawn to improve the design of the approach and test in the field again later.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8916?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(9805, 'Layanan Konsultasi Padi (Rice Crop Manager Indonesia): A digital advisory service for rice farmers in Indonesia', 'Rice Crop Manager Indonesia, known locally as Layanan Konsultasi Padi (LKP), was developed and introduced to address the key challenges for rice production and productivity in Indonesia through site-specific recommendations leading to a proven increase in yields and incomes. LKP is a digital agriculture decision support tool developed by the International Rice Research Institute to identify the best nutrient management practices for specific rice fields using site-specific nutrient management principles. LKP is designed for use by extension workers, crop advisors, input providers, and providers of services who interview a farmer using a computer or smartphone. Collected information is used to calculate recommendations and transformed into a crop management guideline for the farmer in a one-page printout. Each guideline provides an actionable recommendation, which matches the location-specific cropping practices and needs of a farmer.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9805?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(14393, 'Elite lines with multiple resistance genes effective against rice yellow mottle virus', 'Rice yellow mottle virus is a major disease limiting rice productivity across Africa and Madagascar.  Several resistance genes and alleles have been identified in previous studies, but none were available in modern elite backgrounds.  Most of these were found in Oryza glaberrima, and hence extremely difficult to introduce into Oryza sativa (common cultivated rice).  Deployment of these genes has been achieved, including those from O. glaberrima, to very high quality standards.  Elite lines have been registered and shared with AfricaRice and various national partners in ESA.  Testing of lines in Mbe (west Africa) and several sites in ESA (Kenya, Tanzania, Mozambique) have shown the effectiveness of most of these genes, with the exception of rymv2.  A resistance strategy combining rymv1-5 + RYMV3 or rymv1-2 + RYMV3 has been developed.  Plans are being laid to increase the frequency of these alleles in elite material, with some populations already established.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14393?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(15797, 'Optimized biodiversity management of rice based ecosystem for improved ecosystem services and climate change resilience in Cambodian farming communities: progress and results', 'Rice, a staple in Southeast Asia, is a significant source of greenhouse gas emissions, with climate change increasing insect pest risks. Sustainable solutions are crucial for enhancing rice the resilience of the rice sector. This study evaluates farmers’ preferred biodiversity management strategies to improve ecosystem services and profitability. Farmers identified mung bean as the preferred bund crop and watermelon as the preferred dry-season crop following wet-season rice. Between 2022 and 2024, replicated field experiments tested four treatments, varying pesticide application, bund crops, and dry-season planting. Key metrics assessed included arthropod diversity, pest damage, agronomic performance, costs, and greenhouse gas emissions. Results indicated that pesticide-free systems with diversified cropping experienced less thrips damage, maintained stable grain yields, achieved higher benefit-cost ratios, and produced lower emissions compared to conventional practices. Further analysis will focus on the role of biodiversity management in strengthening climate resilience.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15797?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(9558, 'RiceMore: Digital tool for monitoring and reporting rice production to support decision making for domestic and export needs in Socialist Republic of Vietnam', 'RiceMoRe introduces an innovative solution to digitize and enhance the monitoring and reporting processes for rice production. RiceMoRe serves as a valuable tool in the decision-making process by providing accurate and up-to-date information to optimize rice production for both domestic consumption and export purposes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9558?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6249, 'Development of a smart phone application (RiceMore) compatible with monitoring, reporting, and verification (MRV) requirements for carbon credits in rice production', 'RiceMore was built in cooperation between the Department of Crop Production, the Center for Digital Transformation and Statistics - Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development and the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) to support monitoring and management of rice production systems. RiceMo allows systematizing and storing data on rice production which are updated weekly through a network of local officials from the commune level. Data linked with geographic information systems and other information layers to make production planning and natural disaster and pest response more convenient to policymakers, planners, extension agents, and researchers.

The RiceMore application is also compatible with monitoring, reporting, and verification (MRV) requirements for carbon credits in rice production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6249?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5513, 'A web-based based decision support tool for managing Rift Valley fever outbreaks in Kenya', 'Rift Valley fever (RVF) is a zoonotic disease caused by a virus that is transmitted by a wide range of mosquitoes. The disease mainly affects livestock and people. It has been listed by the World Health Organization as one of the nine diseases that has the potential to cause pandemics. Decision makers often face major challenges predicting and responding to outbreaks, and so we have developed this prototype tool for forecasting risk and identifying actions that can be taken to manage the risk. The tool has two components, the first ingests rainfall forecasts from the Global Precipitation Climatology Centre and processes anomalies to identify periods when above normal rainfall is expected. The second component provides actions that need to be taken at various phases of the outbreak ranging from pre-outreak, outbreak, post-outbreak and peacetime periods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5513?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9045, 'Satellite remote sensing-based rice information and insurance in emerging economies (RIICE)', 'RIICE is a remote sensing-based digital platform that deliver near real time, geo-localized, accurate, and updated seasonal information on rice production, yield and climate-driven yield losses (https://www.riice.org). RIICE was developed and operated since 2012 in several Southeast and South Asian countries, including the Philippines, India, Cambodia, and Vietnam. Through the CGIAR Research Initiative on Digital Innovation, RIICE was piloted and scaled in Kenya and Senegal in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9045?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4897, 'African armyworm model frameworks for predicting this pest risks and occurrence developed', 'Risks and occurrence prediction model frameworks of Africa armyworm developed. A system dynamics model was developed by following a structured approach that includes building causal loop diagrams and stocks and flows diagrams. The process started by thoroughly researching and understanding the system i.e. the ecology and biology of the African Armyworm, its life cycle, and the factors that influence its occurrence and population dynamics, such as temperature, rainfall, relative humidity, wind speed, and solar radiation. The methodology combining data reconstruction techniques with rule-based systems and zero-shot learning was used to predict African armyworm infestation severity of yield losses. The model''s proficiency in predicting crop loss was substantiated by an accuracy rate of 0.80, as revealed by the confusion matrix. The production of month yield losses induced by African armyworm as the real outputs of the model is still ongoing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4897?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(3951, 'Cross-functional and cross-crop decision-making platform and tools for continuous improvement and harmonization of processes in Genetic Innovations to support team members manage collaboration in an efficient manner', 'Rules of engagement were needed In order to provide an inclusive, transparent and robust cross-functional and cross-crop  decision-making platform for continuous improvement of  processes in Genetic Innovations. We created an inventory to keep track of and visualize/report the Process Teams'' member assignments in real-time in order to manage people workload and line manager sponsorship. We defined principles for process teams operation in order to support members work more efficiently towards agreed goals, keep everyone well informed, make teams''  work transparent, vand isualize the workflow for everyone, making it possible to make agile decisions​.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3951?phase=1', 4, NULL),
(1520, 'Rural Household Multi-Indicator Survey (RHoMIS): A digital system for building farm household surveys, processing and compiling data, analyzing finding  to quantify more than 40 different aggregate indicators on farm households', 'Rural Household Multi-Indicator Survey, RHoMIS is a toolkit for the efficient implementation of farm-household surveys. It consists of modules for building household questionnaires, a digital infrastructure for compiling data and calculating results, and a compiled database of all the previous applications of the questionnaires. It is intended to increase the efficiency of questionnaire implementation, and address the data wastage caused by fragmented application of questionnaires throughout the CGIAR and agricultural development sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1520?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(18520, 'Feasibility of using an artificial intelligence (AI)-assisted dietary assessment mobile app to characterize the quality of school meals and their contribution to children’s diets validated in Viet Nam', 'School feeding programs reach approximately 368 million children globally and serve as a multifaceted intervention benefiting education, health, and nutrition. Despite these investments, there is limited data on diets and nutritional status of school-age children, particularly in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs). For many reasons, monitoring the quality of school meal programs in LMICs remains challenging. This innovation builds on a collaboration that began in 2020 with CGIAR and the listed national partners from Viet Nam. (Its application in Ghana has been reported by Resilient Cities.) The collaboration developed the PlantVillage Food Recognition Assistance and Nudging Insights (FRANI) app, which offers a cost-effective solution for recognizing foods, providing consumption statistics, and estimating nutrient intakes in children and youth with accuracy comparable to professional dieticians but at a lower cost. In 2024, Sustainable Healthy Diets began a feasibility study to test how this innovation works in primary schools.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18520?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(3545, 'Using business analytical approaches to conceptualize the entire CGIAR portfolio along the two pathways of impact creation and development effectiveness leads to identifying organizational key performance indicators', 'Searching for a simple road map to explain Genetic Innovation initiative linkages resulted in the creation of a two-dimensional process-based Theory of Change (2D-ToC). The 2D-ToC distinguishes between the main trust of a program or project, and capabilities that need to be developed or improved to achieve the main trust. In the case of Genetic Innovation, the ontological analysis has become the foundation for practically aligning initiatives and bilateral projects through a Program Management Platform. During 2024, the approach was expanded using business analytical approaches to conceptualize the entire CGIAR portfolio along the two pathways of value creation, impact or benefit generation and development or organizational effectiveness. This was done for both the 2022-2024 portfolio and the 2025-2030 portfolio. It was shown that portfolio analytics can be used to conceptualize the total CGIAR research agenda and identify Key Performance Indicators, that could be utilized across a hugely diverse research agenda.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3545?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6835, 'Safe planting window and variety recommendation tool for maize', 'Seasonal variability is a major constrainst for many farmers cultivating maize under rainfed conditions in Northern Nigeria. Climate change is resulting in more erractic seasonal weather patterns. The optimum maize planting window recommendation decision support tool aims to proivide farmers with spatially explicit recommendations on optimal variety and planting window recommendations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6835?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8943, 'Genebanks developing a multispectral imaging system (VideometerLab) to automate the gathering of seed morphometry data for seed characterization', 'Seed morphometry determination using a bespoke digital imaging approach developed in partnership with Aarhus University and a Danish private sector company is being mainstreamed in multiple genebanks for fast and accurate quantification of seed morphological features in multiple crops. The technique characterizes with high precision "awkward" seed traits, and further research is being carried out to assess its ability to monitor seed viability and other physiological seed qualities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8943?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2795, 'Global Protocol for a Scoping Review on Capacity development and business models that support seed production and marketing by women and youth', 'Seed production can benefit women as an enterprise and can, at the same time, strengthen their key roles in seed systems (Puskur et al., 2021). However, women seed entrepreneurs face challenges from their weak economic position, limited access to production factors, and intra-household dynamics hampering their efforts to become entrepreneurs, further complicated by societal bias toward women in business (Puskur et al. 2021, citing Adam et al. 2018). This scoping review aims to review available literature, evidence and learning on capacity development and/or business models that have been implemented to support seed production and/or marketing by women, youth and other vulnerable groups in the low- and middle-income contexts (LMICs).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2795?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(8824, 'A cost-effective fungicide seed-dressing for the control of seed-borne bacterial pathogens', 'Seed-borne bacteria impact plant health and yield, and bacterial-contaminated seed is a source for the transboundary spread of pathogens. Due to a lack of effective seed-dressing agents, eliminating seed-borne bacteria involves the hot-water treatment, which is laborious, sensitive to temperature settings, and known to affect seed viability. This study evaluated mancozeb and carbendazim for their bactericidal properties by testing against eight seed-borne bacterial species (Bacillus cereus, Bacillus subtilis, Ralstonia solanecearum, Xanthomonas manihotis, Pantoea stewartii, Xanthomonas glycinea, Xanthomonas oryzae and Xanthomonas vignicola) in three crops (cowpea, maize, and soybean). Seed dressing with mancozeb resulted in 100% suppression of seed-borne bacteria and serves as the broad-spectrum reagent for seed dressing to eliminate seed-borne bacterial infection. Mancozeb is widely available, and doubles as a reagent for preventing seed-borne and bacterial contamination. To our knowledge, this is the first study to validate the effectiveness of mancozeb to control seed-borne bacterial contamination.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8824?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4642, 'Capacity building of animal healthcare workers on pig herd health management', 'Selected animal health care workers from Mukono and Wakiso districts were identified and trained as herd health champions. The training focused on herd health management concept and how to apply it at farm level. The trained herd health champions will train selected farmer groups on herd health management in two districts. This approach is expected to improve farmers’ skills and technical capacity to raise healthier pigs thereby improving their incomes, profit margins and food safety.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4642?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8833, 'Genomic prediction cross performance pipeline to facilitate population improvement for yam breeders', 'Selection of meritorious parents and their matings that maximize genetic gain are key action steps to in yam breeding.  To optimize parent selection and identify their best cross combinations complementary to their genetic characteristics, a Genomic Prediction Cross-Performance pipeline  was developed and integrated into yambase analytical functions. The pipeline utilizes both phenotypic and molecular marker data to compute total genetic and breeding values of the progenies for all potential matings between the potential parents.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8833?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(3977, 'Digital Identities: Enabling farming data traceability using open data sharing protocols with self-sovereign identity in Mexico to allow individuals'' control on their identity and shared information', 'Self-sovereign identity (SSI) is a digital identity that is fully controlled and owned by the individual it represents. Unlike traditional centralized identity systems, SSI allows individuals to have autonomy over their own identity, what information is shared, with whom, and for what purposes. SSI is a relatively new concept, but we argue it has the potential to revolutionize how we manage identity and privacy in digital ecosystems. To explore the potential of SSI in smallholder farmers'' data sharing and management, we partnered with Bluenumber Foundation to develop a data traceability pilot in Mexico for small-scale maize producers to manage their farm management data. We expect SSI can also help producers securely access financial and other services more easily through the certified digital identity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3977?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(3872, 'Pheromone systems developed to lure and kill cowpea pests (pod borer and pod sucking bug) and/or for enhanced recruitment of their natural enemies in the field', 'Semiochemicals from cowpea plant parts have been found to attract Maruca vitrata and its parasitoids Liragathis javana. Few host plant volatiles identified from cowpea have been shown to attract M. vitrata, including butyl butyrate, limonene, 4-ethylpropiophenone, 1H-indol-4-ol, octyl butanoate, and 2-methyl-3-phenylpropanal. However, these attractants have limited applications and have not been demonstrated as semiochemicals in multi-site situations and different legume cropping systems. The GC-MS analysis performed presented some specifics compounds of the attracted cowpea organs. Those specific compounds are the potential attractants for M. vitrata and its parasitoid L. javana. The validation of those chemical compounds for their use in biocontrol strategy for Maruca species is ongoing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3872?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(3231, 'Diversification options in rice-based systems in Cote d''Ivoire', 'Seven cultivation systems were evaluated to find alternative methods of intensifying irrigated rice production to enhance yield and farmers'' incomes: 1. transplanted rice produced once a year with residue removal, 2. transplanted rice produced twice per year with residue removal, 3. transplanted rice produced thrice a year with residue removal, 4. transplanted rice produced in rotation with mung bean once a year with residue retention, 5. transplanted rice produced followed by mung bean and then rice again, once a year with residue retention, 6. dry mechanically seeded rice produced twice a year in alternate rotation with one cycle of mungbean with residue retention,  7. wet mechanically seeded rice produced twice a year in alternate rotation with one cycle of mungbean and residue retention, 8. manually dry seeded rice produced twice a year under no tillage, with one cycle of mungbean in between the two rice cycles with residue retention.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3231?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6434, 'Simple-to-administer and low-priced tool that researchers and policymakers can use to monitor population-level fruit and vegetable intake', 'Simple-to-administer and low-priced indicators are essential tools for monitoring fruit and vegetable intake at the population level. A promising tool is the fruit and vegetable component of the Global Dietary Recommendation score (FV-GDR). The score is based on data collected using the Diet Quality Questionnaire (DQQ). Researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets investigated the relative validity of FV-GDR in comparison to the 24-hour recall method to measure fruit and vegetable intake among 620 Vietnamese and 630 Nigerian adults. The researchers concluded that the DQQ is a promising low-burden, low-cost and simple way to calculate FV-GDR and to monitor fruit and vegetable consumption at the population level. This provides the possibility of evaluating an important aspect of diet quality in low-resource settings.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6434?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(8685, 'A new seed-dressing fungicide formulation for wheat', 'Since 2018, the European Union and several other countries have decided to ban the sale and use of many pesticides containing thiram and thiamethoxam molecules, affecting shipments of seeds treated with these chemicals from entering these countries. Accordingly, a new fungicide formulation should be sought as an alternative to Celest Top (thiamethoxam 262.5 g/l; fludioxonil 25 g/l; difenoconazole 25 g/l) and Vitavax (carboxin 200 g/l; thiram 200 g/l), which are the chemicals most used as seed dressing to treat the wheat seed-borne diseases. The research was conducted using varieties of bread wheat (Faiza and Najia) and durum wheat (Faraj and Jabal). The results revealed that the newly tested formulation (fludioxonil 25g/l; sedaxane 25g/l) was effective against common bunt (100% success rate). The new fungicide formulation outperformed Celest Top in all evaluated parameters related to  production. Further validation with large quantities of seed is due.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8685?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(1015, 'High level engagement for co-creation of collaborative breeding networks: building more equitable partnerships that more effectively address regional priorities, and collectively increase genetic gain and farmer variety adoption', 'Since 2022, ABI-Transform has organized an annual leadership consultations between Genetic Innovation and NARES in Sub-Saharan Africa to assess partnership health and identify priority areas to further improve collaboration. Key decisions and outputs from these meetings have been captured in Aide Memoires which have been endorsed by leaders from CGIAR, NARES and SROs. Resolutions and actions are tracked and pursued by the Core Technical Team that meets quarterly and is composed of representatives from the CGIAR, NARES and SROs. These meetings have highlighted 8 key areas: 1. need for co-creation and increased alignment with national and regional priorities; 2. Increased role for partners in regional breeding networks; 3. Better attribution; 4. Increased funding transparency; 5. Joint ownership and decision making; 6. Need for an annual partnership health check through a representative survey; 7. Need to incorporate Universities and SMEs in regional breeding networks; 8. Need to institutionalize human capacity development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1015?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9472, 'The establishment of a Southern Rice Black-Streaked Dwarf Virus (SRBSDV) monitoring regional network in Asia lead by IRRI', 'Southern rice black-streaked dwarf disease virus (SRBSDV) emerged in China in 2001 and has since spread to Vietnam, Japan, Korea, and most recently, Northern India in 2022. In response, IRRI''s pathologists, with support from PHI, organized an online workshop in November 2022 involving experts from China, India, and Vietnam. Discussions addressed SRBSDV''s situation in India, sharing outbreak experiences, and proposing plans for surveillance and diagnostics. An SRBSDV-BioNet was established for standardized detection, collaboration, and research. In 2023, the network achieved two major milestones: (i) a follow-up hybrid webinar on SRBSDV management occurred on July 5, in collaboration with Punjab Agricultural University, and (ii) the development of standardized detection protocols and guidelines for preventive measures for SRBSDV. Coordinated by IRRI, the network aimed to develop an interim management strategy for SRBSDV in India, providing guidance to local farmers to protect their rice fields from this disease.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9472?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(2168, 'Methodology for analyzing data gaps and needs was developed to identify and remove limitations in data and information that hinder the development of aquatic food systems.', 'Stakeholders in the aquatic food systems make suboptimal decisions due to the lack of quality accessible data and information. Data gaps and needs vary within stakeholder groups, time, and geographies. The current challenge consists in understanding what is the real demand for data for stakeholders in different countries. It is a methodology, based on multi-stakeholder consultation and analysis of data available from different sources, developed to identify data gaps and needs of different stakeholder groups in different countries of interest.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2168?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4467, 'Mechanization in Excellence in Agronomy Initiative: A Community of practice Working Group Meeting report', 'Strategic thematic area on Mechanization in Excellence in Agronomy Initiative: Agro R&D and Appropriate Scale Mechanization and Experimental Auction of Motorized Weeding Services', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4467?phase=1', 2, NULL),
(8904, 'Rapid propagation techniques significantly increased the supply of sweetpotato vine cuttings of promising clones, enabling the establishment of advanced yield trials across breeding programs in Uganda, Mozambique, Tanzania, and Rwanda.', 'Sufficient planting material is a major limitation in sweetpotato trialing. However, rapid clonal propagation methods now enable large-scale multiplication of planting materials within a few months. This approach was used to multiply breeding clones for advanced yield trials. In Uganda, the International Potato Center (CIP), in collaboration with the National Agricultural Research Organization (NARO), Rwanda Agriculture and Animal Resources Development Board (RAB), and Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI), generated 74,450 vine cuttings for advanced yield trials conducted at 20 locations. Each trial was replicated three times, with 45 plants per plot. Additionally, CIP Mozambique produced 2,050 kg of 20 breeding clones for testing in Target Population of Environments (TPE), Multi-Environment Trials (MET), and on-farm trials. In total, 170 on-farm trials, 10 replicated TPE trials, and 7 replicated METs were established. Variety release decisions are informed by data collected from METs and on-farm trials.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8904?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(8738, 'Supplemental irrigation of wheat to mitigate productivity loss when the soil moisture content drops to 60% during periods of drought in rainfed cropping systems in Morocco and across MENA', 'Supplemental irrigation is the practice of providing additional water to crops to support their survival, especially during periods of drought. Wheat cropping systems in Morocco and across much of the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) are predominantly rainfed and vulnerable to poor crop yields or total failure with increasing rainfall variability and high-temperature extremes. Crop yield reduction or total loss can cause economic, social, agricultural, and food security challenges. In situations where supplemental irrigation is possible, irrigation scheduling is done through the in-situ measurement of soil moisture content at a depth of 90 cm. Irrigation water is applied through drippers when the available soil moisture content in the soil profile drops to 60 %. In drought years, the precise application of 28-166 mm supplemental irrigation (depending on drought severity) has been found to increase wheat productivity by 2-3 t ha-1 when compared to rainfed conditions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8738?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1881, 'Basal diet dairy feed formulation of barley straw for improved nutrition and performance of sheep in the Ethiopian highlands', 'Supplementing increasing levels of dried tagasaste (Chamaecytisus palmensis) leaf on the nutrition and performance of sheep feed. Research shows intake and digestibility of dry matter and nutrients will increase as tagasaste supplementation increase from 100 to 400 g/day. Average body weight gain will increase from 20 to 73 g/day, feed conversion efficiency from 0.04 to 0.10, dressing percentage from 40 to 48. As the supplementation increase from 100 to 400 g/day. Proportion of tagasaste leaf in the diet will increase from 20 to 50%, while the ratio of neutral detergent fibre to crude protein will be decreased from 7.9 to 5.3, as the level of supplementation increased. Supplementation of dried tagasaste leaf up to 50% of the diet DM, resulting in an NDF:CP ratio of 5.3, produces no deleterious effects on sheep, and inclusion to this level can be applied for superior growth performance and carcass yield in sheep diets.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1881?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(16936, 'Methodology to assess the effective contribution of multi-stakeholder processes to food systems transformation applied in Honduras', 'Sustainable food systems transformation requires multi-stakeholder processes. The current resources for the actors involved focus more on what needs to be done rather than how it can be done effectively. This qualitative methodology fills that gap and provides practical recommendations for supporting effective multi-stakeholder processes. It builds upon published frameworks and the building blocks assessment guide developed by the United Nations (UN), starting with five best practice principles for multi-stakeholder processes. It then assesses the compliance of the particular process to the principles based on a set of identified indicators, relying on a combination of desk review and interviews. The research team from Sustainable Healthy Diets applied the methodology to three recent food systems transformation processes in Honduras - creation of a national food systems profile, the 2021 dialogues to prepare for the UN Food System Summit, and the development of the Tegucigalpa Urban Food Policy Pact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16936?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6437, 'Online, interactive tool to provide policymakers with easy access to the results of trade-off modelling from Viet Nam', 'Sustainable Healthy Diets researchers from Wageningen University and Research are designing an online interactive tool ("dashboard") for policymakers in Viet Nam. When complete, the online tool will allow users to dive deeper into the results of trade-off modelling and scenario analyses. It will be organized by the desirable outcome areas of food systems transformation: (1) Health and Food Security, (2) Economy and Inclusivity, and (3) Environment and will provide details on selected indicators for each outcome area. One of the first steps in the development of this innovation was an in-country workshop held with stakeholders in November 2023. Workshop participants suggested indicators to include in the trade-off modelling, which, subsequently will appear in the online tool. This is part of this Initiative''s work to promote the use of decision-support tools that stakeholders can use to navigate trade-offs in a fairer, more inclusive and more equitable way.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6437?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(16482, 'Online, interactive tool to provide policymakers with easy access to the results of trade-off modelling from Bangladesh', 'Sustainable Healthy Diets researchers from Wageningen University and Research designed an online interactive tool ("dashboard") for policymakers in Bangladesh. The online tool allows users to dive deeper into the results from a foresight study analyzing the trade-offs between environmental, health and socio-economic indicators of different food scenarios in Bangladesh up to 2040 and 2050. The researchers used MAGNET, a global economic model. They compared a business-as-usual (BAU) diet to a planetary health diet scenario, which aligns with the diet proposed by the EAT-Lancet Commission, ("the EAT-Lancet diet"), and the Bangladesh food-based dietary guidelines (FBDG). The online tool is part of this Initiative''s work to promote the use of decision-support tools that stakeholders can use to navigate food system trade-offs in a fairer, more inclusive and more equitable way.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16482?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(16465, 'Establishing a sweetpotato breeding network in West Africa to address demands of farmers growing sweet potato on over 1.56 million hectares', 'Sweetpotato breeding seeks to enable resource‐poor households in Africa and Asia to improve their diets and raise incomes. In 2024, CIP reassessed the importance and definition of sweetpotato market segments across Africa. It identified four Market segments in West Africa and one in Central Africa covering 41% of the sweetpotato area in Africa.  Two target product profiles were defined to develop biofortified (provitamin A, iron) varieties in collaboration between CIP and the National Root Crops Research Institute in Nigeria.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16465?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(6241, 'Rice-sweetpotato production systems using short maturity sweetpotato variety in Mekong River Delta in Vietnam', 'Sweetpotato is saline and heat tolerant in general, and considered as a climate resilient crop. To promote sweetpotato in the region for smallholder farmers, and address challenges in current sweetpotato seed systems and available varieties, the Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) Initiative through the International Potato Center (CIP) proposes to conduct a breeding pilot project with a 95-day short maturity orange fleshed sweetpotato material which comes from the CIP sweetprotato Asia short duration breeding pipeline (BP00052), and purple fleshed with high-yeilding and high dry-matter content clones. The project duration is planned to be 20 months, starting from April 2023 to December 2024. Sweetpotato true seeds from San Ramon, Peru with elite crosses (humid tropics) will be sent from Lima, Peru to Vietnam and germinated in Dalat, Central Highlands of Vietnam where the temperature is stable year around. Currently, on-farm trials in farmers'' field conditions are being done in Vietnam.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6241?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(8888, 'Pre-released sweetpotato genotypes selected for on-farm evaluation:  triadic comparison of technologies approach.', 'Sweetpotato product development team uses the triadic comparison of technologies approach. In 2024, CIP Mozambique tested 11 pre-released clones under on-farm for multi-stakeholder validation across five provinces (Niassa, Nampula, Zambezia, Manica and Inhambane) ensuring representation of diverse environments and farmer conditions. In each province 5 districts were selected for on-farm trialing and a total of 170 farmers successfully established the trials. The TRICOT design was the experimental design of choice.  Most agricultural actors, including farmers, extension officers and other local government representatives were involved in the setting up of trials and their assessment. Farmer and other stakeholders feedback on performance clones will be utilized and incorporated into decisions of variety release and genetic gains in farmers’ fields will be calculated. The validation system strengthened the selection and adoption of new varieties, integrating farmer needs and gender considerations into breeding advancements.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8888?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6305, 'Co-designing gender responsive circular bioeconomy models targeting informal producers', 'Tailored capacity building on circular bioeconomy models is provided to women and young individuals residing in urban and peri-urban areas. Specifically, women engaged in informal recycling activities such as biomass briquette production and sales receive hands-on training to improve their product quality. Moreover, they are trained on governance and group dynamics, co-creating and launching a sustainable business model that aligns with the local context. This innovation is being tested in Kenya, involving a cohort of 30 women who were informally and individually producing biomass briquettes in Kisumu''s informal settlements. The aim is to empower women by enhancing their ability to produce high-quality briquettes, collaboratively develop a viable business model, and organize the group to produce and sell briquettes on a small and commercially viable scale. Post-training, the group is registered as a cooperative called Tich En Tich Makwangla Women''s Group.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6305?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4898, 'Web Platform "Targeting Tools": user-friendly geographical information system tools for identifying suitable land areas', 'Targeting Tools is a web-based Geospatial Information System (GIS) platform that houses a wide range of spatial datasets, both global and country-specific. It provides Land Similarity, Land Suitability, and Land Statistics tools for use on both desktop and web platforms.
The platform not only facilitates the visualization of datasets stored in its geodatabase but also supports user-uploaded raster format datasets. It enables various types of suitability or risk mapping analyses by combining different datasets.
Users are given the flexibility to establish their own criteria and thresholds to identify suitable areas. Moreover, these tools can be used to characterise the identified areas through the calculation of zonal statistics.  
The tool allows for the integration of multi-dimensional datasets, including bio-physical (e.g. climate, soils, agro-ecology) as well as socio-economic (e.g. poverty, nutritional status, access to markets) data and thus for analyses along the different CGIAR impact areas.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4898?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(17191, 'Bundled market- and consumer-oriented intervention to address key barriers to fruit and vegetable consumption -  affordability, lack of awareness, and low-quality - in Ethiopia', 'Technoserve is managing Horticulture for Growth, a program designed to improve food systems in specific sites across Ethiopia. As a complement to this program, researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets and NEED Nutritional Products and Services, have been developing a bundled intervention for vendors and consumers to test how a combination of coupons, visual branding, food safety and nutrition education, and other video-based behavior change communication strategies can increase consumer demand for fruits and vegetables and stimulate vendors to make improvements in their sales of fruits and vegetables. NEED will implement the intervention and researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets will conduct the evaluation in a combination of urban, peri-urban, and urban sites in Ethiopia. The discussions and written plans reached a promising stage by the end of 2024. Work is expected to begin in 2025.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17191?phase=4', 0, NULL),
(17027, 'Development of African rice (Oryza glaberrima) pangenome using a long-read sequencing approach', 'Ten of the most diverse accessions were sequenced from the 3,000 accessions available in the AfricaRice genebank, using a long-read sequencing approach to develop the pangenome of O. glaberrima.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17027?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(10483, 'Development of a Loop-Mediated Isothermal Amplification (LAMP) method  for detection of seedborne diseases by Burkholderia glumae', 'The  increasing  global  temperature  due  to  climate  change  causes  an  increase  in  new  or  previously  negligible  diseases.  The  incidence  of  Burkholderia  glumae  has  been  reported  to  increase  in  recent  years  in  many  rice  growing  regions.   Burholderia  glumae  is  a  seedborne  pathogen  causing  bacterial  panicle  blight  of  rice.  It  infects  rice  at  different  growth  stages  causing  grain  rot,  sheath  rot  and  seedling  rot  symptoms  depending  on  the  rice  growth  stages. Bacterial  panicle  blight  is  difficult  to  manage  in  the  absence  of  effective  chemical  control  measures. Rapid and early disease detection  is needed to avoid severe yield losses. The development of this diagnostic method and the scaling out through the National Seed Quality Service of the Philippines enables the fast and inexpensive early detection of the agent on rice seeds across the country preventing farmers losses due to panicle blight.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10483?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8036, 'Landscape Crop Assessment Survey system for cost-effective collection of key information to characterize and analyze agriculture systems', 'The  system has been developed to provide timely and actionable information about agricultural systems to decision-makers. It has been pioneered in South Asia under the Cereal System Initiative for South Asia (CSISA). As of spring 2023, more than 50,000 datapoints have been recorded with 25,012 records being publicly available. A key aspect of the LCAS system is that the surveys are designed so that a household interview lasts no longer than 45min - allowing cost-effective collection of the most important variables and large spatial scales.

LCAS consists of modules for each key information aspect to characterize and analyze agricultural systems. Each module consists of a core set of survey questions and corresponding variables and additional extensions that may be added for specific crops or ecologies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8036?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(8996, 'Excellence in Agronomy (EiA) Events: A Global Platform for Facilitating Agronomic Conversations on Sustainable Intensification and Climate Change Adaptation, Uniting Experts,Practitioners for Knowledge Dissemination, Innovation, and Strategy Development in Agriculture', 'The "Excellence in Agronomy (EiA) Events" stand as a pioneering initiative, orchestrating pivotal conversations on vital agronomic themes like sustainable intensification and climate change adaptation. These events form a dynamic platform where a diverse mix of experts, practitioners, researchers, policymakers, and farmers worldwide converge to engage in in-depth discussions, exchange innovative ideas, and share best practices.
EiA''s role in these gatherings is integral. It not only disseminates cutting-edge knowledge across the global agronomy community but also sparks innovation and drives actionable strategies in the realm of modern agriculture. The events are designed to cultivate a synergistic environment, encouraging stakeholders from various sectors to unite, learn collaboratively, and jointly pursue sustainable, climate-resilient agricultural methodologies. This holistic approach ensures that each event not only fosters dialogue but also equips participants with the tools and insights necessary to make a tangible impact in the field of agronomy.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8996?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(8041, 'The AADGG-Dairy mobile app for smallholder buffalo farmers to collect dairy data', 'The AADGG-Dairy mobile app, co-designed by the Africa-Asia Dairy Genetic Gains (AADGG) program and the Digital Innovation (DI) Initiative, enhances real-time data capture and validation while ensuring functionality in low-connectivity areas. Developed in collaboration with the DI and SAPLING initiatives, the app provides rapid data summaries and feedback to support livestock extension personnel. The app features an intuitive interface, guiding extension agents through a series of prompts to collect farm and animal data efficiently. Its offline data capture ensures usability in remote areas, with automatic synchronization when online. Farmers and agents can track individual animals, maintaining comprehensive records on breeding, health, and productivity. This improves farm-level productivity, ultimately leading to higher incomes. In 2024, the app was rolled out in Kenya, Ethiopia, Tanzania, Uganda, and Nepal, with training sessions conducted for users. The AADGG platform (https://portal.adgg.ilri.org/) continues to support multi-country genetic gains in smallholder livestock systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8041?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(12580, 'Timely, accurate, and cost-effective means for high-frequency monitoring of both food affordability and security applied in Ethiopia, Myanmar, and Sri Lanka', 'The affordability of sustainable nutritious food for all is an important dimension of food security. Timely, high-frequency indicators of food affordability are rarely collected in any systematic fashion despite the reality that food price volatility is a driver of food insecurity. Furthermore, even though development agencies have made investments in expanding food price monitoring, they have paid insufficient attention to monitoring wages and to measuring real food wages as a food security indicator. This innovation demonstrates how tracking wages of unskilled workers can be a timely, accurate, and cost-effective income proxy for monitoring food affordability for critical populations. The research team, including members of Sustainable Healthy Diets and Fruits and Vegetables for Sustainable Healthy Diets, apply the method using data from well-known food price crises in Ethiopia (2008, 2011 and 2022), Sri Lanka (2022) and Myanmar (2022).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12580?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(11133, 'Capturing the gender-intentional regional target product profiles (TPP) for rice market segments in Western, Central, Southern and Eastern Africa', 'The Africa Rice Center led the development of gender-intentional regional target product profiles (TPP) across Africa. These 16 TPPs are in active breeding pipelines. The information about these TPPS can be downloaded from the Global Market Intelligence Platform.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/11133?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1886, 'Commercialization of Forage Seed Business in Mixed Farming Systems of the Highlands of Ethiopia', 'The Africa RISING Ethiopian Highlands project validated and promoted various forage species. The limiting factor for wider diffusion and adoption has been limited supply of forages and forage seeds. In this activity, we want to test various scaling and commercialization models. The commercialization model want to test the viability of clustering as an entry, in which case farmers who have bigger land size will be clustered and demonstrated on how to produce forage and forage seeds for commercial purpose. For the scaling, we will refine our models of working with community media and development partners engagement.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1886?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(6829, 'Guidelines for informal food sector engagement towards improved food safety in  Africa by  including informal food markets in national food safety systems in Africa', 'The African Union launched the Food Safety Strategy for Africa 2022–2036 (FSSAI), which recognises the importance of the informal food sector in African food systems. Research by the International Livestock Research Institute (ILRI) and partners has identified key elements underpinning the success of food safety efforts in informal markets. Framed as principles of engagement, these elements are meant to guide policymakers in the successful inclusion of informal food markets in national food safety systems. The development of this document, Draft Guidelines for Informal Food Sector Engagement, has benefited from conversations involving ILRI researchers, other stakeholders in the food safety space (in and outside of Africa), and the African Union Commission (AUC) Secretariat.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6829?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(8035, 'AgCx: A mobile application that assesses the creditworthiness of smallholders based on their cash flow', 'The Agricultural Credit Index (AgCx) is an initiative to redesign Indian smallholders'' creditworthiness based on their cashflows. More than 50% of Indian farmers struggle to access institutional finance, primarily due to inaccurate land records or status as tenant farmers without alternate collateral. Despite this, lenders have limited information for credit risk assessment. To address this, AgCx leverages digital assessments to help all farmers, particularly smallholders, tenants, and women, secure loans. The project enables farmers to obtain loans without needing to visit a bank, thus saving their time and wages, and facilitates prompt loan sanctioning. Furthermore, it allows banks and other lenders to lower their lending costs, extend their reach to farmers in remote locations, and minimize non-performing assets (NPAs).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8035?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(5191, 'Co-development and delivery of agro-climatic bulletin (ACB) through public systems to provide climate advisory to inform local agricultural planning, decision making, and adoption of climate adaptive farm practices in Vietnam', 'The agro-climatic bulletin (ACB) is an agro-advisory tool and system based on a seasonal, monthly, and 10-day weather forecast that aims to provide climate advisory services to local agricultural insitutions, officials, and farmers in Vietnam. It is being co-developed and delivered through the public systems in Vietnam where sub-national offices are integrating the tool on their local agricultural development planning and decision-making. ACBs in Vietnam now has reached more than 200,000 and received funding from the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development (MARD) in 2024 and 2025 to implement and scale its operations in 13 Mekong River Delta provinces.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5191?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(18101, 'Personalized soil report for farmers in the Kiambu and Makueni County Agroecological Living Landscapes (ALLs) in Kenya', 'The Agroecology Initiative Kenya Work Package 1 team developed personalized soil data reports in an effort to provide actionable data feedback to the farmers who participated in the co-design cycle. Prior to establishing the first trials, the team took soil samples. Rather than only using the scientific insights to advise on appropriate soil health measures, the team developed a generic report format for each trial participant that allowed sharing the main information back with each farmer. The report is structured in four sections: (1) general introduction about the project, (2) farmers’ personal data including name, farmer group, ALL, trial focus area, and trial practice, (3) individual soil data report with individual results for 15 soil elements that are relevant for agricultural production, including pH, Soil Organic Carbon (%), Total Nitrogen (%), Phosphorus (P), and Potassium (K) – alongside interpretation guidance ranges, and related personalized interpretations (high; medium; low), and (4) a generic soil data interpretation and recommendations guidance section. The personalized reports were shared with each trial participant during a technical training and a general data feedback workshop session.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18101?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(18539, 'Enhancing Productivity and Profitability: The Agronomic and Economic Benefits of Integrated Rice-Fish Farming Systems', 'The agronomic and economic performances of an integrated rice-fish system were evaluated using a 1200 m² block subdivided into six plots of 120 m² each: a. a fish pond in the middle of the rice plot, b. rice monoculture with no fertilizer application, c. cultivating rice in the middle of the plot and raising fish around the rice, d. rice monoculture with the application of the recommended doses of fertilizers (NKP and urea). The fifth and sixth plots were in symbiotic association i.e., rice and fish were grown in two separate plots, and the water from the fishpond was used to fertilize the rice plot. The association of fish with rice significantly reduced the production cost by approximately 30-67% and enhanced both the profit (by 3-164%) and the producer’s income over rice monoculture.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18539?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7651, 'Development and deployment of digital climate advisory services through the Agvisely Livestock Application for cattle and poultry raisers in Bangladesh', 'The Agvisely digital service for livestock integrates location-specific meteorological forecasts generated by the Bangladesh Meteorological Department (BMD) with species specific biological thresholds for weather variables (Temperature, rainfall, and temperature-humidity index (THI). When a biological threshold is to be breached in next five days'' forecast, the system automatically generates location-specific management advice for livestock farmers. Advisories are based on a decision tree developed by the Bangladesh Livestock Research Institute (BLRI) and the International Maize and Wheat Improvement Center  (CIMMYT).

Agvisely is a smart phone app and web-based service developed by CIMMYT with the of the Securing the Food Systems of Asian Mega-
Deltas (AMD) for Climate and Livelihood Resilience and the Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA) initiatives in collaboration with Bangladesh Department of Agricultural Extension (DAE) and Bangladesh Meteorological Department (BMD) that can be used by extension agents and officers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7651?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2217, 'AgWise crop model and machine learning decision support framework to create recommendations for farmers', 'The AgWise modular decision support framework consists of sequential crop modeling and machine learning steps that use public data on weather and soils, and a variety of agronomic trial data to generate recommendations that can increase yields and profit for farmers. It was created via agreement among data scientists across Centers to support EiA use case analytics and advisory goals. However, AgWise is intended to be "use case agnostic", and thus generalizable to any Use Case and  agronomy recommendation needs of external partners beyond the life of this initiative. The framework includes the logic, work modalities, workflows, data sourcing methodologies, scripts and repositories (with CG Labs as a collaborative workbench), initially focused on generating fertilizer and planting date recommendations for several crops in different agroecologies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2217?phase=1', 5, NULL),
(13252, 'Editing of cassava sweet genes for resistance to cassava bacterial blight (CBB) caused by the phytopathogenic bacterium', 'The aim of our research is to assess the resistance of cassava lines edited in the MeSweet 10a and 10e genes using CRISPR/Cas9 to confer resistance to CBB in the model variety 60444, susceptible to CBB. The goal is to evaluate the impact of editions in the promoter or coding regions for resistance to Xpm infection. Lines exhibiting the most promising mutations (INDELS) in targeted regions will be identified through molecular assays, which then will be established under in vitro and greenhouse conditions to be infected with Xpm strains and evaluatedisease resistance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13252?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(9516, 'Digital platform to support agro-climatic bulletin (ACB) development and tracking in Vietnam', 'The Alliance Asia team are in the early stages of developing a digital platform that will further streamline the process of developing Agro Climatic Bulletins (ACB) and improve the systematic tracking of ACB advisories. The dashboard will be linked to a database containing all historical advisories, from which it will be able to make recommendations to technical working groups on the types of interventions to include, based on past trends. The dashboard will also be a single location for technical working groups to access weather and climate information, along with hazard early warnings. Following the development of the ACB, the dashboard will automatically update the database with the selected advisory. Advisory will continue to be disseminated through the established Zalo groups but with data scraping used to collect information on the feedback provided by users in the form of emojis, which will also be logged in the database', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9516?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(7228, 'Exploring the potential of a hybrid approach for participatory adaptive capacity mapping and interactive dashboard', 'The Alliance of Bioversity & CIAT initially undertook an adaptive capacity mapping exercise as part of an ADB funded project aimed at supporting investments into rice-
based Agri-Food Systems in select Asian Mega Deltas (in Bangladesh, Cambodia, and Vietnam). The adaptive capacity analysis was used to support the identification of priority
areas for targeting interventions, and to ensure proposed investments were cognizant of some of the adoption barriers they would face in different regions.
Following the initial adaptive capacity mapping process, the team went on to develop an interactive platform to present the results of the adaptive capacity mapping exercise. This tool aims to facilitate development programming in the target deltas.

The dashboard exhibits the Adaptive Capacity, as well as the Economic Index, Human Index, Social Index, and Physical Index of the specific provinces and districts of Vietnam, Cambodia, and Bangladesh.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7228?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(17557, 'Digital climate advisory services (DCAS) through the Mekong Farm App for the Beong Ream Community Fish Refuge (CFR) and fish farmers in Cambodia', 'The Alliance of Bioversity and CIAT in collaboration with Worldfish and partners in Cambodia pilot Digital climate advisory services (DCAS) through the Mekong Farm App for the Beong Ream Community Fish Refuge (CFR) and fish farmers in Cambodia. The Mekong Farm Application designs a dedicated group feature for Community Fish Refuge (CFR) and fisheries farms, enabling members to post questions and engage in discussions with technical support personnel. In the group, technicians also uploaded the guidelines and climate advisories specifically tailored for fish-farming. The App include features such as Farmers’ guidelines, question and answers (Q&A), Trade mapping and linkage, Community function and Weather forecast.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17557?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(18725, 'Diversified farming systems through rice-sweet potato production for climate resilience in Cambodia', 'The Asian Mega Delta (AMD) Initiative has established rice-sweet potato experimental trials in Takeo to promote farm diversification for climate resilience among farmers in Cambodia. Through its Learning Alliance mechanisms, reflections from the trials are shared with implementers, farmers and value chain stakeholders. This will inform the design of diversification options to establish proof-of-concept that is tailored to local contexts and conditions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18725?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(6272, 'Community Nutrition Scholars deployment model to deliver nutrition interventions in Southern Bangladesh', 'The Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) Initiative through the International Potato Center (CIP) is implementing a Community Nutrition Scholars Program were select scholars (mostly women) are trained to be traners through training of trainers (ToT) to deliver nutrition-sensitive interventions in Bangladesh. These scholars are expected to capacitate local women and young mothers on various topics such as potato production through zero tillage with straw mulch, improve production technology of orange-fleshed sweetpotato, homestead vegetable gardening, nutrition, health, and hygiene to improve child feeding, food preparation, and hygiene practices in general. A training manual was developed for this to effectively capacitate women, youth, and mothers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6272?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16280, 'The innovation model for integrated decentralized food system governance at the district level in Cambodia', 'The Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) Initiative through WorldFish and International Water Management Institute (IWMI) is working to implement the pilot on The Innovation Model for Integrated Decentralized Food System Governance at the District Level to conduct the research and documentation on improving food system governance. This support the food system decentralization processes, build the connectivity between the community fisheries (CFis), community fish refuge (CFR), the irrigation system, and rice farming as an integrated food production system through improving water governance, promote the collaboration between the CFis, CFR and farmer water user communities (FWUC), community ecotourism and other relevant stakeholders to work together to manage the fishery, water, rice farming, and irrigation management based on collective approaches and interests to produce foods and support the livelihoods of local communities. Currently, two district level technical working groups (TWGs) were established and piloted.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16280?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(16668, ' Biochar as charcoal and carbon-rich material produced by partial oxidation of carbonaceous organic sources such as olive branches, to improve water holding capacity and soil structure in semi-arid areas, Tunisia', 'The biochar technology, whereby organic materiel like olive branches are carbonized through a slow burning process is a low cost technology for small scale farmers. Its major effects are the improvement of soil structure through crumb structure formation, increasing water holding capacity and reduction of need and improved efficiency of mineral fertilizers. It is therefore a well adapted technology in semi-arid areas were rainfall is scarce and improved efficiency of water use is important to increase yields of field crops and trees. The technology is well adapted for small-scale farmers in olive - cereal based production systems where you have abandoned olive tree branches after pruning for processing into biochar. Biochar can be used to increase wheat production as the major and most profitable cereal crop grown in North-West Tunisia', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16668?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3949, 'Biodiversification as a climate-resilient and water-efficient strategy for orchard farmers in Morocco and Tunisia to benefit rural and peri-urban residents', 'The biodiversification procedure is a set of steps guiding farmers and governance actors of orchard farming to design more climate-resilient and water efficient farms. It combines state-of-the-art water and crop models, a multi-objective farm system assessment tool, Citrig, with good agricultural and water management practices.  It is complementary to Morocco''s Green Generation Strategy, especially to the Conservation Agriculture Plan and contributes to national climate adaptation efforts of the Moroccan government.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3949?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(14577, 'Building Resilience, Inclusive Growth & Holistic Transformation (BRIGHT) Integrated Household Survey, Sri Lanka 2024/25: Household Questionnaire.', 'The BRIGHT survey contains several innovations that aim to uncover new insights into important survey methodology issues and shed light on intra-household resource allocation and gender and age differences in individuals’ perceptions on various household welfare dimensions. The two main innovations are:
1) Randomization of the subjective household wellbeing module: Both female and male respondents randomly receive this module and at three different points during the interview. By randomizing the households'' respondents, gender differences in the subjective assessment of household wellbeing can be examined. By randomizing the placement of the module, the sensitivity of reported wellbeing levels to topics surveyed prior to the assessment can be analyzed.
2) Duplication of several modules in the male and female respondent parts, as well as in the parts to multiple respondents of the same gender. These include the employment module where the man responds for himself, and the female responds for herself and all of the other household members; the women’s empowerment module, where both respondents answer questions on empowerment; the debt and savings module, where both respondents answer questions on their own savings; and the food-away-from-home consumption module, where female and male respondents answer questions for their families and their spouses. This innovation allows researchers to analyze intra-household gender and age differences in perceptions on the surveyed topics.
Other innovations include two versions of a water security scale, as well as an energy security scale. This allows researchers to examine which version performs better in capturing access to and quality of water and energy, respectively.  The new modules incorporated in the survey allow for a more granular analysis of various poverty dimensions', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14577?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(404, 'Business model assessment template to assess the viability of digital climate services for effective design and dissemination in South-Eastern Asia and Southern Asia', 'The business model assessment template allows a flexible examination of projected revenue streams under business to business (B2B), business to customer (B2C) and advsertisement. The costs include annualized fixed costs, variable operating costs and initial interactive voice response (IVR) set-up costs. Capacity utilisation part provides an estimate of business volume. All numbers provided are examples. 

The assessment template is crafted to be used by CG program implementers, policy actors, and the private sector particularly financial insitutions to determine and provide evidence on most cost-effective and efficient way of delivering climate advisory services to inform investment decisions and delivery mechanisms. 

In 2023, the model assessment template informed the new business models for the system of the Interactive Voice Response for Mungbean innovation. In 2024, using the model, a partnership between 2 private sector players was established to sustain financing of IVR.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/404?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4657, 'Tropical Poultry Platform (TROP) to catalyze a fair share of benefits to all poultry value chain actors in the Tropics', 'The call for establishing the Tropical Poultry Platform (TROP) has emanated from the need to address the critical bottlenecks constraining the efficient and effective operation of poultry value chains in the tropics. SAPLING will identify key actors and catalyse TROP’s creation. Working alongside national, regional, and private sector partners, we will engage and collaborate with partners, exploiting synergies and complementarities and ensuring alignment and harmonization of actions with like-minded institutions. Key private sector players in the poultry industry, investors, farmers’ groups/associations, civil society organizations and non-governmental organizations. Considering the above, TROP focuses on existing innovation platforms as well as other initiatives. The partnerships to be forged could be crystallized through project implementation/investment proposal agreements, Collaborative Research Agreements, memoranda of understanding and/or simple transactional/contractual arrangements. TROP will be established as a membership-based organization that would mobilize resources both from within its members as well as from potential outside financiers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4657?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5192, 'Digital information system for rice product labeling of carbon-footprints based on yield recovery (DISPLAY)', 'The carbon footprint calculator tool for the rice value chain embeds product-scale emission information into a Quick Response (QR) code that can be displayed on the rice package for transparent tracking and tracing. 

The interface guides users through each step of the field management activities and post-harvest processing that are relevant for measuring the carbon footprint. A selection of default values have been provided based on empirical measurements from scientific experiments or sourced from scientific literature. It allows users the flexibility to enter their own measured values or values from literature that have not been included by default.
The output provides the carbon footprint from the individual stages of the value chain and allows for comparison across different management options for use as a decision tool.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5192?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(3836, 'Cassava Lighthouse: Monitoring Open Access data for the Cassava Sector around the World, providing data driven analysis to all sectors', 'The Cassava Lighthouse is a digital platform that assembles and displays relevant information and analysis related to the cassava sector. It summarizes historical information from major cassava (manihot esculenta) producing countries, including topics of relevance such as production and productivity, trade and competitiveness, policies and incentives, genetics and breeding, pests and diseases. It seeks to provide relevant historical and timely updated information to strengthen the decision-making processes of key value chain actors like producers, processors/industry, consumers, and scientists around the world.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3836?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16561, 'A comprehensive agrifood systems performance measurement system to track progress towards the CGIAR  2030 Research and Innovation Strategy', 'The CGIAR  2030 Research and Innovation Strategy advances “a new strategy to address new challenges” for “transforming food, land, and water systems in a climate crisis”. Operationalizing this strategy necessitates tracking performance across ﬁve deﬁned Impact Areas: Nutrition, Health and Food Security; Poverty Reduction, Livelihoods, and Jobs; Gender Equality, Youth, and Social Inclusion; Climate Adaptation and Mitigation; and Environmental Health and Biodiversity. CGIAR does not yet have a set of performance measures ﬁt for this purpose. This collaborative innovation between CGIAR and scientists at Cornell University aims to conceptualize, develop and prototype a comprehensive agrifood systems performance measurement system to meet this need.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16561?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(2953, 'Green Pass Protocol to facilitate the exchange of phytosanitary clean germplasm from CGIAR centers.', 'The CGIAR Greenpass Phytosanitary Protocol (CGPP) represents the systems approach to managing the phytosanitary health of CGIAR genetic resources by the Germplasm Health Units (GHUs). In 2024, an expert consultation on enhancing phytosanitary procedures for germplasm exchange in Africa was organized. The workshop brought together Heads of Quarantine Agencies from nine African nations (Benin, Cameroon, Côte d’Ivoire, Ethiopia, Ghana, Kenya, Nigeria, Uganda, and Zimbabwe), representatives from the Food and Agriculture Organization Regional Office for Africa, CABI, and the African Union Inter-African Phytosanitary Council (AU-IAPSC). The discussions converged on the pressing need for a harmonized framework to facilitate safe germplasm exchange in Africa. Expert recommendations included identifying priority crops for harmonization, capacity building for officials in plant quarantine agencies, raising awareness about CGPP, and establishing a protocol for accrediting germplasm health facilities as per the ISPM-45. The workshop underscored the critical importance of regional collaboration in improving germplasm exchange systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2953?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(16646, 'Parametric insurance (Index based flood insurance) for climate resilience for smallholder farmers in East and Southern Africa', 'The CGIAR Initiative on Diversification in East and Southern Africa, Ukama-Ustawi (UU), identified the bundling of climate insurance as an innovative climate risk management solution for smallholder farmers in East and Southern Africa. This innovation involved mapping flood-prone agricultural areas near the river, ensuring tailored support for those most vulnerable. The innovation was aimed to mitigate the financial risks associated with recurrent flooding by providing timely insurance payouts to farmers while promoting climate resilience.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16646?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6397, 'Agronomy Community of Practices (ACoP) for technology transfer and agricultural development in countries within the Central and West Asia and North Africa (CWANA) region', 'The CGIAR Initiative on Fragility to Resilience in Central and West Asia and North Africa (F2R-CWANA) is establishing Agronomy Community of Practices (ACoP) in countries within CWANA. So far, ACoPs have been established in Sudan, Egypt, and Uzbekistan. This collaborative network aims to overcome barriers that hinder technology transfer and agricultural development by facilitating knowledge exchange among its members. The ACoP focuses on discussing scientific ideas, innovations, joint publications, local knowledge utilization, and context-specific agronomic solutions to enhance food and nutritional security in the region. Membership is open to individuals actively contributing to agricultural development through relevant practices. Online training sessions have been delivered to familiarize members with the establishment, rules, regulations, and objectives of the ACoP.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6397?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(9341, 'Coordinating the CGIAR Ontologies Community of Practice to support semantic interoperability across the CGIAR’s agricultural research datasets', 'The CGIAR Ontologies Community of Practice harnesses international expertise in knowledge representation and ontology development to produce missing ontologies, identifies best practices, and guides data labelling by teams managing multidisciplinary information platforms to release the FAIR data underpinning the evidence of research impact (Arnaud et al, 2020). Ontologies CoP is instrumental for advancing semantic interoperability across the CGIAR’s diverse and ever-growing agricultural research datasets The CoP stimulates exchanges between domain experts and experts in ontology design, knowledge modeling, ontology-driven applications, and semantic web technologies. The role of agrisemantics in FAIR principles, a role that grows now thanks to rapid advances in natural language (NLP) processing, likely needs to extend to innovative AI-driven digital tools. AI technologies often rely on extensive data encapsulated in large language models (LLMs), potentially lacking precision in specialist areas such as agri-food research. The CoP has already initiated dialogues around this extended roles through webinars.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9341?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(8829, 'Marker-trait association identified, developed and deployed for key traits in yam breeding to mainstream best practices in breeding pipelines', 'The classical approach to developing improved yam varieties is challenging and time-consuming. In 2023, IITA achieved significant progress in modernizing the yam breeding program through the development and deployment of molecular tools. Utilizing the yam product profile, 14 key agronomic and tuber quality traits were identified, leading to marker-trait association studies using various genetic models. Numerous markers across yam genomes were identified and documented. Promising SNP (Single Nucleotide Polymorphism) markers for nine traits and validated SNP markers for seven traits were identified, employing haplotype/allele segregation analysis and investigating putative genes. In marker-assisted selection, over 3,000 genotypes from early-generation trials were selected, and validated markers were tested for product advancement. To uncover genomic regions related to tuber quality traits, additional analyses were conducted, considering five mapping populations for a food quality index based on instrument and sensory assays, leading to the identification of several markers linked to the index.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8829?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(9458, 'Cooperative Climate Cleverness Check: Improvement and testing of tool to assess current farming practice of cooperatives and opportunities for climate smart agriculture', 'The Climate Clever Check (CCC) tool, developed by Agriterra and the Alliance, is designed to integrate climate considerations into cooperative assessments. It provides an accessible, user-friendly way to evaluate climate impacts without the need for specialized knowledge. Unlike more complex tools, the CCC allows for quick, objective assessments by users without special technical expertise. The tool covers the key areas of Food and Agriculture Organizations (FAO)''s climate smart agricultural practices and helps cooperatives prioritize climate issues, identify risks, and design effective response strategies to protect both production and income

Tested in four cooperatives in Mekong River Delta (MRD), the tool offers comprehensive climate assessments. While it works well for agricultural cooperatives, it needs improvements to be fully effective for fisheries and livestock cooperatives. Key upgrades include adding a universal climate risk index, adjusting the weighting of questions, and ensuring an even distribution of questions across categories to avoid bias.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9458?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(3851, 'Climate Security Dialogues Dashboard for researchers and climate action intervention designers to track the rapidly-evolving discourse on climate security and design and implement more responsive and accurate projects, programs, policies', 'The Climate Security Dialogues Dashboard is an interactive tool that collects and analyses online data to display trends about climate security debates in digital spaces. It aims to monitor conversations about climate security to identify the most prevalent and/or relevant topics, actors, places, and events related to these discussions. Analysis draws on innovative research approaches that leverage social media platforms and other digital spaces as proxies for wider public discourse, opinion, and engagement. Currently, the tool features insights from a social media pilot module with historical data from Twitter (currently X), and a News Media module, developed in collaboration with Media Cloud.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3851?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(4081, 'Climate Security Investment Plan Methodology for environmental policymakers, investors for benefitting climate vulnerable rural communities', 'The Climate Security Investment Plan (CSIP) methodology provides a systematic way to develop ready-to-finance interventions with dual climate change adaptation and peacebuilding objectives for financial organizations offering climate finance products and financial regulators. It integrates distant perspectives and methods in climate security and climate finance fields and improves the performance of climate action interventions. ClimBeR climate finance and climate security developed the CSIP methodology through a co-creation process with national stakeholders and consultation of national strategic documents.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4081?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(1111, 'The Climate Security Observatory (CSO) for livestock production systems', 'The Climate Security Observatory (CSO) is an online dashboard targeting policy and decision makers, development and humanitarian practitioners and advisors to help them with targeting, programming and prioritization of investments particularly in areas under climate stress. The CSO uses a mixed method approach to give answers to four lead questions: Where are the most vulnerable areas to climate-related insecurities and risks? Who are the groups vulnerable to climate and security risks that should be targeted? How does climate exacerbate the causes of conflict? What needs to be done to break the vicious cycle between climate and conflict? The CSO brings together robust, localized and contextualized and policy-relevant evidence on the climate-security nexus. It includes a dashboard on climate security programming for climate finance, the climate security sensitivity tool, climate security mapping and an interactive dashboard for climate security dialogues.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1111?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4056, 'Climate Security Observatory (CSO) for providing science-based, site-specific and actionable climate security risk information to policymakers to design and implement interventions for reducing conflict and climate vulnerability of rural communities', 'The Climate Security Observatory (CSO) is an online decision-making support tool providing robust, localized, and policy-relevant evidence on how exactly climate security risks may emerge across different geographic contexts. It aims to answer several research questions on how the climate security nexus operates at the subnational level. To achieve this, the CSO performs a series of mixed-methods analyses (data-driven literature review, pathway analysis, social media issue mapping, governance coherence analysis, network analysis, spatial analysis, econometric analysis), before synthesizing outputs into an open-access online dashboard.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4056?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(20077, 'Climate Security Sensitivity Tool for Livestock Systems: Enhancing Conflict-Sensitive and Peace-Responsive Climate Adaptation', 'The Climate Security Sensitivity Tool  for Livestock Systems (CSSTxLS) is an ex-ante programming assessment tool for conflict-sensitive and peace-responsive climate action in livestock production systems. The CSSTxLS supports practitioners and decision-makers developing adaptation initiatives in avoiding maladaptation and its associated unintended consequences, avoiding conflict relapses, and contributing to peacebuilding.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20077?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(4103, 'Climate Smart Governance Dashboard for environmental policy makers to design and implement multi-sectoral, mid- to -long-term adaptation plans benefiting climate vulnerable rural communities', 'The Climate Smart Governance (CSG) Dashboard provides accurate data on climate-related hazards, population vulnerability, forecast climate scenarios, sector-relevant data (water, health, agriculture, ecosystem), infrastructure and climate-related projects to support coordinated mid- to long-term adaptation planning aligned to global development goals. Specifically, it can help nations undertake the National Adaptation Planning (NAP) process. The CSG Dashboard has been designed with the NAP process in mind to provide a space in which public- and private sector entities can obtain and use up-to-date data to develop effective, well-coordinated adaptation policies; monitor and evaluate progress towards meeting adaptation goals; collaborate for effective use of resources; and share information on best practices and investment opportunities. CSG uses the Early Warning, Early Action, Early Finance (AWARE) platform engine and benefits from the capabilities of the platform that are validated in the South Asian context.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4103?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(256, 'Climate-Smart Risk Maps and Adaptation Plans (CS-MAP) for Rice Production in Bangladesh, Cambodia and Vietnam', 'The climate-smart risk maps and adaptation plans (CS-MAP) is a participatory approach involving experts from various local and national offices for (1) identifying climate-related risks; (2) delineating affected areas and risk levels; (3) proposing corresponding adaptive plans; (4) fine-tuning and verifying propose measures; and (5) developing integrated provincial and regional adaptation plans. Risk maps were developed for normal and El Niño Southern Oscillation (ENSO) years by using technical data, infrastructures, and local observations. 

The CS Maps are designed to be used by policymakers and planners from regional and local offices to inform annual crop planning at province and district levels for scaling of adaptation solutions in rice production. The CS-MAP approach has been expanded to Cambodia and Bangladesh with numerous consultations, evaluations and validation with local stakeholders and farmers to develop and scale the CS-MAP approach for local planning.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/256?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1185, 'Sustainable intensification of cocoa production through the development and dissemination of Integrated Soil Fertility Management option (CocoaSoils Use Case)', 'The Cocoa Soils project aims to enhance cocoa productivity sustainably by developing and sharing Integrated Soil Fertility Management (ISFM) strategies, aiming to prevent deforestation. Targeted at cocoa farmers, this innovation is in the development phase, utilizing the Stepwise Investment Pathway to guide the adoption of Best Management Practices (BMPs). This approach ensures each investment maximizes returns at minimal risk, facilitating a transition to climate-smart cocoa production. The project''s app will assist farmers in identifying and prioritizing management practices based on carefully designed criteria, enabling informed, low-risk investment decisions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1185?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(9206, 'Elite pre-released yam clones tested under on-farm variety verification trials for commercial release nomination', 'The collaborative effort between the IITA yam breeding program and NRCRI established on-farm trials at 18 locations across nine Nigerian states to evaluate two candidate clones, TDr1302042 and TDr1302100, alongside benchmark cultivars Asiedu (TDr8902665) and local checks. Twenty stands of each genotype were planted on mounds, and the trials employed a participatory variety selection (PVS) model involving various end-users and stakeholders. Through structured questionnaires, 500 respondents, including farmers, processors, marketers, and consumers, provided feedback on candidate clone performance. Data collected covered tuber fresh yield, tuber browning, flour yield, peel loss, and proximate analysis. Both test clones demonstrated favorable attributes over the checks, with TDr1302100 exhibiting high food quality in boiled and pounded consumption. Overall, the agronomic, nutritional, and food quality assessment supports the official release of candidate TDr1302100, which has shown remarkable yield potential and adaptation across diverse Nigerian regions. TDr1302100 was officially released in Nigeria on January 11, 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9206?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(2136, 'Improved high-performance computation infrastructure for data scientists in low bandwidth environments', 'The Collaborative GARDIAN (CG) Labs high-performance compute-and-store solution was improved through targeted development to maximize cost efficiency to advance data science for agriculture, particularly in limited bandwidth situations. This infrastructure works more or less like a supercomputer cluster, but is optimized for low bandwidth environments. An authenticated user (typically a data scientist) can choose a high, medium, or low computing environment based on their needs, and CG Labs will spin up a virtual machine for them/their teams to proceed with setting up particular large data sources, analytics jobs etc. This innovation is being improved through use and feedback from other initiatives and non-pooled projects.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2136?phase=1', 9, NULL),
(2175, 'Metadata mining methodology to facilitate integration of databases for aquaculture and fisheries', 'The combining and integration of datasets from different sources are inhibited by poor labelling of variables and metadata by project authors.  This innovation is a script written in R to mine text and terms utilised in Aquatic foods datasets that can be used to characterise those datasets and allow for integration with other data for metanalysis.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2175?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3847, 'Common Bean Observatory: an open access platform for common bean (Phaseolus vulgaris) around the world, providing data driven analysis to all sectors', 'The Common beans observatory is an initiative led by the Alliance Bioversity-CIAT, with support from the Pan-African Bean Research Alliance (PABRA), to establish an information system that provides indicators related to the global bean production context, includes data related to the bean context in each country, production system information, trade and competitiveness information, bean consumption data, inventory of bean technologies, recent news, and will present data on seed systems, varieties and socioeconomics collected by PABRA in a dynamic and interactive way for the benefit of key players in the bean sector. The information displayed on the platform is built based on data provided through open-access primary and secondary information sources from different national and international organizations, which is why we highlight the importance and contribution of these organizations. The observatory is oriented to provide lessons to producers, processors/industry, consumers, scientists and any other actor interested in the crop', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3847?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9729, 'Excellence in Agronomy Village Communication App for extension staff and farmers in Sub-saharan Africa', 'The communication app is an information system that serves as a repository of agronomical information in a communication app, crop management advisories and tools, videos and fact sheets for extension staff and farmers in Sub-Saharan Africa', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9729?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2195, 'Community Based Resource Management (CBRM) Training Package for coastal fisheries in the Solomon Islands for providing technical support and inclusive planning to communities', 'The Community-Based Resource Management (CBRM) Training Package for coastal fisheries in the Solomon Islands was developed to provide technical support and inclusive planning for communities managing their coastal resources. Over four years, a CBRM program was implemented in partnership with the Ministry of Fisheries and Marine Resources (MFMR), Malaita Provincial Government, and WorldFish.
The program engaged 96 communities, raising awareness about CBRM and assessing existing practices. Until now, 32 communities had developed eight management plans, and 72 community facilitators from 51 communities were trained using a CBRM monitoring and evaluation tool. Recognizing the importance of customary land tenure, the Solomon Islands Government has designated CBRM as the primary coastal resource management strategy. This training package strengthens the role of Provincial Fisheries Officers (PFOs), equipping them with the knowledge and capacity to support community-led fisheries management, ensuring sustainable resource use and guiding future CBRM scaling across other provinces.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2195?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8990, 'Comprehensive Agronomy Solutions Hub: Streamlining Digital Data, Enhancing Research, Providing Crop Advisories, Standardizing Guides, Facilitating Mechanization, and Revolutionizing Agricultural Practices for Increased Productivity and Sustainability', 'The Comprehensive Agronomy Solutions Hub serves as an innovative website, disseminating agricultural solutions across four main areas: digital data management, crop-specific advisories, standardized agronomy guides, and mechanization. This website streamlines the collection, management, and accessibility of digital data, significantly enhancing agronomic research and fostering innovation. It offers targeted advisories for annual crops, including recommendations on optimal fertilizer use, planting schedules, variety selection, and weather forecasts. In addition, the hub provides access to standardized agronomy guides and resources for prioritizing practices, thereby establishing best practices in the field.This approach revolutionizes agronomic practices by making them widely accessible, thus markedly improving agricultural productivity and sustainability. The website is catering to researchers, farmers, and other stakeholders, currently features 16 solutions from the listed categories. Moreover, the platform allows users to filter solutions by specific crops or regions, showcasing the global application of the Excellence in Agronomy initiative’s diverse solutions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8990?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4472, 'Planting Date Advisories disseminated to over 1million farmers through audio recording in India', 'The content of the Planting Date Advisories were developed into messages and disseminated to farmers across Bihar through the dissemination platforms of BAU, JEEViKA and IFFCO kisan. The advisories were approved and accepted by the Convergence Platform in the form of text and audio to be disseminated to farmers across the state, as the part of the extension activities (Audio advisories) in the state of Bihar, Convergence Platform consists of members from research universities, public and private partners such as BAU, IKSL, and Jeevika which disseminates the text messages and IVR calls to the farmers in Bihar. ', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4472?phase=1', 8, NULL),
(9553, 'Co-production of knowledge in living labs for people (LL4P): Collaborative community research for food systems transformation in Colombia and Kenya', 'The co-production of knowledge will be used in the Living Labs for People (LL4Ps). Working with teams in Colombia and Kenya, the political ecology framework for the co-production of knowledge will be employed to analyze knowledge systems and improve collaborative, community-based research for food systems transformation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9553?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(5193, 'Cost-Impact Analysis for Emission Reduction Projects (COMPARE) tool to inform low-emission investment planning for rice-based production systems in Vietnam', 'The Cost-Impact Analysis for Emission Reduction Projects (COMPARE) is a tool developed by UNIQUE Forestry and Land Use in conjunction with International Rice Research Institute. This tool is a Microsoft® Excel workbook with built-in formulas and functions that allow automatic calculation of Cost Benefit Analysis indicators and GHG emissions according to the user''s defined inputs.

This Excel Tool is designed to provide a Cost Benefit Assessment showing the economic and carbon impacts of different rice management practices at a regional level in Vietnam. The tool is designed to cater to different user types and allows full flexibility to adapt the tool for advanced users. It enables the user to estimate the benefits, costs, and greenhouse gas savings in a "Business As Usual" (BAU) scenario (broadly reflecting the current mix of rice production methods) and a “Project” scenario (reflecting a desired mix of rice production methods).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5193?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5347, 'Sustainable Healthy Diets established a country coordination unit in Ethiopia to partner with national stakeholders on implementing the food systems transformation agenda and support research implementation', 'The country coordination units in the Sustainable Healthy Diets target countries are a central part of our strategy to strengthen the connections between CGIAR researchers and national partners in food systems policy processes. The Units are comprised of researchers from all work packages (WPs) plus Wageningen University and Research (WUR) and representatives from specific national partners. This institutional innovation is the primary pathway we will use to engage with in-country multi-stakeholder processes that improve the design and implementation of the national roadmaps towards food systems transformation for sustainable healthy diets. In addition, the Units ensure research coherence and efficiency by formalizing information sharing between WPs and with other CGIAR Initiatives working in the country. Sustainable Healthy Diets established the Units in Vietnam in 2022, in Ethiopia in 2023, and plans to establish one in Bangladesh in early 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5347?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(9521, 'Crop Decision Tree (CDT) for development of seasonal Agro-Climatic Bulletin (ACBs) for rice farmers in Mekong River Delta (MRD), Vietnam.', 'The Crop Decision Tree (CDT) is a supplementary tool for systematically developing seasonal Agro-Climatic Bulletins, which are based on forecasted rainfall scenarios for the entire growing season. As part of the Asia-Mega Initiative (AMD), the Alliance of Bioversity International & CIAT collaborated with the Department of Crop Production (DCP) and sub-DCPs across seven Mekong River Delta (MRD) provinces to develop CDTs for rice at regional and provincial levels. The provinces involved include Tien Giang, Hau Giang, Kien Giang, An Giang, Can Tho, Tra Vinh, and Soc Trang.

The development of CDTs was informed by historical seasonal Agro-Climatic Bulletins and the technical expertise of national and provincial agricultural experts. The CDTs consist of:
•        A crop calendar detailing crop growth stages and associated climate risks.
•        Farming recommendations aligned with crop growth stages based on seasonal rainfall scenarios (above, normal, or below average).
•        Farming advisories tailored to crop growth stages under extreme weather conditions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9521?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(445, 'Dairy profitability simulator mobile app for extension agents and dairy farmers to ensure profitability of their enterprise', 'The Dairy profitability simulator is a mobile application that integrates practical and scientific knowledge in an algorithm that projects the expected annual production and profitability of the small-holding enterprise based on known input variables affordable and accessible to the smallholder farmer. The application reveals to the smallholders, the inputs, services and practices that need to be adjusted to ensure profitability of their enterprise. It also enables structured engagement between extension service providers and farmers and enables targeted advisory services to smallholders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/445?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3967, 'Objective-Based High-Accuracy Farmer Segmentation Dashboard for Climate Action Project, Program and Policy Designers', 'The dashboard enables exploration of a rich set of multi-dimensional information such as type of farming activities, the sources of farm and non-farm income, farmer welfare and food security and gender equity concerns specific to the context, and contributes to the design of more comprehensive and accurate farmer segment characterization.  Using flexible, more comprehensive and accurate farmer segments, higher performing, more targeted climate action projects, programs and policies can be designed and implemented. The information provided by the dashboard can be used by local, regional and national policy makers as well as agricultural and environmental intervention designers operating in the public and private sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3967?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(5886, 'Decision Support Tool for Circular bioeconomy Solutions', 'The Decision support tool, using a multi-criteria decision method, is designed to assist decision-makers in selecting appropriate business models for Nature-Positive solutions in the context of circular bioeconomy. This tool makes a holistic assessment of interconnected objectives and the evaluation of alternatives while explicitly considering the priorities of various decision makers and stakeholders. It enables decision-makers and practitioners involved in the planning, implementing, and advocating circular economy initiatives to select appropriate circular economy business models that result in positive social, economic, and environmental outcomes. It uses a user-friendly interface where users can modify inputs and test scenarios and an interactive data visualization dashboard for result analysis.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5886?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(397, 'Development of livestock decision trees to provide climate advisories based on livestock growth stages to extension agents and livestock raisers in Bangladesh', 'The decision tree provides information on growth stage specific climate advisories for livestock driven by tempeature, THI (tempeature humidity index) and rainfall theresholds identified by systematic literature review. The livestock decision trees are now incorporated in another innovation (Agvisely Livestock). The translation of the livestock advisory is completed and it is now integrated to Agvisely application in Bangladesh realising the first dynamic climate advisory for livestock in the country.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/397?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3318, 'Estimating seed demand and seed supply of potato varieties for processing and understanding farmers'' motivations to switch to new varieties and clean seeds in Peru', 'The development and deployment of improved crop varieties have significant effects on productivity, profitability, household income, nutrition, and poverty alleviation.  However, in most countries, farmers’ access to quality seed of recently released varieties remains very low. A first stage literature review that will help to define seed delivery pathways for dissemination of new varieties released by CIP in Peru and in other countries. The objective is to conduct a literature review on the following topics:
•	Product profiles, market segments, seed delivery pathways / seed business models, and dissemination strategies for new varieties
•	Methodologies to estimate seed requirements quantitatively and qualitatively, estimate farmers’ seed demand, and how private companies deal with variability on demand
The review will focus on potatoes. Other crops (especially maize) and vegetatively propagated crops (sweetpotato, cassava, banana, and yam) are included for comparative purposes and as relevant.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3318?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(4085, 'Preliminary Sentinel-2-based host (wheat) greenness/phenology map of Ethiopia for improving integrated spore dispersal and environmental suitability forecasting models for identifying and monitoring susceptible host and green bridging areas (main season)', 'The development of an earth observation method to map the host (wheat) greenness/phenology in Ethiopia has been started using Sentinel-2 satellite time series analysis and a preliminary dynamic wheat greenness map over a pilot area in Oromia, Ethiopia was produced for the 2020 main season using monthly NDVImax derived from Sentinel-2. High-resolution (10 m) static and dynamic (greenness) host wheat distribution maps generated using Random Forest and Sentinel-2 satellite time series; Pre-processed with state-of-the-art methods; Classification based on EthCT2020 dataset; Greenness is expressed through NDVI thresholding (all NDVI values between 0.15-0.8 are considered as green wheat). The model is ready for scaling.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4085?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(4096, 'Preliminary Sentinel-2-based irrigated cropland map of Ethiopia for improving the integrated spore dispersal and environmental suitability forecasting models and better advisory targeting to wheat farmers (2021 off-season)', 'The development of an earth observation method to map the irrigated host (wheat) distribution in Ethiopia has been started using Sentinel-2 satellite time series analysis and a preliminary irrigated cropland map over a pilot area in Oromia, Ethiopia was produced for the 2021 off-season using Sentinel-2 NDVI time series. The method development is still ongoing and product needs to be refined and up-scaled to the national-scale. Existing wheat rust disease early warning and advisory system will benefit from this new information layer in terms of improving the integrated spore dispersal and environmental suitability forecasting models as well as better advisory targeting to wheat farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4096?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(6245, 'Development of a Human Security Index (HSI) for smallholder farmers and households in rural areas', 'The development of the Human Security Index (HSI) aims to fill in the gaps on empirical research that has clearly defined the concept of human security and rendered it measurable. The Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) Initiative''s research aims to create a HSI specifically tailored for rural areas,where communities are often increasingly vulnerable to impacts of climate change. Inspired from the HSI developed by Adger et al. (2020), AMD aims to better capture and assess the unique challenges and security concerns experienced by people living in rural contexts, particularly those exacerbated by climate change. We collected the data for HSI in the Ganges Delta in Bangladesh and Mekong Delta in Vietnam. This initiative seeks to provide a more accurate and context-specific tool for evaluating and understanding human security in rural environments contributing to effective strategies and policies aimed at addressing the needs of vulnerable populations in the face of climate-related challenges.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6245?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(14080, 'Expansion of the Cumbal model: Integrated agrobiodiversity conservation', 'The different activities of implemented by Nature+ WP1 have motivated Agrosavia on continue strengthening and expanding the model of integrated conservation. This model conservation integrates in situ and ex situ conservation activities. To do the expansion of the model, we were invited by Agrosavia to submit two concept notes to continue implementing activities of Integrated Agrobiodiversity conservation. Both concept notes aim to conserve agrobiodiversity and promote ecosystem restoration. One of them was submitted to the call IKI Colombia and the second one was submitted to the Regalias call. We consider these invitations as a great output because it shows that the work of Nature+ WP1 added value to our partners in Colombia and they are in need of new approaches to study agrobiodiversity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14080?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(2927, 'Amending the CG Climate Information Services for Rwanda using a downstream service for predicting fall armyworm and Striga infestation areas', 'The digital advisory is an online and SMS service system that effectively delivers early warning and real time information on production stressors and advises small-scale farmers accordingly. Spatially explicit and multi-scale satellite data and climate and pests/weeds data are uniquely combined in a digital platform, and accurate advisory information is then channeled to end users. For instance, farmers could be advised to plant less cereal crops this season because of anticipated Fall Armyworm infestation for a specific location. Reliable information about abiotic/biotic production constraints will prove critical in managing sustainable cropland productivity. In due time, cropping systems information such as fields are under mono- or mixed-cropping or if fields are irrigated/rainfed will be added in order to improve pest and climate risk forecasting. The production constraints information over time can also be used to guide the scale out of climate and pest-smart agriculture, such conservation agriculture or the “push-pull” system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2927?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(19581, 'Amending the CGIAR Climate Information Services for Ghana using a downstream service for predicting fall armyworm and Striga infestation areas', 'The digital advisory is an online and SMS service system that effectively delivers early warning and real time information on production stressors and advises small-scale farmers accordingly. Spatially explicit and multi-scale satellite data and climate and pests/weeds data are uniquely combined in a digital platform, and accurate advisory information is then channeled to end users. For instance, farmers could be advised to plant less cereal crops this season because of anticipated Fall Armyworm infestation for a specific location. Reliable information about abiotic/biotic production constraints will prove critical in managing sustainable cropland productivity. In due time, cropping systems information such as fields are under mono- or mixed-cropping or if fields are irrigated/rainfed will be added in order to improve pest and climate risk forecasting. The production constraints information over time can also be used to guide the scale out of climate and pest-smart agriculture, such conservation agriculture or the “push-pull” system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19581?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(7881, 'Tailoring the digital Agro-Climate Advisory (DACA) platform in Ghana', 'The Digital AgroClimate Advisory platform has developed a methodology to tailor some of its components to specific countries. This methodology was initially implemented in Ghana, and this involves: (1) service creation, (2) integration of data and contextualization and (3) service delivery, which involves customization of the systems to different conditions such as country or agro-ecological contexts. Service creation involves two steps: (1) collecting data on three major parameters (weather and climate data, soil data and crop characteristics) followed by (2) analysis of data that includes the analytics and climate/weather models, etc. Service delivery involves two steps: (1) bundling weather and climate information together with the crop information (such as crop water requirements, days to maturity, etc.) and crop fertilizer recommendations, and (2) delivering through DACA mobile app.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7881?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(14443, 'Digital Twin Open Data Cube: Repository of raster data products providing structured access to spatial datasets, crucial for modeling and analysis within the digital twin', 'The Digital Innovation team at IWMI has developed a new data system to enhance digital twin analysis, enabling timely natural resource management decisions in the Limpopo River Basin. The Digital Twin Open Data Cube serves as a structured repository of raster datasets, essential for spatial modeling and analysis. Built on the Open Data Cube framework, it optimizes data organization, visualization, and retrieval, facilitating real-time decision-making and long-term environmental monitoring within the Limpopo River Basin digital twin.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14443?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(13356, 'Diversity for Restoration (D4R) India - A digital solution to enhance resilience of trees-based restoration in Western Ghats, India', 'The digital tool supports India''s strategy in land restoration through improving outcomes of tree-based restoration and planting activities. The tool was developed for the Western Ghats, a biodiversity hotspot region, to help with choosing appropriate tree species and seed sources for tree-based restoration or other tree planting activities. Using species distribution and suitability models, the tool can choose appropriate tree species that survive current and future climatic conditions for a specified location. The tool uses a trait-based approach (based on 110 functional traits) to identify appropriate species (from 246 species) from a suitable list of species for the region based on local site conditions and restoration objectives while also accounting for current and future climatic scenarios. A list of species with seed sources based on restoration objectives and species diversity is provided to the user as a guide to select appropriate species and seed sources for restoration of specified location.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13356?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17026, 'A Step-by-Step Plan to Integrate Knowledge Acquired from the Digital Agriculture Plot and Courses into Academic Programs', 'The digital transformation of Latin America’s agricultural sector has driven innovation, yet small and medium-sized producers in Guatemala struggle to access and apply these technologies. To bridge this gap, the Bioversity-CIAT Alliance and CUNORI-USAC developed the Digital Plot, an experimental space for agricultural technologies, complemented by digital training diplomas for teachers. These initiatives aim to modernize agronomy curricula and strengthen capacity for integrating digital tools into education. However, achieving systemic change requires stronger collaboration among stakeholders to align education and research with producers'' real needs. Using Human-Centered Design, this report analyzes CUNORI’s teacher training initiatives and digital curriculum integration, marking a pivotal step in transforming agricultural education and fostering networks that promote digital innovation adoption. This experience serves as a reference for institutions across the region, emphasizing the importance of co-creating localized solutions to address food system challenges effectively.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17026?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(9721, 'Responsible Digital Transformation Toolbox in Agri-Food Systems: Addressing challenges in technology design, access, and system complexity by guiding practiioners and decision-makers', 'The digitalization of agri-food systems calls for an approach that ensures ethical and sustainable technology use. Current frameworks focus on fairness, privacy, and stakeholder involvement but face challenges like an overemphasis on technology and inadequate engagement. This work introduces the Responsible Digital Transformation Toolbox, designed for agri-food practitioners. It categorizes digital innovation challenges into cyber, social, and physical aspects and centers on technology design, accessibility, and system complexity. The toolbox aims to guide practitioners towards sustainable and equitable digital practices in the agri-food sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9721?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(14857, 'Expansion of the Direct Seeded Rice Breeding Network to better represent the target population of environments', 'The Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) breeding network added new partners in South Asia and East and Southern Africa to better represent the target population of environments (TPEs). Further, a new breeding pipeline targeting the upland environments in South Asia was initiated, which will further enhance the coverage of the program. The program now has more than 50 screening sites across all target market segments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14857?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(779, 'In doubled-up legume systems several legumes are strategically combined in the same planting space. Doubled legume systems are a principle of sustainable intensification to increase crop yields per unit land area.', 'The doubled-up legume system involving groundnuts and pigeon pea, comes across as being a perfect combination as the groundnuts only exploit the shallow soil profile and mature relatively faster whereas pigeon peas develop much slower while being deeper-rooted, leading to less competition in the system. Pigeon peas mature at least 2-3 months after the groundnuts, usually providing an increase in overall grain yield from both crops. Pigeon peas also add a large amount of slow decomposing biomass, besides nitrogen rich leaves and stems, which can be used partially as surface mulch or firewood. This benefits the whole cropping system and the household.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/779?phase=1', 9, NULL),
(18424, 'Digital Twin Drought Monitoring Tool for the Limpopo River Basin', 'The drought monitoring tool for the Limpopo River Basin integrates Earth Observation (EO) datasets, meteorological indices, and hydrological modeling to track drought conditions in near real-time. Developed under the IWMI Digital Twin platform, it leverages climate data, precipitation records, and vegetation health indicators such as the Standardized Precipitation Index (SPI) and Vegetation Condition Index (VCI) to assess drought severity and trends. The system incorporates satellite-derived rainfall anomalies, evapotranspiration assessments, and water balance calculations, helping policymakers and researchers understand the impact of drought on water resources, agriculture, and food security. The tool identifies regions most affected by dry conditions, enabling proactive decision-making for drought mitigation and adaptation strategies. By visualizing changes in water availability and vegetation health, it supports long-term planning for irrigation, water allocation, and climate resilience efforts. This comprehensive approach enhances early warning capabilities, helping governments and stakeholders implement timely interventions to minimize the socio-economic impact of droughts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18424?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(17050, 'EcoFarm: A digital advisory tool for farmers engaged in natural farming processes to assist on-farm input optimization, and soil and water quality management', 'The EcoFarm App is an innovative digital platform designed to empower farmers with advanced tools for sustainable agriculture. Leveraging AI-powered analytics, the app evaluates input resources, soil health, and water quality to deliver actionable recommendations. Key features include:

- Resource Optimization: Personalized insights on efficient usage of soil, water, and fertilizers.  
- Crop Advisory: Tailored recommendations for crop selection, irrigation scheduling, and harvesting timelines to enhance yields.  
- Pest and Disease Management: Proactive strategies to mitigate risks using eco-friendly methods.  
- Sustainable Practices: Guidance on organic and natural farming techniques for long-term environmental health.  

By integrating AI and data analytics, the EcoFarm App fosters sustainable farming, helping farmers boost productivity while adopting eco-conscious methods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17050?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17180, 'Cryopreservation protocol for yacon developed and ready for implementation (average recovery rate >65%) once the accessions are tested to be disease free', 'The effect of cold acclimation on in vitro yacon plants prior to cryopreservation was assessed using a set of 28 accessions. Shoot tips excised from in vitro yacon plants, which were incubated for one week at 20 °C followed by two weeks at 7 °C (cold acclimation), showed a significantly higher full-plant recovery rate (67.5%) after thawing from liquid nitrogen (+LN) compared to shoot tips from in vitro plants grown for three weeks at 20 °C. It was decided that the assessed yacon protocol could immediately be applied for routine cryopreservation once the virus elimination process is complete.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17180?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1876, 'Cutting frequency for  three food-feed oat varieties in crop-livestock mixed systems in Ethiopia', 'The effect of different cutting management on the yield and quality of forage oat and food oat varieties (Desho grass and vetch)  was investigated in Ethiopian Highlands. The oat varieties were harvested at the vegetative and maturity stages to explore if this practice can result in a yield and quality advantage over one-time cutting at maturity. For the forage oat one-time cutting at hay stage was compared with two-time cutting at vegetative and maturity stages. The tree Lucerne cutting management trial plots had been established in the 2021 planting season to generate additional evidence on the optimal cutting height and frequency under field conditions.
Evidence on forage harvesting frequency and biomass of food-feed oat varieties generated per year will be bundled with other information to promote wider scaling of tree Lucerne in the Ethiopian Highlands.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1876?phase=1', 7, NULL),
(8964, 'Assessment of the Effectiveness of Fawligen (Spodoptera frugiperda Multiple Nucleo-polyhedrovirus) Against Fall Armyworm on Maize', 'The effectiveness of (Fawligen®) (Spodoptera frugiperda multiple nucleopolyhedrovirus) against FAW in maize was studied in Zambia in 2023 over five weeks. Threshold-based Fawligen sprays applied to plots established from seed treated with FortenzaTM Duo or Fortenza® were compared to calendar-based sprays of Fawligen applied to plots established from untreated seed, untreated control, and Ampligo®/Denim Fit® sprays on plots established from untreated seed. In plots established from untreated maize seed, calendar-based Fawligen sprays (7 in total) effectively reduced plant damage relative to the untreated control at 7, 21, 28, and 35 days after crop emergence (DAE). In contrast, threshold-based sprays (5 in total) reduced FAW damage only at 28 and 35 DAE. When threshold-based Fawligen sprays were applied in Fortenza Duo or Fortenza plots, plant damage was significantly lower than the control throughout the five weeks of assessment only in the former.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8964?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(7337, 'Excellence in Agronomy Initiative (EiA) dashboard to monitor data being collected by EiA use case personnel - and allow rapid adjustments', 'The EiA data monitoring dashboard is a prototype R-shiny deployment that uses data submitted by Use Cases to the ONA data management system to allow Use Case personnel to assess the status and quality of data collection in near-real time. This allows Use Cases to quickly course-correct as needed, to ensure higher quality data, and address any issues related to missing or erroneous data. The dashboard allows Use Case personnel to look at collected data in tabular form, and by enumerator - as also in visual form to get a birds-eye view of data collection by region, for instance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7337?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7394, 'Tocho energy bar made from Tarwi- a native species whose nutritional value is underestimated', 'The energy bar made from Tarwi, an Andean legume with high nutritional value and protein. Its nutrirional value has preveously been underrated. Tarwi grows in the heights of the Puno region of Peru. It is used in form of flakes. Different recipes can be made by varying amount of sugar and other ingredients. the product can be consumed by everyone, of different age and gender.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7394?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(7289, 'The Enviro-Champs: Establishing a framework for a technologically upgraded community environmental monitoring network for water quality monitoring', 'The Enviro-Champs are the unsung young heroes of South Africa, bridging the crucial gap between nature and communities. Originating from the Mpophomeni township in KwaZulu-Natal, this initiative, under the Duzi-uMngeni Conservation Trust (DUCT), began by empowering local youth to monitor environmental issues like sewage leaks and illegal dumping. Their efforts quickly extended to tackling water leaks, improving waste management, and enhancing flood preparedness, addressing vital needs within the socio-ecological landscape of South Africa. Technological tools such as group text messaging and Excel for data management have bolstered their activities. Today, approximately 1,000 Enviro-Champs contribute significantly to environmental monitoring, ecological restoration, and food security efforts in KwaZulu-Natal. Their success in various regions showcases the initiative''s potential for national expansion. Supported by UNICEF''s youth upliftment initiative, YOMA, the Enviro-Champs program is poised for broader national and possibly international growth, demonstrating the power of youth engagement in environmental conservation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7289?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(8733, 'Pathways towards improved economic resilience for women in agrifood systems', 'The evidence synthesis collates evidence on how gender norms facilitate or hamper women’s abilities to achieve women''s economic resilience in agrifood systems, specifically in the context of climate change.  In the synthesis, fifteen overarching gender norms which play out at individual, household and / or institutional levels that affect women’s economic resilience, are identified, and described. The synthesis covers the production domain of the agrifood system as well as the consumption domain.  Better understanding on how gender norms affect women’s ability to build economic resilience opens up opportunities for targeted gender-transformative development interventions. The evidence synthesis proposes an Economic Resilience Pathways Conceptual Framework that could be used to structure further research into women’s economic resilience in the face of climate change. As such this innovation might act as a guide for policymakers, practitioners, and stakeholders that are seeking to strengthen women''s economic resilience in agrifood systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8733?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8854, 'Identification of best integrated disease management (IDM) protocols for wheat blast management in Bangladesh and Zambia', 'The experiments in Bangladesh demonstrated the effectiveness of the fungicide Nativo and exhibited the importance of seed and foliar fungicidal treatment, timely sowing, and use of healthy seed for sowing. The experiments in Zambia failed due to the very dry conditions that hindered the wheat blast disease development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8854?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5039, 'The EzyAgric app, designed by CIAT, provides smallholder farmers with an integrated service platform to support farm management and good agricultural practices, and access to agri-inputs and credit in Uganda.', 'The EzyAgric is an App accessible on the Google Play Store, bundling various digital services targeting farmers and agri-input merchants. The services include Farm Manager for undertaking a Cost Benefit Analysis, and proper record management is accessed; Agri-shop, where a platform to digitally purchase genuinely affordable price agri-inputs from certified bulk agro-input manufacturers while providing an end-to-end feedback loop and a traceability trail; EzyCredits where farmers receive credits calculated based on the records generated from Farm Manager and Agrishop transactions as collateral, redeemable for purchase exclusively via the Agri-shop; Agri-Extension provides on-demand information and training tailored to specific agronomic needs. The Produce Market links farmers to aggregation centers established around off-takers and payments made directly to the farmer’s phone. A toll-free call and SMS platform is provided where non-smartphone farmers can access the services. The working model integrates women and youth with smartphones as community agents/service providers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5039?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(7650, 'Management of fall armyworm on summer maize in Lebanon', 'The fall armyworm (FAW) is the most important insect pest of maize crop worldwide, and it has recently become a harmful and invasive pest of summer planted maize in Lebanon. To save maize crop, a field trial was carried out during the summer of 2023 in the farmers’ field at Qob Elisa village to study the effect of pheromone traps and spraying with environmentally safe and low-risk insecticides. The results obtained showed that the peak of FAW population in the maize field was during the period of August 22 to September 15. The management of FAW before reaching this date is very important to avoid the high FAW infestation in the field. FAW damage on maize production in the field that used pheromone traps and recommended insecticides was almost unobservable compared to neighboring farmers, who followed their own practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7650?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(14618, 'Fall armyworm (Spodoptera frugiperda) management using pheromone-based mass trapping data on summer maize at the PHI platform in Qob Elisa, Lebanon', 'The fall armyworm (FAW), *Spodoptera frugiperda*, threatens irrigated summer maize production in Lebanon and the West Asia-North Africa region, jeopardizing food security and farmer incomes. A 2024 field study in Qob Elias, Bekaa Valley, evaluated an Integrated Pest Management (IPM) approach using pheromone traps and insecticides to manage FAW in imported maize cultivars. Monitoring with pheromone traps guided timely insecticide applications, targeting peak pest activity between September 10 and 29. Early planting and pre-peak interventions were crucial. The IPM strategy significantly reduced FAW infestation to negligible levels compared to traditional methods used by neighboring farmers. Knowledge-sharing sessions, supported by ICARDA and LARI scientists, encouraged local farmers to adopt these sustainable practices. This study emphasizes the effectiveness of pheromone traps in reducing pest populations and insecticide use, offering a practical solution for controlling FAW while promoting sustainable maize production in Lebanon.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14618?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4221, 'Farmer Interface Application (FIA) validated and operational for Fall Armyworm and cowpea pod borers and pod sucking bugs', 'The Farmer Interface App FIA (FIA–niébé) developed to scout for Fall Armyworm (FAW) and cowpea pod bug (Clavigralla tomentosicollis). The FIA–niébé was demonstrated to farmers, extension agents, and researchers during several training sessions in Benin and Mali. Scouting for FAW eggs and neonates is difficult and time consuming. To optimize scouting intervals, it was hypothesizing that the optimum timeframe for the FAW female arriving to lay eggs in sown maize fields could be predicted. This was done by back-calculating from interval censored data of egg and neonates collected in emerging maize seedlings. Early time of ovipositing FAW after sowing was recorded in field experiments. Using temperature based phenological models it was estimated that about 210 accumulated degree days is needed for early detection of neonate larvae in the field. This work is meant to provide new insights on timely pest detection and to guide precise timing of control measures.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4221?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16098, 'The Feed Assessment Tool (FEAST 3.0): a systematic method to assess local feed resource availability and use', 'The FEAST 3.0 app represents an upgrade from the existing desktop application to a mobile-friendly Android app and web platform. The tool supports the development of intervention strategies to enhance feed efficiency and improve animal productivity. It comprises of a concise quantitative questionnaire, completed by experts with guidance from facilitator, generates a short, structured report. This report includes quantitative data on feed availability, quality, and seasonality, providing valuable insights to inform effective intervention strategies. Currently undergoing review and testing, the app will soon be finalized and made accessible via Google Playstore, enabling experts to efficiently assess feed resources and design effective strategies to optimize livestock production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16098?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(2486, 'Protocol for the assessment of seed degeneration in late stage breeding materials of cassava in Tanzania', 'The first round of the degeneration protocol has been completed, involving an assessment of 11 genotypes from AYT and 3 controls, at Chambezi Research Station, Tanzania. Two of the 11 genotypes had low incidences of cassava brown streak disease as well as the highest yields. All genotypes were replanted for a second round of evaluation to be completed in 2024. The aim is to validate a protocol to assess the likelihood of degeneration amongst advanced breeding materials for cassava.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2486?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(7395, 'FOG Monitoring for detection and monitoring of fog Low-cost equipment for monitoring fog and analyzing the intensity and frequency of fog on the coastal hills', 'The FOG Monitoring tool is a low-cost equipment for the detection and monitoring of the occurrence of fog in ecosystem areas of Lomas and dry forests, to estimate the amount of moisture collected in the period of greatest occurrence of fog. The data collected is displayed in real time through a web platform. It is an important tool designed for farmers, agropastoralists, fishers but also used by Researchers and extension agents.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7395?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(4587, 'Food Security Simulator - Kenya: A microsimulation tool to assess the potential impacts of price or household income shocks', 'The Food Security Simulator is an innovative and easy to use, MS-Excel-based tool for assessing the potential short-term impacts of food price or household income shocks on food security and people’s diets. The Simulator is an ideal tool for first-cut forward-looking evaluations of direct, household-level outcomes of economic crises and policy responses in a timely manner. Key indicator results are summarized in concise overview tables and visualized in graphs. The underlying data include estimates from representative household survey data and rigorous, sophisticated food demand models to capture consumer behavior.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4587?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(17904, 'Food Systems Profile as a knowledge tool to enhance the capacity for health and agriculture staff in the Mekong River Delta in the implementation process of food systems transformation', 'The Food Systems Profile for the Mekong River Delta is a strategic knowledge tool offering a comprehensive analysis of the region''s food systems, including key drivers, components, and outcomes. It establishes a baseline for assessing the current state and future progress, addressing challenges and opportunities for transformation. Designed to enhance capacity, the profile can be integrated into university curricula and professional training for health and agriculture staff in the Mekong River Delta. By equipping stakeholders with evidence-based insights and practical frameworks, it supports Vietnam''s National Action Plan for Food Systems Transformation, fostering a transparent, responsible, and sustainable food system by 2030.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17904?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(14153, 'Forage observatory: open access platform with relevant indicators for tropical forages that will provide data driven analysis to all sectors globally', 'The forage observatory is an innovation led by the Alliance Bioversity-CIAT to establish an information system that is expected to present relevant information for tropical forages from around the world using secondary and primary data sources', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14153?phase=4', 0, NULL),
(8707, 'Foresight Community of Practice: Transformative Tools for Food, Land, Water Systems', 'The Foresight Community of Practice (CoP) aims to bring together foresight researchers and practitioners interested in transforming food, land, and water systems, with a focus on low and lower-middle-income countries. This is our community.

The practices we are interested in discussing include models and tools, data and metrics, and practical foresight analysis for enhanced priority setting, policy, and investment decision-making.

Note that these practices range from back-end model development and data analytics to generate actionable metrics, to the use of this knowledge for decision-making support.

The Foresight CoP has a discussion forum for its members to exchange information and knowledge and to engage in meaningful discussions on a wide variety of foresight-related topics.

The Foresight CoP discussion forum was utilized this year to gather feedback on the tools and websites we launched, as well as to engage with trainees participating in courses under the initiative.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8707?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8708, 'On-line training platform to enhance foresight analysis capacity amongst policy advisors in CGIAR target countries', 'The Foresight Initiative on-line training platform allows capacity development beyond in-person training. The capacity development is focussed initially on introduction to quantitative foresight modeling.

The focus of the learning platform is not on content but on the ability to host these courses and show them to the intended audiences', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8708?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(16810, 'Forum theatre play “She Inherits, She Sells” uses ''Theatre of the Oppressed'' format to help communities address challenges faced by rural women''s cooperatives in Morocco, stemming from societal gender norms', 'The forum theatre play titled "She Inherits, She Sells" was developed to engage entire communities in exploring, analysing, and solving the challenges faced by members of rural women''s cooperatives in Morocco, rooted in societal gender norms. Using the ''Theatre of the Oppressed'' format, the play begins with actors portraying  a scenario of oppression where the oppressed character(s) fail to overcome the challenges. The play then restarts, inviting audience members to participate as active ''spect-actors.'' These participants can step into the roles of the oppressed character(s), attempting new strategies to overcome the oppression, and other characters met along the way whose responses may either support or resist these interventions. The format of the play brings to light the challenges individuals face due to societal norms and provides a platform for collective analysis, exploration, and practice of possible pathways towards liberation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16810?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3712, 'End-to-End Approach for Increasing fruit and vegetable consumption to improve diet quality, nutrition,and health outcomes; whilst improving livelihoods, empowering women and youth and mitigating negative environmental impacts.', 'The FRESH initiative is developing an end-to-end approach to increase fruit and vegetable intake and in turn improve diet quality, nutrition and health outcomes while also improving livelihoods, empowering women and youth and mitigating negative environmental impacts. 

Worldwide intake of fruit and vegetables is too low contributing to poor quality diets and in turn, high prevalence of malnutrition and diet-related non-communicable diseases.​ Key barriers to increasing intake exist include the availability, accessibility, affordability and desirability of fruit and vegetables. The integrated end-to-end approach will address each key barriers by implementing co-located approaches that simultaneously address constraints to increasing the desirability, availability, affordability and accessibility of sustainable, safe and healthy produce and ultimately intake of fruits and vegetables, evaluating these approaches to help build evidence on what works and inform policy and program design.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3712?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8109, 'Developing and fine-tuning Fusarium wilt of banana IDM approaches in India', 'The Fusarium wilt IDM work in India focusses on: Development and evaluation of consortia of bioagents possessing multiple functions for the management of Foc TR4. Elucidating the mechanism of action (Transcriptomics, microbiome, LC-MS-MS analyses, SEM analysis etc) of these biocontrol consortia. Studying the effectiveness of flooding, crop rotation (with paddy rice), growing resistant banana cultivars/mutants (with or without biopriming), application of organic amendments (e.g., cakes, FYM, Vermicompost) on reducing incidence and symptom expression of Foc TR4. The bioagents Trichoderma sp. and Trichoderma asperellum were highly effective in controlling Foc and enhanced plant growth. Applications of groundnut and gingelly cakes and water stagnation led to significant reductions in Foc symptom expression. Research has so far focused on screenhouse and lab work. Field evaluations of integrated approaches for the effective management of Foc TR4 in Grand Nain will be established in early 2024 at a disease hot spot area in Northern India.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8109?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(3869, 'G+ tools: gender-responsive systematic customer and product profiling for breeding programs', 'The G+ approach for gender-responsive breeding, developed by CGIAR scientists since 2018, offers an integrated, systematic, and evidence-based protocol for breeding new crop varieties. The approach builds on work by the CGIAR Excellence in Breeding (EiB) Platform on the concept of a “product profile”, which describes the traits that different actors want in a new variety, giving plant breeders a target. The G+ approach complements this with two additional tools. The G+ plus customer profile characterizes client groups targeted for new varieties, considering gender differences in knowledge, assets, and decision-making which influence adoption. This makes it easier for breeders to develop the right product for the right customers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3869?phase=1', 8, NULL),
(6758, 'Gender and Youth Assessment: Enumerators'' Manual', 'The Gender and youth assessment enumerators'' manual describes the various steps and procedures to be followed during the conduct of household and field surveys of The Excellence in Agronomy Use Cases.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6758?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(18440, 'Production of Genetically Improved Abbassa Nile Tilapia (GIANT) strain Generation 18', 'The Genetically Improved Abbassa Nile Tilapia (GIANT) strain - Generation 18 is an advanced tilapia strain developed by WorldFish at the Abbassa Research Center in Egypt. The GIANT 18 exhibits superior growth performance, feed efficiency, and resilience, making it highly suitable for aquaculture and requested by farmers in the area.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18440?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(13797, 'Research Enabling and Oversight Unit to guide genome editing research', 'The Genome Editing Initiative is establishing a Research Enabling and Oversight (REO) Unit to provide strategic leadership on the current and future use of GEd, spanning the range of issues that impact product life cycle decision-making.  The REO Unit will monitor global needs, maintain consultation with diverse stakeholders including regulators and seed systems, and undertake impact projections to inform rigorous Eschborn-aligned prioritization. WP1 will work alongside Market Intelligence and Accelerated Breeding to evaluate and continually assess current and future GEd opportunities that complement GI’s portfolio of conventional breeding. Continuous prioritization and phase-gate informed decision-making will ensure efforts target step-changes in genetic gains for demand-driven traits and promote the incorporation of traits that meet the needs of women and youth, significantly contributing to the Genetic Innovation outcomes ‘Farmers have access to and use climate-resilient, nutritious, market-demanded crop varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13797?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(8605, 'Establishment of national or regional networks for rice disease surveillance and monitoring', 'The Global Biotic Stress Monitoring and Screening Network (Global BioNET) was established, aiming to enhance the surveillance and monitoring of rice diseases and pests, crucial for early detection and effective management. In 2023, substantial progress was made in setting up national and regional surveillance networks in Bangladesh, India, and Vietnam. BioNET-Bangladesh was launched after identifying major rice diseases and experts through a survey. In India, global BioNET was launched in agreement with the "All India Coordinated Research Project on Rice" (AICRPR), a well-established system for monitoring rice diseases and pests. In Vietnam, the BioNET-Vietnam network was marked by a scientific workshop that focused on enhancing cooperation among research institutions, improving the evaluation of rice varieties, and enhancing research on resistance genes. In 2024, BioNET was introduced to African rice pathologists. These activities represent major steps in addressing rice diseases and pests at both national and global levels.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8605?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6888, 'Global Market Intelligence Platform (GloMIP) for sharing market intelligence that informs targeting, design, and delivery of products and technologies, guiding R&D investment decisions for CGIAR, its partners, and investors.', 'The Global Market Intelligence Platform (GloMIP) serves as a global public good, enabling the crowd-sourcing, sharing, and analysis of market intelligence data. It provides insights on market segments (CGIAR target regions), target product profiles (breeding blueprints), impact opportunities (development indicators), and investor analytics (projected cost-benefit analysis). The platform aims to make this valuable information freely accessible to all key stakeholders— including donors and investors, program and breeding leads, Genetic Innovation management, scientists and researchers—across both CGIAR and non-CGIAR crops and regions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6888?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(15239, 'Global Rice Breeding Program (GRBP)', 'The Global Rice Breeding Program (GRBP) is the first crop-focused breeding research program of the CGIAR. It is established with the vision to create a global CGIAR-NARES rice breeding organization across IRRI, AfricaRice, and CIAT that leverages CGIAR’s global science and capacity to develop and deliver improved rice germplasm and market demanded rice varieties (inbreds and hybrids) for the target countries/regions. It institutionalizes germplasm and products exchanges among the three Centers, develops and adopts a common breeding strategy and framework, aligns market segments / breeding pipelines across the Centers, and will establish a CGIAR-NARES governance system with joint strategic planning in consultation with NARES partners.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15239?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(12585, 'Methodology for building participatory national food system monitoring dashboards tested with stakeholders in Bangladesh, Ethiopia, and Honduras', 'The goal of food system dashboards is to offer users the opportunity to monitor or assess national food systems. One challenge is how to strike an acceptable balance between the complexity, specificity, and multi-sectoral nature of food systems while also ensuring that the information remains accessible and usable for decisionmakers. Rarely are these dashboards co-constructed with local stakeholders. This case study from Sustainable Healthy Diets describes how a replicable yet rigorous methodology for co-creating food system dashboards was applied in Bangladesh, Ethiopia, and Honduras. The three pilots demonstrate that it''s feasible to integrate comprehensive and multi-sectoral inputs from national stakeholders into dashboards that are functional, evidence-based, and reflective of the country’s specific features. The methodology has the potential to progressively build capacities of decisionmakers to make more informed and evidence-based decisions to curb negative outcomes of food systems, like the triple burden of malnutrition.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12585?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(17933, 'Guidelines on developing nutrition-sensitive agri-food projects in the Mekong Delta, Vietnam', 'The guideline offers comprehensive instructions for designing nutrition-sensitive agri-food (NSAF) system projects in the Mekong Delta. It aligns with the Poverty Reduction National Target Program (SPR-NTP) and supports the National Action Plan on Food Systems Transformation (FST-NAP) in the region. The document outlines the fundamentals of NSAF projects, steps for project development, and the formulation of monitoring and evaluation (M&E) plans. Co-developed by MARD and published in 2024, the guideline was distributed to agriculture and health personnel in provinces participating in the Poverty Reduction Program. The approval of the guideline was authorized by Decision No. 2048/QĐ-BNN-KTHT by MARD on 28 June 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17933?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1124, 'Healthy Eating Index for Vietnam developed for researchers and governments to assess and monitor the extent to which populations are following dietary recommendations', 'The Healthy Eating Index (HEI) measures dietary quality in accordance with Vietnam''s nationally-recognized food-based dietary guidelines (FBDGs). A robust HEI has the potential to reveal and monitor how well the general adult population (20 years and older) is adhering to specific, culturally appropriate and actionable recommendations for a healthy diet. The Vietnamese HEI was developed by the National Institute of Nutrition with researchers from Wageningen University and Research (WUR) involved in Sustainable Healthy Diets. Results from a CGIAR-supported doctoral dissertation applying the HEI to a nationally representative household survey dataset suggested it was effective at measuring adherence to the Vietnamese FBDGs. This innovation can a valuable tool for future research to examine associations between dietary quality and health-related outcomes and relationships between dietary quality and the environmental impacts of diets and for program implementers in government and NGOs to monitor interventions focusing on improving dietary quality.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1124?phase=1', 5, NULL),
(6188, 'Empowering Informal food Vendors: Enhancing Business Skills, Practices, and Food Safety through Training and Mentoring to empower informal food vendors through the Vendor Business School', 'The human population is increasing globally and food systems need to be transformed to ensure supply of healthy
and nutritious diets, and to address the challenge of food insecurity. It is estimated that by 2050, more than two-thirds of the world will be living in urban areas. Informal markets, especially those in low- and middle-income
countries are critical for the food supply in cities. CGIAR Resilient Cities initiative is working towards upgrading these
markets, to not only provide safe food, but also ensure that they are better organized, thus increasing their efficiency
and potential to meet the food demand. Entrepreneurs, especially women, need to be supported to thrive in their
businesses, and contribute to the sustainability of these markets. To this end, a mentorship program, specifically
targeting women vendors in the dairy sector in Kenya, but with potential to be adapted to meet the needs of other
business types, has been developed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6188?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(9211, 'Functional yam breeding pipeline for development and delivery of genetic gain in CG/NARES breeding network', 'The IITA''s yam breeding currently operates with two functional breeding pipelines, white yam, and water yam, that serve three product profiles defined for the market segments (https://cimmyt-eibp-prd.azurewebsites.net/bpdc/my-page). The white yam breeding pipeline serves two market segments: early maturing bushy/semi-dwarf white yam with less tendency to climb and intermediate or late maturing white yam. Each pipeline, based on market demand and consumer preferences, is at a different stage of modernization. In line with germplasm development, and during 2023, the yam breeding has simultaneous work with its NARES and advanced research partners. The NARES including UAC in Benin Republic, SARI/CRI in Ghana, CNRA, Bouake/Côte d’Ivoire, NRCRI/Umudike in Nigeria, Ethiopia, Uganda, and DR Congo. The breeding program has developed and shared progenies originating from high-performing crosses in the NARSE network.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9211?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(18954, 'Genomic tools for selecting and deploying best-bet forages for farmers use', 'The ILRI Genebank houses a vast forage genetic pool that can be leveraged to address feed shortages and enhance farmers'' resilience to climate change. However, traditional methods of forage characterization and screening have been expensive and time-consuming. To overcome this challenge, we have developed and applied genomic tools to accelerate the selection of optimal forage accessions and expedite the deployment of genebank materials for use by farmers. By utilizing DNA markers, we have identified core collections of forage species, including cowpea, lablab, Sesbania sesban, Rhodes grass, oats, Urochloa spp., buffel grass, and Napier grass. Consequently, candidate best-bet varieties were identified and prepared for national variety verification trials. The Ethiopian Institute of Agricultural Research (EIAR) is evaluating the performance of the promising subsets to submit applications for the registration of new forage varieties in Ethiopia. This milestone provides livestock keepers with new forage options, improving feed supply, livestock productivity, and livelihoods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18954?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6832, 'Excellence in Agronomy Initiative (EiA): Dashboard for EiA and external users to assess impact of Minimum Viable Products (MVPs) developed by Use Cases', 'The Impact Dashboard allows a performance assessment of MVPs developed by EiA Use Cases relative to agreed agronomic gain Key Performance Indicators, including productivity (yield), profitability, nutrient use efficiency (NUE), water use efficiency (WUE) and others. The dashboard is a simple R-Shiny tool that is constantly evolving, and presents results of analysis to assess whether MVPs being field-validated or piloted  result in improved performance based on agreed indicators of success for for each Use Case, by crop - as Use Cases progress along EiA''s 7-step process of MVP development, from inception to scaling.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6832?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1243, 'Impacts Dashboard for Genetic Innovation v2.0', 'The Impacts Dashboard for Genetic Innovation was internally launched within CGIAR Genetic Innovation to assist researchers, managers and investors in visualizing Impact Opportunities and prioritizing Seed Product Market Segments (SPMS). The current version 2.0 features 93 indicators of Impact Opportunities across the five Impact Areas (Nutrition, Health and Food Security; Poverty Reduction, Livelihoods and Jobs; Gender Equality, Youth and Inclusion; Climate Change Adaptation and Mitigation; and Environmental Health and Biodiversity) and a cross-cutting category. It features two filters that enable researchers identifying global Impact Opportunities for prioritization at three levels: (1) globally, (2) based on all CGIAR SPMS (Market Segment Footprint), and (3) based on all active CGIAR SPMS that are currently served by Breeding Pipelines. Prioritization can be conducted at seven aggregate levels: (1) CGIAR Regions, (2) CGIAR Sub-regions, (3) Centers, (4) Crops, (5) Breeding Pipelines, (6) SPMS, and (7) Countries. Impact Opportunities are downscaled from national to SPMS levels using Impact Area-specific scaling methods (e.g., based on targeted area, targeted calories produced, targeted production value, share of agricultural GDP, crop-specific greenhouse gas emissions, and crop-specific global genebank accessions). Users can visualize priorities at the three levels for all seven aggregates in three ways: (1) based on a single indicator through a treemap and (2) a global map, or (3) based on multiple indicators through two area charts, one for breadth indicators and another one for incidence indicators. The Impacts Dashboard for Genetic Innovation v2.0 is a prototype that will be integrated into the Global Market Intelligence Platform in 2023, which is one of the core innovations of the CGIAR Initiative on Market Intelligence.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1243?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(9946, 'Innovative Urochloa humidicola planting systems using stolons: A successful case in the Colombian Orinoquia', 'The implementation of Urochloa humidicola cv. Tully (Humidicola Grass) CIAT 679 has become a strategic option among ranchers of the region, as it offers greater productivity when adapting to the soils acids of low fertility. In this booklet, technologies are shown that have potential to improve conditions productive and environmental of the area, and that can be replicated on other farms and environments with even greater benefits, If the agronomic conditions are favorable.
For the current year (2024) We are taking a step forward in developing a new manuel that provides a detailed roadmap for transforming livestock farming in the Orinoquía, promoting practices that not only increase productivity and income for ranchers, but also ensure the long-term sustainability of natural resources. The experience of Hacienda San José serves as a replicable model and demonstrates the viability and tangible benefits of these practices in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9946?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3451, 'Improving food safety through informed demand: System to catalyze consumer demand for safer food through broad-based testing and provision of information on the relative food safety risk of alternative products.', 'The informed demand-led approach for the improvement of food safety comprises three key activities. First, reliable data on the level of food safety hazards or relevant production processes is collected across a set of foods within a category (for example, the level of aflatoxin in different types of grain or whole meal vs. refined flour; the hygiene practices of different vendors of fresh foods; the level of lead in different types of cookware). Second, this information is provided to consumers, who are expected to utilize the information to make safer choices. Third, additional rounds of information are collected at regular intervals (and again provided to consumers) to allow producers or markets with significant problems to benefit from corrective action. This approach aims to harness consumer demand for safer food to be translated into action, thus steering markets to provide safer food.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3451?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7397, 'INFRARED-HOT AIR DRYER -an  eco-friendly hybrid dryer to dehydrate fruits and vegetables for companies and researchers', 'The Infrared-hot air drier is an eco-friendly hybrid drier used to dehydrate fruits and vegetables. Itis suited for companies and researchers. The prototype combines two methods of infrared and hot air drying, and also incorporates the Arduino open source platform to continuously monitor the temperature, humidity, time and weight of the product. The implementation of the hybrid dryer allows the dehydration of the food for its microbiological preservation and in turn delays many undesirable reactions. Dehydration reduces packaging, handling, storage and transportation costs, since the weight of the food is reduced, and in some cases the volume. The hybrid dryer can generally dehydrate fruits and vegetables.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7397?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(1010, 'Dashboard of ex post impact assessment studies relevant for informing foresight modelers conducting parameterization of foresight modeling tools.', 'The Initiative has been developing a pioneering innovation: a cutting-edge Ex Post Impact Assessment Dashboard. This tool showcases its innovation by providing a dynamic platform for foresight modelers, researchers, and policymakers. It enables the seamless integration of ex-post impact assessment studies into foresight modeling, setting a new standard in informed decision-making. This innovation is undergoing a rigorous process of ideation, proof of concept, pilot testing, and controlled and uncontrolled testing before roll out.. This transformative dashboard leverages rich metadata tagging to ensure FAIRness, enhancing its utility and accessibility. Its potential extends beyond modeling to serve as a valuable resource for analyzing the adoption and impact of past innovations, solidifying its position as a game-changing innovation in the field', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1010?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17885, 'Intensified farming through rice-rice production systems in the coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) builds on the work of the Sustainable Intensification Innovation Lab (SIIL) Project in Bangladesh through the International Rice Research Institute. Their joint efforts aim to implement a transformative project in the polder zone, targeting the untapped agricultural production potential of the region. Currently, the rice-rice production system was introduced in the southcentral (SC) and southwest (SW) coastal zones to increase land productivity. Since river water remain non-saline in most rivers of SC region, the farmer pumped water from the polder canals that entered inside the polders during high tides. While the river water becomes saline in the dry season in SW region and the farmers utilized groundwater (where salinity permits) for rice irrigation.  The farmers cultivated climate-resilient, nutritious and salt-tolerant HYV rice in both wet and dry seasons and harvested 10-12 t/ha/yr that significantly contributed to their food security.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17885?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6566, 'Diversified and intensified farming through rice-maize production systems in the coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) builds on the work of the Sustainable Intensification Innovation Lab (SIIL) Project in Bangladesh through the International Rice Research Institute. Their joint efforts aim to implement a transformative project in the polder zone, targeting the untapped agricultural production potential of the region. Since waterlogging is the main constraint for wide-scale adoption of improved production systems, the project implemented innovative catchment-level water and crop management for sustainable farming in the polders of coastal Bangladesh. Currently, the rice-maize production system is being introduced. The farmers cultivated HYV rice in the wet season that almost doubled the productivity. While maize cultivated under semi-mechanized practices, provides improved livelihood for farmers where most fields remain fallow in the dry season. The system productivity of rice-maize (9.3-11.4 t/ha/yr) increased by 2-3 folds over farmers’ practice and has potential for food security and feed sources for the livestock and fish.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6566?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6894, 'Diversified and intensified farming through rice-sunflower production systems in the coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) builds on the work of the Sustainable Intensification Innovation Lab (SIIL) Project in Bangladesh through the International Rice Research Institute. Their joint efforts aim to implement a transformative project in the polder zone, targeting the untapped agricultural production potential of the region. Since waterlogging is the main constraint for wide-scale adoption of improved production systems, the project implemented innovative catchment-level water and crop management for sustainable farming in the polders of coastal Bangladesh. Currently, the rice-sunflower production system is being introduced. The farmers cultivated HYV rice in the wet season that almost doubled the productivity. While sunflower was mostly cultivated under semi-mechanized practices that provides livelihood and income generating opportunities to farmers where most fields remain fallow in the dry season. The system productivity of rice-sunflower (10.3-14.1 t/ha/yr) increased by 2-3 folds over farmers’ practice and has potential to reduce edible oil import.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6894?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7797, 'Formulation of models and maps for climate change impacts on hydrology, crop production and salinity damage in the Asian Mega Deltas (AMDs)', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) partnered with Wageningen Environmental Research (WENR) to develop and simulate models to generate maps to depict climate change scenarios and impacts on hydrology, crop production, and salinity damage in the Asian Mega Deltas (AMDs). The models and maps can be used by researchers and policymakers to come up with climate resilient and adaptation plans.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7797?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(6899, 'Diversified and intensified farming through rice-fodder production systems in the coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) pursues establishment of rice-fodder production systems in the coastal zones of Bangladesh to enhance feed availability that is crucial not only for the well-being of livestock but also for improving the nutrition of the coastal communities in Bangladesh through milk and meat production. The performance of six fodder crops was evaluated under rainfed environment during 2022-23 and 2023-24 dry seasons to document productivity in the coastal zone and palatability by the livestock. The crops were: Barley, Proso Millet, Foxtail Millet, Maize, Sorghum, and Triticale. Among them, maize produced the highest green biomass, followed by sorghum and foxtail millet, while barley and triticale produced the lowest biomass. The consumption rate of cross-breed cows was significantly higher than that of local cows. The palatability findings suggest that sorghum, maize, foxtail and proso millets have the most potential as fodder crops in the coastal polder zone.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6899?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(9765, 'Introduction of land shaping to farmers to harvest rainwater that can be used by rice farmers during the dry seasons in India', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) together with the Indian Council for Agricultural Research (ICAR) and local partners have developed land-shaping technologies by transforming the lowland rice landscape to overcome certain climatic hazards and improve land productivity. It also aims to provide rice farmers opportunities to improve their livelihoods during the dry season where water is scarce thereby enhancing livelihood security. Inland shaping, the surface of farmland is modified primarily for harvesting rainwater to create a source for irrigation especially during the dry season, reducing salinity and drainage congestion, and practicing integrated farming around the year. 

Currently, the land shaping technology is now being introduced in a small scale utilizing rice fields of 2 farmers for tirals. The trials are conducted to establish proof of concept and for further refining the design of the technology to ensure effectiveness and suitability to local and farmers'' conditions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9765?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7004, 'Framework for gender-sensitive and participatory validation and evaluation of the climate-smart maps and adaptation plans (CS-MAPS) at community level', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega Deltas develops climate-risk maps and adaptation plans for the targets regions following a participatory mapping approach (CS-MAP). In CS-MAP, local knowledge is very important in identifying affected areas, the level of climate risks and adaptation measures taking into account local contexts. As the outputs of CS-MAP, climate risks and associated adaptation plans have been identified for every agriculture land area by local stakeholders from different administrative levels.

This framework considers farmer-level verification of proposed climate-adaptation measures that will provide important guidance for AMD and local planners to create its significant impacts at deltas level. The framework aims to understand farmer/community perspective on climate risks and workable options to cope with their risk-prone conditions, identify the grassroots constraints and willingness of male and female farmers in adoption of adaptation measures and empower farmer/community by involving both male and female farmers in climate-planning and decision making processes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7004?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6908, 'Creation of a learning alliance as a network approach to support learning and alignment across stakeholder groups in scaling innovations in Can Tho, Vietnam', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega-Delta (AMD) aims to improve the deltaic production systems by working with farmers and local governments through identifying, synthesizing, evaluating, and scaling interventions that help adapt or mitigate climate change. The learning alliance is an approach to bring together a multi-stakeholder network to support learning and alignment of practices related to an innovation. It supports the identification of relevant stakeholders, timely learning activities to observe both on-farm and off-farm innovations, then reflection and experimentation on new practices that support the use of the new skill, technique or tool.

In 2022, a learning alliance workshop was done and designed to initially establish learning alliances for a network of diverse stakeholders that has an interest to address climate-related issues in Cambodia. At present, the learning alliance has already conducted several learning and knowledge exchange activities to support strengthening the value chains around different production systems in Can Tho.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6908?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6907, 'Creation of a learning Alliance as a network approach to support learning and alignment across stakeholder groups in scaling innovations in Prey Veng, Cambodia', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega-Delta (AMD) aims to improve the deltaic production systems by working with farmers and local governments through identifying, synthesizing, evaluating, and scaling interventions that help adapt or mitigate climate change. The learning alliance is an approach to bring together a multi-stakeholder network to support learning and alignment of practices related to an innovation. It supports the identification of relevant stakeholders, timely learning activities to observe both on-farm and off-farm innovations, then reflection and experimentation on new practices that support the use of the new skill, technique or tool.

In 2022, a learning alliance workshop was done and designed to initially establish learning alliances for a network of diverse stakeholders that has an interest to address climate-related issues in Cambodia. At present, the learning alliance has already conducted several learning and knowledge exchange activities to support strengthening the value chains around different production systems in Prey Veng.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6907?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6906, 'Creation of a learning alliance as a network approach to support learning and alignment across stakeholder groups in scaling innovations  in Takeo, Cambodia', 'The Initiative on Asian Mega-Delta (AMD) aims to improve the deltaic production systems by working with farmers and local governments through identifying, synthesizing, evaluating, and scaling interventions that help adapt or mitigate climate change. The learning alliance is an approach to bring together a multi-stakeholder network to support learning and alignment of practices related to an innovation. It supports the identification of relevant stakeholders, timely learning activities to observe both on-farm and off-farm innovations, then reflection and experimentation on new practices that support the use of the new skill, technique or tool.

In 2022, a learning alliance workshop was done and designed to initially establish learning alliances for a network of diverse stakeholders that has an interest to address climate-related issues in Cambodia. At present, the learning alliance has already conducted several learning and knowledge exchange activities to support strengthening the value chains around different production systems in Takeo.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6906?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(10363, 'Digital crop maps and forecasting maps of salinity vulnerable areas in Mekong River Delta', 'The innovation aims to develop a digital crop map and forecasting map of vulnerable areas affected by salinity risks (Tien Giang province as pilots). The result will be used to develop a saline intrusion map for crop production and a handbook on adaptation solutions for salinity risk areas. It synthesizes data distribution locations of fruit trees and rice at the district level and update data on saline intrusion and irrigation systems. Futher, it enables data aggregation and digitalization about planting areas, types of crops, data on saline intrusion by location and time and synthesizes digitized data on the current status of rice and fruit production, irrigation systems, and risk areas of saline intrusion under different scenarios. By the time of reporting at the end of December 2024, a WebGIS website had been completed, demostrating the agricultural farming regions in the Mekong Delta prone to saline intrusion.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10363?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3042, 'Characterizing rural food environments to identify factors influencing access to and purchase of nutritious food for consumers living in poverty', 'The innovation aims to identify which factors in the rural food environment or food markets influence access to and purchase of nutritious food for consumers living in poverty. The innovation revolves around the idea of spatial mapping and market surveys of the food environment. It involves geospatial mapping of different types of markets and retail food shops, conducting a market census and survey of the market and retail food environment, and finding the food availability and price of food in local markets. Building on the evidence, we explore if an improvement in the food environment (e.g. diversity of food, placement of food etc.) leads to improved access to nutritious food by consumers living in poverty.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3042?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5368, 'Empowering Fish Farming Communities: Leveraging Social Media for Sustainable Farming and Healthy Diets', 'The innovation capitalizes on social media as a medium to disseminate information on sustainable and climate adaptive farming practices including building awareness on healthy diets. It is directed towards fish farming communities and is facilitated by WorldFish. 

It builds on a bilateral project (The Right Kind) which set up a digital platform using social media tools for the aquaculture sector in an attempt to find an alternative method of engaging with farmers and to bring fish farmers from across the country under one umbrella. Facebook was selected as the social media platform since it is a popular method of communication, is free to use and already in use by the farming community.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5368?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4937, 'Scaling up an information clearinghouse to accelerate agricultural technology adoption in Uganda', 'The innovation described here is a crowd-sourced information clearinghouse, similar to trustpilot.com or yelp.com, to help smallholders secure quality inputs and services such as improved varieties and agronomic recommendations. Farmers use the clearinghouse to rate agro-input dealers as well as the products and services they offer; these ratings are then used to rank the dealers, with the ranking made available to both farmers and dealers in an appealing and marketable manner. A recent randomized trial of a prototype clearinghouse conducted with 350 dealers and 3500 farmers in 11 districts in Uganda found significant positive impacts on input use, enabling farmers to grow more food on less land, and indirect impacts on climate resilience and the environment. Dealers also received more customers, sold more, and earned higher revenues, although there are gender biases observed in the ratings for women dealers that can remedied through a more gender-intentional design at scale-up.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4937?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2131, 'Towards a One CGIAR germplasm licensing strategy to foster wide and equitable use of CGIAR-center germplasm by public and private entities', 'The innovation develops common germplasm licensing approaches for use by CGIAR centers under One CGIAR. It is being developed by an inter-initiative work group – including Seed Equal, Genebanks and Accelerated Breeding - for review, discussion and endorsement by Centers and the System Management Board.  

At this stage, few centers have systematic germplasm licensing approaches in place, approaches differ between centers, and there is a lack of awareness among public and private sector entities how to access CGIAR Center germplasm for research, breeding or commercial use. The innovation seeks to implement more streamlined approaches and conditions to foster wide and equitable use of Center germplasm by public and private entities under One CGIAR. These approaches need to be aligned with International Treaty obligations, Article 15 commitments, and the CGIAR Principles on the Management of Intellectual Assets.

In 2024, a new draft version of the licensing strategy for One CGIAR was prepared.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2131?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1531, 'Socio-technical bundle for Sustainable Land Management/Climate Smart Agriculture for restoring landscape health for sustainable intensification, integrated resource management, production and natural resource conservation by farmers in Ethiopia', 'The innovation in this activity is developing of integrated framework which can be helpful to packaging sustainable land management /climate smart agriculture socio-technical recommendation which are applicable along landscape continuum.  Further  advisory tools for targeting and scaling of packaged technologies will be developed. This innovation will be developed for experts and extension agents working in the extension system', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1531?phase=1', 2, NULL),
(14883, 'A decision tree to drive best practices in trait discovery and deployment programs', 'The innovation introduces an online, interactive decision tree to drive best practices in Trait Discovery and Deployment (TD&D) programs. The overall purpose is to increase the efficiency and speed of introducing novel genetic variation to crop breeding populations and ultimately deliver it to farmers'' fields.  The decision tree encompasses trait genetic scoping, determining market-trait associations, genetic validation, introgression, phenotypic validation and deployment through elite donors. Target users are practitioners in the TD&D space. It is relevant to all CGIAR Impact Areas.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14883?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(5161, 'Basal NPK fertilizer timing, cowpea living mulch and leaf stripping for intensification of smallholder maize-livestock farming system in northern Ghana', 'The innovation is a bundle of three technologies for intensification of maize-small ruminants farming system in northern Ghana. The basal NPK fertilizer timing focuses on applying the fertilizer at planting stage of maize to reduce nutrient stress especially during the emergence-vegetative stage, enhance nutrient use efficiency and yield. Intercropping of cowpea at one week after planting of maize as a living mulch provides soil surface cover, increases soil moisture retention, adds nutrients through biological nitrogen fixation, suppresses weeds growth, and increases yield and food diversity. Harvesting of maize leaves below the cob leaf at silking stage (Leaf stripping) offers quality forage for livestock without compromising grain yield. The stripped maize leaf forage has a 40% higher crude protein content than the crude protein content of feed required for the body maintenance of livestock. Feeding strip maize leaf to small ruminants increases live body, slaughter, and carcass weights.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5161?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1023, 'Cropping calendar advisories and bundled services for smallholder maize farmers and extension agents in the Guinea Savannah zone _GAIP Ghana', 'The innovation is a digital advisory tool designed to provide cropping calendar advisories to farmers in Northern Ghana. This platform delivers a comprehensive suite of agronomic advisories, including optimal planting times, thinning schedules, fertilizer application guidelines, weeding, pest management strategies, and harvest and postharvest operations. Aimed at enhancing resource use efficiency and mitigating drought risks, the tool empowers farmers to become more resilient against climate variability. It is intended for use by farmers seeking to optimize their agricultural practices and improve crop yield and sustainability in the challenging climatic conditions of Northern Ghana', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1023?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9908, 'Improvded soybean agronomic advisory, market and weather services in Northern Ghana (GHANA-SOY/ESOKO Use Case)', 'The innovation is a package of agronomic solutions addressing knowledge gap on agronomic technologies, insufficient access to input (certified seeds, legume fertilizer, inoculants), low yields and limited access to adequate and timely market, price, and weather information, to sustain soyabean production in Northern Ghana. The agronomic solutions developed is being deployed to farmers through ESOKO digital platform (the demand partner''s platform) and other scaling channels such as video sessions, demonstration/field days and community sensitization helping farmers improve their productivity and increase profitability. The digital agronomic, weather, and market advisories have been currently deployed to over sixty thousand smallholder farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9908?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(128, 'Transformative leadership program for Water, Energy, Food, Ecosystem (WEFE) nexus champions', 'The innovation is a transformative leadership program for professionals working in the water, energy, food and environment (WEFE) sectors. Its aim is to create WEFE nexus champions to advance the uptake and implementation of people-centric WEFE approaches, while contributing to greater equality and social inclusion among professionals in these sectors. The program’s specific objectives are:
1.	Develop the technical knowledge and analytical, advocacy, and negotiation skills of WEFE sector professionals on WEFE nexus approaches and their operationalization.
2.	Develop the transformational leadership capacity of women and men across WEFE sectors to participate meaningfully in nexus discourses, policy processes, decision-making, planning, and implementation.
3.	Develop strong and collaborative networks for co-developing and implementing WEFE nexus solutions.
4.	Capitalize on women professionals’ assets and develop a cadre of professionals aware of and able to address structural barriers to gender equality and social inclusion within the WEFE sectors.
The course is based on adult learning approaches, such as transformation and experiential learning, and including a mentorship element. Participants are professionals (with an emphasis on women) from government, non-governmental organizations (NGOs), civil society organizations (CSOs), natural resources management networks, and the private sector. Developed in Kathmandu, Nepal, the program is designed to be rolled out to the provinces and local levels and other geographies. The innovation was conceptualized and co-developed by GREAT International, Governance Lab, International Water Management Institute (IWMI), and the Alliance of Bioversity and CIAT (ABC).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/128?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1183, 'Supporting diversification and climate resilience in coffee systems through improved agronomy and advisory services for smallholder farmers.', 'The innovation is an App-based digital tool, which agronomists from the demand partner organization and farmer leadres can use to identify and to guide climate-smart and best agricultural practices in coffee farms. A stepwise approach will be used to set increaing packages of the agricultural practices (package 1= basic, package 2 = package 1 + other agricultural practices).  Packages of the agricultural practices will be developped based on the identified farmer groups and coffee farms. The digital tool will be able to assist in identifying the typology along which the household belong, based on some basic socio-economic conditions of the household. The second part of the digital tool will assist in idenfiying the package of agricultural practices that the household can accomodate and the types of the needs for him/her to move to the second level of agricultural practice. Biological coffee is targeted, excluding mineral fertilizers and pesticides.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1183?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(16693, 'Integrated Package for Improving Small Ruminats'' Health in El Kef and Siliana, Tunisia', 'The innovation is developing and testing integrated practices for flock management, nutrition and health, that are adapted to the specific context of the Tunisian Agroecology Living Landscape in Kef and Siliana. The purpose is to enhance the overall health, welfare, productivity, and resilience of small ruminants’ flocks while adopting agroecological practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16693?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(2643, 'Agriculturally Based Light Engineering producing farm machinery, spare parts, and other tools sign cost-sharing joint venture agreements to open new and innovative markets stimulating smallholder access to mechanization and diversification.', 'The innovation is in public-private partnership demonstrated through the JVAs to stimulate farm machinery market development. This output from Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia – Mechanization Extension Activity (CSISA-MEA) is associated with output 2.5 under work package two of Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA). The Initiative and CSISA-MEA worked to increase overall business growth and knowledge gained on high value farm machine and spare parts necessary for diversification. In this reporting period, scaling work in greater Jashore and Faridpur of Bangladesh utilized JVAs to support lead firms to employ a range of demand-creation approaches to raise awareness of new agricultural technology and sell their machines.  The total value of sales in the reporting year was USD 2,365,017.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2643?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2416, 'Contribution of pollinators analysis method(s) to understand change over time and increase nutritional security.', 'The innovation is the development of a methodological approach to assess the level of pollinator ecosystem services and dependence of current cropping levels in selected Indian state on pollination services. The particular innovation is the methodological approach that is in development, and which aims to assess pollinator contributions to the growth of more nutritious crops in selected states. The innovation is aimed at supporting policy makers and agricultural development planners that support extension systems and encourage farmers to utilize diverse cropping patterns.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2416?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(2645, 'Business linkages and scaling activities facilitated between Agriculture Based Light Engineering (ABLE) firms and retailers in Bangladesh to stimulate mechanization and farm diversification', 'The innovation is the linkage between Agriculture Based Light Engineering (ABLE) firms and Dealers. The value of sales by ABLEs enterprises boost to dealers through the links facilitated by the activity. This output from Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia  – Mechanization Extension Activity (CSISA-MEA) is associated with Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA). The initiative and CSISA-MEA worked to organize linkage between Agriculture Based Light Engineering firms and Dealers, 48 ABLE enterprises (19 in Jashore. 2 in Faridpur, 6 in Kushtia, and 21 in Bogura) were linked with 68 dealers (39 in Jashore, 10 in Faridpur, 6 in Kushtia, 6 in Bogura and 7 in Cox’s Bazar). This resulted in sizable sales, in Bogura, 16 ABLEs enterprises sold spare parts and machines worth USD 508,554.40 through 50 dealers. The total value of sales by ABLEs enterprises to dealers through the links facilitated by the activity was USD 969,371.50.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2645?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9254, 'NARITA 17: A New Matooke Hybrid Banana Variety Developed Through Data-Driven Selection for Yield and Quality', 'The innovation is the release of NARITA 17, a new matooke hybrid banana variety developed by IITA and NARO for Uganda. Selected through a rigorous, data-driven process, NARITA 17 meets key product profile requirements, including high yield and favorable sensory attributes. Its advancement was based on performance data and stakeholder consensus during a program advancement meeting.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9254?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2957, 'Integrated agronomic management strategies to enhance performance of mixed farming systems in Malawi through increasing cropping intensity', 'The innovation is to determine the effect of water management through tied ridges on productivity of different maize-cowpea cropping systems under variable soil fertility conditions, determine nitrogen fixation and cycling for an improved cowpea variety intercropped with maize and determine soil moisture dynamics in rely intercropping systems with or without tied ridges. 
More so, it seeks to investigate the interactions between rainfall received, nutrient management and soil type.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2957?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(7242, 'Phenotyping breeding clones for quality traits using sensory panel, texture analyzer and a digital imaging system (DigiEye) in Mozambique', 'The innovation lies in advancing methods to assess sensory traits, particularly mealiness, hardness, brownness and fibrousness in sweetpotato, thereby enhancing the selection and adoption of varieties. Sensory panels, comprising 12 individuals, were employed to evaluate 114 genotypes. Concurrently, a texture analyzer provided objective data on hardness, facilitating high-throughput phenotyping. Additionally, DIGeye photo scans were utilized to develop models for mealiness and brownness. Notably, subjective assessments of hardness correlated strongly with those obtained via the texture analyzer, validating its utility for selection purposes. While this innovation offers promising avenues for sweetpotato breeding and selection, further evidence is required to refine mealiness models developed by DIGeye. This initiative targets breeders, researchers and stakeholders in sweetpotato value chain and aims to optimize variety development by prioritizing sensory traits. Future plans involve refining and validating these methods to streamline the breeding process and meet consumer preferences effectively', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7242?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(16402, 'Participatory development of a wetlands management plan in Attapeu province, Lao PDR.', 'The innovation mobilizes stakeholders to co-develop a management plan for the Nonglom wetlands, which include a permanent lake surrounded by agricultural and residential areas. The wetlands face increasing pressure from unsustainable fishing and cash crop farming yet lack proper management. Stakeholders including local fishers, farmers, local specialized groups like the Lao Farmer Association, Lao Women Union (LWU), Village Youth Group (VYG), Village Land Use Unit (VLUU), village heads of Hom and Thamaleuy, and government entities such as PAFO and DAFO were involved in the whole process.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16402?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(13857, 'Participatory development of forages for inclusive integration in crop-tree-livestock systems upland-extensive mixed farming systems in Lao PDR', 'The innovation of integrating forages into crop-tree-livestock systems was co-designed with active participation of farmers, local stakeholders and researchers to diagnose challenges (using gender-sensitive analysis tools), develop inclusive interventions and implement those interventions. Multi-location demonstrations were strategically positioned to raise awareness of the innovation and to gather information about the performance of newly introduced drought-resistant and shade-tolerant forage varieties and their preferencing by farmers that could be further applied in on-farm trials. On-farm trials were developed with innovative incentive mechanisms that reward the provision of ecosystem services and applying tricot methodology that empowers farmers to identify the most suitable technologies for the local conditions of their own farm.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13857?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(2325, 'Experimental tools for analyzing market intelligence demand and impacts on investments by breeding professionals', 'The innovation provides experimental tools to examine how access to market intelligence (MI) information impacts breeding investment decisions and potential heterogeneities in decision making by various characteristics (e.g., breeder and institutional characteristics). The tool also allows to examine breeders’ willingness to pay for market intelligence. The tool can be implemented by means of an online lab-in-the-field experiment. This method will allow to analyze the impacts of different types of market intelligence, investigate underlying behavioral mechanisms, and elicit demand for market intelligence in a more controlled setting, allowing us to estimate causal effects of market intelligence and variations in the way the intelligence is presented.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2325?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(1861, 'Cereal-legume strip cropping', 'The innovation shows that cereal-legume cropping system shows that crop spatial configurations differ on effects they pose on productivity and overall economics of cereal-legume intercrops', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1861?phase=1', 7, NULL),
(1226, 'Supporting sustainable soybean production in Northern Ghana (SSPiNG) through improved agronomic advisory services (GHANA-SOY/ESOKO Use Case)', 'The innovation targeting soybean production in Northern Ghana introduces four key MVPs: site-specific fertilizer recommendations, varietal suitability maps, location-specific maize-soy intercropping systems, and a mechanization platform. These components complement existing digital agronomic, weather, and market advisories, reaching over sixty thousand smallholder farmers. Addressing challenges like limited mechanization, insufficient access to input credit, and low yields, this initiative offers a holistic solution. Features include customized agronomic advice, a digital credit system for inputs, direct linkages to certified input suppliers, and real-time market insights, all delivered through the ESOKO, the demand partner. ESOKO is pivotal in this transformation, providing a user-friendly interface on basic mobile phones, serving as a vital information hub and bridging the gap between farmers and agricultural services. This strategy not only addresses immediate agricultural challenges but also promotes a sustainable, technology-driven ecosystem, enhancing productivity and profitability in Northern Ghana''s soybean sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1226?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(17820, 'Charting agroecological pathways: Multicriteria priority mapping for India’s sustainable transition', 'The innovation titled "Multicriteria-Based Priority Mapping: Charting Agroecological Pathways in India" addresses the dual challenge of food security and environmental sustainability. Using a multi-criteria decision-making framework, it integrates diverse datasets such as net primary productivity, fertilizer consumption, rainfall patterns, groundwater levels, the aridity index, and others. Two distinct agroecological priority maps were created to reflect different stakeholder perspectives. The first focuses on food security by prioritizing rainfed, low-productivity zones, while the second emphasizes environmental restoration in high-input, degraded areas. The limited overlap in the highest priority class indicates divergent stakeholder views. These maps, developed through the Analytical Hierarchy Process, guide targeted areas for priority resource allocation and sustainable farming practices while balancing yield improvements with environmental conservation. The innovation highlights the importance of participatory decision-making, including priority zonation, dynamic policy formulation, and periodic updates to address evolving challenges. This approach offers a replicable model for scaling agroecological practices globally.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17820?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1698, 'Improved dual-purpose adaptive chicken strains for smallholder farmers in Ethiopia', 'The innovations aim to develop improved chicken that grows fast and produces more eggs compared to the existing smallholder poultry production system. Imported commercial chicken lines lack adaptation characteristics when managed under smallholder systems. As a result the performance is usually low. They also do not meet the farmer preferences such as plumage and skin colour, mothering ability among others. This innovation entails developing various crossbred combinations that meets the needs and aspirations of smallholder farmers using improved inbred lines. It will also include evaluating the performances of the crossbreds on station and on-farm to identify best combinations using reciprocal crossing. The names of the four inbred lines are: LIL20: BIL20 Local ILRI 2020: Barred NHIL20: ILRI 2020 Brown ILRI 2020 WIL20: White ILRI 2020 The naming is subjected to change.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1698?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(1675, 'Development of an integrated multi scale modeling in mixed farming system methodology to achieve multi-objectives', 'The integrated multiscale modelling approach can be applied to assess synergies and trade-offs among interventions and benefits considering possible scenarios in the variety of sites and large-scale scenarios which are possible combination of different socio-technical innovations at different scales.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1675?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(5196, 'Provision of agro-advisories to mungbean farmers in Bangladesh through interactive voice response (IVR) system', 'The interactive voice response (IVR) system provides automated real-time weather alerts, warning farmers to harvest their mung bean ahead of forecasted heavy rainfall events which could damage their crop. Harvest warnings are voice calls that are based on a 5-day weather forecasts. It is available and accessible to farmers in Bangladesh without smartphones. 
Currently, the Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) Initiative is allocating resources for the continuous development, improvement, maintenance, and scaling of the system to achieve impacts at scale. Provision of harvest warnings to mungbean farmers were continued and partnerships for sustainably financing the business model around the innovation was secured.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5196?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(13256, 'High-fidelity genome editing tool for banana using Cas-CLOVER', 'The International Institute of Tropical Agriculture (IITA) has developed the highly versatile Cas-CLOVER system as an alternative to CRISPR-Cas9 for gene editing in banana. The Cas-CLOVER system operates through a dimeric mechanism, utilizing two guide Ribonucleic acid (RNA) and the Clo051 endonuclease, and has proven effective in inducing mutations across a variety of plant species, including banana. In light of intellectual property (IP) concerns and licensing challenges associated with CRISPR/Cas9, the Cas-CLOVER system offers a valuable alternative for gene editing in crops like banana.  We are currently developing excision tools, such as the piggyBac system, to enable the creation of transgene-free bananas using Cas-CLOVER, ensuring that no foreign genetic material remains in the edited plants.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13256?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(19792, 'KAZNET: Using digital innovation and social learning to collect and disseminate near-real-time information on markets performance, rangelands conditions, and household food and nutrition security in the drylands', 'The International Livestock Research Institute (ILRI) has been implementing an open-source digital crowdsourcing tool known as KAZNET (which stands for "Kazi kwa net" translated in Swahili to mean "working within a network") to alleviate data collection constraints and monitor resilience indicators in the drylands of Kenya and Ethiopia. KAZNET includes a dissemination component, where information gathered through the platform is processed in real time and channeled back to communities, development practitioners, and governments. The KAZNET app has a dashboard through which different types of information can be visualized. The graphic presentation of the information allows for the visualization of trends while also eliminating the need for more than basic literacy to understand the information presented. The disseminated information contains indicators of markets and rangelands performance. In addition, KAZNET disseminates information about the types of food consumed by households, livestock mortality, and the strategies used for coping with drought.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19792?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(20351, 'The International Potato Center and national research programs (Malawi, Zambia, Mozambique, Tanzania, Rwanda, Uganda) finalized target product profiles, prioritizing white, orange and purple fleshed sweetpotato enhancing clone sharing across subregions', 'The International Potato Center together with national agriculture research programs (Malawi, Zambia, Mozambique, Tanzania, Rwanda and Uganda) finalized target product profiles with thresholds of priority traits determined. In each country two major market segments namely White Fleshed and Orange Fleshed sweetpotato were identified and target product profiles developed. Target Product Profiles for the purple fleshed sweetpotato though with a small market share were developed in Uganda, Malawi and Mozambique. Breeding is now focused based on pipeline/market segment, selection and advancement of best clones more accurate guided by target product profiles. Resources only allocated to clones with high potential to win on the market. Within subregions of East and Southern Africa sharing of good clones has been enhanced especially those that meet specific target product profiles of supported country. Capabilities of national research programs are different, and some depend on products developed in other countries for agronomic testing and variety release.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20351?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4931, 'Application Programming Interface (API) enabling the Source-selective and Emission-adjusted Greenhouse Gas Calculator for Cropland (SECTOR) data tool for rice cropland to better calculate greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions', 'The International Rice Research Institute in collaboration with the SeaSpray Labs is developed and introduced the Application Programming Interface (API) which source-selective and emission-adjusted GHG calculator for rice cropland and that enables the SECTOR tool to connect to other digital tools for calculating greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions. SECTOR offers a high range of flexibility in terms of sourcing emission and activity data, and in selecting a range of scales for aggregation. SECTOR can allow GHG emissions verification and quantification for monetizable carbon credits, as the rice sector carbon market develops in Vietnam. It is now being used by research and practitioners in climate change adaptation and mitigitation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4931?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9310, 'Innovation in Economic Modeling for Growth, Poverty Reduction, and Food Security Insights', 'The interrelationship between economic growth, poverty, and food security is highly complex, with relatively few studies conducted on this topic in recent years. To address this gap, the Growth, Poverty, and Undernutrition Monitoring Model (GPUMM), developed by IFPRI, serves as an economic modeling tool designed to better understand these connections holistically by employing a macro-micro modeling approach linking a macroeconomic model with micro-level household survey data. 

We have completed the model calibration, enabling us to provide a comprehensive and consistent view of how macroeconomic changes influence income distribution, which in turn drives poverty and hunger rates over time. 

Using this model, we developed projections of how economic growth will affect poverty and undernutrition rates through 2026. This projection results help countries in tracking their progress toward achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 1 (No Poverty), SDG 2 (Zero Hunger), and SDG 8 (Decent Work and Economic Growth).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9310?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(16696, 'Controlled designation of origin for olive oil in Ellès-Sers, El Kef, Tunisia', 'The introduction of a Geographical Indication (GI) for local olive oil in Ellès-Sers is an innovative labeling approach to valorizing the diversity of local cultivars and highlighting the region''s unique olive oil characteristics. This initiative empowers small farmers, who primarily use traditional agronomic practices, by protecting their local expertise rooted in sustainable practices.
Focusing on agro-ecological specialization offers significant advantages for the community, safeguarding the historical significance of this olive-growing site as part of Tunisia''s heritage. With its multi-centenarian plantations of ancestral cultivars, Ellès-Sers can maintain its distinct identity while developing a competitive business model.
By forming farmer organizations, producers can collaborate to enhance the value of their olive oils and promote regional knowledge, positioning olive oil as a catalyst for rural development and economic growth while preserving cultural heritage.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16696?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6893, 'Investor Dashboard: A tool hosted in the Global Market Intelligence Platform for crop breeding prioritization by CGIAR and partners', 'The Investor Dashboard will provide an ex-ante impact assessment across the five Impact Areas for each market segment (20 CGIAR crops and forages) considering variety traits from the Target Product Profile database. These potential benefits will be combined with internal costing information to generate pipeline investment cases. These cost-benefit figures will enable stakeholders (donors, breeding leads, GI management) to prioritise activities within Genetic Innovation for the entire CGIAR breeding portfolio.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6893?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(13732, 'Improvement of genetic gains in the market segment for the TMeLS-I (Transplanted Medium Maturity Long Grain Soft Grain - Irrigated) breeding pipeline in rice with NARES partners engagement', 'The IRRI TMeLS-I (Transplanted Medium Maturity Long Grain Soft Grain - Irrigated) market segment has been benefiting nearly 6M hectares in South Asia, with 6 NARES partners in India and 3 in Bangladesh. OneRice strategy has shortened breeding cycle length from 6 to 3 years. Breeding materials tested in the 2023 wet season were generated using marker assisted selection in Rapid Generation Advance. After field evaluation in the TPE (target population of environments) sites, genomic selection was used to generate multiple-location summaries with gBLUP (genomic best linear unbiased prediction) estimates. GEBV (genomically estimated breeding value) estimates were generated for both tested and untested lines. High performing lines were selected for stage 2 testing. Comparison of average yields of current year tested materials against previous years showed positive genetic gain trend. The 1.77% rate of genetic gain was achieved through NARES engagement, strict implantation of population improvement, and predictive breeding methods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13732?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(20372, 'Establishment and expansion of One IRRI-NARES Breeding Network for rice breeding modernization', 'The IRRI-NARES Breeding Network is managed by a steering team comprising platform leaders, IRRI research unit leads, regional and modernization leads, and NARES coordinators. Their combined expertise helps shape strategies for implementing the network across different regions, ensuring alignment with breeding goals and agricultural priorities. Once the strategy and trial plans for NARES locations are established, IRRI’s network management team collaborates closely with breeding pipelines to oversee trial execution. This involves breeding pipeline leads, physiologists, pathologists, NARES coordinators, breeding and modernization leads, and seed systems experts working together to ensure efficient trial management and data-driven decision-making. This collaborative framework strengthens institutional governance, fosters knowledge exchange, builds capacity within NARES, and enhances relationship management. By leveraging expertise and shared resources, the network facilitates effective breeding strategies, broadens engagement, and drives agricultural innovation, ultimately contributing to more resilient and productive cropping systems in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20372?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1787, 'A unified data and research portal where researchers, public and private sectors, and policy and decision-makers can access data, evidence, and stories of impact and change on aquatic foods', 'The lack of robust and coherent data on aquatic food systems is a major barrier to realizing their potential for sustainable development and public health. Limited data (or access to it) hinders informed policy and investment decisions, leading to undervaluation and underinvestment in the sector by public and private stakeholders.
The data-to-action portal addresses this gap by providing a unified platform where researchers, policymakers, investors, and other stakeholders can access data, evidence, and stories of impact related to aquatic foods. It also enables collaborators and scientists to contribute new datasets and insights, fostering a dynamic and evolving resource.
An AI-driven storytelling model is under development to further enhance the portal’s capabilities. This model will transform research data into compelling narratives, making complex insights accessible and actionable, supporting decision-making, and driving greater engagement across sectors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1787?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(6337, 'Creation and expansion of a Local Technical Agro-Climatic Committee (LTAC) for the provision of climate advisory services to rice farmers in Lower Mekong provinces in Cambodia', 'The Local Technical Advisory Committee (LTAC) is a dialogue process among a diversity of local actors including scientists, technicians, representatives from the public and private sectors, and farmers, which seeks to understand the climate’s possible behavior in a locality, and to generate agro-advisories to reduce risks associated with expected climate variability. This dialogue is  used to create an agroclimatic bulletin that contains the region’s climate prediction, as well as climate smart recommendations around decision-making for each productive sector. In general, climate predictions produced by each country’s meteorological service are provided in the LTAC meeting, and the LTAC members, from various institutions, identify climate-smart agro-advisories to the climate predictions, and then the climate predictions and the agro-advisories are shared with local extension officers and farmers in the form of agroclimatic bulletins.

The LTAC was piloted in Battambang and Kampong Speu and is now being expanded to other Lower Mekong provinces in Cambodia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6337?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16953, 'Local Technical Agroclimatic Committees: Co-innovation and scaling in Guatemala', 'The Local Technical Agroclimatic Committees (MTAs) were first developed in Colombia in 2014 to deliver tailored climate information to farmers, supporting them to adapt to climate variability and enhance resilience. Since 2017, this innovative approach has been successfully scaled out in Guatemala, where it is led by the Ministerio de Agricultura, Ganadería y Alimentación (MAGA). By 2024, the MTAs had reached over 56,000 rural Guatemalans. With 21 active MTAs distributed across the country, the initiative ensures nationwide support for farmers, providing critical Climate Information Services to enhance agricultural decision-making. The implementation emphasizes gender and social inclusion, ensuring equitable access to resources and information. This model represents a transformative approach to integrating localized climate services into rural development, driving resilience and sustainable agricultural practices in vulnerable communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16953?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(18027, 'Improved protocol for drying of soya bean seed germplasm', 'The longevity of soya bean seed germplasm is improved if seeds are initially dried at 40C compared with immediate drying in the genebank drying room at 17C. Fumigation after initial drying was also better for seed longevity compared with fumigation before initial drying. These results indicate how the post-harvest process needs to be optimized to increase seed longevity and reduce the requirement for regeneration due to declining seed viability.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18027?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9294, 'Multi-stakeholder platform for managing complex and multifunctional landscapes for improving the sustainable management of the basin resources to support livelihoods in Zambia', 'The Lower Kafue Basin Multi-stakeholder platform(MSP) launched in October 2023, continued to be operational as a nexus-governance innovation especially through small grants of USD 20,000 which enabled the 3 Working Groups to work on activities demonstrating that it is possible to collaborate across different sectors bringing in state, non-state, communities and the private sector. The MSP had 4 meetings in 2024.

The MSP is crucial in helping negotiate land-water-food trade-offs. It facilitates the timely investment in effective, resilient aquatic food systems, informed by research, to address these threats and challenges and supporting the transition to a more just, nutritious, healthy, lower-carbon and climate-resilient food system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9294?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(10078, 'A farmers-focused monitoring, reporting, and verification framework (MRV) for tracking fertilizer-induced nitrous oxide emissions', 'The main contributor to anthropogenic nitrous oxide emissions is the utilization of agricultural nitrogen, with as much as half of the nitrogen applied through fertilizers being released into the atmosphere. To address this issue, the establishment of a monitoring, reporting, and verification (MRV) system is crucial for devising effective mitigation strategies. In response to this need, a practical MRV framework for monitoring nitrogen emissions from fertilizers is proposed, which can be readily adopted by farmers and local policymakers. Additionally, recommendations are provided for the implementation of a coordinated network to enhance the efficiency of such monitoring and mitigation efforts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10078?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(15774, 'Yogurts made from local milk and flavored with natural extracts from local non-timber forest products and agricultural products to foster new business models for improved livelihoods in Burkina Faso', 'The main dairy product consumed by the population of Bobo-Dioulasso is yogurt. The majority of dairies make yogurts based on imported milk powder and flavor the yogurts with artificial flavors. The Bobo-Dioulasso dairies that are members of ALL and that process locally produced fresh milk have started to diversify their yogurt production by flavoring them with natural extracts of non-timber forest products and local agricultural products (pineapple, sweet banana (poyo), coconut, date, ginger, horchata (chufa), kinkéliba, mango, mint, moringa, nere, monkey bread (théodo), orange-fleshed sweet potato, tamarind, zeguene (balanites)). And it works. There is an outlet for these yogurts that we can qualify as ''''agroecological yogurts'''' because they are made from real milk and flavored with local natural flavors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15774?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4267, 'Artifical intelligence-based image recognition for accurate varietal identification to increase cost-effectiveness of data collection without compromising on data quality for farmers, extentionist, survey teams', 'The main purpose of this work is to develop and test artificial intelligence-based image recognition software for varietal identification. Artificial intelligence-based methods will be ground-truthed and compared to other methods commonly used for varietal identification, such as DNA fingerprinting and farmer recollection. The vision is to arrive at low-cost and scalable methods for varietal identification. The created algorithms will be included in the VarScout app and the user can use for varietal identification.

In 2023, great progress has been made and proof of principle has been established: AI-based image recognition models for varietal identification in wheat resulted in a precision close to 87.5%.

In 2024, data collection protocols for potato and sweetpotato where developed and implemented. An AI-model for varietal identification of potato varieties is being developed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4267?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17314, 'Manual for the measurement of lentil grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity developed for researchers and extension agents', 'The manual  contains the standard operating procedures for the measurement of lentil grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity provides the step by step procedures for grain yield above ground biomass sampling. This manual s intended for researchers and extension staff who conduct agronomy research on lentils.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17314?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17327, 'Manual for the measurement of chickpea grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity  developed for researchers and extension agents', 'The manual,  developed for researchers and extension agents, codifies the standard operating procedures for the measurement of chickpea grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity which is useful in agronomy related research for the crop', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17327?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(9564, 'Piloting of web-based version of Digital system for Rice activity Monitoring and Reporting (RiceMoRe)', 'The Mekong River Delta is undergoing a remarkable digital transformation in the region’s rice production management with the Rice production monitoring and reporting system (RiceMoRe). Developed by the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) in collaboration with the Department of Crop Production (DCP), and the Center for Agricultural Digital Transformation and Statistics (DTS) of the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development’s (MARD), RiceMoRe provides a platform for standardizing and recording time-series rice production activity and the adoption of low-emission practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9564?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1976, 'Protocol for exploring production, business conditions, and demand for rice in the Colombian Caribbean region', 'The methodological steps proposed in this guide seek to support the Producers'' Association of Leticia and the Canal del Dique in market research. The tool uses a value chain approach to evaluate their current situation or position at the production and business level in the rice chain, their relationships or potential commercial alliances in the primary grains market, and the market or demand for rice in the region of interest for green paddy, dry paddy, white rice, and other by-products. This methodology proposes to approach these issues through qualitative research that combines the collection of secondary information, semi-structured interviews with key actors in the sector, chain mapping exercises, and observation and inquiries at key locations where the product is sold. The methodology aims to strengthen capacities and generate tools that allow access to markets, and has already been applied in the departmentof Bolivar.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1976?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5195, 'Methodology for methane emission reduction by adjusted water management practice in rice cultivation', 'The methodology is applicable to measures that reduce anaerobic decomposition of organic matter in rice-cropping soils. Such measures include changing the water regime during the cultivation period from continuously to intermittently flooded conditions and/or a shortened period of flooded conditions, using the alternate wetting and drying method, adopting aerobic rice cultivation methods, and switching from transplanted to direct-seeded rice (DSR). This methodology can be applied to large and small-scale or micro-scale projects or PoAs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5195?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(18179, 'Performance Evaluation of Commercial Grass Cultivars in Guatemala', 'The methodology provides a framework for establishing field trials to assess the adaptability, productivity, and nutritional quality of commercial grasses under the climatic and soil conditions of Izabal, Guatemala. A pilot field trial was conducted in collaboration with a local livestock association and academia in the Izabal department, Guatemala. Experimental plots were established on both well-drained and poorly drained soils, testing eight commercial grasses, including hybrids of Urochloa and Megathyrsus maximus. Each treatment was replicated and included both technical and participatory evaluations by local livestock farmers. The results can provide key information for designing forage management strategies adapted to the climate and local conditions, to strengthen livestock productivity in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18179?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(10955, 'The miniSASS mobile app for river health assessment', 'The miniSASS is a tool for citizen scientists to investigate the health of rivers. The miniSASS mobile app uses machine learning to facilitate the identification of the invertebrates living on the stream bottom.  The app then uses the identifications to calculate the river health score based on the tolerance of each invertebrate type and then submits this data to a global mapping platform', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10955?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16487, 'Toolkit developed and piloted to support actors facilitating subnational implementation of the national action plan for food systems transformation in two provinces in Viet Nam', 'The Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development designated Dong Thap and Son La provinces as pioneering provinces for subnational implementation of Viet Nam''s National Action Plan for Food Systems Transformation (NAP-FST). To start, the provincial governments needed to create implementation plans with prioritized activities for their provinces. Sustainable Healthy Diets was invited to provide technical and coordination support to the provinces to meet this goal. An engagement process, comprised of several activities including capacity building, prioritization, and multi-stakeholder consultations, was designed and implemented in 2024. Recognizing the benefits of documenting the process for use in other provinces or even by other countries embarking on subnational implementation of their national action plans, Sustainable Healthy Diets has drafted a toolkit of resources for actors facilitating engagement activities that support subnational implementation of national/federal-level food systems transformation political processes. The toolkit will be further refined for dissemination in 2025.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16487?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(16603, 'Semi-automated planter piloted for use in planting rice accessions for regeneration and characterisation in IRRI genebank', 'The Minoru transplanter model RXE-61 is a ride-on machine that can transplant a maximum of six rows with fixed 33-cm spacing and variable distance between plants. This machine was imported from Japan and arrived at IRRI last April 14, 2023. The first step is to familiarize the usage, maintenance and safety of the machine. Optimization on growing of multiple and single seedlings was achieved based on standards. Other aspects such as land preparation, soil and water depth are also important considerations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16603?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(2137, 'Production of early generation seeds for seed producers in Ethiopia', 'The mixed cropping system in the highlands of Ethiopia is dominated with cereal-legume cropping system. Recently, potato, garlic and banana are becoming important crops due to expansion of small scale irrigation. Early generation planting materials of potato, garlic and banana is critical bottlenecks to scale farmer preferred varieties and bring positive impacts on food and nutrition security in the highlands of Ethiopia. A total of 7.1 t of  planting materials of five varieties of potato and one variety of garlic and 200 suckers of five varieties of banana were produced for next cropping season.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2137?phase=1', 9, NULL),
(2128, 'Production of early generation seeds of farmer preferred cereal and legume varieties for community seed producers in Ethiopia', 'The mixed cropping system in the highlands of Ethiopia is dominated with cereal-legume cropping system. Recently, potato, garlic and banana are becoming important crops due to expansion of small scale irrigation. Early generation seeds of cereals and food legumes is critical bottlenecks to scale  farmer preferred varieties and bring positive impacts on food and nutrition security in the highlands of Ethiopia. In 2021/22 cropping season breeder seeds of 14 farmer preferred varieties of bread wheat', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2128?phase=1', 9, NULL),
(18398, ' ScalA-PB: Using the tool that connects peace, sustainability, and agriculture is now on your mobile phone', 'The mobile version of ScalA-PB is now available for Android devices, offering an innovative solution to assess sustainable impact in agricultural systems. This tool allows users to analyze how projects, policies, and practices influence economic, social, and environmental aspects, facilitating informed decision-making and strengthening resilience in conflict-affected environments.

Designed for researchers, farmers, policymakers, and communities, ScalA-PB provides a practical and efficient way to tackle the challenges of sustainability, climate change, and peacebuilding. Its intuitive interface and specialized functions enable users to collect, analyze, and visualize key data, driving the adoption of evidence-based sustainable solutions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18398?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3246, 'Scoring of market smart varieties: A methodology to determine how well the varieties match with market intelligence and determine its impacts to farmers and consumers', 'The Monitoring, Evaluation, Learning, and Impact Assessment component of the Market Intelligence Initiative is developing a methodology for rating varieties in terms of how well they match the market intelligence developed through the initiative; as a proxy for how impactful they might be for farmers and consumers, if adopted. We plan to develop a system to score varieties in terms of their market intelligence. We plan to develop this scoring system in much greater depth in 2023, in collaboration with MELIA focal points from other initiatives in the Genetic Innovation action area. This system will compare the chosen varieties to the Target Product Profiles (TPPs) developed under MI Work Package 2, on various dimensions.

MELIA is also collaborating with MI WP3. Randomly selected farmers will receive nudges to encourage them to adopt varieties promoted by WP3. We will exploit this random variation to establish causal relationship between adoption and impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3246?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(5540, 'Inclusive multi-stakeholder knowledge sharing platform on mixed farming system for farmers, and relevant stakeholders in Halesi Tuwachung Municipality, Khotang district and Gurbhakot Municipality, Surkhet district to support farmers.', 'The Multi-Stakeholder Agriculture Knowledge Hub (MSAKH) is an innovative platform that fosters collaboration among local and municipal stakeholders to address agricultural challenges, share experiences, and promote sustainable mixed farming systems. Its primary goals include empowering farmers—particularly women, Dalits, and marginalized groups—by improving their access to resources and advocating for policy changes.
The key objectives of the platform are to enhance inclusive participation, facilitate knowledge sharing, strengthen advocacy, build capacity, support monitoring and coordination, and scale innovative agricultural practices.
In March 2024, four MSAKHs were established—one each in Wards 6 and 7 of Halesi Tuwachung Municipality and Wards 12 and 14 of Gurbhakot Municipality. These hubs were formed in the presence of the research team, social mobilizers, municipal staff, and ward chairpersons. Each MSAKH comprises diverse stakeholders, including farmers, agro-entrepreneurs, local elected representatives and officials, financial institutions, community-based organizations (CBOs), and the private sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5540?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5344, 'Business model for the sustainability of the provision of digital climate advisory through interactive voice response to mungbean farmers in Bangladesh', 'The Mungbean Interactive Voice Response (IVR) is a system that provides harvest alerts to mungbean farmers in Bangladesh. A business model for this digital application is required for sustaining it in longer term. In 2023, new business models for the system is supported. First model explored the Mungbean value chain to check if value chain actors like exporters are interested to cover the costs of their  farmers supplying mungbean to the exporter firm.  The second model is with microfinance that provides loans to farmers and developing a bundled model that provides the harvest warning as an add-on service to the loans for mungbean farmers. In 2024, microfinance institutions and private company agree to integrate the IVR in the provision of loans to farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5344?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3856, 'Musa Observatory: an open access platform for banana and plantain around the world, providing data driven analysis to all sectors', 'The Musa observatory is an initiative led by the Alliance Bioversity-CIAT to establish an information system that provides indicators related to the global Musaceae (bananas and plantain) production context for the benefit of key stakeholders in the sector. It includes data related to the context of Musaceae in each country, production system information, trade, and competitiveness information, Musaceae consumption data, inventory of technologies related to the crop, and recent news. The information displayed on the platform is built based on data provided through open-access primary and secondary information sources from different national and international organizations, which is why we highlight the importance and contribution of these organizations. The information intends to provide lessons to producers, consumers, industry/processors, intermediaries, scientists, or any other actor related to the musaceas value chain.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3856?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1169, 'MyEiA platform: Comprehensive Use Case Management, Evidence Tracking, and Resource Tool for Effective Partnership Onboarding and Work Package Assessment', 'The myEiA platform digitizes the Use Case framework, transforming the delivery of agronomic solutions through a comprehensive 7-step workflow for Use Case activities.

It streamlines team management, resource allocation, and partner onboarding, enhancing efficiency and scalability. As a customizable innovation management platform, myEiA supports Use Case and scaling workflows, integrating multi-disciplinary task forces to scale agronomic solutions effectively.

Ready for regional deployment within ASSAPs, myEiA fosters team collaboration and supports the expansion of scaling workflows, driving impactful agronomic innovations at scale.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1169?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9018, 'Designing Evidence-Based Models for Central Highlands Ecoregion Foodscape, Enhancing Soil and Water Efficiency, and Localizing Seed Varieties', 'The Nature Conservancy and CGIAR Excellence in Agronomy Initiative are collaborating to develop a strategic use case for the Central Highlands Ecoregion Foodscape, focusing on scientific research and evidence-based solutions. The innovation comprises three Minimum Viable Products (MVPs):

MVP 1 - Scenarios and monitoring systems to ensure water availability in source areas (water towers) and its sustainable use in neighboring ecologies.
MVP 2 - Integrated regenerative portfolios targeting efficient and sustainable land, soil, and water (including river) management.
MVP 3 - Regenerative solutions to improve water use efficiency, resilience, and climate adaptation in semi-arid agricultural landscapes.

These MVPs aim to enhance sustainable resource management, bolster ecological resilience, and address climate challenges across diverse ecosystems within the Central Highlands. This partnership underscores the importance of integrating scientific innovation with practical strategies to promote sustainability and resilience in foodscapes and beyond', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9018?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8510, 'Recicle+ Network: Promoting Circular Entrepreneurship in Colombia', 'The NATURE+ Initiative aims to establish circular economy innovation hubs in target countries to create an ecosystem for innovation and entrepreneurship in circular economy. In Colombia, partnering strategically with Servicio Nacional de Aprendizaje (SENA), which operates 118 training centers nationwide, a decision was made to form a collaborative network (Recicle +) instead of creating a new center. This network includes SENA, circular economy businesses, and other key players, forming a supportive ecosystem. With the involvement of universities, research centers, incubators, accelerators, and private sector entities, the network aims to broaden its impact. This diverse collaboration seeks to cultivate a supportive environment, encouraging knowledge exchange, training, coaching, and the promotion of circular economy entrepreneurship.
In 2024, a number of activities have been organized such as innovation challenges, student hackathons, hands-on workshops have strengthened capacities in students, farmers, and local communities, encouraging sustainable practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8510?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(6587, 'No-till seeder adapted in public-private partnership to Moroccan crops and soil types for Moroccan smallholder farmers to practice zero tillage Conservation Agriculture', 'The no-till seeder machinery has been developed to support Morocco''s national plan of having one million hectares of farming land under Conservation Agriculture practices by 2030 (Generation Green 2020-2030). Developed from a local prototype by an ICARDA, INRA and local manufacturer team, using technology imported from dryland farming machinery in Australia, the no-till (zero tillage) seeder allows smallholder farmers in Morocco to sow seeds directly into the soil (direct seed) without causing any mechanical modification of the soil structure. No-till practices are expected to improve the resilience of dryland smallholder farmers by reducing soil erosion and increasing the amount of carbon dioxide, water, and organic matter retained in the soil.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6587?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7405, 'Partners'' satisfaction survey tool is developed to gather feedback from Excellence in Agronomy partners to improve the initiative''s ways of working with the agronomy community', 'The objective of Excellence in Agronomy Initiative is to deliver an increase in productivity and quality per unit of input (agronomic gain) for millions of smallholder farming households in prioritized farming systems by 2030, with an emphasis on women and young farmers, showing a measurable impact on food and nutrition security, income, resource use, soil health, climate resilience and climate change mitigation.  This can only be achieved through and is being implemented in partnership with organizations such as yours.   
 
Thesurvey tools were developed to gather feedback from partners and learn how we can improve our performance in working with partners and for reporting.  These include End of Meeting Evaluation  survey tool,; Knowledge Attitude Skills and Practice survey/ study; partners’ satisfaction and partnership efficacy surveys.  Each of the results are useful for the teams to inform adaptive management, results reporting and contribute to the body of evidence and rationale for adjustments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7405?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7413, 'End of Learning Event survey tool for Excellence in Agronomy scientists to capture feedback on their involvement and assess the initiative performance around six dimensions of modern agronomy', 'The objective of Excellence in Agronomy Initiative is to deliver an increase in productivity and quality per unit of input (agronomic gain) for millions of smallholder farming households in prioritized farming systems by 2030, with an emphasis on women and young farmers, showing a measurable impact on food and nutrition security, income, resource use, soil health, climate resilience and climate change mitigation.  This can only be achieved through and is being implemented in partnership with other organizations.   
 
The End of Meeting Evaluation survey tool was developed to gather feedback from Excellence in Agronomy scientists and researchers during the learning events to systematically capture feedback on their involvement in the initiative and the performance of the initiative   in relation to the six dimensions of modern agronomy   Each of the results are useful for the teams to inform adaptive management, results reporting and contribute to the body of evidence and rationale for adjustments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7413?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7409, 'Partnership efficacy survey tool', 'The objective of Excellence in Agronomy Initiative is to deliver an increase in productivity and quality per unit of input (agronomic gain) for millions of smallholder farming households in prioritized farming systems by 2030, with an emphasis on women and young farmers, showing a measurable impact on food and nutrition security, income, resource use, soil health, climate resilience and climate change mitigation.  This can only be achieved through and is being implemented in partnership with other organizations.
 
To learn how we can improve our team''s performance in working with our partners and for reporting, we apply systematically four tools: End of Meeting Evaluation; Knowledge Attitude Skills and Practice survey/ study; partners’ satisfaction and partnership efficacy surveys.  Each of the results are useful for the teams to inform adaptive management, results reporting and contribute to the body of evidence and rationale for adjustments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7409?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(19136, 'Validation of four novel chemicals for rice seed treatment', 'The objective of this study was to assess the efficacy of seed treatments for eradicating bacteria from rice seed. We evaluated 2 synthetic (Team and Fluidioxinol) and two biological products (ANT FM 30P, RB-99TM or Antinfek 30P) using seed plating technique. Our findings show that Teams is still more efficacious, followed by Fluidioxonil, which corroborates the results obtained by Germplasm Health Unit at IITA. ANT FM 30P, RB-99TM were effective in eliminating bacteria but were not effective against seed-borne fungi. There is need to conduct further testing on additional fungicides to identify those that are deemed acceptable, considering that "Team chemical" has been banned in the European Union and other countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19136?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5447, 'Promoting a Seed Supply Chain for Genetically Improved Farmed Tilapia and Piloting Aquaculture Based on it.', 'The objective of this work is to boost sustainable fish supplies and increase the profitability of tilapia farmers in Nigeria, by establishing a climate-resilient smallholder aquaculture industry based on Genetically Improved Farmed Tilapia; to enhance social well-being and economic empowerment of rural Nigerians, especially including women, children, and youth; and to save foreign exchange associated with fish imports. In partnership with the private sector, this existing innovation has established a parent population of Genetically Improved Farmed Tilapia to breed and produce their fry for out-grower farmers to grow to market-size fish, and for hatcheries of small and medium-sized enterprises to produce fry in a decentralized seed supply chain for Genetically Improved Farmed Tilapia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5447?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(2956, 'Optimizing a traditional Malawi lakeshore zone maize-cowpea relay intercropping system for food, fodder and soil fertility benefits', 'The objectives of this study are: to determine the effect of water management through tied ridges on productivity of different maize-cowpea cropping systems under variable soil fertility conditions; to determine biological N2-fixation and N cycling for an improved cowpea variety and two cowpea land races in sole or relay intercropping with maize, and Determine soil moisture dynamics in rely intercropping systems with or without tied ridges; Apply the SIAF framework and quantify gains and tradeoffs for the system; To evaluate the potential contribution to fodder for the maize-cowpea relay intercropping system; To use maize-cowpea relay intercropping data for model parametrization and application', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2956?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(4107, 'An ODK-based tool for real-time surveillance and reporting of banana bunchy top disease by surveyors, quarantine and extension service agents', 'The ODK-based surveillance tool was developed for the banana bunchy top virus (BBTV) surveillance to enable early detection, map the geographic spread of the virus, and deploy control measures. The tool also streamlines the minimum data set necessary for decision support and provides real-time access to data for the users and decision-makers. The tool was used to delimit surveys in Tanzania from 2022 to 23. The same was scaled in 2024 for use in Uganda and Zambia, and its use is continued in Tanzania and Nigeria to assess the BBTV status in over 300 locations in these four countries. Separate models were developed in Uganda and Zambia, and partners (Zambian Agricultural Research Institute - ZARI in Zambia and the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries - MAIFF in Uganda) were trained in the application. The tool is now being used independently by partners after one training session with users.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4107?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(16123, 'On-farm Feed Advisor (OFA) app: a mobile tool designed for extension agents to optimize and balance nutrients in the diets of dairy cattle and buffalo', 'The On-Farm Feed Advisor (OFA) App, originally developed on the Ionic framework, faced significant upgrade challenges due to deprecated libraries. To resolve this, the app was successfully migrated to React Native, incorporating enhancements to improve functionality and user experience. Additionally, a robust web application was developed, providing a comprehensive dashboard for seamless data management and parameter tracking. This effort was a collaborative achievement of the Digital Innovation (DI) and SAPLING Initiatives. The revamped mobile app and web dashboard are now fully operational and reintroduced to users with advanced features. The mobile app is available on both Google Play Store and iOS App Store, ensuring broader accessibility. Updated links to the app have been added under the evidence section.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16123?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6197, 'On-farm Water, Energy, Food, and Carbon footprint (WEFC) nexus index for the quantitative assessment of on-farm integrated resources management', 'The on-farm Water, Energy, Food, and Carbon footprint (WEFC) nexus index has been developed to enable the quantitative assessment of on-farm integrated resources management in wheat and faba bean farming systems in Egypt. The WEFC index is computed as the weighted arithmetic average of the four pillars of (1) water: on-farm saved water consumption/national maximum water consumption; (2) energy: on-farm saved energy consumption/national maximum energy consumption; (3) food: on-farm agricultural yield/national maximum yield; and (4) carbon dioxide (CO2): on-farm saved CO2 emissions/national maximum CO2 emissions. The WEFC nexus index values are, therefore, indicative of resource consumption (inputs) and agricultural production and CO2 emissions (outputs). The index can be used to quantitatively assess the nexus performance of site-specific packages of practices under certain conditions and quantify the impact of new interventions on the nexus status to drive transformative actions at the farm level.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6197?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(13506, 'Online training course for researchers and extension agents  on building climate resilience of rainfed systems through agricultural water management', 'The online training course enables participants to learn climate-smart agriculture, ensuring resilient food systems and sustainable livelihoods', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13506?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13739, 'Online course on Emerging Techniques and Innovations in Agricultural Development for extension personnel, research and development staff and field staff working with smallholder farmers', 'The online training course tackles agricultural mechanization and innovation, with a focus on sustainable agriculture and climate change resilience. It is designed for extension personnel, research and development staff and other field staff who work with smallholder farmers to enable them promote new mechanized and digital innovations for improved farm productivity and efficiency of operations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13739?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6200, 'Online Water Accounting Dashboard for the Souss Massa Basin, Morocco, to visualise past, current, and predicted future water status parameters such as water availability, water balance, and long-term hydrological changes', 'The online Water Accounting Dashboard for the Souss Massa River Basin, Morocco, presents past, current, and potential future water status parameters such as water availability, water balance, and long-term changes in hydrology in an easily accessible, visual, and interactive manner for diverse stakeholders such as water management agencies, policy- and decision-makers. The Souss Massa River Basin has been hard hit by drought in recent years, resulting in reservoir water levels at historic lows. This innovation aims to bridge the communication divide between research findings and decision-makers to facilitate the informed, sustainable planning and management of scarce freshwater resources.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6200?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(8487, 'The Open Ontology-Based Interoperable Information Asset Metadata Schema (OIMS) tool-box to support data managers and providers with standardizing and organizing metadata.', 'The Open Ontology-Based Interoperable Information Asset Metadata Schema (OIMS) is a flexible and extensible metadata schema designed to standardize and organize metadata for various information assets like datasets, documents, models, and publications, making them more accessible, transparent, and reusable. In order for data managers and data providers to use the approach, a set of tools is needed to support them.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8487?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(18318, 'Demonstration of efficient manure-fed biogas digesters in smallholder systems for clean energy and fertilizer production', 'The optimized production and use of biogas (clean fuel) and organic fertilizer from livestock manure management improve energy efficiency and agricultural sustainability. This system processes animal waste and other organic materials to generate biogas and bio-slurry. It enables small-scale agricultural producers and livestock keepers, particularly those with minimal GHG emissions, to contribute to a low-carbon rural economy through improved agricultural practices. By promoting a circular economy on farms, it enhances productivity while supporting climate change mitigation in livestock farming. Additionally, it improves nutrition and agricultural output by using bio-slurry for food production and biogas for cooking and farm operations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18318?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(348, 'Definition of the portfolio of distinct, achievable Target Product Profiles, by all CGIAR breeding programs, provides the basis for collaboratively assessing and agreeing on the most impactful CGIAR breeding targets', 'The organizational innovation develops tools and approaches for taking stock of current CGIAR breeding pipelines and mapping them to impactful, gender-relevant and gender-intentional, in-demand, feasible target product profiles (TPP’s) - each a unique combination of productivity, adaptation, resilience, quality, processing and consumer traits, that is aligned to a sub-regional market segment, since 2023 using the Breeding Portal. Each TPP contains the traits required and targeted scores. As per November 2024, CGIAR and NARES defined a total of 607 TPPs aligned to Breeding Pipelines and Market Segments. Traits were aligned with 14 distinct value propositions that drive Impact Area tagging of TPPs. Market Intelligence uses the information to develop pipeline investment cases that describe benefits to the five Impact Areas by breeding pipeline. The innovation makes transparent and optimizes the focus of current and new CGIAR breeding pipelines. Through the 2024 review, the quality of TPPs has improved.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/348?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1131, 'Extended version of the Global Food System Sustainability Index:  a policy relevant tool that can help decision-makers design policies that are more effective', 'The original Global Food System Sustainability Index (GFSSI) considered four dimensions of food system sustainability (food security & nutrition, environment, economic and social dimensions) and covered 97 countries from low, middle and high-income regions. One limitation of this index, however, was that the four dimensions were not equally represented, with both the economic and social dimensions depending on a limited number of indicators. In the 2022 paper, CGIAR researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets and their co-author built an extended version of the GFSSI by adding several indicators to the two dimensions where the representativity was weak: the social and economic dimensions. They used the extended GFSSI to investigate whether all four dimensions contribute equally to the change observed in the sustainability of food systems across countries, or whether some dimensions are more important than others, and if so, which dimension(s) and for which (group of) countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1131?phase=1', 5, NULL),
(16586, 'Global Rice market segments and product concepts', 'The output led to the division of global rice area into meaningful market segments that can be targeted through independent breeding pipelines. The activity identified a total of 96 market segments across the globe, out of which 13 were identified as priority for IRRI breeding programs. CIAT and AfricaRice have also aligned their breeding pipelines to this framework.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16586?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1617, 'Tracking Adaptation in Livestock Systems (TAiLS): tool designed to enable governments to track and report on climate change adaptation in the livestock sector', 'The Paris Agreement encourages countries to track and report on their progress in adapting to climate change. But there are no suitable frameworks and tools are not suited for tracking adaptation for international reporting, more so for the livestock sector. TAiLS tool is designed to enable governments to track if adaptation is taking place across space and time and its outcomes. The tool has been co-produced in consultation with livestock keepers and government officials in Ethiopia, Kenya, and Uganda. It integrates a set of indicators related to three dimensions, i.e., climatic hazards, impacts, and adaptive capacity and actions. The tool is also customized to leverage on the existing dataflows at national and subnational levels of government.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1617?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16979, 'Participatory Climate Risks Management Approach in High Nutritional Value Chains: Dairy Case in Guatemala', 'The participatory methodology for climate risk analysis in the dairy value chain is a strategic approach designed to identify and manage climate risks affecting this agro-food chain, with the goal of strengthening food security and nutrition in low- and middle-income countries. The guide provides tools for organizations, NGOs, and development agencies to develop participatory strategic plans, involving stakeholders in a process that includes chain prioritization, risk analysis, and the design of adaptation strategies. This methodology, which has been piloted in the dairy value chain in Guatemala, allows for the assessment of climate events'' impacts on the production, processing, and distribution of food, ensuring a contextualized and sustainable approach to mitigate the effects of climate change on the availability and quality of dairy products.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16979?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(351, 'Towards a partnership strategy for CGIAR collaborative breeding networks that promotes equitable and mutually beneficial partnership models and is considerate of the diversity of regions, crops and needs', 'The partnership strategy assesses historically grown partnership models and creates transparency on partner inclusion. In 2023, 55 regional crop improvement networks were defined based on discussions with CGIAR breeding programs. 26 of these networks are based in Africa. A country prioritization tool developed in 2022 that incorporates crop acreage, world bank income status and existing partnerships was deployed in 2023 with 22 crop x region breeding networks in Africa and 11 crop x region breeding networks outside of Africa. Network members were prioritized according to a three-tier system: Level 1, Level 2 and Spill Over. By 2024, more than 90% of all center-by-crops had categorized partner countries accordingly. The partnership strategy provides an objective approach for networks to prioritize their focus and investments in Level 1 and 2 countries, where the impact is likely to be greatest, whilst ensuring other countries have access to international public technologies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/351?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3693, 'High-Throughput Pipeline developed by IIRRI for Quick Detection of Rice Bacterial Blight Dissease to monitor Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae (Xoo) Populations in Asia', 'The pathology team at IRRI developed 40 single nucleotide polymorphism markers that can distinguish 12 Asian Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae (Xoo) (a rice bacteria) populations and used them to monitor the bacteria (Xoo) population in Asia. The distribution of 12 Xoo populations was mapped out and the effectiveness of Bacterial Leaf Blight (BLB) resistance genes in different countries was predicted. The data is currently uploaded on PathoTracer app.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3693?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(7612, 'Plant Health Innovation Platform at Qob Elias, Lebanon: farmer participatory approaches for wide dissemination of pest management innovations', 'The PHI-IP in Lebanon was selected in Qob Elias village, 30 km far from ICARDA’s Terbol station. Through consultation meetings with lead farmers, LARI and ICARDA scientists agreed to validate IPDM options to manage (i) viruses and aphids on faba bean, and (ii) fall armyworm insect on maize. In addition, the incidence of seed-borne viruses in faba bean and chickpea seed lots was determined, as 86% of farmers at the PHI-IP site get their seeds from local markets. Neighboring farmers were invited to visit on-farm demonstration of IPDM innovations hosted by farmers at the PHI-IP site. Fields of lead farmers were used as training sites for neighboring farmers throughout the growing season. These fields were visited by around 100 farmers (12 females) in addition to the agricultural experts. The discussion between LARI & ICARDA researchers, and lead farmers with other farmers during the field days was very useful.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7612?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(3038, 'Genomic surveillance maps for geminiviruses in Southeast Asia and potexviruses in the Americas affecting cassava using the Nextstrain web tool', 'The pipeline developed during 2022 has been validated for new genetic data. We have made this available as a service (online open maps) to the cassava disease community to support disease monitoring. 

Sequencing strategy is described in detail under a Technical Report in WP1 (pending Cgspace code). Cassava common mosaic virus (CsCMV) genomic surveillance peer-reviewed publication is under preparation.

CWBD-fungus genetic surveillance was prepared under a sequencing workshop carried on in Laos during 2023. The map show the diversity of the fungus and more information is available in an early 2024 publication', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3038?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(17832, 'Policy guidance tool for agroecological transition', 'The policy guidance framework and tool 
- provide valuable insights into policies and programs that are conducive of agroecological transitions, serving as a guidance tool to effectively utilize resources from existing policies and programs for sustainable agroecological transformations.
- includes detailed mapping of activities within policy and their synergies with agroecology, area-specific coverage, information on subsidies, and recommendations for policy convergence. 
- identifies potential organizations, schemes, and initiatives that can support the development and implementation of agroecological practices.
- addresses the prevalent issues of policy information gap, siloed implementation and the thin distribution of resources, thereby enhancing the scalability of agroecological practices.
- fosters more effective and coordinated efforts in promoting sustainable ecosystem in India by providing a structured approach to policy analysis and integration
The potential users of the tool include national level policy makers, State and District administration, Food system actors, Self Help Groups, Farmers Producers Organization, and Farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17832?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(10263, 'Production of saline tolerant potato variety under zero tillage using rice straws during the fallow winter season for farmers in rice-based ecosystems in West Bengal, India', 'The potato production through zero-tillage using rice straw as mulch (PZTM) is an innovative technique to sustainably intensify agricultural production with potato uses few inputs and residues from the previous crop without disturbing the soil. It has the potential to address issues being exacerbated by climate change like excessive input use leading to detrimental environmental effects, water scarcity, and worsening air quality due to rice straw burning and, as such, significantly contribute to a sustainable agroecological transformation in India. Key to this practice is the efficiency principle, by increasing resource-use efficiencies of major inputs. It recycles straw as mulch, creating value for a residue that would have been burned otherwise. As this technique is associated with economic gains as well as women’s empowerment, it improves rural livelihoods and promotes gender equity, and as such adheres to the principle of human and social values.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10263?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5334, 'Production of saline tolerant potato variety under zero tillage using rice straws during the fallow winter season for farmers in rice-based ecosystems in Bangladesh', 'The potato production through zero-tillage with straw mulch (PZTM) is an innovative technique to sustainably intensify agricultural production with potato uses few inputs and residues from the previous crop without disturbing the soil. It has the potential to address issues being exacerbated by climate change like excessive input use leading to detrimental environmental effects, water scarcity, and worsening air quality due to rice straw burning and, as such, significantly contribute to a sustainable agroecological transformation in Bangladesh. Key to this practice is the efficiency principle, by increasing resource-use efficiencies of major inputs. It recycles straw as mulch, creating value for a residue that would have been burned otherwise. As this technique is associated with economic gains as well as women’s empowerment, it improves rural livelihoods and promotes gender equity, and as such adheres to the principle of human and social values.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5334?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(12832, 'Expediting trait discovery and validation through construction of ready-made discovery populations', 'The primary bottlenecks in trait development efforts are the time and expense involved in (A) creating biparental populations for identifying or validating a putative Quantitative Trail Locus (QTL), and (B) introgression of major genes into an elite background; (C) validating a gene is effective in the elite background and (D) determining the absolute effect size of a gene. All of these factors may be addressed by creating pre-prepared advanced introgression populations such as CSSL libraries. To facilitate this, over 29 fixed-line populations, mostly advanced-introgression Chromosome Segment Substitution Lines (CSSL) libraries, have been constructed and are available.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12832?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8841, 'Carbon project in the Colombian Orinoquia: Transforming grasslands for emission reductions', 'The project “Natural silvopastoral systems in the Colombian Orinoquia Region” is a grouped carbon project comprising a set of activities to improve grassland management in approximately 150,000 ha of Vichada and Meta, Colombia. The crediting period is 2020 to 2040 applying the Verra Methodology for Improved Agricultural Land Management VM0042 Version 2.0. The project primary objective is to achieve a reduction in overall emissions by focusing on carbon sequestration. This will be accomplished mainly by implementing adaptive multi-paddock grazing with improved deep-rooted grass cultivars, specifically Urochloa humidicola. An estimate of the annual average and total GHG emission reductions and removals are approximately 270,000 t CO2eq per year and 5,400,000 t CO2eq, respectively. Prior to the implementation of the project, livestock practices in the regions are characterized by annual burning of savannas and low-quality animal feed. The project was successfully listed in the pipeline of the Verra Registry in November 2023.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8841?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7390, 'Airclean innovation for monitoring and predicting air quality', 'The project aims to develop a system for monitoring and predicting air quality in Metropolitan Lima. This will be achieved through sensors and real-time data analysis, alerting companies and citizens about health and environmental risks. The project seeks to address air pollution, provide useful information for public and operational health, thereby creating environmental awareness. An interactive web platform will be developed that will allow users to access air quality data, view predictions and receive alerts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7390?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(7392, 'Nutritious and sustainable bread "Visia" produced by reusing two by-products, beer bagasse and milk whey, for general public consumption.', 'The project consists of creating a bread with high fiber and protein content, using sustainable ingredients such as beer bagasse,milk whey and whole wheat flour. This unique combination provides a healthy and delicious option for bread lovers. The use of beer bagasse and milk whey, byproducts of the food industry, reduce food waste and promote the circular economy. This bread benefits digestive health helps with weight control and offers a source of vegetable protein.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7392?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(7343, 'Pusquy Thani, a fermented dairy product high in probiotics and protein, mixed with local gum overo, for general public consumption', 'The project consists of producing a dairy product based on Kefir mixed with overo gum, a natural stabilizer and preservative obtained from the native Overo species, which is produced and harvested in the dry forests of northern Peru. The objective is to develop a product with probiotic characteristics, which is accessible to the public, as well as promote the consumption of fermented foods and support the conservation of Peru''s dry forests.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7343?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(15051, 'Progress report of Aflasafe activities in Madagascar, co funded by the World Bank Food Security Resilience Program for Madagascar', 'The project focuses on developing Aflasafe technology to combat aflatoxin contamination in key agricultural crops in Madagascar. The project aims to reduce aflatoxin levels, ensuring food safety and compliance with international standards for aflatoxin levels in crops such as maize and groundnuts. By utilizing biocontrol strategies the project addresses the critical issue of mycotoxin contamination, enhancing food quality and safety. The ultimate goal is to improve public health, increase access to global markets, and support sustainable agricultural practices in regions affected by aflatoxins.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15051?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(15052, 'Progress report of Aflasafe activities in Sierra Leone, co funded by the World Bank Food Security Resilience Program for Sierra Leone', 'The project focuses on developing Aflasafe technology to combat aflatoxin contamination in key agricultural crops in Sierra Leone. The project aims to reduce aflatoxin levels, ensuring food safety and compliance with international standards for aflatoxin levels in crops such as maize and groundnuts. By utilizing biocontrol strategies the project addresses the critical issue of mycotoxin contamination, enhancing food quality and safety. The ultimate goal is to improve public health, increase access to global markets, and support sustainable agricultural practices in regions affected by aflatoxins.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15052?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9898, 'Improving household nutritional status and income through Orange fleshed sweetpotato consumption and commercialization by farmers and communities in Malawi', 'The project multiplied  clean sweet potato planting material in greenhouses and through Ukama Ustawi trained Decentralised vine multipliers (DVMs).  Farmers therefore accessed orange fleshed sweet potato (OFSP) seed from the research stations and from  DVMs. Public extension workers were responsible for beneficiary registration and provided short training to sweet potato seed beneficiaries.  This innovation is relevant  because it is high in beta carotene a precursor for vitamin A which  contributes to a reduction in Vitamin A deficiency and provides  income to smallholder farmers through its commercialization.  In addition, this innovation is drought tolerant, can be grown throughout the year, even in marginal soils  and hence can provide reasonable yield hance  contributing to food security under drought  conditions or in relatively poor soils .', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9898?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(18428, 'River Virtual Reality Tour to improve water resources understanding', 'The project produced an immersive VR experience presently only at a single river site on the Olifants River (Balule), allowing users to explore and gain a spatial understanding of key water resources of the area. This tour was developed under the CGIAR Initiative on Diversification in East and Southern Africa in collaboration with CGIAR Digital Innovation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18428?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(6895, 'Diversified and intensified farming through rice-vegetables (mungbean, okra, chili) production systems in the coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'The project strategically selected mini-watersheds called “Learning Hubs (LHs)”, where the sluice gates are operated by water management groups of coastal polder zones to create favorable water environment for scaling improved cropping round the year by cultivation of high-value rabi crops (maize, sunflower, mungbean, okra and chili) and HYV rice in a contiguous area in dry and wet seasons. The LH farmers harvested 4.5-6.8 t/ha paddy that almost doubled the wet season productivity over traditional rice varieties. In 2023 and 2024 dry seasons, a total of 905 men and 310 women cultivated rabi crops on 160 ha in 15 LHs of the coastal zone, in which 26% farmers cultivated chilli, mungbean and okra under conventional method. The mean yield of chilli, mungbean and okra was 457 kg/ha, 687 kg/ha and 7205 kg/ha, respectively and most farmers used these crops for home consumption that reduced family expenditure and improved household nutrition.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6895?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(6752, 'Protocol for Crop Cut Surveys', 'The protocol for crop cut surveys provides a guide to researchers and users a step-by-step process to conduct a crop cut survey in the field and calculation of the crop cut harvest.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6752?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7983, 'A Policy Practice Index Tool for tracking policy formulation and implementation in member countries', 'The purpose of the tool is to support policy analysis, tracking the adequacy and success in agricultural policy formulation and implementation among ASARECA member countries in the East and Central African region. With ASARECA, Ukama Ustawi organized a workshop with ministerial representation to validate this tool in 2023. In this workshop, results of the tool testing survey, covering 11 policy dimensions in 26 responses from 14 member countries, were presented. In 2024, Ukama Ustawi and ASARECA will organize a workshop to introduce this tool to ministeries and train policy analysts in technical advisory teams.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7983?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8856, 'Identification of best integrated mycotoxin management practices for the control of the mycotoxin deoxinivalenol by researchers in Zambia', 'The quantification of the mycotoxin deoxinivalenol work has not been done in Zambia, thus the results will be presented in next year''s report', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8856?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(14350, 'Effective QTL for improving grain zinc in rice', 'The qZn5 Quantitative Trail Locus (QTL) was previously reported as contributing to grain zinc in rice, however validation was lacking and no elite material appeared to contain the gene. Deployment of this QTL into an elite background was completed, to a purity of >99.3% recovery of the elite genomic background.  Evaluation of the near-isogenic elite deployment products showed that qZn5 indeed does produce about 4ppm increase in grain zinc, which represents approximately a 40% improvement over the baseline level in the elite recurrent parent. The increase in grain zinc was apparent in milled, polished grain, so this represents zinc that would be available in rice consumed. Evaluation of the elite lines also showed no further penalty for yield (which was seen in early-generation products), so there are no penalties for breeders to use this QTL. The QTL interval was reduced by 90% to just 300kb/1.5cM.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14350?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(2793, 'Conservation methods developed for rare wild bean species', 'The rare bean species Phaseolus hygrophilus, collected in the Costa Rican rainforest and preserved in CIAT’s gene bank, has proven difficult to conserve due to its inability to produce seeds. To address this, a tissue culture method has been optimized, and the species is now maintained both in tissue culture and as a living collection in specialized growth chambers. These plants are propagated using grafting techniques and cuttings. For Phaseolus albicarminus, a separate grafting and layering protocol has been developed for vegetative propagation. However, this species has not been successfully preserved through tissue culture and is instead maintained as a living collection in growth chambers.
The conservation methods are being used to conserve and produce seeds of wild bean species in the field by genebank staff working on the regeneration of the bean collection.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2793?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(8581, 'Reference Diet Deprivation (ReDD) index: a tool to measure diet quality and explore how policies are driving dietary change', 'The Reference Diet Deprivation (ReDD) index is estimated from household consumption survey data and measures the incidence, breadth, and depth of diet deprivation (the voluntary or involuntary shortfall in consumption) across multiple food groups. It can be used to compare diet quality across households or populations or track changes in diet quality over time. The advantage of the ReDD index is that it can be integrated into an economic model to examine changes in diet quality under different food and agriculture policy scenarios or external shocks. More recently, the ReDD index has been used as part of the underlying data for the Food Security Simulator, a tool for assessing the potential short-term impacts of food price or household income shocks on food security and people’s diets. It is showing to be a useful tool for researchers and decisionmakers to assess diet quality and examine the drivers of dietary change.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8581?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(419, 'Radical collaborations to achieve climate justice for women, girls and marginalized communities', 'The report highlights innovative ways through radical collaborations to achieve climate justice for women, girls and marginalized communities. One route to solutions is through partnerships, between governments and development partners and community-based women’s groups and networks, that give equal voice to women from low-income, climate-affected communities to define climate and water problems and solutions. Such collaborations would, despite their obvious merit, be ‘radical’ because women in climate-affected communities today often are regarded as mere ‘beneficiaries’. However, our experience shows that given the space and resources to do so, women can lead innovation on climate solutions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/419?phase=1', 4, NULL),
(17188, 'Optimization of the germination test protocol for viability monitoring of Cytisus proliferus seeds in genebank collections', 'The research investigated methods to improve the germination of Cytisus proliferus seeds, a perennial legume with significant potential for livestock feed. Different scarification techniques were examined to effectively overcome seed dormancy. These methods included the use of sulfuric acid, to chemically break down the seed coat; boiling water, a thermal method to soften the outer layer; potassium nitrate solutions, to facilitate germination by penetrating the seed; and the excision of embryos, a physical approach to remove the embryo from the seed. Each of these techniques was assessed for its efficacy in promoting germination and improving seed viability.  Excised embryos and boiling water at 80 oC for 15-25 minutes were most effective at increasing germination percentages compared to other scarification methods. The study concludes by recommending optimized germination protocols for genebank viability monitoring of this species, prioritizing practical, less labor-intensive methods for broader application.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17188?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(19201, 'The effect of different harvesting frequencies on the performance of food-feed oat varieties under rainfed conditions to increase productivity levels', 'The research involved participatory on-farm trials with three nationally registered oat varieties: the forage variety Bate and the food varieties Sorataf and Hulegeb, tested under various cutting treatments after establishment during the main rainy season.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19201?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(8855, 'Identification of best integrated mycotoxin management practices for the control of deoxynivalenol by researchers in Mexico and Zambia', 'The resistant variety Sumai3 exhibited the lowest DON content in all experiments when compared to the other wheat lines, demonstrating the importance of host resistance on DON management. Of the four fungicides tested, triazoles Folicur and Tilt exhibited better effects than those containing strobilurins, which tended to increase DON content. No clear trend could be observed among treatments for the experiments on fungicide application timing and nozzles.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8855?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8850, 'Identification of best integrated disease management protocols for Fusarium head blight in Mexico and Zambia', 'The resistant variety Sumai3 exhibited the lowest infection in all experiments when compared to the other wheat lines, demonstrating the importance of host resistance. Additionally, the results indicated the best performance of the fungicide Folicur, and the best time for fungicidal application at anthesis. The three nozzle types used in this study exhibited similar control effects.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8850?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4868, 'Rice Activity Monitoring and Reporing System (RiceMoRe) website to allow multi-level data reporting and access to rice cropping maps for rice stakeholders in Vietnam', 'The Rice Activity Monitoring and Reporing System (RiceMoRe) is an online interactive monitoring and reporting system that allows multi-level data reporting to produce interactive maps for rice status and coverage of rice production in Vietnam. It is designed to provide site-specific and real-time information on different aspects of rice production such as growth stages, adoption of mechanization, crop establishment and harvesting methods, and other techonologies adopted at farm level and present it in a more structured and visually appealing through maps. It aims to provide comprehensive information on rice production status and coverage to government institutions and staff, researchers, rice value chain stakeholders, and other concerned parties to inform local agricultural planning, decision-making, and other economic activities around rice. 

The RiceMore is now turned over to the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development (MARD) from continued implementation and scaling in Vietnam.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4868?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9051, 'Crop yield estimation using Artificial Intelligence for Rice Crop Manager (RCM) database in Odisha, India', 'The Rice Crop Manager (RCM) database contains 283,346 recommendations across 31 districts in Odisha, eastern India. RCM''s functionality can be extended beyond fertilizer recommendations to include comprehensive crop management decision-making and the development of support systems for various stakeholders. The methodology employs advanced technologies such as machine learning to create a model that selects optimal parameters for generating these recommendations. The target yield has also been estimated using a calibration and validation ratio of 70:30, with performance evaluation primarily based on R-square, Root Mean Square Error, and Mean Absolute Error calculations. The analysis indicates that the approach is suitable for the data collected and can be automated to generate more valuable information for farmers'' benefit.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9051?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(13660, 'Rice Sustainability Hub: A knowledge hub for rice carbon credit projects', 'The Rice Sustainability Hub hosts resources, tools and templates to support project developers and validation and verification bodies (VVBs) seeking to develop and validate projects that reduce methane emission from rice cultivation. The hub focuses on promoting sustainable rice activities in Vietnam with selected documents also made available in Vietnamese. Many of the resources and tools are also relevant for projects located in other countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13660?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16741, 'Rice-field fisheries pond system for enhancing climate resilience and farm productivity for farmers in Cambodia', 'The Rice-Field Fisheries Pond for Enhancing Climate Resilience and Productivity integrates climate-resilient practices into traditional rice-fish systems. Key innovations include the installation of inlets and outlets to facilitate water exchange between ponds and rice fields, promoting fish migration, and improving water management. The inclusion of aquatic plants covering 30-40% of the pond area enhances biodiversity and supports ecosystem health, while strategically placed shelters protect fish and deter illegal activities. This system optimizes fish and rice production cycles, allowing farmers to achieve at least two cultivation cycles per year. In addition to boosting yields, the approach enhances climate resilience by ensuring a consistent fish supply, even during periods of water scarcity or erratic rainfall. The innovation has demonstrated significant increases in both rice and fish production—57% and 67%, respectively—while improving profitability for farmers, making it a sustainable solution for food security and income stability in rural communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16741?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(20391, 'High-throughput phenotyping models for cassava and sweetpotato trait selection and breeding', 'The RTB Breeding Innovation project aimed to accelerate breeding by enhancing propagation and

screening for virus resistance in cassava and sweet potato. The project focused on developing rapid

propagation techniques, virus resistance screening protocols, and promoting cross-crop collaboration

among researchers on root, tuber, and banana (RTB) crops.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20391?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(14380, 'The Rural Energy Insecurity Experiences Scale: a tool to measure how much energy shortage rural households in low- and middle-income countries face', 'The Rural Energy Insecurity Experiences Scale is a tool to measure how much energy shortage rural households in low- and middle-income countries face. It captures their experiences with energy poverty in a way that can be compared across different places. The results help show how energy shortages affect other important issues like nutrition, gender equality, environmental sustainability, and poverty.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14380?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5736, 'A decision support tool for preventing and controlling Rift Valley Fever (RVF) epizootic', 'The RVF tool is a collaborative effort between the One Health Initiative and the Digital Innovation Initiative, designed to analyze climate-epidemic interactions and support decision-making for Rift Valley Fever (RVF) outbreak prevention and control in the Greater Horn of Africa. It includes a decision-support system for managing RVF epizootics, enabling timely and effective responses, and integrates a disease information management system for efficient data compilation and dissemination. The tool enhances disease surveillance in high-risk areas by combining trapping systems with community reports and supports cost-effectiveness studies on RVF prevention strategies, informing contingency planning. It provides meteorological data, including rainfall and precipitation at county and sub-county levels, and incorporates RVF modeling for risk assessment. Additionally, it serves as a key resource for prevention and control efforts, featuring an online news bulletin that enables users to generate customized scientific advisories with geospatial visualizations and professional reports.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5736?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13048, 'Sampling Frames Tool: Empowering Researchers in Data Collection, Validation Trials, and MVP Testing through Interactive Google Earth Engine-Based Web Application for enhanced Decision-Making', 'The Sampling Frames tool is a public web tool providing support to design data collection strategies for validation trials, surveys, or testing Minimum Viable Products (MVPs). It guides users through a simple interface to choose the most relevant environmental clusters, reducing variance due to environmental factors and making trial results more representative in the target area.  

The tool supports Use Cases in understanding agroecological heterogeneity and aids decision-making within their focus areas. It relies on Google Earth Engine and offers a simple, interactive, and adaptive web interface that requires minimal computing skills, making it more accessible than code-based systems. Users can easily ‘click through’ selections, such as focus area, variables, and cluster number, to generate outputs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13048?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(12180, 'Multiplication and dissemination business model, for tropically-adapted and farmer-preferred improved chicken breeds, for value-chain actors, research and development partners in Lower and Middle Income Countries (LMIC)', 'The scaling business model is proposed based on approaches as suggested by Lundy et al. (2012) that includes three major steps. First, mapping the existing business model using the nine caveats and identifying major challenges and opportunities. The existing business model was mapped using data from baseline and marketing assessment surveys focusing on inputs supply, production, marketing, institutional support, vertical and horizontal coordination along the value chain. Second, identifying business cases using lessons learned from previous tropical poultry genetics solution activities in Sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia. Third, developing the new business model considering the business cases identified above. The proposed scaling business model has six  major components: establishing partnership, organizing and building capacity, access to inputs and services (i.e . feed, heath, finance, breed, training and extension services), production and support, value addition and marketing (through aggregators, wholesalers, retailers and processors), monitoring and optimization.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12180?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(7357, 'Scaling Scan assessment tool integrates climate change and social responsibility for gender/social inclusion analysis', 'The Scaling Scan is an assessment tool for individuals and groups to explore what is required to scale an innovation in a specific context, the implications this has for project management and collaborations,
and the potential trade-offs on the environment and social dynamics (focused on a gender and climate change perspectives).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7357?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(20354, 'IRRI, in collaboration with India''s ational Seed Research And Training Centre, develops a seed accelerator network for rapid rice varietal turnover in India', 'The Seed Accelerator Meet 1.0 convened on April 22, 2024, by the IRRI South Asia Regional Centre (ISARC) and the National Seed Research And Training Centre (NSRTC), fostered collaboration with 58 participants from public, private and community seed agencies and jointly designed an action plan for seed system improvement strategy. This platform facilitates discussions on the latest findings from on-farm testing, current rice breeding strategies, and the potential integration of NARES/publicly bred varieties through a comprehensive scaling network. A key feature of the event was promoting the acceleration of varietal turnover in rice, which is crucial for improving agricultural productivity and resilience.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20354?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(275, 'Seed Product Market Segment Database for CGIAR and NARES breeding programs', 'The Seed Product Market Segment Database (SPMSD) contains over 600 market segments identified using the 8 market segmentation criteria, and covering crops and subregions where CGIAR and NARES currently target investments in crop breeding and seed systems. For each market segment, descriptors are presented to describe the segment. These descriptors (e.g., area under production, production volume, value, total and rural population, population in the market segment footprint (total, in poverty and undernourished)) provide an understanding of the size and relevance of each segment. A consistently defined and described set of SPMSs provides the basis for setting priorities based on comparisons of market segment data and information (e.g. area, rural population in poverty, yield and yield potential, value of crop); informing the design of a Target product profile using a common language for discussions with NARES, breeders, social scientists, seed companies and CGIAR leadership and funders on prioritization and investment levels', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/275?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(2958, 'Digital agro-advisory tool to implement decision making in nutrient management for maize and barley', 'The SI-MFS team is developing and piloting an agro advisory tool that will facilitate knowledgeable decision-making by farmers about the best practices in nutrient management. Generated from research-based data integrated with detailed biophysical co-variates, the advisories will also be site- and context-specific. Farmer access to the advisory services will be ensured through customized dissemination channels, including video, Interactive Voice Response, Short Messaging Services, radio, and traditional extension channels. Male and female farmer preferences and constraints will be factored in during the entire process to ensure adaptive learning. The advisory tool will provide site and context advisories for farmers in 3 Woredas during the incubation phase (2023-2024) and upscale to 10 million Maize and Barely growing farmers in Ethiopia until 2030.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2958?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(19634, 'Entrepreneurship Ecosystem model for sheep and goat value chain in pastoral systems', 'The Small Ruminant Tools for Rapid Assessment of Pastoral Systems (STRAPS) initiative supports sustainable sheep and goat production in pastoral systems by integrating traditional knowledge, modern breeding techniques and sustainable practices. Developed by the International Livestock Research Institute (ILRI), RECONCILE and the Baringo County Livestock Department, STRAPS provides four e-tools to assess three groups of actors in the value chain: pastoral communities, individual producers, service providers and market agents. Using the Open Data Kit platform, STRAPS generates actionable data to improve productivity, strengthen market links and address systemic challenges. By fostering an entrepreneurial ecosystem, the initiative enhances pastoralist livelihoods, boosts climate resilience and supports sustainable livestock systems in semi-arid regions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19634?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17311, 'Manual for the measurement of faba bean grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity  for reseachers and extension agents', 'The Standard Operating Procedure for the measurement of faba bean grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity provides the step by step process of harvesting samples from the field trials. This  Standard Operating Procedure is intended for researchers and extension agents who conduct agronomy research on faba bean.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17311?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(6754, 'Standard operating procedure on yield gap decomposition to ground the Use Cases under Excellence in Agronomy and to prioritize development of agronomy solutions based on factors that reduce crop  yields', 'The standard operating procedure on yield gap decomposition to ground the Use Cases of the Excellence in Agronomy describes the framework of yield gap decomposition, the concept of yield levels and yield gaps, the data requirements and data collection and yield gap decomposition analysis. This is developed for researchers and scientists who implement yield gap decomposition to prioritize the development of agronomy solutions based on the most relevant agronomic constraints.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6754?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6564, 'Measurement of cowpea grain yield and aboveground biomass by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessments  in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists', 'The Standard Operating Procedures  (SOP) for measuring cowpea grain yield and aboveground mass at maturity by crop cut at plot level provides the step-by-step procedure to determine the cowpea grain yield and above ground biomass in on-farm trials. The SOP, reviewed by key agronomists from various One CG centers involved in the Excellence in Agronomy Initiative, provides the standard methods for agronomic gain assessments in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6564?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4201, 'Determination of cassava root yield and aboveground biomass by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessments in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists', 'The Standard Operating procedures for cassava harvesting and for measuring cassava root and aboveground biomass provides the step-by-step guide for the measurement of cassava yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and calculation procedures for these parameters. The SOP, reviewed by key agronomists from various CG Centers involved in the Excellence in Agronomy Initiative, codifies the standard procedures to be followed for agronomic gain assessments in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4201?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4198, 'Measurement of barley grain yield and aboveground biomass by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessment in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists', 'The Standard Operating procedures for measuring barley grain yield and aboveground biomass provides the step-by-step guide for the measurement of maize grain yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and calculation procedures for these parameters. The SOP, reviewed by key agronomists from various CG Centers, codifies the standard procedures for agronomic gain assessment in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4198?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4195, 'Measurement of maize grain yield and aboveground biomass by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessments in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists', 'The Standard Operating procedures for measuring maize grain yield and aboveground biomass provides the step-by-step guide for the measurement of maize grain yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and calculation procedures for these parameters. This SOP, reviewed by agronomists/scientists in various CG Centers, serves as the standard for agronomic gain assessment in field trials. The SOP is developed for researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4195?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6562, 'Measurement of soybean grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity by crop cut at plot level', 'The Standard Operating Procedures for measuring soybean grain yield and aboveground mass at maturity by crop cut at plot level provides the step-by-step procedure to determine the soybean grain yield and above ground biomass in on-farm trials.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6562?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4199, 'Measurement of teff grain yield and aboveground biomass by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessments in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists', 'The Standard Operating procedures for measuring teff grain yield and aboveground biomass provides the step-by-step guide for the measurement of grain yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and calculation procedures for these parameters. The SOP, reviewed by key agronomists from various CG Centers involved in Excellence in Agronomy Initiative, codifies the standard procedures for agronomic gain assessments in field trials for the use of researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4199?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(6563, 'Measurement of potato tuber yield at maturity by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating  procedures  for agronomic gain assessments in field trials developed fro researchers and extensionists', 'The Standard Operating procedures for the measurement of potato tuber yield provides the step-by-step guide for the measurement of potato tuber yield at plot level and calculation procedures for these parameters. The SOP, reviewed by key agronomists from various One CG Centers involved in the Excellence in Agronomy Initiative, codifies the standard procedures/methods for agronomic gain assessment in field trials developed for researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6563?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4187, 'Measurement of rice grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessment in field trials developed for agronomists', 'The Standard Operating Procedures for the measurement of rice yield and above ground biomass provides the guidelines and procedures for measuring rice grain yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and how to calculate these parameters. The SOP, developed for researchers and extensionists, is important for the agronomic gain key performance indicator assessment in field trials.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4187?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4186, 'Measurement of sorghum grain yield and aboveground biomass by crop cut at plot level: Standard operating procedures for agronomic gain assessment in field trials developed for researchers and extensiosists', 'The Standard Operating Procedures for the measurement of sorghum yield and above ground biomass provides the guidelines and procedures for measuring grain yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and how to calculate these parameters to compute the agronomic gain. This SOP is developed for agronomy researchers and extensionists.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4186?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4184, 'Measurement of wheat grain yield and aboveground biomass at maturity for crop cut at plot level', 'The Standard Operating Procedures for the measurement of wheat yield and above ground bomass provides the guidelines and procedures  for measuring grain yield and aboveground biomass at plot level and how to calculate  these parameters', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4184?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(18222, 'Standard Operational Procedure Current Practice Assessment Add-on Survey  developed for researchers and extension staff', 'The Standard Operating Procedures on current practices assessment aims to enable researchers and extension staff to collect data on Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) (yield, net profit, labor productivity, nutrient use efficiency, water productivity, and carbon balance), and current practices from land preparation to residue management after harvest (including labor input, organic input, inorganic fertilizer application, number of irrigations, number of herbicide applications, number of other pesticide applications, price of inputs, etc.). These data will be used for benchmarking agronomic gain KPIs and understanding crop management and biophysical factors affecting these indicators. It involves farmers’ interviews and field measurement (e.g., field size, soil sampling, crop cut). It may also include laboratory analyses. This manual presents the standard operation procedure of the current practice assessment survey', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18222?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(6569, 'Soil sampling (disturbed and undisturbed), handling and storage for soil chemical, biological and physical properties', 'The Standard Operating procedures provides the step-by-step guide for soil sampling, handling and storage for soil, biological and physical properties and calculation procedures for these parameters.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6569?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6579, 'Determination of the minimum number of plants and the area to be harvested for correct crop yield', 'The Standard Operating procedures provides the step-by-step guide for the determination of the minimum number of plants and the minimum area to be harvested for correct crop yield determinations and calculation procedures for these parameters.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6579?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(16453, 'Improved protocol for the germination of pigeonpea wild species', 'The study evaluated the impact of hydropriming on the germination of five pigeonpea wild species: Cajanus cajanifolius, C. scarabaeoides, C. reticulatus, C. sericeus, and C. acutifolius. Seeds were scarified and soaked in four treatments: no soaking (T1), 12 hours (T2), 16 hours (T3), and 24 hours (T4), then sown in a glasshouse using a Completely Randomized Design. ANOVA showed significant effects of species, treatments, and their interactions. Among the species, C. sericeus showed the highest reduction in days to emergence (60%), while C. acutifolius showed the lowest (26.66%). The 16-hour soaking treatment (T3) was the most effective for improving germination. These results offer valuable insights for optimizing germination and using wild pigeonpea species in improvement programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16453?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(14521, 'Incidence and distribution of seed-borne viruses of chickpea and faba bean seeds in Tunisia', 'The study investigates the incidence and distribution of seed-borne viruses in faba bean and chickpea crops in Tunisia, focusing on their economic and agricultural significance. Seed samples from 20 locations across Tunisia were tested using tissue-blot immunoassay (TBIA) for six viruses: Broad Bean Mottle Virus (BBMV), Broad Bean Stain Virus (BBSV), Pea Seed-borne Mosaic Virus (PSbMV), Bean Yellow Mosaic Virus (BYMV), Alfalfa Mosaic Virus (AMV), and Cucumber Mosaic Virus (CMV). Among 4,587 faba bean seedlings, only BYMV was detected in two seed lots with a seed transmission rate of 1.7%. For 4,000 chickpea seedlings, AMV and PSbMV were identified in three and nine seed lots, respectively, with seed transmission rates of 1.0% and 3.2%. The highest PSbMV transmission rate (8.3%) was found in two chickpea lots. These findings underscore the need for regular monitoring, improved seed quality, and further research to mitigate virus transmission and enhance crop productivity in Tunisia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14521?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7238, 'Solar Explorer: Guide for Sub-Saharan Africa Groundwater Irrigation Investors', 'The Sub-Saharan Africa Solar irrigation Explorer is an online tool designed to support investors who are interested to understand the potential of investing in groundwater-sourced solar irrigation in Sub-Saharan Africa. It calculates the size of solar systems required for key irrigated crops and the economic return from the solar-based irrigated production given climatic, groundwater and crop-specific requirements.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7238?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(780, 'Gliricidia intercropping into maize cropping combines trees and crops to increase diversity, productivity, profitability, and environmental management. This is relevant for resource constrained smallholder farmers', 'The sustainable intensification initiatives of Southern Africa use Gliricidia sepium, a leguminous tree species which is well adapted to the environment for their sustainable intensification practices. The importance of this system is to grow the trees in hedge rows or as dispersed trees so that leaves can be utilized in a “chop and drop” fashion with limited labour needed for transporting the biomass from one plot to the other. The spatial arrangement also aims at keeping the competition as low as possible while reaping the benefits of the agro-forestry species. Maize-Gliricidia hedge-row and Maize-Gliricidia disbursed shading are the two main systems. In the first one, maize-legume rotations are planted at normal plant population, in addition, hedgerows of Gliricidia trees are planted in rows spaced 5 m apart with an in-row spacing of 1 m. In the second, the disbursed shading system aims at developing larger Gliricidia trees that provide shade, less competition and in addition some green leaves that can be applied to the soil after pruning every year. One tree is usually planted every 10 m in rows 5 m apart.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/780?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9559, 'A Temperature-Dependent Phenology Model for the Sweetpotato Whitefly Bemisia tabaci MEAM1 (Hemiptera: Aleyrodidae)', 'The sweetpotato whitefly, Bemisia tabaci (Gennadius) MEAM1, is a globally distributed pest with a significant economic impact due to its ability to transmit numerous plant viruses. Understanding the temperature-dependent population growth is crucial for managing this pest effectively. Studies were conducted on B. tabaci MEAM1''s development, mortality, and reproduction across seven constant temperatures (12° to 35°C). The Insect Life Cycle Modeling (ILCYM) software was employed to create a phenology model, predicting life-table parameters based on temperature. The model successfully predicted population development between 13.9 °C and 33.4°C, with the highest finite rate of increase observed at 26.4°C. Validated with field data, this model provides valuable insights into the species'' potential distribution based on temperature, aiding in the design of pest management strategies globally.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9559?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(16919, 'Development of a Technical Protocol for Standardized Detection of Transgene-Free Rice Lines', 'The Technical Protocol for Standardized Detection of Transgene-Free Rice Lines via qPCR aims for the Certification of Exemption in the Philippines to enable Transgene-Free Lines to be Regulated as Conventional Breeding Lines. This protocol will ensure accurate, reliable identification of transgene-free lines, facilitating compliance with regulatory standards and supporting the seamless integration of these lines into breeding programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16919?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(18340, 'Production of Genetically Improved Fish Tilapia (GIFT) TiLV-resistance G20 families', 'The third selection of Genetically Improved Farmed Tilapia (GIFT) TiLV-resistant G20 line marks a step in enhancing resistance to Tilapia Lake Virus (TiLV). Through selective breeding, WorldFish expects this generation to demonstrate greater resilience, improved survival rates, and stronger disease resistance, contributing to higher productivity and sustainability in smallholder and commercial aquaculture systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18340?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(12427, 'Protocol and tools to assess causes of reproduction failures in small ruminants in Mali', 'The tools package is composed of qualitative, household level questionnaires and sero-prevalence survey targeting priority disease negatively affecting the reproductive performance of small ruminants in Mali.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12427?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(17911, 'Training materials on piloting nutrition-sensitive agri-food systems for provincial and district staff in the Mekong Delta, Vietnam', 'The training materials aim to equip stakeholders with the knowledge and tools needed to advance nutrition-sensitive agri-food (NSAF) projects in the Mekong Delta. Co-developed by the Department of Cooperatives and Rural Development (DCRD), the materials are designed to enhance knowledge and capacity, as well as foster collaboration between the agricultural and health staff. The materials include presentations and a training document, which are structured into five sections, covering the rationale, project design, implementation strategies, and evaluation processes of the NSAF approach. The materials target agricultural sector officials, health sector officials, laborers mass organizations, and others.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17911?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8508, 'Refinement of deep learning detection models to enhance a digital tool for pest and disease surveillance of banana pest and diseases', 'The Tumaini app has undergone significant upgrades to improve banana disease detection. FasterRCNN models were replaced by more advanced YOLOv8 models, resulting in faster and more accurate detections. A step-by-step intelligent detection algorithm, developed with expert phytopathologists, guides users through symptom scanning, improving accuracy by identifying the most affected plant parts and comparing them to reference images. Region-specific AI models were also developed for different continents, increasing the total number of models from 7 to 35, further enhancing detection precision. New diseases, such as Moko in Latin America and Leaf Spot in India, were added to the detection system. Tumaini now requires user registration, ensuring data security and allowing plant detections to be linked to specific users. Additionally, users can map and georeference healthy or diseased banana plants, sharing this information in real-time through the online dashboard. Lastly, This new Tumaini version includes gender disaggregation to collect data on users.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8508?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7287, 'The Tumaini smartphone app and platform: for a series of banana disease symptom recognition, surveillance support and disease occurrence mapping.', 'The Tumaini app, an AI-based tool for banana disease recognition, has seen a complete makeover in 2023. The Tumaini platform now includes a mapping feature where diseased plant records are being visualized. In 2023, Bioversity staff [Guy Blomme, Walter Ocimati, Aman Omondi and Miguel Dita] provided large amounts of field survey data points for inclusion in the Tumaini mapping platform. Contributions to fine-tuning of the Tumaini symptom recognition/detection app were provided by Guy Blomme and Miguel Dita. An updated version of the Tumaini app will be released by April 2024, while the mapping platform is already online [see: https://tumainiaiapp.org]. The mapping platform will also function as an early warning system for the various banana diseases.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7287?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(8558, 'TUMAINI Banana Disease Surveillance Dashboard : a new innovation for Musaceae protection', 'The TUMAINI Banana Disease Surveillance Dashboard has emerged as a transformative tool, achieving seamless spatial integration across the global banana landscape. This platform bridges the Already developed Tumaini app and a recently developed online dashboard, enabling precise mapping of banana plant locations and extents. It plays a crucial role in outlining production areas and offers broad applications as a versatile resource. The resounding success of the Alliance''s collaboration has paved the way for the inception of the next-generation map. So far, over 50,000 GPS points have been detailed, tracking five diseases (Fusarium wilt, Xanthomonas wilt, Banana bunchy top virus, Moko, and Leaf spot) in more than 15 countries in the global south. The database, consisting of around 50,000 mapped plants, was compiled from regional surveys, cellphone image metadata, and predominantly drone image data, which constitutes about 70% of the total database.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8558?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(15771, 'Digital tools for advising dairy farmers engaged in agroecological transition: CoProDScope (advice on managing farm co-products) and Jabnde (advice on formulating balanced diets for dairy cows)', 'The two digital tools, CoProdScope and Jabnde, were designed for farmers living in sub-Saharan Africa, to co-design strategies for managing farm co-products in fodder, manure and mulch (CoProdScope) and to co-design balanced and economical diets for dairy cows. Both tools were used in the co-design, experimentation and assessment of agroecological technologies carried out within the framework of the Initiative with dairy farmers of the Bobo-Dioulasso milkshed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15771?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(12188, 'Animal identification and traceability system for livestock keepers and other stakeholders for selection of superior genetics based on livestock farmers'' good animal husbandry practice in Nepal.', 'The unique animal identification protocol is developed for tagging dairy buffaloes and other livestock species (cattle, goat, sheep, pig, dog etc.) which will be used to select superior genetics based on performance records. Animal Identification Tracking System (AITS) is a necessary component in livestock development to help in identifying and tracing animals. The identification of animals enables selection of those with superior performance, monitoring of their health status, managing and monitoring productivity in herds and tracking the movement and trade of animals and animal products. It will also help target beneficiaries with government schemes and health insurance companies to identify insured animals and reduce unwanted  compensation claims.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12188?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2101, 'Integrated stock assessment modelling in digital fisheries monitoring for targeted species in Timor-Leste', 'The unreported nature of many small-scale fisheries means they have insufficient data to conduct assessments of fish stock health, a prerequisite for certification as sustainable fisheries. It also often causes them to be automatically characterised as illegal, due to the term "IUU fishing" - Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated fishing.    
This innovation is a novel approach to pilot a LBB (Length-based Bayesian Biomass) analysis of important targeted species in Timor-Leste into the fisheries monitoring and automated analytics system workflow.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2101?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8969, 'Urban Diets and Food Environments (UFED) Tool to provide guidance for researchers and public actors for addressing issues related to food environments.', 'The Urban Food Environments and Diets (UFED) Tool is proposed to provide guidance on the following questions:

1) What assessment methods, tools, and indicators are currently available to address the different dimensions of food environments and diet;
2) Which combinations of assessment methods and indicators should stakeholders use to answer urgent questions on diets and nutrition; 
3) How can considerations of resources such as time and money be incorporated into that decision-making process; 
4)  What insights can be provided into design, adaptation, and implementation considerations for assessment and measurement in urban and peri urban contexts. 

The tool will also serve to identify gaps and/or need for new tools, such as highlighting areas for testing or further data collection.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8969?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(13589, 'Development of a protocol for the isolation of protoplasts from cassava (Manihot esculenta) from friable embryogenic callus', 'The use of protoplasts in gene editing has revolutionized crop improvement, as these are plant cells lacking a cell wall, facilitating the direct manipulation of their genetic material. This approach is particularly relevant for crops like cassava (Manihot esculenta), a staple food in tropical and subtropical regions that faces significant challenges, including susceptibility to diseases and the presence of toxic compounds in its tissues. This protocol is drafted such that it does not only optimizes the conditions necessary for successful protoplast regeneration but also enhances the efficiency of the gene-editing process, enabling the faster and more reliable production of modified plants.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13589?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(9527, 'Improving the the Agro-Climatic Bulletin (ACB) provided to farmers using human-centered design methods in the Vietnam Mekong Delta', 'The use of the Human-Centered Design (HCD) approach improved the Agro-Climatic Bulletin (ACB), providing better quality, and consistency of advisories, and accessibility to farmers. This process focused on farmers for their practical use, and the Technical Working Groups from the district and provincial government for their development and dissemination of the ACB. The improvements include (1) an enhanced ACB template, in which practice cards are based on historical data, with expert inputs replacing manual recommendations; (2) a user-friendly design featuring icons, pictures, and a more engaging design and color scheme; and (3) supplementary training materials with new videos and guides integrated as QR codes within the ACB practice cards. By using separate practice cards, advisories can be tracked over time and across different locations, providing opportunities for further analysis, and improvement, and as a step towards ACB digitalisation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9527?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(18105, 'Vision-to-Action (V2A) transformation map to facilitate participatory visioning and monitoring & evaluation processes.', 'The V2A Transition Pathway/Transformation Map supports three activities: (1) Facilitating the V2A process and workshops, (2) Developing and implementing participatory MEL, and (3) Sharing results with participants and other audiences. While visions, behavior changes, and actions are context-specific, the map provides a uniform way to capture V2A outcomes. 

The map includes the V2A Vision in the upper right corner, with behavior change domains (or “rays”) flowing towards the vision, intersected by time “rings” that sequence actions. Behavior change “notes” detail required changes, which can be coded and expanded in an Action Plan template.

This map helps identify MEL indicators in V2A step 5. If done separately, data is entered into a digital template for customized maps and follow-up activities. The illustrated map also supports later MEL sessions, offering a clear framework for planning and tracking progress.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18105?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(273, 'Video-based Product Concept Testing (VPCT) tool for market segmentation of maize seeds', 'The Video-based Product Concept Testing (VPCT) tool is a novel participatory research method, which can be used to gather insights and preferences to inform future market segments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/273?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6338, 'Vulnerability assessment framework to identify agricultural livelihood hotspots in Asian Mega Deltas region', 'The vulnerability assessment (VA) framework is an approach used in mapping hotspot agricultural livelihoods of selected crops in the Asian Mega Deltas region. The framework was developed based on existing literature to identify the strengths and limitations of various VA approaches. Based on the developed framework, vulnerability is a function of three components: exposure, sensitivity, and adaptive capacity. Each of the three components is represented by a set of diverse indicators which is spatially aggregated into a shapefile to define a location-specific resolution as the unit of analysis. It is now currently being used by CG researchers to conduct their studies in uncontrolled environments and in designing their research projects. The framework is now integrated for adaptive capacity mapping innovation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6338?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(18566, 'Water-Energy-Food Nexus Decision Support System: A Sustainability and Resilience tool for India', 'The Water-Energy-Food Nexus Decision Support System (WEF Nexus DSS) is an advanced framework for addressing challenges in the WEF nexus, designed for policymakers, development practitioners, researchers, and other stakeholders involved in sustainable resource management. It integrates modelling, data analytics, and scenario-based assessments to support decision-making. Key innovations include surface and groundwater management integration, district-level typological mapping for irrigation planning (e.g., Ganges District Typologies), and tools like the WEF Trade-off Index (WEFTI) to assess sectoral trade-offs and synergies. The framework emphasizes policy convergence to enhance coherence, balance trade-offs, and maximize synergies. This approach enables national and local authorities, institutions, and researchers to make informed decisions, promoting resilience and sustainability in interconnected systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18566?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4089, 'Wheat Blast Early Warning System model for supporting farmers and enabling timely decision-making in Zambia and Bangladesh', 'The wheat blast caused by a fungal pathogen, Magnaporthe oryzae Triticum (MoT) pathotype, was detected in Bangladesh for the first time in 2016 and 2019 in Zambia. The disease is expanding in these countries and threatening to spread to neighboring regions. An early warning system for the wheat blast is very important for informing farmers about the blast risk so that management actions like fungicide application can be done in a timely manner. The wheat blast early warning system has been developed in Bangladesh, and the prototype is validated for its suitability for early warning advisory to farmers for effective management of the disease. The same model will be validated in Zambia and promoted in both countries to benefit more farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4089?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(8802, 'Wheat Blast Early Warning System (EWS) for Bangladesh', 'The wheat blast early warning system is fully functional in Bangladesh, and advisory messages are sent to extension agents and farmers via email and other channels during the critical wheat growing period (January to April 4), alerting them to significant infection risks for selected locations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8802?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7418, 'The Women''s Empowerment in Migration Index (WEMI) helps governments, development partners and practitioners understand sources of and dynamics of disempowerment of women migrants.', 'The Women''s Empowerment in Migration Index (WEMI) measures the multiple dimensions of agency, resources and achievements of female migrants. It builds on CGIAR''s large body of work in developing women''s empowerment metrics. The WEMI tries to unpack empowerment along the entire migration pathway looking at women''s decision making about migration and work, control over assets and income, attitude towards employer violence, access to social networks, access to training and various aspects of self-efficacy and mental health as a migrant.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7418?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(6795, 'R workflows to manage and analyze data for yield gap decomposition by data scientists and analysts.', 'The workflow outlines the processes and step-by step guide to manage and analyze data for yield gap decomposition by data scientists and analysts. It also includes concepts and definitions of yield levels, yield gap and yield gap decomposition and describes the analytical models for yield gap decomposition. Three main data-driven methods have been used for yield gap decomposition in agronomy research: (a) boundary line analysis, (b) stochastic frontier analysis, and (c) machine learning methods. The material documents how to implement these three methods in R are provided with reproducible examples. The R workflows have been used in analyzing data from surveys done by Use Cases in the Excellence in Agronomy to understand the factors causing yield gaps and to prioritize the development of agronomy solutions to close the yield gaps.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6795?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(15964, 'New rice varieties released in East and Southern Africa', 'There were market-demanded varieties released through the national systems in East and Southern Africa (ESA): 11 varieties released in Burundi, 4 varieties in Kenya, 1 in Tanzania, and 4 in Zambia. These 20 varieties have advantages of agronomic traits like early maturity, salinity (salt) tolerance, lodging resistance, tolerance to drought, tolerance to Resistance to rice yellow mottle virus (RYMV), leaf spot and blast, and high tillering ability.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15964?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3301, 'Joint IITA, CIP, Bioversity and Nigeria national partner design of seed demand and seed delivery tools for vegetatively propagated crop seed in geo-targeted regions of southern Nigeria', 'These are a series of tools/survey instruments which are designed to characterize seed demand for vegetatively propagated crops (VPCs) in two southern states of Nigeria (Oyo and Cross River). The tools aim to collect information for major target VPCs, including cassava, banana, sweetpotato, potato and yam and to serve as instruments that could potentially be used on a wider scale to assess seed demand characteristics more broadly at country level. The pilot level intruments developed during 2022 were for: 1. Characterizing the VPC seed demand and sales of traders in major markets in the target states; 2. Assessing the production capabilities and institutional characteristics of seed production units in a range of national partners; and 3. Determining the seed needs of product profiled varieties of VPC crops at selected sites in the targeted regions of southern Nigeria.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3301?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(7689, 'Improving Product Advancement Meetings that advance varieties from idea to production within 7 CGIAR Center.', 'This activity examines product advancement processes across seven CGIAR centers and their national partners. The study assessed how agricultural products progress from breeding to market launch through interviews, standardized questionnaires, and observations of product advancement meetings. The evaluation covered diverse crop programs: CIP''s potato breeding, IITA''s soybean development, rice improvement at both IRRI and AfricaRice, CIMMYT''s sorghum program, ICRISAT''s groundnut breeding, and common bean advancement at the Alliance of Bioversity International and CIAT.

Key findings revealed varying adoption levels of the One CGIAR stage-gate process, highlighting the importance of partnerships, market segmentation, and Target Product Profiles. Challenges in licensing, seed production, and commercialization planning were identified. Recommendations focus on defining core activities, clarifying partner roles, implementing market-segment-focused breeding, and formalizing product advancement meetings to enhance efficiency across the CGIAR system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7689?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(15685, 'Agroecological Business Model for the Dairy Value Chain of Bobo-Dioulasso (Burkina Faso)', 'This AgroEcological Business Model (AE BM) integrates and articulates three agroecological business models of upstream actors of the Bobo-Dioulasso dairy value chain: AE BM of dairy farmers, AE BM of milk collectors and AE BM of dairy processors. Each BM integrates agroecological technologies into their value proposition such as: agriculture-livestock synergies and recycling among farmers; economic diversification of services provided to farmers and processors among collectors; economic diversification and the promotion of local food traditions among dairy processors. The overall AE BM integrates agroecological principles such as fair trade, connectivity, and participation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15685?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5708, 'Crop diversification: An analytical framework for understanding the political economy of policymaking', 'This analytical framework was developed by the author to facilitate an understanding of the political economy of policies for crop diversification in Bangladesh. It seeks to unravel the intricate connections linking economic processes, political policies, and social institutions. The analytical framework presented here is a practical tool by which to analyze the political economy of agricultural policymaking.  It presents the processes, methods, tools, and techniques followed by the various actors in policymaking, to aid the reader’s understanding of, specifically, the political economy of policies pertaining to crop diversification. It will help us understand the relevant policies, programs, projects, resource use, impacts, scale, good practices, and learnings from specific initiatives. The results of such an exercise may help steer the focus of future policy and development in the agriculture sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5708?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6890, 'Lime profitability calculator is an app developed to evaluate the profitability of agricultural lime applications for maize, given empirical response functions in East Africa (Ethiopia, Rwanda and Tanzania)', 'This app allows users to evaluate the profitability of agricultural lime applications for maize, given empirical response functions in East Africa (Ethiopia, Rwanda and Tanzania). It is intended to enable systematic exploration of how yield gains translate to profitability for farmers, under different assumptions that the user may make.

The lime profitability calculator helps farmers by indicating the yield that can be produced with the application of a certain amount of lime, including the net revenue, marginal variable cost of revenue and average variable cost of revenue due to lime application', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6890?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2532, 'Guidelines for best management practices (BMPs) in Nile tilapia culture, and a curriculum for promoting commercial aquaculture in Nigeria', 'This comprehensive tilapia biosecurity manual is an integral component of the initiative to establish a robust GIFT breeding population in partnership with the largest tilapia breeder and producer in Nigeria to disseminate fry and fingerlings for hatchery production and grow-out across Nigeria. These manuals provide crucial guidelines and protocols for ensuring the health and biosecurity of tilapia hatchery and grow out operations. It is expected to provide instrumental in guiding fish farmers across Nigeria to protect their stocks from diseases. This, in turn, will enhance yields and ensure the long-term success of their aquaculture endeavors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2532?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4985, 'Exploring Gender Dimensions in the Use and Resistance of Antimicrobials in Agricultural Policy: A Conceptual Framework for Understanding How Antimicrobial Use and Resistance Transmission Affects Different Genders in Agriculture', 'This conceptual framework and guiding questions are developed to serve as a starting point for gender-sensitive analysis and selection of gender-specific and gender-transformative policies targeting Antimicrobial Use and Antimicrobial Resistance and how that affects different gender in agricultural settings.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4985?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(13497, 'Online training course on AKILIMO: Trainer''s Course on Cassava Agronomy', 'This course addresses cassava productivity challenges in smallholder farming. It equips learners to train extension workers and growers in AKILIMO-based agronomic interventions, covering good practices, disease management, and digital advisory services.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13497?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13748, 'Online training course on Promoting the Adoption of Conservation Agriculture to Enhance the Resilience of Cereals-Based Systems for researchers and extension staff', 'This course aims to facilitate the adoption of conservation agriculture and the promotion of its principles to improve crop productivity and soil health in cereals-based systems. Researcher and extension staff will gain insights into Conservation Agriculture (CA) practices in drylands, with a particular focus on the three principles of direct seeding and their profound impact on crop productivity and soil health. Moreover, the course will cover essential topics necessary for successfully transitioning to CA, equipping participants with the necessary skills and knowledge. By providing shared knowledge and presenting real-world data from CA use cases in Morocco and other regions, this course will enhance participants'' capacity in implementing conservation agriculture practices effectively.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13748?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13745, 'Online training course on mechanized and direct seeded Rice for agricultural professionals to enhance cost effectiveness and reduced labour in theory and practice globally', 'This course empowers agricultural professionals to promote mechanized and precise Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) practices, emphasizing cost-effectiveness and reduced labor, through theoretical learning and practical training.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13745?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13510, 'Online training course on decision-making in a changing climate for extension staff and field personnel', 'This course equips field personnel and extension staff  with the necessary knowledge and skills to effectively address the challenges posed by climate change in perennial tropical cropping systems such as cocoa, coffee, and tea. By enhancing their understanding of climate impacts and enabling them to utilize climate data within a comprehensive framework, this course aims to empower the learners to prioritize and implement adaptation strategies that can mitigate climate risks and foster resilience in cocoa, coffee, and tea production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13510?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(15976, 'Capacity development innovation on gender transformative approaches in agrifood systems: a course in four modules', 'This course has been developed by IITA through the Alliances Module of the CGIAR GENDER Impact Platform and in collaboration with the CGIAR Initiative on Gender Equality. The are four modules that can be used for capacity strengthening of researchers, practitioners and policy makers in the agrifood sectors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15976?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(13726, 'Online training course on Developing Representative Sampling Frames for Multi-Location On-Farm Research', 'This course helps researches  develop representative sampling frames for efficient agricultural research, considering both bio-physical and socioeconomic factors, enhancing research effectiveness.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13726?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13501, 'Online training course on Human Centered Design: Where People Power Innovation for Exceptional Impact', 'This course is an introduction to integrating Human-Centered Design (HCD) into a project. The course modules will show the steps involved in planning for research, ideation, and testing that puts the innovation users at the center  of innovation development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13501?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13722, 'Online training course for researchers on Data Collection Tools, Data Quality Control, and Methods and Tools for Decomposition of Yield Gaps', 'This course teaches trainees to design data collection plans, collect data, and analyze yield gaps in agriculture using R and Python, fostering improved crop yields.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13722?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(13493, 'Gender transformative Online training course on inclusive agronomic solutions to enhance productivity and empower rural communities globally"', 'This course will help  researchers and extension personnel gain the necessary knowledge and skills to deliver agronomic solutions that cater to the unique requirements of rural women and men, leading to enhanced productivity and remarkable advancements in the agricultural sector. By adopting gender transformative approaches, they will learn how to promote inclusivity and empowerment, ensuring that individuals of all genders and ages thrive in agricultural practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13493?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13512, 'Online training course for researchers on Introduction to Randomized Control Trial (RCT) for Impact Assessment', 'This course, developed for researchers,  explores scaling agricultural innovations and the role of Randomized Control Trials (RCTs) in impact assessment, offering participants a deep understanding and practical skills in using RCTs for innovation development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13512?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(5839, 'CIMMYT supports the development of a ground water dashboard in Nepal to enhance decision-making to providing a centralized, accessible platform for real-time data collection, storage, and analysis of groundwater resources', 'This dashboard aims to contribute to Nepal’s capacity to build a digital groundwater monitoring system through a small pilot project in Nepal’s Tarai region. This website is developed as a product in partnership with CSISA-Resilience project and Nepal’s Groundwater Resources Development Board. The dashboard reports the groundwater trends year after year, the Nepal Government/Department of Water Resources and Irrigation (DWRI) has deployed its resources to collect data on ground water level and real time data is projected in the dashboard. Work going on to transfer the dashboard to the DWRI/Nepal Government''s system, so that they can operate the dashboard without CIMMYT''s assistance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5839?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(18125, 'Decision-support tool for identification of conservation and restoration hotspots for native Asian tree species', 'This decision-support tool helps to identify priority areas for the conservation and restoration of native Asian tree species: 
a)	Areas where most natural habitats have been converted to other land uses, but which are predicted to remain suitable for the target tree species under future climate conditions. These areas are suitable for forest and landscape restoration with the target tree species.
b)	Areas where human threats to the target tree species remain limited and large and relatively undisturbed populations are predicted to occur. These areas are suitable for expanding in situ conservation through protected areas and other effective area-based conservation measures (OECMs), and as quality seed sources for restoration.
c)	Areas which are predicted to become unsuitable for the target species in a changing climate. These areas should be prioritized for germplasm collections, to conserve the species’ genetic diversity ex situ. 
The tool includes information of >200 tree species across South, East and Southeast Asia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18125?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(3336, 'Decision Support System (DSS) for policy makers to assess and mitigate health risks from microbiological water pollution in watersheds', 'This DSS will use watershed modelling and monitoring to analyse loads, fate, transport and human exposure to microbiological pollutants (including zoonotic pathogens) via water flow pathways. Such DSS will allow policy makers to assess risks from microbial pollution in watersheds, and to test ex-ante the cost-effectiveness of solutions (e.g. improved treatment of livestock waste) to manage and mitigate health risks. So far the watersheds monitoring plans have been formulated for Hindon (India) and Akaki (Ethiopia) and first water samples collected. Modelling conceptualization has started.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3336?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(12622, 'Development of a Machine Learning Model for Predicting Rice Blast Disease', 'This early innovation presents a novel approach to assessing Rice Blast (Pyricularia oryzae) severity in rice plants through machine learning and remote sensing. Using a visual scale from 0 to 9 to gauge infection severity, the research collected data from nine trials conducted between 2022 and 2024 at CIAT in Palmira, Colombia. These trials included various rice genotypes and control varieties to benchmark resistance levels. Drones equipped with RGB and multispectral cameras gathered data, later processed with Agisoft Metashape and Phenoi software to extract vegetation indices. Machine learning models were then developed using PyCaret and Scikit-learn, with datasets balanced through oversampling and undersampling techniques. The Random Forest classifier performed best, achieving up to 0.98 accuracy for binary classification (resistant vs. susceptible) and 0.91 accuracy for multiclass classification. This innovation underscores the potential of integrating machine learning and remote sensing for accurate disease monitoring in agriculture focusing on rice diseases.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12622?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(4220, 'Framework to assess the potential of social protection programs to support women''s and girls'' improved responses to climate change in low- and middle-income countries', 'This framework conceptualizes how social protection can shape the effect of climate change on women and girls’ well-being. The conceptual framework highlights a set of pathways whereby social safety nets, by building household and women’s resilience capacities, can improve how women, households, and communities respond to climate change. This framework will be used to summarize the existing evidence on how social protection leads to improved and inclusive responses to climate change, and where knowledge gaps may exist.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4220?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(4629, 'A guide to developing quantitative tools for measuring gender norms in agrifood systems', 'This Guide is intended for researchers and practitioners who wish to better understand and transform gender norms as the root cause of gender inequalities and women’s disempowerment in agrifood systems (AFSs). Understanding the impact of gender transformative interventions, as well as the links between gender norms and other AFS outcomes, requires high-quality data and measurement tools designed to measure changes in gender norms. Yet, prior to the development of this Guide, there was no existing, ready-to-use tool to measure and track gender norms specific to AFSs. To address this gap, the CGIAR Gender Equality Initiative developed this Guide, which contains step-by-step guidelines and templates to aid users in the participatory development of measures of gender norms in AFS and, where applicable, draws on empirical examples based on piloting of the Guide in Nigeria and Tanzania.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4629?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8985, 'Comprehensive assessment of salt and water dynamics in polder systems through integrated water modelling approach for advancing water resource sustainability and climate resilience in the Ganges Delta', 'This innovation aims to conduct an in-depth analysis of salt and water dynamics within the 34/2p polder system. The approach combines an integrated modeling approach on surface and groundwater interaction modeling with hydrochemical modeling to understand the factors influencing water and salinity dynamics. This comprehensive assessment will shed light on the area''s unique environmental and hydrological conditions to gain insights into the factors that impact water and salinity dynamics within the specified polders. 

The model, which is now being adopted by CG researchers, will utilize available data on irrigation conditions, groundwater levels and quality to serve as the basis for model calibration and validation, and conducting further scenario analyses. It is designed to creating scenarios and implementing sustainable management strategies to evaluate the effect of different interventions aimed at enhancing agricultural productivity. Governments, local agriculture and water authorities, and researchers are expected to benefit from the results and recommended strategies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8985?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8963, 'Mycotoxin control: Auto CO2-generated anti-rodent hermetic storage cocoons equipped with solar powered Ecowise® monitoring system for small scale farmers and processors.', 'This innovation aims to control insect pest, rodents and mycotoxin in stored cereals. Storage of crops in a non-hermetic system in the tropics not only favors mycotoxin contamination but also leads to insect infestation, rodent attack, and loss in quality. These results in both quantitative and qualitative losses which negatively affects incomes and livelihoods. Smallholder women farmers and processors who store their harvest and market weekly to sustain their lives are the most affected. The objective of the reported innovation was to test an auto-generated CO2-anti-rodent-hermetic storage cocoons equipped with solar powered Ecowise® monitoring system for small scale farmers and processors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8963?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(3155, 'Suitability tool for scaling aquaculture in small reservoirs using geospatial analysis in northern Ghana', 'This innovation aims to map the potential for the introduction of aquaculture in small reservoirs using geospatial analysis in northern Ghana. The suitability mapping combines open access GoogleEarth-engine based tool for semi-automated mapping surface areas of small reservoirs using Sentinel-2 imagery with secondary data sets to characterize small reservoirs on, for example, landscape position, erosion risk (land use, slopes), distance to roads, markets and settlements, etc. The tool will be accessible in open access online so that small reservoirs can be queried to assess their suitability for aquaculture development. A Training of the Trainers approach will also be followed to transfer the ability to use the suitability tool to local authorities, officers from the Ministry of Fisheries and Aquaculture Development and from the Fisheries Commission.
The suitability tool is now finalized, and its model has been validated by future users.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3155?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6707, 'Using typologies to target private sector investments in gender equality and social inclusion in agrifood supply chains: Methodology and good practices', 'This innovation aims to support agrifood companies, Voluntary Sustainability Systems (VSS), and other actors involved in downstream nodes or supportive functions of agrifood supply chains and markets (e.g., in trade, research, policy and programming) to enhance gender equality and social inclusion in their supply chains. The innovation is a toolkit designed to help: 1) understand the diversity of actors, with an emphasis on women, involved in upstream nodes of a given supply chain, 2) assess the differentiated benefits, contributions, and barriers these diverse groups of women (and men) experience within their suuply chains, and 3) identify entry points for improved targeting and tailoring of value chain initiatives to improve the terms of participation for different groups of women and promote gender equality within agrifood supply chains. This innovation is a collaborative effort between the CGIAR Initiatives on Gender Equality and Rethinking Food Markets.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6707?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(9337, 'Decision Support Tool for Sustainable Aquaculture Development for key stakeholders that can support farmers in reducing risks in aquaculture development in Myanmar', 'This innovation aims to support informed decision-making to support profitable and sustainable aquaculture development, reduce hydrological and climatic risks to fishponds, and guide value chain actors to target their investments. 
The goals are to reduce poverty and improve food and nutritional security. The decision support tool is based on four types of indicators: present and future climate hazards, aquaculture requirements, biophysical characteristics and socio-economic characteristics.  The tool is being developed for Myanmar at the township level. After validation with stakeholders, a user-friendly online web-based interface will be developed to disseminate the innovation to its end-users. In parallel, training will be organized for NGOs, community-based organizations, and private sector actors who can then support farmers in reducing risks in aquaculture development.

In 2024, the Aqua-DST has been finalized through consultations, users were trained and finally the tool is available open access.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9337?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(8719, 'Starter kit for enhancing gender equality through Voluntary Sustainability Systems (VSS)', 'This innovation aims to support Voluntary Sustainability Systems (VSS) supporting the development of agrifood value chains and markets to enhance gender equality and social inclusion in value chains. The innovation is a toolkit designed to help integrate gender in: 1) VSS organizations, 2) their standards, and 3) their programming. The innovation draws on a range of outputs (audio-visual, briefs, papers, guidance materials, etc.) developed under the CGIAR Initiative on Gender Equality as well as other relevant outputs.  See related podcast on "Tackling gender inequalities: how to get started in your scheme" here: https://www.isealalliance.org/get-involved/resources/podcast-tackling-gender-inequalities-how-get-started-your-scheme, and blog, “Where to start with developing a gender strategy?”  https://www.isealalliance.org/sustainability-news/where-start-developing-gender-strategy, which points out, “ISEAL and the CGIAR are developing materials to support sustainability systems in developing their gender policies and strategies.”', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8719?phase=3', 0, NULL),
(3417, 'Incentives for adopting best practices in food safety to improve food hygiene and to reduce of zoonotic pathogens exposure in Kenya', 'This innovation assesses the impact of incentives for good hygiene behavior at upstream value chain nodes by integrating a slaughterhouse hygiene inspection into the regular duties of meat inspectors, who assess hygiene standards. Slaughterhouse workers receive performance-based bonuses linked to these inspections, encouraging compliance. Slaughterhouse owners and regulatory authorities oversee implementation, ensuring alignment with food safety regulations and public health goals.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3417?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16397, 'Mechanization packages, composed of two-wheel tractor, trailer, reaper, chopper grinder, mower, bailer and basin digger, for agroecology transformation in Zimbabwe', 'This innovation bundle consists of machinery such as a two-wheel tractor, trailer, reaper, chopper grinder, mower, bailer and basin digger. The package helps reduce the drudgery associated with agroecology transition for smallholder farmers who rely mainly on household labor. The Agroecology Initiative provided (financed) this bundle to eight service providers operating within the communities where the Agroecology Living Landscapes (ALL) have been established. ALL farmers have been testing the efficiency of this bundle at reducing the drudgery of technology adoption associated with agroecology transition.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16397?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(8919, 'Protocol for the Inspection and Certification of Quality Declared Seed (QDS) to be used by QDS producers themselves to self-declare the quality of their seed', 'This innovation comprises a methodology that will enable seed producers of vegetatively propagated crop to self-declare the quality of their seed. The protocol will initially be tested with a group of 20 quality declared seed (QDS) producers of cassava in Tanzania. Seed producers will make use of digitial tools, including Seed Tracker and PlantVillage Nuru to help them to self-assess, and results will be sent electronically in real-time to the regulatory authority - the Tanzania Official Seed Certification Institute (TOSCI). Gender balance will be taken into account when selecting seed producers to participate in the experiment and gender will also be considered in the analysis of results.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8919?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(20338, 'Discovery of Novel Sources of Resistance to Sweetpotato Leaf Curl Virus (SPLCV) in Cultivated and Wild Sweetpotato Relatives', 'This innovation contributes to breeding virus-resistant sweet potato varieties to improve crop resilience and yield. The focus is to identify novel sources of resistance to Begom virus (Sweet Potato Leaf Curl Virus - SPLCV) in sweet potato and its wild relatives. It involves co-infecting test genotypes with SPLCV and Sweet Potato Chlorotic Stunt Virus (SPCSV) to assess disease response.  Inoculum has been prepared and used to evaluate the response of 75 sweet potato genotypes inoculated using a double grafting method to ensure successful infection. 26 genotypes have showed resistance (score ≤3), t', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20338?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(126, 'A disaggregated water-energy-food systems model to study the economywide effects of water and energy interventions in the face of climate change.', 'This innovation contributes to the discussion on water-energy-food nexus that has remained at the forefront of development policymaking. Understanding the nexus between water, energy, and food security requires a well-customized model that traces the supply and use of water, energy, and land resources and their spatial allocation. The two-stage production nesting structure in the standard IFPRI Computable General Equilibrium (CGE) model is extended to a seven-stage nesting structure where various types of water (surface and groundwater) and various types of energy (hydro and non-hydro) are explicitly combined with irrigated land such that the close substitutability and complementarity between water, energy, and land are well captured. A disaggregated Social Accounting Matrix (SAM) that represents the agro-ecological specificity of agriculture and resource distribution (irrigation) is generated. The model and data are used to analyze the economywide effects of water and energy interventions in the face of climate change-induced weather variability. In 2024, the model was used to assess how agri-food system investments priorities in Ethiopia could change under climatic and world price risks. To analyze this, additional outcome indicators - including changes in poverty and diet quality – were introduced.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/126?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(16398, 'Integrated Agroecological Knowledge: Empowering Farmers in Zimbabwe Through Seed Fairs, Field Days, and Farmer Field Schools', 'This innovation development integrates seed fairs, field days, and farmer field schools into a dynamic framework promoting sustainable agriculture. Seed fairs celebrate agricultural heritage, offering farmers access to diverse seeds while fostering community engagement and partnerships. Field days demonstrate agroecological practices, providing hands-on learning and exposure to tools, techniques, and technologies in real-world settings. Farmer field schools equip stakeholders with skills in soil health, ecosystem restoration, and climate adaptation, fostering collaboration for resilient landscapes. Together, these activities create interactive platforms for knowledge exchange, innovation, and sustainable farming, emphasizing agroecology''s role in economic and social development. The bundle strengthens local seed systems, highlights sustainable practices, and encourages partnerships among farmers, agribusinesses, and organizations, driving agroecological transitions, enhancing food security, and building climate-resilient landscapes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16398?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(5328, 'Analytical approach for researchers to conduct mixed-method multi-country assessment of how social protection shapes gendered impacts of climate change', 'This innovation develops an analytical approach for researchers to pair quantitative and qualitative research in multiple countries, to assess how social protection shapes the gendered impacts of climate change. The quantitative approach entails merging household survey data from randomized controlled trials on social protection interventions with weather data from satellite data or remote sensing. Doing so allows estimating the effects of climate hazards on gendered outcomes, and assessing how those effects change when climate hazards overlap with receipt of social protection. The complementary qualitative approach entails interviewing women and men in the same setting on how they perceive climate change and social protection’s role in helping them adapt, and what changes to social protection could better support them. The combined approach allows researchers to assess what impacts social protection has on gendered impacts of climate change, how and why patterns vary by context, and how to better design social protection.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5328?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5331, 'Evidence-based guidance on effective social protection design for implementers to promote women''s resilience to risk', 'This innovation draws on impact evaluations testing different social protection designs for implementers to promote women’s resilience to risk, in order to provide guidance on which designs are effective in different contexts in the short and longer term. Specific examples of designs tested include varying social protection transfer modalities between cash or in-kind, and adding complementary agricultural extension, nutrition training, gender sensitization, or livelihoods components.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5331?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8515, 'Mainstreaming the Implementation of Genomics-Supported Recurrent Selection Schemes for Enhanced Cassava Breeding and Genetic Gain in CGIAR-NARS Networks to Empower Small-Holder Farmers', 'This innovation encompasses the strategic integration of genomics-supported recurrent selection (GS-RSS) into cassava breeding programs within CGIAR-NARS networks. It aims to expedite the breeding cycle  (traditionally 5 to 7 years due to cassava''s 12 month growth period), by incorporating genomic selection methodologies. These methods will leverage the increasingly accessible and affordable genome-wide DNA markers to predict breeding values, enabling the identification and selection of superior genotypes at early testing stages. The innovation is designed to not only enhance the efficiency and accuracy of selection for desirable traits but also to ensure that the genetic gains translate into tangible benefits in farmers'' fields. By focusing on traits that address social and gender inequalities such as root quality in addition to yield, the initiative seeks to deliver varieties that are not just high-yielding but also meet the specific needs and preferences of diverse groups, thereby fostering inclusivity and equity in agricultural advancements.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8515?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(8983, 'Creation of the Salinity Forecasting System: A Framework for Advancing Water Resource Sustainability and Climate Resilience through Local-scale Hydrological Modeling in the Ganges Delta', 'This innovation entails the development of a salinity forecasting system for the 34/2p Khulna polder in the Ganges Delta, utilizing machine learning and deep learning techniques. The system will leverage readily available historical temporal datasets, including information on rainfall, groundwater level, groundwater quality, river salinity, river discharge, tidal height, land use/catchment characteristics, and water extraction patterns within the polder. These data, along with other pertinent co-variates, will serve as proxies to predict salinity levels. This predictive model aims to provide valuable insights to farmers, guiding them on the optimal timing to initiate their agricultural activities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8983?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(14096, 'Stage-gate process to improve efficacy of genome edited products', 'This innovation highlights the stage gate process setting up common decision making platform for all centers for all genome editing enabling factors like stewardship, regulatory, stakeholder engagement, communications, intellectual property, socio-economic and market analysis among others. It aims to provide detailed guidance through gateway decision points to improve efficacy of genome edited products and attain efficient operations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14096?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(8986, 'Development of Crop Water Usage Metrics in the Vietnam Mekong Delta: A Framework for Advancing Water Resource Sustainability and Climate Resilience through Local-Scale Hydrological Modeling in the Mekong Delta', 'This innovation involves developing precise metrics for actual evapotranspiration on a monthly and seasonal basis for specific irrigation schemes in the Vietnam Mekong Delta. The goal is to enhance water productivity in intensively irrigated systems by developing a satellite-based crop water use of specific irrigation schemes. 

The model will primarily use Landsat 8 and 9 satellite data, in addition to weather and soil hydraulic properties to provide the model will provide estimates of land and water productivity indicators for every 30 x 30 meter land area on days when Landsat satellite data are available. The model is now being adopted researchers at the Initiative level. On-ground validation of data will be pursued by the Initiative Team and a group of researchers. 

By accurately measuring and managing crop water use, this activity will provide the data-driven foundation for water management strategies of government agencies and local water authorities in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8986?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(5311, 'Conservation of tropical indigenous pig Suidae genetic resources using the induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSC) derived from somatic cells', 'This innovation is a new/adapted technology for conservation of Suidae (pig) genetic resources in-vitro based on a stem cell approach.The technology relies of harvesting of somatic cells (such as from an animal’s body), which are then bought back to undifferentiated stem cells, also called induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs), in the laboratory. The iPSCs can be stored in-vitro (under liquid nitrogen) and later used to produce any cell type (undifferentiated stem cells are characterized by their ability to produce any cell type in the body). To date we have successfully demonstrated stable lines of local pig somatic cells in culture. The steps in progress include inducing pluripotency, characterizing and demonstrating their differentiation potential, and later using them in assisted reproductive technologies to revive the pig which cells were stored in liquid nitrogen. This technology will be used to conserve tropical pig genetic resources in Uganda, Vietnam, and potentially elsewhere.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5311?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(8958, 'Field-based assessment of the effectiveness of rust resistance genes under field conditions in Morocco and Lebanon', 'This innovation is about the validation and performance of rust resistance genes. The development of rust-resistant varieties depends on the presence of efficient rust-resistance genes, and the utilization of these genes in breeding programs relies on their effectiveness. Field assessments conducted under natural infection conditions provide valuable information regarding the efficacy of seedling resistance genes against various rust pathogens. Alongside assessing seedling resistance genes, evaluating sources containing adult-plant resistance genes can be considered during the adult-plant stage. The three wheat rust trap nurseries comprising the yellow, stem, and leaf rust resistance genes were planted in several locations in Morocco and Lebanon under natural conditions. The yellow and leaf rust trap nurseries were planted at Tel Amara in Lebanon and Allal Tazi in Morocco, respectively.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8958?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(17386, 'Empowering small-medium enterprises (SMEs) and Communities through Precooked and Dehydrated Bean Products: Enhancing Nutrition, Gender Equality, and Climate-Smart Solutions for Sustainable Growth and Social Impact', 'This innovation offers a sustainable solution to the energy and nutritional challenges of cooking beans. Traditional methods require up to 2.6 kg of firewood for 1 kg of dry beans, contributing to deforestation and greenhouse gas emissions (Kajumba et al., 2022). Beans also take 3-4 hours to cook, limiting their use (Munthali et al., 2022). By boiling beans and using steam or pressure cooking, this innovation preserves nutrients like protein, fiber, and essential micronutrients while reducing anti-nutritional factors. After cooking, beans are dehydrated to extend shelf life, reduce food waste, and improve food security. The precooked and dehydrated beans cut cooking time and energy use, benefiting households by saving time and reducing the burden on women and youth. This cost-effective solution supports SMEs, improves nutrition, saves resources, and mitigates environmental impacts, with potential for wide-scale adoption in urban areas, contributing to food security and sustainability.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17386?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7286, 'A satellite remote sensing-based near real-time monitoring of irrigated areas in the Limpopo River Basin', 'This innovation offers near real-time monitoring of irrigated areas across an entire transboundary river basin. The first version of the system utilized high-resolution Sentinel-2 (optical) satellite imagery data to identify these areas on a monthly basis. It makes use of both expert decision trees and machine learning techniques to identify irrigated regions. Until now, most mapping activities have focused on an annual timescale rather than a near real-time one. This represents a substantial improvement in irrigated area metrics, which directly impacts water availability.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7286?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(18322, 'Protocol for a Tier 2 approach to generate context-specific enteric methane emission factors for cattle production systems using IPCC method', 'This innovation provides a standardized protocol for estimating enteric methane emissions from cattle using a Tier 2 approach under the IPCC methodology. It guides users on data collection and calculation methods, incorporating key factors such as cattle productivity, diet composition, and management practices. By improving the accuracy of greenhouse gas inventories, the protocol supports evidence-based climate mitigation strategies and sustainable livestock development within CGIAR initiatives.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18322?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(18338, 'Protocol for a Tier 2 approach to generate context-specific enteric methane emission factors for small ruminants’ production systems based on IPCC method', 'This innovation provides a structured protocol for estimating enteric methane emissions from small ruminants using a Tier 2 approach under the IPCC methodology. It guides users on data collection and calculation methods, incorporating key factors such as animal productivity, diet composition, and management practices. By improving the accuracy of greenhouse gas inventories, the protocol supports evidence-based climate mitigation strategies and sustainable livestock development within CGIAR initiatives.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18338?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5369, 'Guidance for researchers and development agencies working with women farmers to co-design and implement Socio-Technical Innovation Bundles (STIBs) for strengthening resilience of women farmers', 'This innovation provides guidance for co-designing and implementing context-specific socio-technical innovation bundles (STIBs) that could contribute to women''s empowerment and resilience through participatory processes like multi-stakeholder engagements. This step-by-step participatory co-designing process includes developing inclusive tools and procedures for situation analysis, rapid assessment, and stakeholder engagement for identifying, prioritising, and designing different combinations of STIBs that are implemented through current learning labs and can be rolled out in other projects and initiatives. It will also include indicators, tools, and outcome and impact assessment methods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5369?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(13495, 'Development and validation of quality assurance and quality control molecular markers for common bean', 'This innovation seeks to develop a set of DNA markers based on subtle differences in the DNA sequences of common bean varieties for the quick and accurate discrimination of common bean seeds that look similar when observed with the naked eye', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13495?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(2646, 'Smallholder farmer access to new farm mechanization services at scale in Bangladesh', 'This innovation supports enhanced smallholder access to farm machinery services for new machinery types by supporting affordable fee-for-services provision markets in rural Bangladesh. The initiative and CSISA-MEA worked to facilitate demand-creation events that included traditional demonstrations and farmers'' meetings as well as roadshows and local television advertising. The roadshows and TV advertising were particularly valuable tools for reaching large numbers of farmers in remote areas. Fodder choppers have also been a type of machine that has been strongly promoted by the Activity, both through support to agriculturally based light engineering firms and dealers to market fodder choppers. As a result, the sale of 145 combined harvesters (USD 2,303,267), one mini combine harvester (USD 5293), two Power Tiller-Operated Seeders (PTOSs) (USD 1873), 26 reapers (USD 22,102), 16 rice transplanters (USD 23,262) and 26 fodder choppers (USD 8535) were made during last reporting year.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2646?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(372, 'A methodology for assessing the anticipated return on investment and likelihood of success of CGIAR Trait Discovery & Deployment Projects, as a mechanism to orient upstream investments towards high-impact activities', 'This innovation underpins investment case standardization, implementing a dynamic pipeline developed in 2023 to establish pilot estimates of trait valuation. The approach looks at trait mapping across market segments, leveraging trait experts’ estimation of ultimate benefit of their work to yield benefit and stability. Based on productivity impacts across market segments, additional yield and resulting monetary value are calculated. The framework was applied to 2023 mapped Trait Discovery & Deployment (TD&D) project activities in eleven crops. The analysis highlights the value of investment case standardization and provides valuable input on the need to benchmark estimates in productivity gain, ranging for the same trait from 1% to 15% and 30% to 80% across some programs working on the same TD&D opportunity in the same crop. Leveraging information on investments and understanding the likelihood of success as we advance provides a robust foundation to estimate the relative merit of different TD&D opportunities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/372?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4731, 'Resilient and drought adapted forage cultivars and hybrids to improve productivity, income and livelihoods of households keeping small ruminants in Mali', 'This innovation will promote up to 10 resilient and drought adapted forage cultivars and hybrids to farmers, technicians and forage seed producers who will be trained on forage (seed) production, processing and utilisation. Nine resilient and drought-adapted cultivars and hybrids of Urochloa (Brachiaria) and Megathyrsus (Panicum) grasses together with one forage legume (Crotalaria) are evaluated along with local forages (as control) at multiple locations in Mali to identify the best options for different regions and livestock systems in the country. Researchers and farmers visiting the experimental plots at different growth stages assess agronomic performance, the impact on animal productivity and environment, and profitability. The Tropical Forages Selection Tool https://www.tropicalforages.info/text/intro/index.html may be used for selection of cultivars and hyrids.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4731?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(15005, 'A new era in agricultural training: Bangladesh launches pioneering combine harvester simulator', 'This innovative simulator revolutionizes operator training in Bangladesh by providing a safe, controlled environment for skill development before handling actual machinery. Through strategic collaboration among private sector leaders Metal Agritech Ltd., Abedin Equipments Ltd., and the CSISA-MEA, this technology aims to attract young entrepreneurs to the agricultural machinery sector, ensuring more efficient and safer agricultural operations across the country. This partnership with the private sector marks a key milestone in CSISA-MEA''s mission to promote sustainable innovation across Bangladesh. In alignment with government initiatives to boost the light engineering industry, CSISA-MEA has particularly focused on developing machine manufacturing skills among women and youth. The project actively promotes agricultural service businesses managed by these groups while providing comprehensive training support to machine marketing companies, dealers, mechanics, and operators.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15005?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(352, 'Harmonizing standards for breeding partnerships to enhance equitable and transparent approaches for collaborative germplasm development', 'This institutional innovation seeks to define common, high-level terms and partnership standards for inclusion in standard agreements that outline the benefits and commitments of belonging to a regional crop improvement network. Currently, rules of engagement differ vastly amongst centers and projects, and indeed are often not well described. If successfully adopted, network membership agreements will enhance equitable and transparent partnership approaches and collaboration through: the use of common tools and services; germplasm, information, and data sharing; acknowledging contributions; input and participation in network decision-making; the adoption of a culture of continuous improvement, and others. Draft terms were reviewed in 2023 by the maize, root and tuber, and dryland crops networks. Feedback was used to update the agreement template and make it customizable for individual network needs. In 2024, the African root-tuber-banana networks were the first to endorse the common terms. The agreement was signed by CIP, IITA and 16 NARES.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/352?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3160, 'Checklist for integration of key gender and social inclusion considerations in seed demand of vegetatively propagated crops', 'This is a checklist of key gender and social inclusion considerations that should be integrated into the seed demand tools of vegetatively propagated crops to inform a better understanding of how varied intersectional gender dimensions shape the adoption of improved seed varieties VPC seed systems. Insights from this checklist have been incorporated in the seed demand assessment studies focused on ‘seed and varietal profiling with an intersectional gender lens’ led by the International Potato Center (CIP) Uganda; and ‘consumer market analysis survey for VPC seed supply and demand in Tanzania’ led by Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI), Tanzania, all carried out in 2023. The checklist serves as a guide for engendering seed demand assessment tools.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3160?phase=3', 1, NULL),
(16532, 'Guidelines for African Union (AU)-Member States to engage informal sector towards food safety and food security', 'This is a set of guidelines that are jointly being developed between the resilient Cities initiative and the African Union-IBAR directed to Governments of AU-Member states. They build on lessons learnt from CGIAR research work for over a decade, and identify 3 main principles of government-informal sector engagement and a set of concrete actions for governments to take.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16532?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(12181, 'Mobile application to optimize feed formulations based on locally available feed resources for affordable quality feeds by smallholder poultry producers', 'This is a web-based free poultry feed formulation application based on locally based feed ingredients from different countries (currently Ethiopia, Kenya, Tanzania, Cambodia, Ghana, Nigeria). The App allows the users to meet the nutrient requirement of the chicken with composition of available feed resources and additional consideration on prices. This gives you the best-cost rations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12181?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(18062, 'BrRacemeCounter: Mask R-CNN for Raceme Instance Segmentation in Urchloa grasses', 'This is an adapted implementation of Mask R-CNN on Python 3, Keras, and TensorFlow. The model generates bounding boxes and segmentation masks for each instance of an object in the image. It''s based on Feature Pyramid Network (FPN) and a ResNet50 or ResNet101 backbone.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18062?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(12179, 'Mobile application for digital extension and advisory services (IDEAS) to support smallholder chicken farmers in poultry production and management', 'This is an interactive digital extension and advisory service app (IDEAS) to leverage various components of poultry production and provide inclusive support to smallholder poultry farmers. This App is primarily designed to support farmers in identification and access to chicken breeds available in each country, formulation and use of locally available feeds, disease prevention and treatment, market information, and linkages, creating a platform for discussion and knowledge sharing, and real-time advisory and support services. It is developed in various languages and comprises various visual, interactive, text, and video features that can deliver tailored services for smallholder poultry farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12179?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(1613, 'Standard Protocol to develop gender-intentional target product profile design', 'This is an update on the draft standards developed to define the novel inclusive process of building Target Product Profiles (TPPs) for crop-breeding to deliver improved varieties more efficiently with higher rates of adoption. This provides an end-to-end description of the process and steps required to develop a gender inclusive target product for each market segment identified at the CGIAR Sub region and NARES level. It also defines the key terms in a TPP and describes the relationship between a seed product market segment and a TPP. The draft standard also explains and defines the membership, roles and key responsibilities of the multi-disciplinary team required to develop a gender inclusive TPP and lastly, list all process owners and key contacts. This is used in the Product Development Team meetings.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1613?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(14718, 'Toolbox4seedsystems', 'This is the website hosting the toolbox for seed systems. This toolbox contains the description, user guides, and case studies of different tools to understand root, tuber and banana seed systems. This toolbox had two critical updates the first one was its new version in French. We also added a new tab to promote the use of the toolbox in countries experiencing humanitarian problems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14718?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(17319, 'Manual for the determination of dry cocoa bean yield by pod cut developed for researchers and extension agents', 'This manual containing the standard operating procedure (SOP), developed for researchers and extension staff,  covers the year-round harvest and direct dry bean determination and proposes indirect methods to estimate dry bean yield from observations that do not require repeated harvests', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17319?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17318, 'Manual for the determination of banana bunch, edible fruit pulp, edible dry matter and aboveground biomass yield by crop cut developed for researchers and extension agents', 'This manual describes the standard operating procedures in harvesting dessert banana and cooking banana, EAHB and any other banana type that produces over long periods through the mother plant and several ratoons. This manual was developed for use by researchers and extension agents who conduct agronomy related research on bananas', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17318?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(16434, 'Workflows for conducting spatial ex ante analysis of agronomic interventions', 'This manual provides workflows for conducting spatial ex ante economic analysis of agronomic interventions. We especially leveraged the classic text by Alston et al (1995) that summarized all the methods for evaluating agricultural research and development innovations. Our task was simply to organize the different methods that spawned after Alston et al (1995) as standard operating procedures (SOPs) —i.e., analytical procedures that can be reproducible and replicated in other contexts.  In this manual, researchers will learn how to conduct spatially explicit (mostly gridded) economic evaluation of agronomic innovations with R and other programming software including Octave, and python. Our purpose for these SOPs is to standardize the spatial ex ante economic analysis process with the aim of reducing the amount of time researchers spend on coding the common building blocks of spatial ex ante workflows and improving their efficiency.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16434?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(17322, 'Manual for the assessment of nutritional quality of grain crops developed for researchers and extension agents', 'This manual, developed for researchers and extension agents,  codifies the standard operating procedures for  produce sampling, preparation and analysis methodologies to enable valid
comparisons of the effects of crop varieties, sites, agronomic practices, and their interactions on nutritional quality.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17322?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(17691, 'The true costs of food in Kenya and Vietnam: A data dashboard (OA)', 'This Microsoft Power BI data dashboard allows users to explore key indicators related to the true costs of food production in Kenya and Vietnam. Users can navigate through the dashboard to identify the relative magnitude of environmental and social costs within the country''s agrifood systems, both overall and across various sectors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17691?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(9285, 'Defining harmonized indicators to understand and drive breeding modernization, improve organizational performance, and aid recognition of contributions to the germplasm development process by diverse research teams, individuals and partners', 'This organizational innovation defines a prioritized and harmonized shortlist of indicators, featuring the most impactful indicators for measuring breeding program performance and the impact of modernization efforts.  During 2023, 26 indicators were identified that feature key components of monitoring and decision making across the product development process. During 2024, eight center teams utilized the indicators to establish Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) in their respective centers and programs to track efficiency and performance throughout the product development process. At the same time, indicators that can be sourced from information systems were identified and system interoperability established to drive a dashboard and "a harmonized crop report". The dashboard and report include measures for relevance, partnerships, investment, development progress, scaling, and projected benefits. Conceptually, they are scalable across any CGIAR development pipeline. Indicators establish the success criteria of teams and processes. They provide opportunity to more precisely target improvement and generate transparency.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9285?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2647, 'Strategic financial services and business development for  Agriculture Based Light Engineering (ABLE) firms for farm machinery needed for agricultural intensification and diversification in Bangladesh', 'This output from Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia – Mechanization Extension Activity (CSISA-MEA) is associated with output 2.4 under work package two of Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA). The initiative and CSISA-MEA worked to  facilitate loans from nine financial services. That brought the total amount of finance accessed through the facilitation of links between Financial Service Institutions and ABLEs, dealers, and Machinery Service Provides. The activity facilitated loans from nine financial service institutions totaling USD 935,295 to 29 light engineering firms, two dealers, and 20 MSPs, 17 of whom were women. Most ABLEs  (73% to 80%) want to expand their business, all of which would like to do so by utilizing a bank loan. Most of those with bank loans did not face a challenge obtaining them.  However, approximately 27% to 29% did, with the most difficult challenges being lack of collateral, followed by inadequate documentation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2647?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2648, 'Facilitating business growth and financial access with major banks in Bangladesh to advance farm mechanization and diversification', 'This output from Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia – Mechanization Extension Activity (CSISA-MEA) is associated with output 2.4 under work package two of Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA). The initiative and CSISA-MEA worked to establish business linkage events for farm machinery manufacturers. Those events resulted in USD 363,529 disbursed to 12 Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA) (ABLEs) in Jashore, USD 90,588 to five ABLEs in Faridpur and USD 412,941 to 12 ABLEs in Bogura. Three dealers in Cox’s Bazar received loans facilitated by the activity from BRAC Bank, NRBC Bank, and microfinance institute ASA, worth USD 49,412. In addition to the loans to ABLEs, 14 Machinery Service Providers (MSP)s in the greater Jashore regions received loans totaling USD 6,472 and six MSPs in Cox’s Bazar received loans totaling USD 12,353, 17 were for female MSPs who received loans totaling USD 12,942.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2648?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2649, 'Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia – Mechanization Extension Activity in alignment with Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia support in digital marketing system using low-cost technology', 'This output from Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia – Mechanization Extension Activity (CSISA-MEA) is associated with output 2.4 under work package two of Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia. The initiative and CSISA-MEA worked to boost the same of crucial farm machinery 924 spare parts needed for intensification and diversification. These were mainly for irrigation pumps, threshers, fodder choppers, drive belt wheels, and engine liners and the sales were worth USD18,827. The activity also provided training to 75 Machinery Service Providers (MSP)s on financial tools and loans, with the result that iFarmer (a private company) was able to facilitate loans from Financial Service Institutions for 88 MSPs. These resulted in iFarmer facilitating loans from FSIs for 563 farmers, worth a total of USD 36,283 for the purchase of crop production and livestock rearing inputs, as well as agricultural machinery including 2WTs, PTOSs, choppers, irrigation pumps and power tiller engines.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2649?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2641, '194 Agriculture Based Light Engineering entities with staff training, technical advice, and access to finance stimulating innovation and design of new spare parts and farm machinery for diversification in Bangladesh.', 'This output from Cereal Systems Initiative in South Asia– Mechanization Extension Activity (CSISA-MEA) is associated with Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia. This innovation involves a specific bundling of multiple approaches to improve the performance of ABLEs, specifically by combining training, technical assistance, and access to financial services to enhance the domestic production of agricultural machinery spare parts needed for crop diversification efforts in Bangladesh. The Initiative and CSISA-MEA collaborated to develop manufacturing capacity and expand the business of targeted Agriculture Based Light Engineering (ABLE) firms in Bangladesh. 194 ABLEs signed consent letters agreeing to participate in training and technical support programs. Support for the ABLE to supply spare parts to lead firms for some of the newest technologies to enter the agricultural machinery market in Bangladesh including combine harvesters and rice transplanters resulted in only a small number of orders being placed by the agricultural machinery importer lead firms.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2641?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2770, 'Tools for estimating Demand and Supply of Vegetatively Propagated Crops Seed among Smallholder Farmers', 'This output seeks to provide information that will contribute towards a better understanding of seed demand estimation and varietal preferences for banana, cassava and sweetpotato at the Lake Zone Tanzania. A team comprising of CGIAR and national stakeholders is working towards addressing three key objectives: a) finetuning and integrating tools for demand estimation, (b) modeling and scenario building to improve linkages between seed demand and supply, (c) integrating gender considerations into seed demand characterization tools.

In 2024, data from an experimental auction to assess farmers'' willingness to purchase quality planting material for sweetpotato was analyzed and a draft report developed. 
Key findings include higher WTP Among Commercialized Farmers. Farmers in highly commercialized villages displayed significantly higher WTP for both improved and locally preferred varieties, particularly those sourced from trained multipliers. This suggests that commercialization enhances farmers’ investment in quality planting materials to maximize yield and marketability.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2770?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(14109, 'Reservoir Forecasting: Predictive tool that forecasts future reservoir levels based on various data inputs, helping in planning for potential water shortages or surpluses', 'This predictive tool uses hydrological models, remote sensing data, and machine learning algorithms to forecast future reservoir levels, helping water managers prepare for potential shortages or surpluses. By analyzing historical inflow and outflow patterns, raiting curve and water bodies areas from Digital Earth Africa in monthly basis.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14109?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5042, 'Innovative combination of cash and input loans for smallholder farmers, improving their access to land, labor, and inputs to boost their productivity', 'This product provides a supplemental cash loan bundled with an existing input loan provided to smallholder farmers. In the status quo, eligible farmers receive an input loan via our implementing partner. These input loans provide credit for specific farm inputs, which are often purchased during a season of low liquidity. Agricultural inputs, however, are not the only inputs required for the production of agricultural products. Labor, land, and other forms of capital must combine with agricultural inputs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5042?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(4372, 'Financial and insurance product, enabling particularly women smallholder farmers to obtain cattle for fattening and profitable resale', 'This product provides finance for cattle fattening through a profit-sharing model. Eligible smallholder farmers receive cows purchased by the company and then sell them back to the company at a guaranteed price or at a higher price they find in the market. Farmers keep 2/3 of the difference between the purchase and sale price, and the company keeps 1/3. This intervention is bundled with insurance to protect the farmer against cow death. Other possible services include subsidized high-quality feed and growth monitoring to increase the value of the cow. Women will be specifically targeted for contracts to increase their empowerment in a sphere where they are responsible for physical labor.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4372?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(14148, 'Optimizing Protocol for Protoplast Isolation and Transfection for Potato Shangi Variety', 'This protocol outlines the improved process for Protoplast Isolation and transfection for Potato Shangi Variety for genome editing efficacy.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14148?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(6811, 'R package to estimate lime requirements in acid soils in Sub-Saharan Africa by data scientists and analysts', 'This R package implements different lime requirement models identified through literature review and a new model developed by the authors as explained in Aramburu-Merlos et al. (2023, Geoderma). The R package contains the data and information related to soil pH and model to compute for lime requirements. The package is developed for EiA data scientists and analysts involved in predicting the amount of lime needed to reach a target change in the soil property, for example soil pH, to produce better crop yields in Sub-Saharan Africa', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6811?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6879, 'R functions on using causal random forest and policy machine learning optimization routines for personalized and spatially explicit "agronomic" recommendations', 'This repository contains R functions and an example on using causal random forest and policy machine learning optimization routines to make personalized and spatially explicit "agronomic" recommendations. It also contains the notebook on using casual random forest.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6879?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6436, 'Bundle of retail-level strategies to make fruits and vegetables more affordable and accessible to consumers in urban areas of Nigeria and Viet Nam', 'This retail-level innovation was developed to address barriers in the affordability and acceptability of fruits and vegetables among low-income women in urban and peri-urban areas of Nigeria and Viet Nam. The intervention included a variety of strategies designed to improve: (1) the affordability through a coupon system; (2) the acceptability through a promotional campaign about the importance of eating fruits and vegetables every day; and (3) a combination of both through a loyalty card system and improvements in point of sales, product display, marketing, delivery of nutritional information to consumers, food safety, and customer service practices. The bundled intervention was implemented over twelve months and led to a marginal increase in the proportion of females consuming fruits and vegetables during the intervention period in both countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6436?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(6815, 'Spatial ex ante workflows for evaluating economic and agronomic responses to agricultural liming', 'This set of R scripts provide a workflow to quantify the burden of acid soils in crop production in sub-Saharan Africa. It entails: (a) data acquisition and cleaning, (b) estimation of lime requirements for acid soil remediation, (c) estimation of yield gains due to acid soil remediation, and (d) calculation of the profitability of liming.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6815?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4215, 'Standard Operating Procedure for farmer segmentation generates insights into farm diversity for the improvement of agronomic innovations for farmers and design of scaling strategy in Excellence in Agronomy Use Cases', 'This SOP provides the steps to undertake characterization of farmer typology in Use Cases and generate actionable insights into farm diversity that can be used to increase the impact potential of the Use Cases’ MVP’s and realise inclusive impact across different types of farmers. This characterization is based on the premise that the farm’s biophysical and socio-economic context, as well as the farmer’s capacity, aspirations and interest determine the feasibility and desirability of recommended changes in practices and use of agricultural inputs, as well as access to information channels through which recommendations are disseminated.  ', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4215?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9724, 'Co-Lab: An online collaboration and learning platform for transforming challenges into opportunities (ICTforAg Learning Network)', 'This space serves as a global hub, connecting digital innovators, researchers, investors, policymakers, and other stakeholders. Its aim is to transform challenges into opportunities through collaborative initiatives. Through regular workshops, ideation sessions, and thematic working groups that encourage stakeholders to brainstorm, share ideas, and collaborate on projects; this space fosters awareness and understanding among diverse participants. The collaborative space isn''t just about networking; it''s about co-creating solutions that have a tangible impact on agri-food systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9724?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(13515, ' EiA Add on Surveys online course to collaborate & co-create Minimum Viable Products( MVPs) for farmers, extension agents, and agro-dealers in the Global South', 'This specialized course, offered by the CGIAR Excellence in Agronomy (EiA) Initiative, is designed to equip learners with the knowledge and skills necessary to effectively leverage the integrated survey tool developed by EiA''s Add-ons. Participants will gain proficiency in accessing and customizing Add-on surveys, enabling them to collaboratively co-create Minimum Viable Products (MVPs) tailored to the diverse needs of farmers, extension agents, and agro-dealers in the Global South', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/13515?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4242, 'Standard Operating Procedures and users guide for developing gender and youth responsive agronomic solutions for Use Cases in Excellence in Agronomy Initiative', 'This Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) is designed for Excellence in Agronomy (EiA) Use Case teams with an interest in developing gender- and youth-responsive agronomic solutions. The SOP is a guide to help Use Case teams move from each step in their workflows when designing, validating, and piloting their Minimum Viable Products (MVPs) to ensure they can develop gender- and youth-responsive agronomic solutions by the end of their Use Case. This stepwise procedure first entails carrying out a diagnostic assessment of gender and generational differences in agricultural management practices and technology adoption and the factors contributing to these differences. These steps (6 total) to developing gender- and youth-responsive agronomic solutions are described with some detail in this SOP. The SOP has been used during the implementation of the integrated survey to ground the Use Cases. Reports from 3 Use cases are shown in the evidence.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4242?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(20340, 'Enhanced predictive accuracy and efficiency in genomic-assisted sweetpotato breeding using deregressed best linear unbiased prediction (BLUPs) from the animal model and optimized training populations', 'This study addresses key challenges in integrating genomic-enabled prediction into sweetpotato breeding, particularly genotype-by-environment interactions and constructing effective training sets. Two hypotheses were tested : (i) using deregressed ABLUPs (dABLUPs-best linear unbiased prediction from the animal model) in two-stage modeling improves predictive accuracy in observational trials, and (ii) optimizing training sets can reduce experimental effort while maintaining accuracy. Evaluating 1185 genotypes across six trials in Uganda and comparing four modeling approaches, dABLUPs consistently provided the best predictions, improving predictive ability by up to 37% over other methods. Optimized training sets further enhanced performance, demonstrating the potential for more efficient genomic prediction in sweetpotato breeding.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20340?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(18240, 'Identification and validation of sex genomic markers in Catla and Silver Carp for improved aquaculture breeding', 'This study aimed to identify and validate male-specific genomic markers for two economically important aquaculture species, catla (Labeo catla syn. Catla catla) and silver carp (Hypophthalmichthys molitrix). By leveraging conserved male-specific genomic regions from closely related species—rohu (Labeo rohita) and bighead carp (Hypophthalmichthys nobilis)—researchers identified putatively male-only oligonucleotide sequences. These findings provide a valuable foundation for sex-specific breeding programs, offering potential applications in selective breeding, stock management, and genetic improvement in aquaculture.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18240?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8542, 'Selection of 65 days potato variety/es for the farmers of Bangladesh to catch up early market segment for higher income', 'This study evaluated Participatory Adaptive Trials of early bulking potato varieties in two locations of Bangladeshi over two years. Among ten varieties assessed, BARI ALU 79 emerged as the highest yielder with superior tuber size, adaptability, and potential for improving food security and livelihoods. Gender-sensitive farmer preferences showed that male farmers prioritized yield, while female farmers valued color and culinary traits, emphasizing the need for integrating diverse farmer insights into variety selection. By capitalizing on the early bulking traits of BARI ALU 79, this variety has the potential to significantly improve farmers'' livelihoods and boost food security in Bangladesh. Future collaboration with seed companies and ongoing farmers engagement is crucial for successful adoption and market alignment.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8542?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(5682, 'A system dynamics approach for assessing policies for promoting resilience of maize-cowpea intercropping in Ghana', 'This study sought to evaluate the resilience of intercropping (cowpea and maize) in northern Ghana, using system dynamics modelling approach. A simulation model was developed to provide insights into how this intercropping enables households to remain food secure, boost their income, and maintain natural resource capital, concurrently. The model enables quantitative evaluation of cause(s) and effect(s) of implementing an agricultural technology or new policy on different sustainable intensification domains i.e. production, economics, environment, human condition and social inclusion. The model is targeted to be used by policy makers, technology scaling experts and researchers. The model is scalable to other geographies provided that the site specific inter-linkages between different sub-systems are defined after stakeholders consultation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5682?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(5284, 'Design innovations in leveraging social protection for women''s engagement in sustainable land management practices', 'This sustainable land management (SLM) innovation draws on qualitative formative research, co-design of an innovation with partners, a quantitative impact evaluation testing the innovation, and resulting guidance for partners on implementing the innovation. This project is delivered as part of a multifaceted graduation model social protection program, SPIR II (Strengthen Productive Safety Net Program Institutions and Resilience II) that seeks to further sustain nutrition security and reduce risks to livelihoods for households participating in the Ethiopia’s national safety program, the Productive Safety Net Program (PSNP), which provides consumption support in the form of cash and food transfers. The goal of the SLM innovation being tested is to leverage social protection to promote women’s engagement in sustainable land management practices. Strengthening women’s involvement in sustainable land management is seen as potentially important given that extreme weather events pose risks for poor households in rural Ethiopia and women may be disproportionately at risk from these shocks. For example, Kato et al (2021) find that plots controlled by women in Ethiopia are more prone to soil erosion and of poorer quality in terms of soil depth, and women’s participation in watershed committees is low. The sustainable land management practices being introduced may help protect women and their families from these shocks over the longer term as well as introduce an additional livelihoods avenue. The innovation tested includes providing training (to either women only or to both men and women from same households) and free inputs (tree seedlings, vegetable seeds and tools for home gardening) among participants of the PSNP public works program, thus helping to address constraints to accessing information and inputs, and assessing whether spousal cooperation is important for successful adoption of these technologies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5284?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(17392, 'Systematic mapping of food security and nutrition projects in Guatemala', 'This tool developed by Work Package 5 supports the indicators of "Monitoring and targeting tool mapping key interventions and actors combined with a comprehensive profile at the subnational level to identify opportunities to improve investments in agri-food systems."
It is a platform dedicated to mapping and monitoring key projects in Food Security and Nutrition from International Cooperation in Guatemala, at the municipal level. The aim is to facilitate coordination among donors and other stakeholders to complement efforts and streamline the effective use of resources in implementing actions and programs in Food Security and Nutrition in the country. The official launch of the platform is scheduled for the end of January 2025. Users of the platform will include actors from the private sector, public sector, international organizations, and universities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17392?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(18074, 'AI tool for root segmentation and analysis in tropical grasses', 'This tool is an AI-driven application for segmenting and analyzing root morphology in underground minirhizotron non-destructive root images of tropical grasses. It is designed to provide accurate and efficient root analysis for researchers. This tool is available as a Gradio web application on Hugging Face, with options for local execution.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18074?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(16667, 'Costs and profitability at hand: a calculation tool for small producer', 'This tool is designed to estimate in a simple and user-friendly manner the production costs and potential profitability of the production processes carried out by small producers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16667?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6869, 'R code and shiny app for estimating beneficiaries to agronomy projects by geography  to be used by  researchers and scientists involved in ex-ante analysis', 'This tool is intended to allow users the ability to define expected beneficiaries and benefits to agricultural investments in cases where limited or no data exist for a more elaborate ex ante impact evaluation. It uses data on the geographical distribution of rural populations and agricultural outcomes (production, yield) to help users transparently establish estimated benefit numbers to use at project design stages. This tool is at early stage of development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6869?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(15545, 'Policy Tracker for Projects: A framework and template for developing and tracking progress towards policy and institutional targets', 'This tool provides a framework and template for developing and tracking progress towards policy and institutional targets within the timeframe of the project (or program or initiative).  It is meant to be filled out in a participatory manner with policy stakeholders at the relevant level (e.g. national, sub-national or global).  The existing template is organized for an agroecology oriented project but can be modified to meet the needs of any project aiming to have policy or institutional influence.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15545?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(15626, 'Toolkit for exploring yield growth to 2050: Updating exogenous yield growth parameters in impact', 'This toolkit is composed by guidelines, a manual, and example Power Point slides to update exogenous yield growth. The toolkit is expected to be used during the preparation and implementation of the expert consultations for review and updating of exogenous yield growth parameters to 2050 in the International Model for Policy Analysis of Agricultural Commodities and Trade (IMPACT) of the International Food Policy Research Institute (IFPRI). These exogenous yield growth parameters, commonly referred to as “Intrinsic Productivity growth Rates” (IPRs), represent a component of agricultural productivity that cannot be attributed to price effects, climate change and water shocks — elements which are explicitly captured by IMPACT. Yield growth from IPRs can represent, for example, new technologies, better management, improved input use efficiency, etc. Estimating IPRs to 2050 can be better understood as an exercise to derive future yield growth parameters from an informed extrapolation of observed past trends.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15626?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1107, 'Livestock 4 Climate Security Policy and Governance Toolkit: Ensuring Climate Security-sensitive and Pro-peace Livestock Adaptation and Mitigation. A toolkit which will contain recommendations on how to mainstream climate security in relevant policies for policy makers', 'This toolkit will form an accessible and easily understandable ''checklist'' based toolkit containing practical and actionable guidelines for the design of climate security-sensitive livestock sector-specific adaptation and mitigation policies. Previous research has demonstrated that awareness of climate-related security risks in key climate-related policy outputs across ClimBeR countries is limited, despite the increasing salience of pursuing adaptation and mitigation in fragile and conflict-affected areas. The toolkit will likely focus on both conceptual considerations (appropriate conceptualisations of climate security) and on technical considerations (inclusion and deployment of certain types of analyses and features of policy design). The toolkit will be aimed at policymakers, technical ministerial staff, and supporting entities in the governance space.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1107?phase=1', 0, NULL),
(14494, '[Manual] Training Module: Gender Responsive Digital Inclusion', 'This Training Module on Gender Responsive Digital Inclusion provides a framework to empower researchers, extension agents, and private stakeholders with the skills and knowledge to address gender disparities in digital access and usage. It covers concepts like gender-sensitive design, policy frameworks, and evidence-based practices, integrating real-world case studies and practical exercises to foster inclusive and impactful digital solutions. Designed as an adaptable, two-day program, the module includes facilitator guidelines, group discussions, and monitoring and evaluation approaches to ensure actionable learning. It is aimed at bridging the digital divide and promoting equitable participation across the agricultural value chain.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14494?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(7334, 'Empowering Women: A Video to promote Inclusion in India''s Government Planning', 'This video was prepared by Travelling Tripod  in collaboration with the International Food Policy Research Institute to inspire rural women in Odisha State, India to publicly share with leaders their ideas for assets that might be constructed under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee (MGNREGA) program. The MGNREGA Act envisions a participatory, bottom-up process of choosing which asset will be built by allowing MGNREGA job card holders to recommend assets that would be useful to them at community meetings. Increasing women’s participation in the program is an important development goal for IFPRI and their partners in Odisha.

--
This video was created to inspire women to participate in the Mahatma Ghandi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) program in Odisha, India. MGNREGA is the largest workfare program in the world and is both an employment guarantee scheme and a program by which communities propose and build assets to help improve their livelihoods. This video tells the stories of women who have successfully received assets and encourages women to request assets themselves.

The video is unique in that it tells detailed stories of women who have been successful in requesting and receiving assets under the MGNREGA program. It showcases women that are easy to relate to and provides specific advice and encouragement to women. Given the size of the scheme, the video can inspire women to improve their lives and livelihoods.

The video will be easy to scale. It is only 15 minutes long, and is accompanied by a discussion guide for implementers on what to discuss with women at various points during the video to spur engagement and discussions. It is inexpensive to show and it can be implemented by almost anyone within a community. Both the government and NGOs may find it useful as a resource. We also developed a 6-min version in 2024 based on feedback from stakeholders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7334?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(644, 'Aclimate Guatemala: a user-centered digital agro-climatic forecast system for processing of climate and crop information', 'This web application is being developed by the International Center for Tropical Agriculture CIAT, the national federation of rice farmers FEDEARROZ, the national coffee federation of Colombia, the National federation of cereals and legumes FENALCE, and the Institute of Hydrology Meteorology and environmental studies IDEAM. And, with the support of the United States agency for international development USAID. The methods and models used to generate the data found here are under development test and validation. However, they have a good performance. Initially, they are located in the number of climatic and agroclimatic municipalities for corn and rice in 6 municipalities of Colombia. The unions will follow this platform to offer the possibility of other municipalities and varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/644?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(891, 'Satellite-based regional scale climate data extractor for Central and West Asia and North Africa (CWANA) web-based innovation to ease the weather data acquisition process for NARES, scientists and policymakers', 'This web-app has been developed to ease the weather data acquisition process. This web-app allows the user to extract long-term monthly scale temperature and precipitation data for selected countries in the CWANA region. Users are required to indicate the country of interest and the desired time period. Consequently, the web-app presents annual weather information and time-series precipitation and temperature data as well as meteorological drought indices such as Standard Precipitation Index (SPI) and Standard Temperature Index (STI). Additionally, the platform allows users to generate spatial maps for weather parameters and to export data in the form of maps, tables and figures. This web-app automatically retrieves up-to-date weather data from the ERA5-Land archive.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/891?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9719, 'A WhatsApp-based distance learning network platform: Bridging the digital and knowledge divide by bringing experts and and learners together', 'This WhatsApp-based distance learning network platform is designed to bridge the digital divide by bringing experts and learners together on a single platform. It hosts micro-learning modules delivered through the WhatsApp bots, also accessible through web. Users can connect with experts, gain insights, and explore innovative ideas. This new learning management system (LMS) hosts courses developed by CGIAR and external partners and ensures easy portability and functionality.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9719?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3415, 'Business models for farmers to recover and reuse animal dung, effluent waste for energy recovery & production, biofertilizer, aquaculture and soil nutrients.', 'This will be business models for the financial sustainability of animal waste management. These will be based on real success cases from the global south, and will create incentives for farmers to reduce the release of effluent waste such as livestock waste and aquaculture wastewater into the environment, reducing the transmission of zoonotic pathogens, antimicrobials and Anti-Microbial Resistance into the environment and establish a resource recovery and reuse means.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3415?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(8230, 'Mobile applications to improve inventory management with GRIN-Global Community Edition', 'Three mobile applications for genebank processes: Regeneration, Viability-testing, Germplasm Health, have been finalized and are being deployed. The viability application aims to facilitate users in registering and controlling all activities carried out in the viability laboratory, from material reception to delivering viability results to users. The Regeneration application aims to facilitate users in the detailed recording and control of all their activities in the field, from land planning to delivering harvests to the conservation area (cleaning). The Health Application aims to register and monitor all activities carried out in the health laboratory, from material reception to delivering results to users, both for seed collections and In-Vitro collections.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8230?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6772, 'Prioritizing agronomy in a Changing Environment (PAiCE) Tool for researchers and national partners  to characterize crop production risks and explore potential adaptation solutions for specific crop production in Global South', 'Through discussion-based stakeholder consultations, facilitated by trained researchers, the tool provides a structured approach to characterize and explore potential adaption solutions for specific crop production regions. As a first step, characterization data
for climate challenges and production system characteristics should be aggregated; this helps ensure that discussions are focused on topics that require the wisdom of groups. It is also important to aggregate experimental evidence of the value of different technologies and management approaches to respond to the climate challenge. Following the workshop, areas of uncertainty and evidence gaps should be addressed to enhance initial results. Thereafter, a provisional set of priorities should be shared with a broader set of stakeholders to cross-validate results. As a last step, it is envisioned that results from the PAiCE tool will provide a strong foundation for climate change adaptation by steering investment and helping to coordinate the efforts of partners through action roadmap.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6772?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5335, 'Decentralized extension model by engaging local service providers for technology dissemination and nutrition messaging for producers in Bangladesh', 'To effectively delivery information on agricultural-related technologies and nutrition to local producers in Bangladesh, the WorldFish in collaboration with Center for Natural Resource Studies (CNRS) has developed a decentralized extension model by tapping local service providers. This is also to ensure that continuous support and technical assistance from different stakeholders are being delivered to marginalized communities to promote local economic growth. 

According to the learning service provider model, local fish famers, business owners and nursery operators are taught to offer additional services to aquaculture production. The manner in which additional services are provided to fish famers can vary among service provider types.

The Asian Mega Deltas (AMD) through the WorldFish is leveraging on the model that has proven immediate outcomes started through a bilateral project.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5335?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(10258, 'Bioinputs to improve yields of CCN51 cocoa variety in Ucayali, Peru', 'To evaluate the effectiveness of liquid biofertilizers in improving the yield of CCN51 cocoa. For this purpose, 5 treatments are proposed where the frequency of application of liquid biofertilizers is varied. The biofertilizers are produced by the technical area of Banaqui Curimaná and are composed of multiple sources of fermented organic matter, mineral salts and phosphites.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10258?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(18636, 'Enhancing nutritional security of small farmers though introduction of improved homestead production system with vegetable and aquaculture practices in India', 'To explore the viability and formulate effective strategies to improve resilience, income sustainability and nutritional security of farmers in India, the improved homestead production system with vegetable and aquaculture practices was introduced. It is currently under research to establish proof-of-concept. A recent study was conducted in 15 farmers’ ponds in 2 villages, Ramgopalpur and Chatrakhali - evaluate 3 culture systems: Indian major carps (IMC) 100%, 50% IMC & 50% monosex Tilapia, and 100% monosex Tilapia. Study revealed that the culture of IMC was more profitable (B:C ratio 2.49) in comparison to the system in which 50% (B:C ration 1.68) and complete replacement (B:C ratio 1.62) of IMC with monosex tilapia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18636?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(20366, 'Rapid cycle augmentation scheme for trait development and deployment in rice', 'To facilitate further and more efficient diversification of elite backgrounds, a new strategy for augmentation has been developed.  This turns the linear process of upgrading a particular line with up to 3 new genes into a cyclical recurrent selection process, intercrossing amongst elite progeny to accumulate target genes.  The benefits of this scheme are decoupling the number of input and output lines, and a greatly increased capacity to select for a much larger number of genes with modest/tractable population sizes.  Initial founding lines and crosses are underway to establish this pipeline, initially for the medium maturity program and from there to direct-seeded material as resources permit. The scheme prioritized traits for disease resistance, abiotic stress resistance (e.g. drought) and improving grain quality. These traits aimed to produced breeding lines that would help combat impacts of climate change and increasing consumption of healthier rice without penalizing yield.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20366?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8177, 'Local Indicator Selection Process (LISP) for developing context-specific agroecological performance assessments', 'To generate both locally relevant and global comparable evidence on the impact of agroecology, the AE-I has developed the Holistic Localized Performance Assessment or HOLPA Framework. The goal of the Local Indicator Selection Process (LISP) is to co-develop a set of performance indicators with stakeholders in each Agroecology Living Landscape (ALL) that are relevant for and customized to their farming systems and respond to the vision and interventions that will be implemented in the ALL. Indicators identified in the LISP will be combined with the global performance indicators common to all ALLs, as well as the farm context and agroecology characterization modules to form the Holistic Localized Performance Assessment Tool, which will be implemented in each ALL. ', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8177?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3036, 'Value stream mapping approach to map the impact of food waste along food supply chains in India and Bangladesh', 'To map the impact of food waste across the different stakeholders in the food supply chains in India and Bangladesh, the approach of Value Stream Mapping uses ‘lean thinking’ principles. In so doing, a sustainability performance matrix will be developed for encapsulating the economic, social, and environmental indicators. Based on interation with stakeholders and literature review, the social, economic and environmental indicators have been identified.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3036?phase=3', 1, NULL),
(16615, 'Biometrical Genetics Workflow (Bioflow): CGIAR-NARES breeding analytics and decision support tool designed to enable faster decisions, accelerate breeding cycles, and increase genetic gains', 'To meet the ambition of increasing the rate of genetic gain for key traits in preferred and climate-resilient crop varieties, breeders must make accurate, data-driven decisions quickly. Achieving this requires analyzing large volumes of data from different sources to account for variances arising from breeding populations, environmental conditions, and farm management practices - the genotype-by-environment-by-management (GxExM) complex. This comprehensive approach ensures the selection of fit-for-purpose candidate varieties. To support this effort, Breeding Resources, in collaboration with Accelerated Breeding, developed a breeding analytics and decision-support tool that enables CGIAR-National Agricultural Research and Extension Services (NARES) breeders to access data from different sources, analyze it rapidly, and make informed decisions about which genotypes to recycle as new parents for the next cycle and which to advance as candidate varieties. The tool incorporates modules essential for breeders to conduct comprehensive genetic evaluations of test genotypes, driving efficiency and precision in the breeding process.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16615?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(17061, 'An in-house long read DNA sequencing established for rapid monitoring of pathogen populations', 'To overcome the challenges of high costs, outsourcing delays, and technical demands in DNA sequencing, an in-house setup was established using the portable MinION device by Oxford Nanopore Technology. This long-read sequencing technology provides a more complete understanding of a pathogen''s genome and captures complex regions, unlike short-read sequencing, which relies on sequence assembly and may lose important genomic information. For bacterial diseases in rice such as bacterial blight, long-read sequencing enables the recovery of intact genome sequences without needing a reference genome. Initial sequencing runs on Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae isolates generated 855,000 reads in 22 hours, reducing turnaround time to three days. The MinION’s portability, affordability, and fast sequencing capabilities allow rapid investigation of new pathogen strains and support resistance strategies for rice breeding programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17061?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(5338, 'Leveraging on private partnership models to provide digital climate advisory services to fisherfolk in Myanmar', 'To provide agro-advisory on a weekly and monthly basis to fishers in Myanmar, the Alliance partnered with the Village Link Company Limited to pilot the innovation and reach initial number of farmers. The advisories are based on seasonal and monthly weather forecast with the support of crop decision trees (CDTs) developed by the Alliance. Village Link provides advisories through their digital platforms such as the HTwet Toe application. It is now being piloted in select township in Myanmar. It is envisioned that after succesful piloting, with evidence of reduce agricultural and climate risks, the advisories will be tied up to financing mechanisms to improve access of fishers to production capital through loans.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5338?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5337, 'Leveraging on private partnership models to provide digital climate advisory services to monsoon rice farmers in Myanmar', 'To provide agro-advisory on a weekly and monthly basis to moonsoon rice farmers in Myanmar, the Alliance partnered with the Village Link Company Limited to pilot the innovation and reach initial number of farmers. The advisories are based on seasonal and monthly weather forecast with the support of crop decision trees (CDTs) developed by the Alliance. Village Link provides advisories through their digital platforms such as the HTwet Toe application.
It is now being piloted in select township in Myanmar.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5337?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(7830, 'Automated water level sensor with SMS alerts enhancing informed irrigation decision making and sustainable agricultural practices for rice farmers in Vietnam', 'To scale an innovation in a specific context, an automated water level sensor with SMS notifications is essential, given its implications for project management, collaborations, and potential environmental and social trade-offs. Developed with the aim of assisting farmers in applying alternate wetting-drying practices more effectively, this sensor provides real-time information on water levels, enabling farmers to make informed decisions about irrigation timing. Targeting farmers as the primary audience, the innovation lies in the sensor''s ability to send SMS notifications containing field water level data, facilitating efficient water management and promoting sustainable agricultural practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7830?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(274, 'New publication series designed for quicker access to market intelligence to inform breeding choices of CGIAR, NARES, donors, and companies', 'To strengthen the impact of genetic innovations, there is need for demand- and data-driven processes that guide plant breeding within CGIAR and among its partners (NARS, seed companies). This requires that social scientists (e.g., gender and marketing specialists, and economists), geospatial scientists, nutritionists, and representatives from national breeding programs, among others, engage with breeding and seed system programs providing timely and useful “market intelligence”.  
Publication series designed to communicate market intelligence to CGIAR, NARES, donors, and companies. Efforts here included the design of the brief (layout), hiring copy editors, and designing style-guide and the peer-review process. This serves as the main channel for dissemination of market intelligence to inform breeding choices, highlighting main messages and implications for Genetic Innovations work.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/274?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3325, 'Leaf bud cuttings: a novel method for quicker and more efficient production of yam seeds by farmers in Nigeria and Ghana', 'Traditional systems of yam seed production are slow and inefficient. Leaf-bud cuttings have been tested to determine if they can be used to quickly establish large quantities of yam planting material of improved high-yielding varieties. Techniques for evaluating the performance of leaf bud cuttings (LBCs) were developed and comparisons were made between transplanted and direct planted LBC. Direct planted LBCs gave yields that were more than double those of transplanted LBCs. There were no significant differences in results for the two varieties that were tested. Further research is required to determine the most appropriate situation within the yam seed system in which to use LBCs. Pilot testing of using LBCs in farmers'' small seed plots was done during the 2023 cropping season in Bwari, Abuja, Nigeria, and the results was encouraging with farmers excited about the technique.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3325?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(17818, 'Crop diversification in homegardens to enhance food security for cacao farming families in the Peruvian Amazon', 'Traditionally, homegardens have been essential for ensuring access to healthy food and conserving local biodiversity. However, their application in intensive systems like Amazonian cacao cultivation, which requires specialized labor, has been limited. The high dependence on cacao exposes families to economic risks. To diversify production and improve food security, we propose co-creating family gardens based on agroecological practices, selecting complementary crops to ensure a stable supply of healthy food.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17818?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4688, 'Integrated business model for crop diversification and bio-fortified rice for the rice producers in Bangladesh', 'Transforming Agrifood Systems in South Asia (TAFSSA) with the Linkage of Cereal Systems Initiative for South Asia (CSISA)  has started to work on increasing crop diversification by increasing crop intensification and through integrated market systems development. In the 2022/23 rabi season, TAFSSA has started working in three districts (Rangpur, Dinajpur and Nilphamari) in northern Bangladesh on area expansion of diversified crops by community approach and value chain development through an integrated business model. With the collaboration of the Department of Agricultural Extension (DAE), Bangladesh Agricultural Research Institute (BARI) and local seed companies TAFSSA provided the seeds of three diversified crops (e.g., Mustard, lentil, and green pea).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4688?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(14528, 'Field-based Assessment of the Effectiveness of Rust Resistance Genes Under Field Conditions in Lebanon, Morocco and Tunisia in 2024', 'Trap nurseries comprising yellow, stem, and leaf rust resistance genes planted under natural infection in two locations in Lebanon and Morocco. Despite the availability of differential seeds, the trap nurseries were not planted in Tunisia. In Morocco, a high level of susceptibility to leaf rust was observed in several leaf rust genes, including Lr10, Lr11, Lr12, Lr13, and others across different locations. In contrast, resistance was noted in genes such as Lr2b, Lr3, Lr9, Lr19, Lr34, and others. In Lebanon, field observations indicated differences in yellow rust resistance across two locations, with various resistance genes (e.g., Yr1, Yr2+, Yr3, Yr5, Yr6+, Yr15, and others) remaining effective at Tel Amara and Kfarchakhna. In Marchouch, stem rust resistance genes like Sr21, Sr11, and Sr38 showed effectiveness, while others, including Sr5, Sr6, and Sr36, were ineffective. Details of effectiveness of Yr, Sr, and Lr genes in Morocco and Lebanon is provided.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14528?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5024, 'Combining improved vegetable seed varieties with extension support and certification of smallholder farmers for greater and more efficient vegetable productivity and marketing.', 'Two main innovations are improved seed varieties and the extension approach of the East West Seeds - Knowledge Transfer (EWS-KT) . The improved vegetable seed varieties result from advanced breeding techniques by East-West Seed with enhanced traits like yield, disease resistance, and adaptability to diverse conditions. Improved vegetable seed varieties empower smallholder farmers'' production capacity, increasing productivity while conserving resources. This ensures that agriculture remains at the forefront of sustainable development and food security. The extension approach provides timely, accessible, and tailored information to smallholder farmers via EWS-KT’s extension agents, enhancing their decision-making and crop management. It also encompasses the innovative approaches of publicly certifying trained farmers – to increase innovation diffusion and product value – and educating smallholder farming households to optimize income use through an inclusive decision-making process. These innovative approaches hope to empower farmers with actionable knowledge for sustainable farming that can better their livelihoods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5024?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3724, 'Isotope techniques for the introduction of climate-resilient varieties in cassava and banana production system', 'Under the project ''Consortium for Improving Agriculture-based Livelihoods in Central Africa'', the Centre of Nuclear Nuclear Techniques in Food and Agriculture coordinated research towards development of isotope techniques to make cassava and banana production systems more climate change resilient. Using precipitation data from local measuring stations and ''Climate Hazards Group InfraRed Precipitation with Station data'' satellite imagery data for Rwanda, Burundi, and DR Congo, variability and trend analysis has been executed to understand the change in water availability and frequency in dry spells. In collaboration with the Alliance Bioversity & CIAT, a field experiment was conducted to validate and up-scale the use of stable carbon isotope techniques for assessing the intrinsic water use efficiency of cassava under field conditions. This research facilitates understanding of the plant’s behavior under drought in the field at an early bulking stage. This and other indicators will be included as parameters for breeding drought-tolerant cassava.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3724?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(3207, 'FISHTRAC: Real-time monitoring of water quality dynamics using fish-attached sensors', 'Understanding how fish respond to stressors, such as water pollution and altered flows, is important to monitor aquatic ecosystem health. Fish’s behavioral responses are shown to be up to 100 times more sensitive to environmental stressors than any other established line of evidence, and they are used in various environmental monitoring, conservation, and research programs. Recent advancements in sensor-based telemetry studies showed fish behavior could be observed remotely in real-time, providing prospects for enhanced natural resource management activities. In the Limpopo River Basin, we support the University of Mpumalanga in developing FISHTRAC, a real-time water quality monitoring system using fish-attached sensors, and testing its functionality. In 2022, based on FISHTRAC’s prototype, we improved the system’s real-time monitoring capabilities. The improved FISHTRAC was deployed to a school of Tigerfish in the Crocodile River. In 2023, we collected more data to establish relationships between fish behaviors and environmental variables.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3207?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(16485, 'Scale to assess food safety experiences and concerns of consumers in low- and middle-income countries in development to diagnose constraints to consumption of sustainable nutritious foods', 'Unsafe food creates a cycle of disease and malnutrition, affecting infants, young children, elderly and the sick. Fresh foods, particularly, fruits, vegetables, and animal source foods, are both more nutritious and riskier from a food safety perspective. Researchers from Sustainable Healthy Diets and Resilient Cities are developing a scale to assess food safety experiences and concerns. The focus is not to objectively assess or quantify food safety risks or how nutritionally adequate diets are. Rather, this measure will capture the subjective food safety experiences and concerns of consumers and how consumers respond to these concerns in choosing their diets. By the end of 2024, the team completed the background research in three countries and identified an initial set of candidate scale items. The next phases of the project will refine the scale and field test it in surveys around the world.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16485?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(15795, 'Water champions to promote One Health in Ethiopia', 'Until recently, water was notably absent from the national One Health planning and committees, where human, animal, and environmental health converge to address complex health challenges. However, Ethiopia’s Water Champions have intervened to ensure that water assumes its rightful central position within the One Health framework. Water management experts from relevant institutions are now identified and nominated to serve on national One Health committees. These experts participate in comprehensive training programs and are equipped with the necessary knowledge to inform committee deliberations on the role of water in pathogen transmission and on water management solutions to safeguard public health.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/15795?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(8229, 'Climate-smart and resilient rice production practices in the Mixed Farming Systems', 'urrent rice farming practice in Bangladesh is  labor intensive, economically unviable,  environmentally unsustainable, and negatively affected by climate change impacts. There is a strong need to improve climate resilience and sustainability of the rice production system. The Mixed Farming Systems Initiative is testing two climate-smart agricultural pratices, namely Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) and Nano-Urea fertilizer as a bundle of innovation in the crop-livestock system. Mixed Farming Systems is testing their potential with the aim to combine them, where and when appropriate, with the other innovations tested (eg. Intensifying and diversifying cropping systems for better crop-livestock integration) to improve productivity, profitability, sustainability, and resilience of the crop-livestock system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8229?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(2170, '20 Use Cases (agronomic solutions) co-created between CGIAR (centers/other key partners) under the request of public or private sector partners across farming systems, crops and geographies.', 'Use Cases are set of agronomic solutions packaged as individual agronomic content or bundled with other complementary products such as inputs, output markets and insurance packages to enhance uptake. These solutions have been demanded by either public or private sector partners and being developed jointly with CGIAR centers and other key partners in the value chains.  The agronomic gaps identified as basis for the development of the 18 Use Cases were based on farming systems, geographies, gender and youth constraints for which makes them gender and youth responsive solutions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2170?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9284, 'Deep Learning based Realtime Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) Detection for Banana Fields', 'Using a custom trained Yolo-v8 deep learning model, we''ve achieved the capability to survey banana fields via drones, successfully identifying healthy and infected plants. While further testing and validation are necessary, the groundbreaking aspect of this development is the establishment of a real-time disease detection pipeline.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9284?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(8569, 'AI Powered Realtime Banana Fusarium wilt detection using Unmanned Aerial Vehicles', 'Using a custom-trained YOLO-v8 deep learning model, we expanded detection capabilities to include video analysis, enabling real-time disease identification. We successfully surveyed banana fields via drones, identifying healthy and Fusarium-infected plants. While further validation is needed, the most groundbreaking aspect is the establishment of a real-time disease detection pipeline using drones. Over 8,000 drone images from Fusarium-infected fields in Vietnam and Peru were analyzed. In 2024, we trained a YOLO-v9 model to differentiate between Banana Xanthomonas Wilt (BXW), Fusarium wilt, and healthy plants, using datasets from the Democratic Republic of Congo for Banana Xanthomonas Wilt and Vietnam for Fusarium wilt. The model achieved a mean average precision (mAP) between 68-80% across all categories foe YOLO-v9, with Faster R-CNN slightly outperforming YOLO-v9 for Fusarium detection.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8569?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4643, 'Model farms approach to demonstrate benefits of an integrated herd health package on pig health and productivity in Uganda', 'Using a farmer field school approach, four model farms were set up by renovating existing housing units through ILRI-farmer co-funding arrangement. The model farms were renovated to showcase integrated technology innovations (herd health package): improved housing, health, welfare, piglet care, biosecurity measures, improved feeds and genetics. The key elements of the herd health package include improved floor drainage, safe water, welfare, reduced piglet deaths & disease burden, improved feeding and breeding. This will be followed by learning visits facilitated by herd health champions. This is expected to spur adoption of the integrated innovation elements, which improve health, survival, growth rates and productivity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4643?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(2635, 'A suite of Improved agronomic management techniques for more precise establishment according to landscape position for direct seeded rice in Bangladesh', 'Using a multicriteria analysis assessment approach, data from experiments conducted by CSISA with BRRI, in alignment with TAFSSA, showed that compared to puddled transplanted rice, both direct-seeded rice (DSR) methods (direct machine drilling and broadcasting) reduced production costs, labor use, specific energy, and total and yield-scaled global warming potential. In addition, DSR had higher energy use efficiency and similar productivity. Performance for direct seeded rice was best when established with a two-wheeled tractor seed drill, and where established on highland or medium highland where flood water in the early spring aus rice season could not damage emerging seedlings after stand establishment. These recommendations have been passed on to the Department of Agricultural Extension as a suite of recommendations used by extension agents to advise farmers on how to grow DSR rice.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2635?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(6901, 'Cluster Farmer Field School (CFFS) Model for scaling agronomic innovations via strategic operation of sluice gates of polder systems in coastal Bangladesh', 'Using Farmer Field School (FFS) model, the Department of Agricultural Extension (DAE) has successfully disseminated agricultural technologies across Bangladesh. But there is no significant adoption of improved technologies in 1.2 million hectares polder zone mainly because of prevailing hydrology that the FFS did not consider as the determinant in scaling innovations. The project identified water stagnation and waterlogging are the primary agronomic constraints, and that community coordination and cropping system synchronization is needed for wide-scale adoption of improved production systems. The “Cluster Farmer Field School (CFFS)” model was developed integrating water management groups with FFS to scale agronomic innovations managing hydrology of the coastal Bangladesh. This innovation was employed to strategically operate the sluice gates for water intake and drainage, aligning with high and low tides of the peripheral rivers of polder ecosystems during key intercultural operations in wet and dry seasons to 14 Learning Hubs in 2023 and 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6901?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6243, 'Development and deployment of the Rice Crop Manager (RCM) for rice farmers in West Bengal, India', 'Using the principles of Site-Specific Nutrient Management (SSNM), the International Rice Research Institute developed the Rice Crop Manager (RCM), an application that provides farmers with crop management recommendations tailored to their fields and rice growing conditions through the internet and text messages. In addition to nutrients, RCM also addresses other constraints that limit rice yield and farmers’ profit. These constraints can vary across areas, growing conditions, varieties and climate scenarios. 

For West Bengal, RCM will be developed for the rice growing farmers using secondary data on soil crop managemen characteristics to generate recommendations. To develop RCM algorithms, a yield-gain approach will be used to calculate nutrient rate to achieve a target yield, as a direct function of the estimated yield gain with added nutrient. The results nutrient omission plot technique (NOPT) trials will be used to customize and develop RCM with special focus on salinity management.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6243?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(6974, 'Uxtools4ag - a resource for innovation researchers and partners to learn and engage with User Research methods', 'Uxtools4ag is an online resource for user research methods designed for CGIAR innovation researchers and designers and their partners in the digital ecosystem. User research is a cornerstone of designing human-centered and inclusive digital innovations. Uxtools4ag provides easy-to-use and applied guidance and support for integrating user research in the digital development process. Uxtools4ag was launched in November 2023. The vision is to have a digital space that inspires learning and experimenting with user research and other tools that are important to build human-centered digital products.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6974?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(20295, 'Value Addition of Cassava Flour as a Post-Harvest Management Innovation and  capacity development of women and youth in post-harvest management using high quality cassava flour', 'Value addition is an important post-harvest management technique. The use of High-quality cassava flour, is derived from the got from the processing of cassava roots, for various culinary and industrial applications. Producing high-quality cassava flour and processing food products from it is an essential endeavour to enhance food security, income generation, and economic development in West and Central Africa', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/20295?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4984, 'Fish-based (ComFA+Fish) products to tackle malnutrition in Zambia', 'Various fish-based products have been introduced in communities in several countries in Africa and Asia by WorldFish and partners. In particular, dried fish powder that utilizes nutrient-dense small fish can contribute to making healthy, affordable, and sustainable diets more accessible for vulnerable communities in low- and middle-income countries. In Zambia, nutrient-enhanced complementary foods for Africa and dried fish powder (ComFA+Fish) products and recipes for enhanced nutrition, particularly for mothers and infants in vulnerable households, have been developed and tested with mothers, caregivers, small-scale fishers, fish processors, fish traders, the private sector, and government representatives. These include fortified chibwaba fisashi, kapenta chutney, fortified maize porridge, fortified bean-vegetable soup, fortified cassava bites, and fortified nutri-biscuits. The ComFA+Fish products have been widely accepted in the communities around Lake Kariba in Zambia and were recommended for scaling in communities where malnutrition is high.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4984?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(907, 'An Artificial Intelligence-based farmers'' agricultural information demand prediction model for digital extension services in India', 'Various information and communication tools are used in two-way advisory services to interact with farmers. However, the utility and impact of such interactions are not well understood, primarily due to the lack of quality data at scale. To advance knowledge, we are developing a spatiotemporal prediction model of agricultural information demand in India by analyzing communications between farmers and the Kisan Call Centers. As the early stage of innovation, we processed more than 30 million records of communications that took place between 2004 and 2022 and validated macro trends in the data. We are further analyzing this database to identify spatiotemporal patterns of information needs, linking with explanatory variables such as climate and market trends that appear to trigger certain types of risks. Once fully analyzed, we anticipate the model can predict future systemic shocks in advance and help decision-makers prepare for measures to manage the risks in advance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/907?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(2125, 'VarScout - digital ecosystem for collecting, monitoring, and visualizing plant varietal data for farmers, extensionists, survey teams, funders, private companies', 'VarScout is a digital ecosystem that allows for cost-effective, accurate, and streamlined varietal data collection by different users (e.g., farmers, extension agents, government officials, private companies, researchers, etc.), and visualize data in real-time for effective decision-making on investments in breeding and seed systems for all CGIAR mandate crops globally. VarScout was currently tested for maize, beans, and potatoes in Kenya, Peru, Mozambique, and Zimbabwe.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2125?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9700, 'Optimizing pepper breeding lines: selecting locally adapted peppers for variety release in Tanzania', 'Vegetable adaptation trials are crucial for evaluating different varieties under specific growing conditions, aiding farmers in identifying the most suitable options for their region. In Tanzania, where peppers are an important income generating crop, FRESH core partner World Vegetable Center is conducting adaptation trials on 18 locally adapted pepper varieties - 6 chili peppers, 6 habanero peppers, and 6 sweet pepper varieties . These varieties were selected based on traits like yield, perceived disease resistance, and physical characteristics that may impact marketability. The adaptation trials have successfully identified promising pepper breeding lines outperforming the currently materials at three locations in Tanzania. The top-performing pepper varieties will progress to the World Vegetable Center''s variety development process, contributing to the enhancement of crop productivity and quality in the region. The release of the habanero, bell pepper and chili varieties will be the first ever publicly released pepper varieties in Tanzania.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9700?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(9697, 'Optimizing tomato breeding lines: selecting locally adapted tomatoes for variety release in Tanzania', 'Vegetable adaptation trials are crucial for evaluating different varieties under specific growing conditions, aiding farmers in identifying the most suitable options for their region. In Tanzania, where tomatoes are an important income generating crop, FRESH core partner World Vegetable Center is conducting adaptation trials on nine locally adapted tomato varieties. These varieties are selected based on traits like yield, perceived disease resistance, and physical characteristics that may impact marketability. The adaptation trials have successfully identified promising tomato breeding lines outperforming the currently materials at three locations in Tanzania. The top-performing tomato varieties will progress to the World Vegetable Center''s variety development process, contributing to the enhancement of crop productivity and quality in the region. This development and release of these tomato varieties will be the most recent locally adapted tomato variety release in the last 12 years.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9697?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(9706, 'Optimizing vegetable varieties: improved, locally adapted vegetable cultivars in Benin', 'Vegetable adaptation trials play a pivotal role in assessing diverse varieties under specific growing conditions, aiding farmers in selecting the most suitable options for their region. In Benin, where farmers encounter challenges in accessing improved vegetable varieties, the FRESH core partner World Vegetable Center has identified 23 improved, locally adapted varieties (1 okra, 11 tomato, 7 chili pepper, and 4 habanero). Through iterative workshops and consultations with key stakeholders, these varieties will undergo the World Vegetable Center''s variety release process. This basket of new, improved vegetable varieties empowers local seed companies offer and distribute desirable seed to farmers, offering substantial potential to enhance vegetable supply and address challenges faced by farmers in Benin.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9706?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(14524, 'Detection of Four Legume Viruses (AMV, CMV, BYMV, PSbMV) in One Multiplex RT-PCR Test Applied at ICARDA’s Seed Health and Virology Laboratory', 'Viruses such as Alfalfa mosaic virus (AMV), Cucumber mosaic virus (CMV), Bean yellow mosaic virus (BYMV), and Pea seed-borne mosaic virus (PSbMV) significantly impact legume crops in the WANA region. These RNA viruses are transmitted via aphids and seeds, threatening the productivity of crops like faba bean, lentil, and chickpea. Current detection methods, primarily ELISA and TBIA, are costly and labor-intensive, requiring separate tests for each virus. Additionally, obtaining specific antisera is challenging. To address these limitations, ICARDA’s Seed Health and Virology Laboratory developed a validated multiplex reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) assay. This molecular tool efficiently detects and differentiates the four viruses in a single, sensitive test, streamlining virus diagnostics and enabling high-throughput screening of samples. The approach enhances cost-effectiveness and precision in managing virus-related threats to legume crops in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14524?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(14712, 'Decision support tool on Water-Energy-Food-Environmental (WEFE) Nexus for improving water productivity in water-scarce regions of Central Asia', 'Water productivity constitutes one of the keyways to expand Water Energy Food Ecosystems (WEFE) benefits in water scarce contexts, yet water productivity is variable and in cases low. This innovation is focused on development of a Decision Support System (DSS) that enables planners and managers to identify solutions (e.g., resources allocations or crop mixes) that improve productivity. The DSS is a computer-based platform reveals the productivity associated with alternative investment choices, which can support investment decisions that optimize water productivity and WEFE benefits in different geographies. Key users of the DSS platform are water planners and managers, as well as those in related sectors (i.e. Energy, Food and Ecosystems).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/14712?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8470, 'Development of a Digital Twin for water resources management in the Limpopo River Basin in southern Africa', 'Water resource management in any basin, like the Limpopo River Basin with its intermittent supplies, is complex due to factors like rainfall, water allocation, quality, ecosystems, and risk to citizens. Balancing these factors often exceeds most managers'' capabilities, despite the existence of numerous data sets. 
This project aims to develop a Digital Twin, a real-time, data-backed digital replica of the ground situation. Enhanced with machine learning and AI, it will integrate available data and models, providing easy and intuitive access to information. Grounded in real data, its outputs will be of tangible value, making it indispensable to water resource managers once fully developed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8470?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6927, 'Seasonal water availability forecast in the Limpopo River Basin using a machine learning and hydrological model', 'Water resource management is a critical concern in semi-arid regions Southern Africa, where seasonal variations in water availability can have profound socio-economic and environmental impacts. This study focuses on the application of the Soil and Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) model to enhance seasonal forecasting of water availability in the Limpopo River Basin as a part of larger transboundary river basin digital twin.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6927?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(8570, 'Virtual Food Policy Coach: a customized AI chatbot for food policy research', 'We created a virtual food policy expert using a customized AI chatbot system to understand the benefits and limitations of chatbots in supporting food policy research. A comparative assessment was made of the results obtained from asking the same food policy-related questions to different AI sources, including the customized AI virtual food policy expert. Chatbots proved useful for summarizing research and providing initial information. However, their usefulness depends on the volume of information uploaded and their ability to draw from various sources. While they can answer a specific set of questions, for detailed and country-specific information, human experts are still needed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8570?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(18238, 'Payments for Ecosystem Services tender approach for Incentivizing nature-positive farming practices in Northern Vietnam', 'We describe an approach for incentivizing nature-positive farming practices, comprising of: (1) Best-Worst Scaling workshops to identify farmers'' preferred practices, (2) Collecting competitive group tender bids from interested farmer groups for implementing the practices. Groups quantify their targets for implementation, and determine the in-kind compensation they require in return of ecosystem service provision, (3) Technical guidance in implementing the practices, (4) Distribution of the agreed rewards upon successful completion.
We applied the approach with indigenous farmers from in four communes in Northern Vietnam. Most preferred practices were improving agrobiodiversity by integrating native trees in farming systems and restoring erosion-prone lands through tree planting. In total 11 bids involving 81 farmers from 9 villages were selected for implementation, based on e.g. cost per area restored, species choices, and equity considerations (e.g. gender balance). In total 52 hectares were restored, 18,270 trees planted, 3 smallholder nurseries established, and rewards worth US$11,578 paid out.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18238?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4139, 'Uber H3 level 6 hexagons Database for characterization of sub national units for key countries', 'We develop a novel approach for spatial analysis based on databases characterizing sub-national units in various countries using Uber H3 level-6 hexagons—a spatial grid system. Uber''s H3 system optimizes spatial data exploration, enhancing geographic information analysis across various data types: crops, livestock, population, and climate. Unique identifiers in H3 hexagons aid data aggregation, outperforming square pixels for broad spatial coverage. Note: Hexagon size may differ from actual pixels like SPAM; values vary by location and underlying pixels. Interpret as important indicators, not absolute values. Our database serves researchers, policymakers, and organizations keen on foresight analysis. We actively promote H3 system integration into existing models, offering documentation, tutorials, and support. Preliminary appraisals ensure system accuracy and reliability. This innovation provides invaluable insights using efficient spatial data analysis for foresight purposes, benefiting stakeholders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4139?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(2887, 'Vegetatively Propagated Crop decision support tools for seed distribution programs to manage epidemics.', 'We developed a draft guide and a draft brief about decision support for distributing disease-free seed to help manage invasive pathogens. This guide (and brief) is now available at the webpage, and we will work to add more case studies in 2025 with the Seed Equal team and make them widely available and develop applications in Seed Equal projects.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2887?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(2876, 'Vegetatively Propagated Crop decision support tools for clean seed replacement in farmer fields.', 'We developed a draft guide and a draft brief about decision support for the frequency of clean seed replacement.  In 2024 we developed these drafts further with the Seed Equal team and made them widely available and developed applications in Seed Equal projects.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2876?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(3802, 'Gender inclusion checklist to support crop breeding organizations and farm station management staff to help assess and improve gender inclusion in their workplace', 'We developed a gender inclusion checklist specifically for breeders and management of farm operations to improve inclusion of women in the workplace. The document includes an action scale assessment system to ensure gender inclusion initiatives are implemented on the organizational and farm station levels.  This checklist will help managers and breeders acknowledge the strengths and weaknesses of their organization’s gender inclusion initiatives and measures. It will also help guide and encourage the discussion of gender-related issues within within the organization.  We suggest improvements to increase women staff in five key Action Areas: work organization, Improvement of work equipment, making facilities inclusive, capacity development, and compliance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3802?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(19240, 'Tm-shift markers designer pipeline', 'We developed a pipeline for the design of Tm-shift markers to use for marker-assisted selection needs from the bean program. The code was deposited on the Github repository (https://github.com/Bioinflows-Bioversity-CIAT/tm-shift_designer).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19240?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(8968, 'Implementation of Diversity for Restoration (D4R) to guide species and seed source selections for trees-based restoration in the Colombian Amazon and Central America.', 'We expanded the Diversity for Restoration (D4R) tool to the Colombian, Ecuadorian and Peruvian Amazon and Central America, which includes approx. 270 tree species for which we carried out suitability modelling under current and future climate, collected species characterization data, and for which we are collecting seed supply data with our project partner CorpoAmazonia which focuses on 70 species. The tool is aimed for extension workers, technical support staff, donors, institutors, etc.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8968?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5370, 'Socio-technical innovation bundles dashboard developed by CGIAR''s Initiative on Gender Equality and designed to inform research on women empowerment and resilience', 'We have co-developed and co-designed a dashboard with an interactive map that dynamically displays the global distribution of socio-economic datasets, data, and tools that facilitate bundling in different local, regional, and thematic contexts. Gender and socioeconomic studies from the CGIAR Gender Impact Platform were used to compile the existing STIBs, and more data from learning labs in Kenya, Ethiopia, and India will be added subsequently. Additional socio-economic and gender inventory data has been extracted from FAO’s Agricultural Innovation Systems and the World Bank, but not clustered into different bundles. Out of the 5504 datasets currently housed in the dashboard, 450 have been assessed for their alignment with EMPOWER work package definition of STIBs. The remaining 5054 datasets are inventory data. The inventory data contains 151 from the Living Standards Measurement Study (LSMS), 1314 from FAO’s Agricultural Innovation Systems (AIS), and 3589 from the World Bank’s Agriculture Data.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5370?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(16900, 'Development and deployment of conventionally-bred high-grain Zinc rice varieties', 'We have developed and released high-zinc rice varieties with 8-10 ppm more grain Zinc content compared to the popular rice varieties. These high Zn rice varieties have 40-50% more grain Zn content in the milled rice in comparison to popularly cultivated rice varieties (12-16 ppm baseline), which provides 25 to 30% of the estimated average daily Zn required by the population, which is sufficient to overcome health risks associated with severe Zn deficiency in the vulnerable populations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16900?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(17310, 'Characterization of rice heat MAGIC population to map QTLs/genes useful for heat tolerance rice breeding', 'We have developed rice heat Multiparent Advanced Generation Inter-Cross (MAGIC) population, sequenced, characterized under controlled phytotron and heat-stress field conditions. Mapped Quantitative Trait Loci (QTLs) and genes conferring heat tolerance and identified promising heat-tolerant MAGIC lines.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17310?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7424, 'My Farm Trees: Digital platform with blockchain to incentivize farmer- and community-led tree-based restoration of degraded landscapes', 'We have piloted and trained a wide range of stakeholders in the use of the myfarmtrees mobile phone based applications.
This included the seed collection stakeholders in Kenya and Cameroon involved in native tree seed systems
The platform enables documentation and verification of seed sources, nursery propogation and on farm trees.
The platform provides digital payments to farmers to incentives trees on farm.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7424?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(3082, 'Validation and feasibility of AI-assisted dietary assessment mobile app (FRANI) for nutrient intake and personalized nudging to improve diets in female youth in Urban Ghana', 'We previously developed and assessed the validity and feasibility of using innovative AI mobile technology that provides offline real-time diagnostics and tailored "nudging" on dietary intake as a strategy to improve diets and nutrition of adolescents living in urban settings in Ghana and Viet Nam. The innovative AI app FRANI (Food Recognition and Nudging Insights) was developed and tested in younger female adolescent girls aged 15-17 years with promising results. This study aims at further developing the app and evaluating it’s use in older adolescent females aged 18-24 years. Our hypothesis is that by addressing knowledge-related barriers to healthy food choices using information tailored to existing circumstances, adolescents will improve behaviors related to food consumption and diets, and potentially improving behaviors of their peers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3082?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(17571, 'Development and deployment of Iron, Zinc and provitamin A-enriched transgenic rice varieties', 'We successfully deregulated the Provitamin A enriched Golden Rice variety in the Philippines, also successfully secured food and feed safety approvals in Canada, USA, Australia, and New Zealand, and it is under review in Indonesia and Bangladesh. We have developed multi-nutrient-rich rice breeding lines stacked with Iron, Zinc, and Provitamin A. These lines are in the advanced stages of field evaluation in our confined field facility at the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI). The regulatory package dossier for high Iron and Zinc transgenic rice has been finalized for submission to regulatory agencies in the target countries.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17571?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2851, 'SenseMaker+: adapted version of SenseMaker ethnographic research tool that applies a gender lens to analyze climate risks, vulnerabilities, and resilience in project locations', 'We use Sensemaker, a transdisciplinary digital ethnographic tool that allows combining data analytics with personal, unique human stories and experiences. By integrating a feminist approach in digital innovation, we created a transformative approach, “Sensemaker with a gender lens.” We attempted to capture robust and real-time analysis of climate risks, vulnerabilities, and resilience in our project locations. We designed the signification framework by engaging the preliminary respondents in the research design. The framework included i) a prompt question: and ii) a predefined series of “signifiers,” or questions to allow respondents to explore their narratives further.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2851?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(3145, 'Conventional polymerase chain reaction (PCR) assay for detection and characterization of bacterial leaf blight in wheat applied by ICARDA’s Germplasm Health Unit', 'Wheat plays a crucial role in global food security, sustaining millions of smallholder farmers. Bacterial leaf blight or leaf necrosis disease, caused by Pseudomonas syringae pv. syringae (Pss), poses a threat to wheat crops, affecting yield and quality. Accurate disease detection is vital for effective management strategies. A validated molecular tool, a standardized conventional polymerase chain reaction (PCR) assay for Pss, has been developed by ICARDA’s Germplasm Health Unit. This innovation enables precise monitoring of leaf necrosis disease occurrence and development. The PCR assay is now available for National Plant Protection Organizations, contributing to enhanced disease control measures. Given wheat''s significance, these advancements promise to fortify food security and safeguard the livelihoods of smallholder farmers globally.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3145?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(6567, 'Irrigation water measurement: Standard operating procedures for quantifying the irrigation water used and for assessing water use efficiency and  water productivity  in agriculture systems', 'With declining water availability due to climate change, making the most use of it is crucial in adapting agricultural 
systems to a changing climate. Furthermore, optimal use of water through irrigation scheduling (when and how 
much) can increase the quantity and quality of produce. Aside from the positive effects on reducing water application 
in agricultural systems, positive effects have been found of improved water management on grain filling, size, color 
and shelf-life of produce, and marketable yield — resulting in higher market prices and income of farmers. In rice-based systems, in particular, co-benefits of improved water management on greenhouse gas emission mitigation have been reported.

The Standard Operating Procedures, developed for the use of water scientists, agronomists and other researchers, provides the step-by-step procedure to determine the irrigation water use and the total amount of water applied in the field to evaluate water use efficiency and productivity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6567?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(19571, 'Decentralizing GIFT seed supply system in Nigeria – Scaling model for species diversification​', 'With fish consumption rising globally but per capita supply declining in sub-Saharan Africa, Nigeria’s reliance on single-species aquaculture limits sector growth. To address this, a decentralized GIFT (Genetically Improved Farmed Tilapia) seed supply system is being established to increase access, improve growth performance, and support species diversification. Following the successful transfer of GIFT to Premium Aquaculture Limited (PAL) in Ogun State, a breeding population was established, demonstrating a 20% higher growth rate over 60 days compared to older strains. Best management practices (BMPs) were developed, and training programs expanded to farmers. Satellite hatcheries were set up in Delta State and expanded to two more states, increasing farmer access.
This innovative scaling model fin Nigeria not only improves the availability of high-performance tilapia seed, but also creates opportunities for expanding other species value chains, fostering a more diversified and sustainable aquaculture industry in Nigeria and beyond.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19571?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(7246, ' Optimizing Sweet Potato Breeding Pipelines:Utilizing Desired Gain Selection Indices for Enhanced Parent Selection in Collaboration with ABI-accelerate. Improving Hybrid Breeding Programs for Uganda, Mozambique, and The National Agricultural Research Organization(Uganda)', 'With the support of the quantitative genetics team of ABI-accelerate, CIP has implemented the Pesek Baker and the desired gains selection index to select parents the sweet potato breeding programs of 1) CIP Uganda 40 parents for the hybrid breeding scheme,2) CIP Mozambique -50 parents for the hybrid breeding scheme and NARO (Uganda NARS) 50 parents for reciprocal recurrent selection.   Progenies these new sets of parents are under evaluation across the sweet potato breeding programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7246?phase=3', 1, NULL),
(16567, 'Online training/education material shared through private sector digital platforms for smallholder farmers  in Ayeyawardy Delta Regions in Myanmar', 'Within the context of the Asian-Mega Deltas Initiative Work Package 1 and building ongoing work on climate information services in Myanmar, the project team from the Alliance of Bioversity International and CIAT in collaboration with Village Link Company Limited focused on the development of e-training materials to promote Smart Aquaculture and Smart Agriculture using short video format to provide more in-depth information which can complement the current agro-advisories developed by Village Link in the Irrawaddy Delta, Myanmar. This is an opportunity to support farmers with more specific, qualified, and user-friendly information so that farmers can implement agricultural practices in more appropriate ways.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/16567?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(2596, 'Demand-side innovations to accelerate varietal turnover (such as seed trial packs, cooking demonstrations, consumer trial packs, crop insurance, agricultural advisories, leveraging endowment effects, nudging, social distance)', 'Work Package 3 (Behavioral Intelligence) is testing two innovations to address behavioral barriers to adopting new varieties across 6 different experiments. One intervention is the provision of free trial packs of seeds, which helps farmers overcome aversion to risky experimentation with new varieties. The trial pack is only meant to cover a portion of a farmer''s agricultural land, but large enough for farmers to learn and experiment with how the variety works. The second intervention is a cooking demonstration and free sample of the "end product" (such as flour). This is meant to help farmers overcome risks of experimentation with varieties that they may not want to consume and misperceptions that improved varieties do not taste good.  We also test site-specific interventions, including crop insurance in Kenya and Ethiopia, agricultural advisories in Nigeria and Kenya, leveraging endowment effects and nudging in Uganda, and social distance with champion farmers in India.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2596?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(19112, 'Performance tracking dashboard for Aquatic Food interventions: Integrating MEL data for adaptive management and strategic intelligence', 'WorldFish and its partners use a MEL System to track aquatic food interventions through key indicators, supporting a better understanding of project implementation and impact. The innovation is a dashboard that allows users to visualize the status of deliverables, capacity development activities, technical reports, and knowledge products stored in the WorldFish MEL System. It also provides insights into their characteristics and relevance.
From these data, a quarterly performance assessment report is produced. This focuses on deliverables, capacity development efforts, knowledge production, and reporting status, with data analytics helping to interpret trends. This tool simplifies performance tracking in the aquatic food sector by offering a strategic view of how new data and knowledge are produced, where they are generated, and which research directions are emerging. By improving access to structured information, the dashboard enhances decision-making and investments and supports the continuous refinement of research and development efforts in aquatic foods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/19112?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(18250, 'Promoting Resilient Nutrition-Sensitive Deltaic Agri-Food Systems in Cambodia', 'WorldFish and the International Institute of Rural Reconstruction have jointly introduced cost-effective nutrition-sensitive actions, business cases, and technologies for marginalized and smallholder farmers. The co-produced knowledge was shared with various stakeholders including producers, consumers, teachers, and students through various innovative training workshop approaches to enhance their capabilities and nutrition-sensitive agri-food systems in Theay and Boeng Preah communes of Ba Phnum district, Prey Veng province, Cambodia. Among these innovative approaches include the conduct of nutrition education in schools and communities, the use of demonstration farms, cooking demonstrations, the establishment of farmer producer groups, and exchange visits among others. These approaches are being scaled to other locations in the Mekong Delta.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/18250?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(2470, 'Innovative strategies to scale up fish powder for the nutrition of infants and young children in Zambia', 'WorldFish is promoting fish powders by fostering public-private partnerships to scale up the availability, utilization, and consumption of fish powder to improve the health and well-being of infants and young children during the first thousand days of life, pregnant and lactating women, and other vulnerable people. Innovative strategies to scale up the availability of fish powder to improve the health and well-being of infants and young children during the first thousand days of life, pregnant and lactating women, and other vulnerable people are now being explored utilizing earlier research findings and mobilizing sub-national and national partnerships. The innovative strategies include promoting complementary innovations along the value chain such as technologies to mill, package and market fish powder and training local fish producers in capture fisheries and aquaculture to meet food safety standards for a quality product.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2470?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9124, 'Strategies for the management of seed yam reinfection and seed degeneration in seed yam production plots', 'Yam mosaic virus (YMV) transmitted by aphid vectors is the most important production constraint to seed and ware yam production in West Africa. Due to the lack of host resistance, protecting clean seed yams from YMV reinfection by aphids is crucial to prevent yield losses and seed degeneration. Trials were organized to identify methods to avoid YMV infection using two Dioscorea rotundata varieties. Approaches used include: (i) use of tall growing cereal as a barrier to interfere with aphid movement, and (ii) 3% (v/v) mineral (paraffin) oil sprays to prevent aphid feeding. The second-year trials in 2024 continued this model but only using insect-proof net instead of tall growing cereal as a barrier as mineral oil sprays were ineffective. YMV incidence increased from the first month after planting, with the highest incidence of 70% in control after three months. There is a delay in infection in protected plots.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9124?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(2955, 'Integrated agronomic management strategies to enhance performance of mixed farming systems in Malawi', 'Yield stability is achieved, system productvity increased', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2955?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(7261, 'Yogurt three power- High nutritional value yogurt to help reduce children''s anemia".', 'Yogurt Three Power is a functional yogurt with 3 high sources of protein and iron: spirulina, qushuro (Andean seaweed) and bovine blood. Probiotics will be added to these inputs, to later go through an encapsulation process. The already enriched capsules will be added to the yogurt, obtaining a product with high nutritional value. The encapsulation process will ensure correct assimilation by allowing the nutrients and probiotics to reach suitable areas of the digestive tract, thus differentiating them from existing dairy products. This product seeks to contribute to the fight against anemia in children in Peru.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7261?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(9262, 'A scalable, multi-actor in vitro system for mass production and distribution of four released matooke hybrids, enabling timely access to improved planting material through public and private sector collaboration.', ' This innovation introduces a decentralized, scalable system for the mass production and distribution of four released matooke hybrids—TARIBAN1 to TARIBAN4—in Tanzania. It combines the in vitro multiplication capacities of two public research labs (TARI Arusha and Dar es Salaam) and two SMEs (Crop Bioscience Solutions and Mauri Mazuri) to ensure a reliable supply of quality planting materials. By linking production to nurseries and distributing plantlets through both public and private channels, the innovation increases farmer access to improved banana varieties. It supports faster adoption, enhances varietal reach, and strengthens the role of public–private partnerships in seed system development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9262?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6700, 'Development and piloting the conceptual framework "Agency and Behavior Change in Transforming agri-food systems" ACT, dedicated to researchers and practitioners', 'A systems perspective is crucial to understanding behavior change processes in agri-food systems transformations. Drawing from three existing frameworks, we developed a conceptual framework of agency and behavior change for agri-food system transformations. It is meant to go beyond the “technology transfer” paradigm in agri-food system research for development, to guide researchers and practitioners in a systems approach that improves understanding of what is enabling or impeding individual and group agency and behavior change in agri-food systems. The Agency and Behavior Change in Transforming agri-food systems (ACT) framework also enables a systematic approach to identifying and addressing gender, social inclusion and and power asymmetries. We expect that along with the use of this framework, more formative research will be conducted to understand food system actor experiences, constraints, and opportunity spaces. We expect the impact of this approach to research and action will be to more effectively achieve agency and behavior change targets, and resulting agri-food system transformations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6700?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(814, 'User interface of e-Agrology directed to farmers as a one stop shop where farmers can consult localized decision support for the management of their fields', 'AGROTUTOR, a decision-making framework for smallholder farmers in Mexico, Peru and Colombia, prioritizes practicality and actionable information. Unlike offering unique recommendations per plot, it provides scenarios, benchmarks, and tools empowering farmers to make informed, tailored choices. Operating on a circular knowledge approach, it collects farmer data and feedback, enhancing advisory services. 
AGROTUTOR''s flexible architecture integrates information from diverse sources, allowing farmers to compare results from different models and data sources. Its strength lies in geo-localized data, using AI models for crop-specific optimization, weather updates, pest identification, and agronomic recommendations. Historical training data spanning a decade helps to identify limiting factors, provide insightful comparisons among soil and climate clusters. The data wallet feature and digital identity ensure farmers'' data rights and introduce a compensation model, treating their data as an asset. With modular and online/offline functionality, AGROTUTOR also fosters peer-to-peer knowledge sharing through WhatsApp community groups.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/814?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(856, 'The Herd Dynamics Model (HDM) component of the Linked Economic and Animal Systems (LEAS) model, a tool for or policy analysis on livestock management in Ethiopia', 'Animal systems often receive less attention or are treated separately from crops in national agricultural strategies and investment plans. To address this, the Foresight Initiative developed a standardized Herd Dynamics Model (HDM) to analyze trends and investments in animal systems. Supported by a global livestock database with reconciled historical data and demographic parameters, the HDM enables integrated analysis of crop and animal system priorities when embedded in agrifood system models. Piloted in an Ethiopia case study, and in early second-phase testing in Rwanda, Tanzania and Nigeria, this system reduces the cost of modeling animal systems in national planning.

Building on this foundation, the Linked Economic and Animal Systems (LEAS) model now includes sheep and goats alongside cattle. Work on crop-livestock interactions in semi-subsistence economies was presented at ICAE 2024. The model has expanded to Rwanda to analyze livestock interventions, with IFPRI collaborating with ILRI to advance and align livestock modeling efforts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/856?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(662, 'Novel Indicators for an Agri-Food System Dashboard Empowering Evidence-Based Policy for Sustainable Growth', 'As agricultural growth accelerates and food systems gain focus, the need for robust indicators to quantify the scale and composition of agri-food systems has become critical. Addressing this gap, we developed two pioneering indicators: AgGDP+ measures total value added within key agri-food system segments, while AgEMP+ tracks total employment, including informal and self-employment, with gender disaggregation. Utilizing data and statistical imputation, we provide consistent estimates at national, regional, and global levels for 2010–2019.

To continually improve, we plan to gather the most recent Input-Output Tables (IOTs), Supply Use Tables (SUTs), and other essential data to update and expand measurements to additional years. With new Social Accounting Matrices (SAMs), we aim to extend coverage to more countries. Complemented by an online dashboard offering open access, these indicators provide the foundation for evidence-based policy and analysis of agri-food systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/662?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(456, 'An innovative genetic improvement strategy for sheep and goat that involves the end users, farmers, in the design and implementation of the scheme', 'Breeding programs that are community-based cover a range of situations. Typically, they target low input systems with farmers within a limited geographical area who have a common interest to work together for the improvement of the genetic resources of their animals. Community-based breeding programs (CBBPs) focus on indigenous stock and consider farmers’ needs, views, decisions and active participation, from inception through to implementation. Most importantly, they provide a participatory and bottom-up approach. CBBP combines farmer training to improve selection methods, consisting of pooling community flocks to create a larger gene pool from which breeding animals can be selected, scientific support to provide farmers with information on different breeding options and data collection to monitor how well individual animals perform. The entire community flock is treated as one. Livestock keepers who choose to participate in the program are organized into sheep/goat breeding associations, many of which later evolve into formal cooperatives.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/456?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2252, 'e-Agrology Central America (Guatemala, Honduras): Digital monitoring system for sustainable and climate-smart production', 'e-Agrology Central America is a data collection system for agriculture that allows capturing detailed agronomic data from farmers'' plots and research trials. It collects approximately 500 variables including agronomic practices, costs, yield, field visits, polygons, postharvest and commercialization. It works both in web and mobile applications and it is ready to be used in the region including specific catalogues of crops, seeds, products, political divisions, ethnic groups and agronomic cycles.  Customizations were also made to the currency configuration and a conversion tool was built-in to convert local to universal units of measure such as cuerdas, squares, tasks, pounds, and quintals. Additionally, it has a Project Management module, specifically developed for AgriLAC which allows to register Innovations, Training and Knowledge products.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2252?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(634, 'Managing time in rice-based systems to close yield gaps and improve climate resilience of smallholder farmers in eastern India', 'Excellence in Agronomy is developing a demand-driven, spatially explicit planting date advisory prototype to optimize resource use and enhance profitability in rice-wheat systems, addressing landscape heterogeneity and monsoon variability. This digital agricultural service, focused on small holder farmers in Eastern Gangetic Plain (EGP), India, includes a data-driven dashboard enabling informed decision-making for timely rice planting, based on a multi-criteria assessment of irrigation availability, investment costs, risks, and forecast information.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/634?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(5078, 'Political Economy and Policy Analysis (PEPA) Sourcebook: A guide to generating evidence for National Policies and Strategies (NPS) for Food, Land, and Water Systems Transformation', 'Food system transformation require reforms at global, national, and local levels to address prevailing structural weaknesses exacerbated by multiple crisis. As context matters, the question of what works where, why, and how for scaling innovations for achieving impact requires the application of political economy and policy analysis. Evidence-based from political economy analysis are critical to understanding the [dis-]enabling policy, institutional and governance conditions contributing to the success or failure of development interventions and innovations. This Political Economy and Policy Analysis (PEPA) Sourcebook for food systems transformations is a unique collection of frameworks and analytical tools for unraveling the interaction between policies and economic processes to unpack the power relationships, ideas, policy risks and decision making. This spans across food and nutrition; land and water; and climate and ecology systems to design politically feasible and technically sound development programs for achieving greater impact at the national and local levels.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5078?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1495, 'Human-centered design for CGIAR and partners to enable more human-centered and inclusive digital innovations through research, capacity building, and use case support', 'Human-Centered Design (HCD) is a foundational approach centered on meeting user needs in product and service creation. As an open-ended, iterative process, HCD seeks to creatively address problems with solutions that are desirable, technically feasible, and financially viable. In CGIAR digital innovation development, our focus is on systematically integrating and scale HCD into innovation design to overcome challenges such as high investment costs, low adoption rates and low gender inclusivity. We do this by focusing on 1) Creating an enabling environment for HCD in CGIAR  and 2) Systematically integrating HCD in innovaiton processes.  To reach 1) we design capacity building and training programs, we adapt and ideate tools and resources to support researchers in making their processes more human-centered. For 2) we work directly with selected projects and use cases, contributing to the human-centered innovation design process. CGIAR researchers and their partners are primary users of the innovation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1495?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9410, 'Measuring, Reporting and Verification (MRV) framework for monitoring deforestation emissions from Colombia', 'In Colombia, the sector that contributes the most to national emissions is Agriculture, Forestry, and Other Land Use (AFOLU), where the loss of natural forests for conversion to other land uses has a significant impact. Monitoring non-deforestation associated with agricultural and livestock supply chains at the farm level is a challenge. Therefore, one of the main challenges faced by the public-private initiative of the Zero Deforestation Agreements is to develop agile, cost-effective, and transparent tools and protocols to monitor compliance with non-deforestation commitments in these supply chains. The protocol developed serves as an implementation tool for the Zero Deforestation Agreements (ZDA) and can serve as a reference for other sectors and stakeholders, including the financial sector. The document provides a guide for information collection and the processes of MRV (Monitoring, Reporting, and Verification) of non-deforestation resulting from agricultural activities, thus contributing activity data to national inventories.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9410?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(8903, 'Demand creation trials to popularize new sweet potato varieties among value-chain actors in Uganda.', 'In Uganda, CIP and NARO established five demand creation trials to promote two newly released varieties (NAROSPOT 6 and NAROSPOT 7 O) and 1 pre-released variety(D20) among the value chain actors in Uganda. Two field days for creating awareness among farmers for the for the uptake of varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8903?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(4637, 'Multi-stakeholders Innovation platform to support the scaling of small ruminant production technologies including vaccines and marketing in Mali.', 'Multistakeholder platforms are used as a mechanism to harness collective action to address low involvement of grassroots stakeholders and empower livestock value chain actors to scale up innovations to increase livestock productivity. The main stakeholders of the livestock value chain delivery included small ruminant producers, private veterinarians, vaccine producers, feed and forage producers and sellers, traders of live animals, extension, public veterinary services, amongst others. Actors directly supporting farmers such as financial institutions and community leaders were also included. Multistakeholder platforms facilitate the delivery of livestock vaccines and the creation of business models for Brachiaria seed and fodder production and sale, and the market linkage between producers and traders of live animals. Multistakeholder platforms  serve as a mechanism for scaling all the innovation under Sustainable Animal Productivity (SAPLING) in Mali.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4637?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(457, ' An experimental vaccine for the control of East Coast Fever, developed in partnership with an animal health company and used by livestock keepers to reduce morbidity and mortality in herds.', 'The assessment of an experimental vaccine for the control of East Coast fever (ECF). Thelieria parva, the causative agent of ECF, is a killer parasite infecting cattle. The experimental vaccine is based on the use of nanoparticles to present and deliver p67C antigen. In future the vaccine will be developed by an animal health company and used by livestock keepers to significantly reduce morbidity and mortality in their cattle herds, with a focus on cattpe type parasites. The new technology will also validate the nanoparticle platform for the delivery of subunit vaccines for livestock.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/457?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(168, 'Circular Bioeconomy Innovation Hub in Ghana: A Multistakeholder Platform for scaling circular economy innovations to minimize urban environmental pollution', 'The Circular Bioeconomy Innovation Hub (CBEIH) is one-stop-shop for training, research, advisory support, collaboration and knowledge sharing. The hub unites 17 stakeholders from the public and private sectors, research institutions, non-governmental organizations, and education to promote circular bioeconomy initiatives. Operating in the organic waste-to-resource value chain, a co-ownership model that ensures shared goals and resources for co-design and co-implementation is used for maximum collective impact. The hub aims to showcase, optimize, and promote existing circular bioeconomy innovations while fostering the development of new, accessible innovations. This is accomplished through five strategic operational areas: demand-driven capacity building; research for innovation and scaling; advisory support for policy development and implementation; monitoring, evaluation, and learning; and information sharing, collaboration, and partnerships. To promote innovation adoption, the hub also works with scaling partners.
In 2024, the hub has successfully organized and co-organized several multi-stakeholder workshops on waste valorization. It is currently piloting waste source-segregation in schools in Ghana, targeting not only students but also aiming to transfer knowledge and experience to catalyze change in their homes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/168?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5275, 'Climate Security Programming Dashboard for Climate Finance (CSPDxCF) to assess conflict sensitivity of proposals for international financial institutions and climate funds', 'The Climate Security Programming Dashboard for Climate Finance (CSPDxCF) is a comprehensive tool designed for initial conflict sensitive assessments and guidance for projects funded by the Green Climate Fund (GCF) along with other climate funds and financial institutions. In the project design phase, CSPD assists Accredited Entities (AEs) and Direct Entities (DEs) in identifying operational risks and entry points for peace-informed action, providing tailored guidance on integrating climate strategies with security programming. Additionally, CSPD enhances AEs and DEs due diligence and risk management, optimizing project design in fragile and conflict-affected settings.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5275?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(2917, 'Customizing Digital AgroClimate Advisory Mobile Application in Ghana to support agricultural value chain actors', 'The Digital AgroClimate Advisory (HingaSmart) is designed to support agricultural value chain actors with bundled agro-climate advisory services. HingaSmart has been customized to generate context-specific agro-climate advisories for Ghana and other countries. To contextualize the HingaSmart app for Ghana, key crops from the three major Agro-Ecological Zones (AEZs) were mapped using the Ghana land use map. The highest-ranked major crops per AEZ were then subjected to a detailed analysis to determine key descriptors, including region, district, altitude, maximum and minimum temperature, fertilizer recommendations, crop water requirements, crop coefficient (KC) per crop stage, and the seasonal average KC for each crop. In the second year, additional location-specific data on crops and fertilizer recommendations were collected. This information was integrated with climate data to digitally deliver context-specific, location-based, and actionable recommendations along agricultural value chains in Ghana.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2917?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(5290, 'Design innovations in making social protection gender-transformative to reduce normative constraints for women and girls', 'The innovation brings together evidence review, co-design of a specific innovation with partners, an impact evaluation testing that innovation, and resulting guidance for partners on implementing the innovation. The goal of the innovation is to make social protection gender-transformative, thus addressing normative constraints for women and girls. The specific example of the design innovation tested in the impact evaluation includes engaging men in gender dialogue sessions, focusing on gender roles in domestic work, and building this component on to a graduation model social protection program in Ethiopia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5290?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3855, 'integrated Future Estimator for Emissions and Diets (iFEED) inclusive assessment framework to inform policies for climate-resilient and nutrition secure futures', 'The integrated Future Estimator for Emissions and Diets (iFEED) is an inclusive integrated assessment framework that combines climate-food-emissions modelling with land use dynamics, trade and nutrition analysis as well as expert judgement to develop policy recommendations towards a climate-resilient and nutrition-secure future. iFEED assesses both climate-smart agriculture and food and nutrient supplies under different climate change and policy scenarios. iFEED has been used in Malawi, South Africa, Tanzania, and Zambia. Through ClimBeR, iFEED is continuing to be developed in Zambia and is expanding to Kenya. iFEED is led by the University of Leeds (lead of ClimBeR Work Package 3) in collaboration with several partners, including the Food, Agriculture, and Natural Resources Policy Analysis Network, UK Met Office and other partners. A technical working group consisting of policymakers, researchers, and other stakeholders is engaged at every stage of the development process to ensure results are policy relevant.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3855?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(10494, 'Research tools on the impact of gender norms on women''s economic resilience to climate change in order to develop gender transformative approaches to improve women''s resilience', 'The project developed a toolbox to understand the impact of gender norms on women’s economic reliance to climate change impacts. The adapted qualitative tools are used to identify and map the key stakeholders in agricultural and fisheries value chains and ii) understand which norms constrain women and other groups from participating in and benefiting from work in the different nodes of the value chain iii) how women and other value chain players at the different nodes of agricultural value chains are coping with climate change impacts and also make use of Vignettes and attitudes and behaviour assessment plots to analyze whether or not gender norms that may affect value chain actors are weakening or shifting. This analysis will also allow us to see under what conditions actors can deviate from the norm and how that impacts their economic resilience to climate change impacts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10494?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6222, 'Nexus Capacity Scorecard: An Assessment Tool to Enhance Institutional Readiness for Sustainable Water-Energy-Food-Environment (WEFE) Nexus Approaches', 'The Water-Energy-Food-Environment (WEFE) Nexus Capacity Scorecard (NCS) is part of a toolkit developed to support organizations to assess their institutional processes, readiness, and capacities in understanding and implementing integrated approaches across the water, energy, food, and environmental sectors. It helps to identify opportunities, possible gaps, and inform the planning of tailored nexus capacity-building efforts, programs and interventions, and/or their monitoring over time. The tool can be used by any organization (public, non-governmental, civic, private, research, etc.) and in multiple contexts. It is adaptable to different program need and can also serve as a launchpad for discussions, within and across organizations and sectors, for improving WEFE nexus practice and collaboration.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6222?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(9441, 'Gender Equality and Social Inclusion Strategy for the CGIAR Initiative on Aquatic Foods', 'This CGIAR Initiative on Aquatic Foods: Gender Equality and Social Inclusion (GESI) Strategy is intended for CGIAR scientists and development practitioners, project managers and Work Package (WP) leads working in the initiative. Other CGIAR initiatives will be encouraged to use the strategy to promote GESI in Aquatic Foods research and project cycle management, and in their engagements with policymakers. The goal is for the Strategy to help guide the work of collaborating partners and other stakeholders who engage with CGIAR, and other research and development organizations working to empower Aquatic foods smallholders. The purpose is to ensure that women, youth and marginalized people, and their families and communities, live better lives and engage in sustainable livelihoods through aquatic foods. Effective strategies for scaling gender equality and social inclusion will be developed through close partnerships with governments, research and development partners.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9441?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5043, 'Optimal groundnut plant density for improved grain yield, fodder availability and quality in smallholder farming systems of northern Ghana', 'This innovation addresses the challenge of low grain yield and fodder availability in groundnut-small ruminant farming system in northern Ghana. Traditionally, smallholder farmers plant groundnut at a density of less than 10 plants/square meter. Planting ground at a density of 22 plants per square meter increases the rate of canopy closure over the soil surface which protects the soil from erosion, improves water infiltration as well as soil moisture retention, increases grain and fodder yields. The plant density of 22 plants per square meter can be achieved by either sowing the groundnut seed at a spacing of 30 cm inter-row × 15 cm intra-row or 40 cm inter-row × 10 cm intra-row. Feeding groundnut fodder generated from the 22 plants per square meter to sheep increases dry matter digestibility, live weight gain and nitrogen concentration of manure relative to feeding those generated from the farmer practice.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5043?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3548, 'Enterprise Breeding System, a data management system for breeding data, operations, analytics, and decision support, that will accelerate and increase the cost effectiveness of breeding programs', 'We completed an updated version of the Enterprise Breeding System, a data management system for breeding operations, analytics, and decision support. The new version facilitates breeders and their support personnel in their daily work, speeding up and reducing  workload in key processes like planting, crossing, harvesting, sampling, and phenotype and genotype data collection, so that they can more efficaciously produce improved germplasm.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3548?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(455, 'Co-operative for women and men beef-cattle artificial inseminators, for improved artificial insemination sevice provision and increased income generation by the service providers.', 'We will develop improved gender-sensitive business models for beef-cattle artificial insemination (AI) service providers in Vietnam, with a focus on economic performance and social responsibility. This will support the creation of business opportunities for animal health-care workers (offering the AI services), resulting in income generation and diversification. An increased number of AI service providers, offering quality service and semen from farmer-preferred breeds, is also likely to impact positively on adoption rates of AI, in turn-lead leading to increased beef-cattle productivity and profitability of smallholder beef cattle enterprises. To date, participatory business-model studies have been completed, and a key constraint of lack of access to liquid nitrogen identified.  To address this, we are supporting interested AI service providers to formulate a co-operative including shared access to liquid nitrogen.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/455?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4104, 'Zambia Drought Management System for environmental and agricultural policy makers to design and implement science-based, integrated, and practical drought interventions benefiting climate vulnerable rural communities', 'Zambia Drought Management System (ZADMS) for environmental and agricultural policy makers', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4104?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4979, 'Information service, targeting smallholder bean producers with strategic messages on good agricultural practices affecting bean quality from production to postharvest management, as a follow-up to existing training by farmer cooperatives.', 'The innovation consists of images, voice, and text messages sent by phone on good agricultural practices in postharvest and bean cultivation as a follow-up to existing training by a farmer cooperative. The technical lead will send these messages to the producers according to the phenological stage of the crop. In addition, the producers will be sharing information about crop development (like possible plagues, etc.) with the technical lead to prioritize assistance in specific regions. This innovation will strengthen the capacities of farmers to produce and handle the grains to achieve better quality and income.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4979?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5371, 'Animations and online visual presentations dedicated to teaching and reflections on the uptake of socio-technical innovations by women farmers', 'As most agricultural farmers especially women get older, their responsibilities increase and their patience with longs hours of face to face meetings and trainings becomes a problem. Farmers are showing interest in receiving information from the value chain through animations and visual presentations. They prefer listening to an animation of three to five minutes and having a discussion to fill any needed gaps. This way they have more time to attend to other productive or household activities. Also using animation has increased the reach of information and technologies to farmers (where to source seed, varieties to plant, preparation of land, post harvest losses, nutrition and multistakeholder platforms) through social media channels like Whatsapp.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5371?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2097, 'Catalyzing equitable, sustainable and smallholder-friendly solar energy service markets for enhancing rural livelihoods in Eastern Gangetic Plains', 'The conventional approach to promoting solar irrigation has been through offering subsidies for individual farmers to adopt solar pumps. However, this approach suffers from problems like poor targeting and elite capture, underutilization of created assets and challenges in delivering high quality O&M services. Further, the highly fragmented nature of landholdings render ownership of solar pumps by individual farmers financially and technically infeasible. Another approach has been to support community assets that are owned and operated by user groups. This can improve asset utilization but the problem of underutilization remains; it can also suffer from ''collective action'' problems as the responsibility for upkeep of assets gets distributed. With support from IWMI-Tata Program, WLE and CCAFS, IWMI has piloted ''solar irrigation service providers'' in Bihar and Jharkhand (India). Likewise, IDCOL - a government owned company in Bangladesh - has supported village-level solar irrigation enterprises on a large scale. As part of its work in Work Package #3, NEXUS Gains will build on this work and support the conceptualization, development, field testing, documentation and scaling of alternate business models for expanding access to solar energy applications for rural livelihoods in eastern Gangetic plains.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2097?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(869, 'GenderUp for scaling: A method that supports innovation teams in gender responsible scaling', 'GenderUp is a conversation-based method that supports projects and research teams in scaling agricultural innovations responsibly and socially inclusive. By making users think through the scaling process, the actual impacts for different social groups (positive and negative) and anticipate unintended consequences, GenderUp strengthens users’ capacity to scale agricultural innovations and achieve desired impacts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/869?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(2933, 'Prevalent races of wheat rust pathogens in Lebanon, Morocco, and Tunisia in 2022', 'During the 2022 rust surveys in Lebanon, Morocco, and Tunisia (WP1-OP2), rust samples were collected from survey sites. The collected rust samples were submitted to the Regional Cereal Rust Biocontainment Laboratory in Izmir, Turkey using the sampling kits and import permits granted by Turkish quarantine authorities. Viable samples were used in Races analysis. The race analysis confirmed the presence of stem rust races TKKTF and TKTTF in Lebanon and TKKTF, TKTTF, THTTF, and TTTTF in Morocco', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2933?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(458, 'Reproductive platform delivering services, such as fixed-time artificial insemination and sires’ certification, in sheep and goat Community-Based Breeding Program sites leading to genetic gain in small and medium-scale livestock systems.', 'This innovation revolves around the development of artificial insemination (AI) protocols that are adapted to low-input systems where central AI labs do not exist and where basic commodities are lacking. It aims at democratizing access to improved genetics for small ruminant farmers. The platform is a network of labs supporting the breeding programs in Ethiopia. The innovation resorts to co-investment with partners in low-infrastructure, mobile labs that are operational under remote, rural conditions. AI relies on the use of fully certified sires that are genetically superior. Vaccination against infectious diseases mitigates the risk for these sires to transmit diseases. Inseminated females are synchronized with prostaglandin-based protocols that yield clean estrus, are easy to use, cheaper and are adapted to breeds under inter- tropical and equatorial African condition. The fixed time AI protocol relies on the use of fresh, non-cooled semen that is collected, assessed, processed and subsequently packed and used.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/458?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(62, 'International cassava breeding networks accelerate genetic gains for cassava demanded by the rapidly growing processing industry; national collaborators in Colombia release two varieties, strengthening smallholders'' income opportunities', 'Cassava is widely used for local artisanal processing and on-farm consumption, with an increasing demand for large-scale processing to starch, animal feed and ethanol. In collaboration with IITA, public and private partners, and farmers globally, CIAT improves cassava for starch (tapioca flour), higher-yields, resistance to pests and diseases, and provitamin A content. Breeding pipelines are designed to pursue market-specific needs: 1. Good cooking quality cassava; 2. High and stable dry matter cassava, 3. Biofortified cassava, and 4. Waxy starch. To speed up genetic gain, rapid cycling for high-heritability traits, more precise testing approaches early in the breeding process, flowering induction, and genome-wide predictions have accelerated breeding cycles which are currently at 3 years. Inbred-parent-based hybrid cassava breeding could greatly speed up selection gains. Between 2022-2024, partners in Vietnam and Colombia released 10 varieties, three of them with waxy starch for specialty uses.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/62?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(40, 'Rice breeding in Africa blends indigenous germplasm and modern breeding approaches to develop resilient, market-demanded varieties, adapted to diverse ecologies so to increase the competitiveness of local production', 'Older low-yielding varieties still dominate in large areas of Africa. Farmers are not benefiting quickly enough from genetic gains from recently improved varieties, including those that can withstand climate change related impacts. In collaboration with national research systems, international research partners, and farmers, AfricaRice seeks to provide farmers with superior, market-demanded rice varieties, adapted to local growing conditions. The rich reservoir of genetic resources present in the indigenous Africa rice germplasm pool is combined with modern breeding approaches to increase the rate of genetic gains. Current breeding cycle time is 36 months. Eight breeding pipelines target sixteen market segments, encompassing about 12.6 M ha of rice area. In 2023, target product profiles were validated with stakeholders in 10 countries. 20 African countries routinely assess candidate varieties that identify those most likely to meet farmers'' needs. Between 2022-2024, partners in 15 countries registered 60 varieties, 43 selected for climate resilience traits.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/40?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(453, 'Improved performance and market orientation of sheep fattening by women and youth organized in groups and cooperatives in Ethiopia', 'An approach to improve performance and market orientation in sheep fattening by women and youth facilitated through collection action; to increase their access to training in improved feed and feeding technologies, husbandry, entrepreneurship skills and financial literacy; to enhance participation in market-oriented sheep fattening; and to create an enabling environment by strengthening stakeholder engagement through community-of-practice platforms.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/453?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(10035, 'Integrated Striga Management for Maize', 'Using Striga tolerant maize hybrids and a popular benchmark Striga-susceptible variety, we tested three options for Striga management in western Kenya through on-farm trials. The options were maize sole crop, maize intercropped with soybean and maize intercropped with Desmodium. Farms with heavy Striga infestation were chosen to host the trials. Eight farms were selected in three counties in western Kenya to host the trials. The trials were farmer-managed with researchers only collecting data at the critical stages. Data were collected on the number of Striga plants that had germinated in the plots and harvested field weight.  The differences in performance between the Striga tolerant hybrids and the commercial hybrid were very clear for both grain yield and Striga germination. A farmer field day was hosted at one of the sites and a flyer on Striga management were distributed to farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10035?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(3860, 'Integrated disease management of Fusarium Head Blight (FHB) in the smallholders'' cropping systems in Mexico and Zambia', 'Varietal resistance against Fusarium Head Blight (FHB) is incomplete and is subject to environmental conditions and pathogenic variation; thus an integrated disease management package involving resistant germplasm, fungicide application, cultural control methods etc. is often indispensable. Under the Plant Health Initiative (PHI), in 2022, we evaluated a set of wheat genotypes with resistance to wheat blast in field experiments in Mexico and Zambia along with different fungicide ingredients, application timing, and spray nozzles, to identify the best management approach under smallholders'' wheat cropping systems in the local environments. Final results will be available in 2023.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3860?phase=1', 0, NULL),
(2966, 'Protocols to develop country-specific livestock greenhouse gas emission factors in smallholder and pastoral systems in Africa south of the Sahara', 'Accurate reporting of livestock greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions is important in developing effective mitigation strategies. This innovation is a set of  activity data collection protocols to collect animal activity data on-farm and calculate GHG emission factors for enteric methane from cattle and small ruminants which can be applied in smallholder and pastoral livestock systems in Africa. Animal activity data include parameters such as animal numbers, herd composition, animal live weight, feed quality and quantity, manure management, and others. The protocols also describe the equations needed to calculate GHG emission factors. These emission factors can be used to develop national livestock GHG inventories on a Tier 2 level (i.e., with country-specific data) and to test the GHG mitigation potential of management interventions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2966?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(12030, 'The CGIAR initivative AgriLAC develops prototypes of more nutritious food products with the inclusion of bean, maize and sweet potato flours to enhance processing and marketing in Colombia', 'Protocols for the development of food products from biofortified bean, corn and sweet potato flours with consumption and commercialization potential in the department of Cesar, Colombia, carried out in collaboration with AGROSAVIA and the University of Cesar.  The purpose of these protocols is to generate processing and marketing options for biofortified beans, corn and sweet potato produced in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12030?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(1039, 'CGIAR Barley Breeding Toolbox: A diversity panel to facilitate breeding and genomic research in the developing world', 'Breeding programs in developing countries still cannot afford the new genotyping technologies, hindering their research. For it we have assembled an Association Mapping panel to serve as CGIAR Barley Breeding Toolbox (CBBT), especially for the Developing World. The germplasm set was designed to be representative of the varieties grown in the Developing World, with high genetic variability and of public domain. The CBBT preserves good balance between row-types and good representation of both phenology allelic combinations and subpopulations of a larger World collection. The CBBT lines and its 50K SNP genotypic data is available to researchers worldwide as a collaborative tool to underpin the genetic mechanisms of traits of interest for barley cultivation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1039?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3011, 'Regional Gender and Plant Health Network in Southeast Asia is established to mainstream gender in plant health research towards more equitable and inclusive impacts', 'This is an institutional innovation development in which the regional network on gender and plant heath was established in Southeast Asia as a model case with a potential to expand to other regions. This network enables the plant health initiative to mainstream gender as a key research agenda towards gender equitable and inclusive technology adoption and scaling. 50 members from 34 institutions in 8 countries joined the network, 80% of them are pathologists, entomologists or agronomists. The first face-to-face workshop was held in Bali in December to identify priority research areas, and gain gender-responsive research knowledge and skills. Six cross-country, interdisciplinary research proposals were developed with a primary focus on gender-related research questions: Maize fall armyworm, Banana fusarium, tomato diseases, mango fruit fly, rice pests and diseases, poultry New Castle disease. The plant health initiative will offer regular support and online meetings to strengthen their research capacity and implementation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3011?phase=3', 1, NULL),
(3416, 'AgIncentives policy indicators of Agri-Food Market and Policy Analysis Models (AMPAM) to assess potential trade-offs associated with scaling interventions and innovations along food value chains.', 'An integrated framework of global and country Agri-Food Market and Policy Analysis Models (AMPAM) is implemented to assess the potential for and trade-offs associated with scaling interventions and innovations that create jobs, boost incomes, and reduce environmental footprints along food value chains. A key component of AMPAM is the consistent and comparable set of indicators of degrees of policy support to agriculture and food sectors by type of support, food commodities, and countries. This set of policy indicators has been prepared in collaboration with the AgIncentives Consortium (FAO-IFPRI-IDB-OECD-World Bank). Together with the agrifood market database and the AMPAM modeling framework, the policy indicator dataset will be a critical ingredient for the scenario analysis the Initiative will undertake to support decision-makers in assessing the full potential, scalability, market opportunities, priorities for, and trade-offs associated with incentive schemes, and investments and innovations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3416?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(7269, 'Solar-based small scale irrigation innovation bundle for smallholders and production of high-value crop in Ethiopia', 'A solar pump for small-scale irrigation offers an effective solution for mitigating and adapting to climate change. With funding secured from the CGIAR initiative and bilateral projects, we are demonstrating a solar pump-based irrigation package through a living lab approach. With this we  brought together farmers, suppliers, microfinance institutions, and policymakers to explore options for scaling up through improved access to financing for farmers.

Building on this, we will continue to develop and test intervention packages, including optimized fertilizer use, agronomic practices, crop selection, and water management, as part of the Scaling for Impact program. These sites serve as learning hubs to train farmers, raise awareness among policymakers, and engage microfinance institutions and suppliers.

In collaboration with other system actors, we will  develop pathways for scaling these interventions to maximize their impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7269?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3341, 'Using accurate detection tools to develop a successful management strategy for lentil and chickpea viruses in farmers'' fields in the highlands of Ethiopia.', 'Since 2019, farmers in the highlands of Ethiopia are losing their chickpea and lentil crops because of virus epidemics, and lentil production area in Ethiopia was reduced due to the virus infection. Based on FAOSTAT, lentil production (tonnes) and Area Harvested (ha) in Ethiopia decreased from 166,274 tonnes and 113,685 ha during 2016 to 113,018 tonnes and 84,512 ha during 2020, respectively (FAOSTAT, 2022). Knowing the exact identity of viruses affecting both crops is essential for breeding for resistance and crop management purposes. To achieve this, field surveys were carried out for four consecutive seasons (2019-2022) in collaboration between ICARDA and Ethiopian researchers, using up-to-date detection tools to monitor virus incidence and distribution on lentil and chickpea crops in the highlands of Ethiopia. Data generated can be used by plant pathologists, scientists, breeders, plant protection organizations, policy makers, National agricultural research, and extension agencies to mitigate the viral diseases', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3341?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(782, 'Creating cost-effective home-mixed livestock fattening rations using hay, grain or pod shells of Mucuna pruriens and other legume hays to substitute expensive commercial supplements', 'This innovation involves incorporation of coarsely-ground Mucuna pruriens grain as a unique, cost-effective ingredient and energy-protein dense substitute in maintenance or fattening rations of ruminant livestock, where standard commercial supplements would usually contain a combination of cereal grains (e.g. maize) and legume oil cakes (e.g Soyabean, cotton seed etc), which are themselves generally expensive.  These expensive ingredients make the commercial concentrates out of reach for most farmers.  In contrast, Mucuna is cheaper and provides both energy and protein in one grain.  Once ground, the Mucuna meal will be constituted together with home-made tropical forage legumes (e.g. Lablab purpureus) hay to make a complete diet which smallholder farmers can brand and trade among themselves. Incorporation of legume hay reduces the amount of poor quality grass hay and cereal crop residues that will be included as basal forage in the complete diet and will simultaneously improve the digestibility of the poor quality residues, leading to improved animal growth.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/782?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(28, 'International wheat breeding networks accelerate genetic gains for climate-resilient, nutritious, market-demanded varieties with durable disease resistance for smallholders in Asia, Africa, and Latin America', 'The CIMMYT Global Wheat Program is one of the most important public sources of high yielding, nutritious, disease- and climate-resilient wheat varieties for Africa, Asia, and Latin America; its lines are found in varieties sown on more than 60 million hectares worldwide. The program works with ICARDA and more than 200 local research and breeding institutions, sharing climate-resilient, Zinc-biofortified, high- yielding, disease and pest tolerant wheat lines and data. National partners are active participants in breeding, many of them managing precise phenotyping platforms or using the lines for local breeding efforts. Molecular breeding tools, bioinformatics and speed breeding have resulted in a cycle time of 3 years in spring wheat. Between 2022 and 2024 partners registered 139 varieties, including 121 selected for climate resilience (drought and heat tolerance), and 88 varieties biofortified for Zinc and Iron that lower malnutrition in particular among women, adolescent girls and young children.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/28?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3573, 'New generation of fish friendly irrigation innovations (sluice gates, irrigation canals, dams) to strengthen the resilience and sustainability and livelihoods of fisheries-dependent communities in Asia and Africa', 'This nature-based solution serves to integrate fisheries into irrigation systems. The innovation produces mutual benefits to both fisheries and irrigation systems through allowing fish migration, the creation of new aquatic habitats for fish species in irrigated lands and the enhancement of inland fisheries productivity through integrated systems, such as irrigated rice-fish farming systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3573?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(6139, 'Integration of research and innovation processes that facilitate the co-design and testing of context-specific agroecological innovations:  A process for stakeholder engagement in Agroecological Living Landscapes ​', 'The Agroecology initiative aims to contribute to achieving agroecological transition through developing and scaling a range of innovations in 8 countries across three continents. It proposes to do so by using a participatory action-research approach involving the key stakeholders of the Agroecological Living Landscapes (ALLs), multi-stakeholder territorial spaces in each country on which the Initiative targets its transformation and innovation efforts. A major pillar of such an approach consists in codesigning the required innovations and corresponding processes as well as effecting the required changes in behavior/practices by the concerned actors. At the heart of the initiative’s ambition and approach lies the ability to mobilize and support ALL stakeholders in co-designing and implementing the diverse activities required to develop the innovations needed in each context. Hence, the initiative has designed a tool to support this process.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6139?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(2968, 'Surveillance and monitoring the cereal rust diseases in Lebanon, Morocco, and Tunisia in 2022', 'Tracking the movement of the wheat rust pathogens and monitoring their pathogenic variabilities are the main pillars in successful breeding for durable rust resistance and disease management programs. Despite the severe drought conditions in wheat growing areas in CWANA in 2022, the rust surveillance was coordinated by the Regional Cereal Rust Research Center-Turkey in Lebanon, Morocco, and Tunisia, following the BGRI rust surveillance protocols. The national rust surveillance teams at the Lebanese Agricultural Research Institute, Institut National de la Recherche Agronomique (Morocco), and INSTITUT NATIONAL DES GRANDES CULTURES-TUNISIA (Tunisia) conducted the rust surveys in target countries. The GPS coordinates of survey sites, the status of rust diseases, and crop phenology were collected and shared with the online Global Cereal Rust Surveillance and Monitoring System (https://rusttracker.cimmyt.org) following the BGRI standard rust surveillance protocol (https://rusttracker.cimmyt.org/wp-content/uploads/2011/11/2013-Updated-BGRI-protocols-v2-web.pdf).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2968?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4656, 'Farmer-friendly application (app.) for improved household nutrition, including consumption of animal source foods', 'This is a user-friendly nutrition education application (app.) targeting smallholder farmers as primary users. The App helps build smallholder chicken farmers'' knowledge in human nutrition including animal source food consumption.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4656?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(411, 'Stata command file that simplifies the calculation of all versions of the Women''s Empowerment in Agriculture Index (WEAI)', 'The WEAI ado file is a Stata user-written program that can calculate all versions of the Women''s Empowerment in Agricultural Index (WEAI) depending on the options specified by the user. WEAI is a standardized, survey-based tool that has been widely used to track gender equality and measure empowerment, agency, and women''s inclusion in the agricultural sector (Alkire et al. 2013). Since the WEAI''s release in 2012, an abbreviated version of the WEAI (referred to as A-WEAI) and a project-level version of the WEAI (referred to as pro-WEAI) have been developed (Malapit et al. 2017; Malapit et al. 2019). The tool allows users to specify the binary adequacy indicators, empowerment cutoff, and indicator weights. WEAI provides a decomposition by indicator and, when specified, by sub-groups. Note that because the WEAI requires data from women and men, users are required to specify a variable (and values) identifying the sex of respondents.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/411?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(950, 'Incorporating World Modeler tools for visualization of metrics and controlled application of models to generate metrics, into the foresight web portal', 'Foresight Initiative WP3 strives for the enhanced access, transparency and use of foresight metrics and tools, through. an outward facing foresight web portal. The initiative is developing that platform now - that both has direct tools on it and links out to other resources. World Modeler tools for visualization of metrics and application of models  could possibly be part of our platform and could fill in the gaps there. The World Modeler system could become a nice complement on the data/analytics side to the rest of the foresight portal and, likewise, has the potential help with scenario design, projected benefits analysis, and potentially quite a bit more. The intended users would be foresight analysts in focus country policy think-tanks.

While we intend to ensure gender and climate change is targeted, it is currently not yet clearly  present in the initial evidence, so these tags will be updated as the innovation develops.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/950?phase=1', 0, NULL),
(3607, 'A data migration strategy for future Enterprise Breeding System users is formalised and allows for quick, efficient adoption of EBS', 'Modern breeding is a data rich process, and requires data management software designed for the task of generating, tracking, storing and using the information produced by breeding programs for breeding decisions.  Many programs are currently not using a formal data management system; others are using older legacy systems.  With the Enterprise Breeding System becoming available, these programs can now adopt the new software.  Data migration into a new database is a complex process with many idiosyncrasies depending on how a breeding programs has been managing its data before adopting a new system, hence a clear and transparent strategy was needed.  Scope, process, and timelines will be defined so new adopters can quickly get value of the system. An important point of agreement for scope was the definition of a minimal set of operational data that would allow breeders to use the system within a year of beginning adoption.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3607?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4942, 'African Asian Dairy Genetics Gain platform, supporting dairy-cattle recording for enhanced on-farm decision-making and identification of top-bulls, used by dairy cattle producers, artifical insemination centers, and other dairy value-chain actors.', 'The African Asian Dairy Genetic Gain (AADGG) platform is an International Livestock Research Institute (ILRI)-led investment by the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation (BMGF) that has been developed and tested in Ethiopia and Tanzania and scaled to Kenya, Uganda, Nepal, Nigeria, Zambia, Burundi and Rwanda. The platform uses on-farm performance information and basic genomic data to identify and promote wide use of appropriate superior purebred and cross-bred bulls for artificial insemination (AI) delivery and planned natural mating for smallholder farmers in Africa. The platform has different components: online and offline data capture system, data storage, analytics and genomic evaluation and selection of top ranked bulls and dams. The platform provides summary to enable informed decision and monitoring of progress. The platform has recently been revamped and a new digital application for capturing farm data in both cattle and buffalo developed and launched.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4942?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(7297, 'Data-driven advisories for farmers to optimize fertilizer and yields for maize in Kenya using the AgWise recommendation framework', 'The AgWise generalizable decision support framework was used to develop maize fertilizer recommendations for the county governments, extension, and e-voucher system in Kenya. This was in response to a request from the government and partners to increase maize yields by 30% over existing average yields. The workflow was based on machine learning and crop modeling approaches, and generated fertilizer advice that: (1) Accounts for soil nutrient deficiencies using on-farm data and digital soil maps; (2) Considers fertilizer response under smallholder management (survey data); (3) Considers multi-year data and rainfall, but permits refining based on seasonal forecasts; (4) Allows customised yield targets at (sub) county level relative to current production; and (5) Can generate recommendations for different fertilizer formulations. The recommendations will be field-validated in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7297?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4659, 'Tropically adapted and productive chicken breeds (for different flock sizes) that meet the breed and trait preferences of women and men chicken keepers in Ethiopia, Kenya and Tanzania', 'Growth, egg production performance and economic feasibility will be evaluated for candidate breeds (strains) across different agroecologies. Brooded and vaccinated chicks (28 to 56 days-of-age depending on the country) will be issued to on-farm households the three agroecologies. The male chickens will be evaluated on body weight until 20 weeks-of-age, while the females will be evaluated on body weight and egg productivity until 72 weeks. The test will be integrated with promotion of access to inputs and support services (feeds, flock health, training, markets).  The strains will be tested at three different flock levels (with a  household receiving 25, 50 or 75 birds of a randomly assigned strain). This arrangement will allow development of viable business models that consider productivity levels, contribution to livelihoods (income, women empowerment, and nutrition), techincal feasibility and sustainability of impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4659?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(6701, 'Biodegradable Banana Fiber Paper as a Pesticide Carier for Sustainable Pest Management in Agriculture', 'The innovation has continued to be tested in the field under uncontrolled farmer field conditions during  2024 with positive results on potato and sweet potato. It is being additionally being assessed for use on other crops and against multiple spoil-borne pests and diseases using different products with good prospects. A business plan is being developed as well as the economic and practical feasibility for deployment in East Africa. Manufacturers have been approached and together with distributors, the development of the innovation as a crop protection product in East Africa is proceeding well.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6701?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(1003, 'Genomic selection-based breeding programs for dairy cattle, based on performance records provided by farmers, linked to natural sire and artificial insemination-based delivery systems', 'Milk production in developing countries is dominated by smallholder dairy farmers, each keeping 1-3 dairy cows. Majority of these farmers are not extracting optimum benefits, because herd and cow production and productivity levels are low. Small holder dairy farming is constrained by lack of sustainable breeding program that delivers adaptable high yielding dairy animals that suits the different production system, inadequate access to various services and inputs, and limited access to information or farmer education. The problem was addressed through innovative application of ICT and genomic technology.  The data generated is used to define more robust breeding objectives, develop realistic selection indices, and the information is ultimately used to select top ranking bulls and cows for breeding under local production conditions. The African Dairy Genetic Gains project has established a pipeline that harness performance data captured that incorporates genomic data for genomic prediction for milk yield and efficiency.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1003?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1025, 'Fertilizer recommendation tool for six priority crops plugged in the Smart Nkunganire System (SNS) in Rwanda to deliver site-specific fertilizer recommendations to extension agents and smallholder farmers', 'The Smart Nkunganire System (SNS) was created in response to the Rwandan Ministry of Agriculture''s (MINAGRI) request for enhanced digital extension services and precise, site-specific fertilizer recommendations for essential crops. The project aims to develop a digital advisory tool integrated within the existing SNS platform, managed by BKTechouse. This tool is designed to provide customized fertilizer recommendations for six priority crops in Rwanda: cassava, maize, wheat, potato, rice, and beans, making these recommendations accessible to both extension agents and farmers through the SNS platform.

This initiative is further supported by making these tools and methodologies available to the Rwanda Agriculture and Animal Resources Development Board (RAB), aiming to enhance the innovation''s development. This strategic approach ensures that the digital advisory tool effectively meets the specific needs of Rwanda''s agriculture sector, providing tailored support to improve productivity and sustainability.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1025?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(9070, 'Milk quality monitoring platform: Collecting ,analyzing quality and price data in Milk Collection Centres for informed decision making Uganda', 'This platform provides raw (anonymized) data of over 14062 milk samples submitted by milk collection centers in Mbarabra, Kirihura, Kazoo, and Ntungamo in Uganda. The data was recorded through 75 milk analyzers installed by the Ugandan Dairy Development Authority in randomly selected local milk collection centers. The platform allows one to select a custom date range (e.g., the last 30 days) and obtain the average butter fat (%), average SNF (%), average protein (%), and average added water (%) of samples taken for each milk collection center. The platform provides the average price paid (in UGX) and the average milk quantity (liters). The platform also reports the total number of samples submitted to each Milk Collection Centre (MCC) during the selected period. In addition to averages by MCCs, the platform also provides the evolution of overall averages over the chosen time frame (using moving averages).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9070?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(7319, 'Carob: A framework to standardize legacy agricultural data', 'Carob is an open-source community project that standardizes and aggregates agricultural research data. The Carob project cleans and transforms agricultural research data from experiments and surveys into a standard format and aggregates individual data sets into larger databases that can be used in further research. It is the Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) framework supported by the CGIAR Excellence in Agronomy Initiative to support predictive agronomy analytics.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7319?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1777, 'Modeling Service Center (MSC): providing long-term training to researchers across the Agricultural Policy Research Institutes in Africa (ANAPRI) network, on economywide analysis, including food policy modeling and shock analysis.', 'In 2022, IFPRI and ANAPRI (formerly ReNAPRI) collaborated to establish the Modeling Service Center (MSC) at ANAPRI''s Secretariat in Lusaka, Zambia. IFPRI conceptualized the modeling community of practice (MODCOP), which evolved into the MSC, and shaped the Policy Prioritization through Value Chain (PPVC) Analysis II proposal funded by BMGF. The MSC focuses on training researchers in economywide analysis, including food policy modeling and climate impact assessments. In 2023, it conducted four training events for ANAPRI partners, offering tiered programs for ambassadors, affiliates, and fellows. By 2024, the MSC became independently operational and self-funded, with ANAPRI fully embracing the model. IFPRI continues to provide advisory support and capacity building to strengthen MSC activities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1777?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7312, 'Data-driven advisories for farmers to optimize fertilizer and yields for potato in Rwanda using the AgWise recommendation framework', 'The AgWise generalizable decision support framework was used to develop potato fertilizer recommendations in Rwanda. This was in response to a request from the Rwanda Agriculture Board to optimize fertilizer rates for potato, either by reducing rates and achieving the same yields, or increasing yields by 10-20% over existing average yields obtained applying the blanket recommendation. The workflow was based on machine learning and crop modeling approaches, and generated fertilizer advice that: (1) is compatible with the national fertilizer subsidy scheme complexities; (2) avoids major rework of current system for delivery; (3) tailors advice at agroecology level while providing evidence to refine factors that will capture withi-agroecology spatial variation; (4) considers commonly used fertilizer formulations; and (5) supports validation by providing fertilizer packages and treatment structures. The recommendations are being field-validated or analyzed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7312?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(340, 'A Program Management Platform allows to align a programmatic agenda across initiatives and non-pooled projects, enabling Program-wide learning and reducing transaction costs for staff and research leaders', 'Genetic Innovation is a large endeavor, involving 21 crops, a large number of staff, partners, funders and projects. In collaboration with Breeding Resources and Scriptoria Ltd UK, Accelerated Breeding implemented a Project Management Center (PMC), to make workplans transparent across the two initiatives and several non-pooled projects. It became obvious that uploading workplans by project attracts high transaction costs and does not facilitate cross-initiative/project learning, mostly because interventions are framed and clustered differently.  Therefore, a Program Management Platform (PMP) was developed and tested with 20 non-related workplans of a wide range of bilateral projects. It enabled alignment of all (100%) projects to a common results framework that e.g. could be applied to one or several Program or Accelerator Theory(-ies) of Change. The PMP received positive feedback because it makes the agenda transparent, enables cross-project/initiative learning, and reduces transaction costs. It could facilitate Program and Accelerator implementation effective 2025.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/340?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(354, 'Assessing national partners strengths and capacity development priorities for increasing the effectiveness and satisfaction that partners derive from CGIAR center collaborative breeding and deployment activities', 'This institutional innovation provides tools and approaches for partner assessments and characterization to identify current strengths, partners'' own change ambitions, capacity development priorities, and define partner roles and responsibilities in regional networks. Also, NARES are assisted in defining their own improvement plans, informed by reviewers insights. Since 2019, 128 in-depths assessments have been conducted in Africa and Asia. Critical developments in 2024 include: 1. Assessment of a 36 NARES programs; 2. Piloting of Scaling Breeding Operations (SBO) grants with three NARES in Africa (NRCRI, NARO and ZARI) to support their crop programs to implement improvement plans and 3. Support to cost 41 breeding pipelines in order to build accurate budgets and optimize resource allocation.  Many networks are now reaching the point where most Level 1 partners have been assessed. It is becoming feasible to develop a regional breeding strategy with responsibilities attributed within networks based on network member capacity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/354?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(17, 'Yam breeding network in Africa addresses breeding challenges to develop consumer-demanded, more productive, pest and disease resistant White and Water Yam varieties; Nigeria releases five Yam varieties', 'In collaboration with national yam breeding programs in Nigeria, Cote d’Ivoire, Ghana and Benin, IITA yam breeding seeks to develop and deploy end-user preferred White Yam and Water Yam varieties with higher yield, greater resistance to pests and diseases and improved quality, targeted at West Africa. Together these collaborators seek to address common yam breeding challenges: reducing lengthy crop cycles to 3-4 years, poor seed multiplication ratio, poor understanding of genetic diversity, limited breeding enabling technologies, and limited current capacity in West Africa. Significant strides were made in enhancing the efficiency of breeding programs, including defining the yam market segmentation, refining the yam product profile, deploying marker assisted selection, and use of quantitative genetics methods to optimize breeding approaches. Yam consumers participated in elite clone selection. Between 2022 and 2024, the National Root Crops Research Institute registered five white yam varieties in NIgeria, one of them in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/17?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(59, 'Cassava breeding networks targeted at African smallholders accelerate genetic gains for disease resistant, productive varieties with better end use characteristics for home-processing and sale', 'Significant work has been done to understand farmers'' and market demands for cassava and incorporate such insights within IITA-CIAT-NARES collaborative breeding programs that pursue greater productivity, disease resistance, and end-use traits. Breeding pipelines are oriented towards different processing and nutritional requirements for Cassava - when used fresh, dried, granulated, as paste, or for industrial purposes. In Eastern Africa, IITA is spearheading the regions efforts in developing and sharing dual resistance to cassava mosaic and brown streak diseases, two of the most devastating viruses in the world. Between 2022-2024, highlights included: finalizing the definition of market segments and target product profiles, working with national partners in 13 countries on both early- and advanced-stage breeding activities, systematizing product advancement with stakeholders, and initiating on-farm trialing through classical and Triadic-comparison-of-technologies (TRICOT) methodologies. Partners in seven countries released 42 varieties. Infrastructure improvements enable high-throughput root-quality phenotyping and more efficient breeding, germplasm-cleaning, multiplication, and exchange.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/59?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(32, 'West and Central African maize breeding network accelerates genetic gains for climate-resilient and nutrient-enriched varieties that meet the increasing demands for food and feed by the formal and informal markets', 'In collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, maize breeding efforts in West and Central Africa seek to improve yields, drought tolerance, Striga and pest resistance within four breeding pipelines: 1. Early and extra-early maturing yellow and orange maize for fresh maize market, poultry feed and home consumption, 2. Early and extra early maturing white maize primarily for processing and home consumption, 3. Intermediate and late maturing yellow and orange maize for fresh maize market, poultry feed and home consumption, 4. Intermediate and late maturing white maize primarily for processing and home consumption. Orange and yellow varieties are also biofortified for Provitamin A. Current breeding cycle time is 3 years, and has been reduced through the use of functional markers and genomic selection to accelerate the rate of genetic gains. Between 2022 and 2024, partners in six countries released 47 varieties, selected for drought and heat tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/32?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(58, 'Cowpea breeding network in Africa accelerates genetic gains for more productive, resilient cowpea varieties, based on an improved understanding of gender-specific end-user requirements, to benefit smallholders in Africa', 'Globally, cowpea is cultivated on about 14.5 million hectares. In Africa, cowpea is more of a women''s crop. The overall productivity is very low, with average yields ranging from 100 to 400 kg/ha. This is due to several biotic and abiotic constraints. CGIAR-NARES collaborative cowpea breeding seeks to develop higher-yielding, disease- and pest-resilient varieties that meet the requirements in local markets. Breeding pipelines differ by maturity, so to fit different season lengths and cropping systems. They consider that cowpea may be used for both grain and fodder, and both as boiled grain and for making home-made flour. Breeding is a cyclical process with new, further improved varieties forthcoming on a regular basis, building on the feedback of farmers and markets. Large-scale farmer feedback is received using the Triadic-comparison-of-technologies (TRICOT) methodology. Current breeding cycle time is 4 years. Between 2022-2024, partners in four countries registered 35 varieties for use.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/58?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(61, 'Banana and plantain breeding networks in Africa accelerate genetic gains for disease- and pest resistant varieties with durable fruit quality, meeting local preferences and reducing food losses', 'IITA banana and plantain improvement aims to produce high-yielding, disease and pest resistant varieties with market-demanded fruit quality that can be effectively multiplied for large-scale smallholder use. In collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, the focus is on three breeding pipelines, cooking matooke and mchare banana for East Africa, and plantain for Central Africa and West Africa. The current cycle time is 9 years. 2022-2024 highlights include: Better definition and prioritization of market segments and target product profiles, the introgression of disease and pest resistant lines into locally adapted strains; the first ever selection of 4 high yielding Mchare hybrids with resistance to Fusarium, a global threat to banana; establishing much improved genomic sequence information and improved (digital) phenotyping; increased on-farm trialing and farmer-participatory food tasting; and strengthening partner networks and decision making for variety advancement; the release and promotion of four varieties in Tanzania.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/61?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(34, 'Maize breeding networks in Eastern Africa use cutting-edge tools and large-scale farmer feedback to develop and deploy climate-resilient, disease and pest-resistant varieties', 'Maize breeding in eastern Africa aims at improving adaptation to climate change, reducing the yield gaps, and enhancing household food security and incomes. In collaboration with an array of public and private sector partners, and farmers in Ethiopia, Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda, three breeding pipelines pursue early-, intermediate- and late-maturing high-yielding white maize varieties for food use, with drought tolerance, greater nitrogen use efficiency, and resistance to Maize Lethal Necrosis, Gray Leaf Spot, Turcicum Leaf Blight, Maize Streak Virus, and ear rots. The targeted area is 8.3 million hectares.  Cutting-edge tools/technologies/approaches, including doubled haploids, molecular markers, rapid-cycle genomic selection and high-throughput phenotyping, enable a breeding cycle time of 3-5 years. Large-scale on-farm testing captures smallholders'' varietal preferences on over 450 farms. Between 2022-2024, partners in 10 countries released 43 maize hybrids, all deriving from pipelines selected for greater climate resilience. Fall-army worm resistance allows farmers to reduce pesticide use.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/34?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(35, 'Maize breeding networks in Southern Africa use cutting-edge breeding tools and large-scale feedback from men and women farmers to develop and deploy climate-resilient, disease and pest-resistant, and biofortified varieties', 'Maize breeding in southern Africa aims at reducing yield gaps, improving climate resilience, providing more nutritious food, and increasing smallholders’ incomes and livelihoods. In collaboration with public and private sector partners, and smallholder farmers in Malawi, Mozambique, Zambia and Zimbabwe, two breeding pipelines pursue early- and intermediate-maturing high-yielding white maize varieties for food use, with drought tolerance, nitrogen use efficiency, and resistance to the major diseases. An additional breeding pipeline develops provitamin A-enriched varieties for eastern and southern Africa. The area targeted is 10.3 M ha. Cutting-edge tools/technologies, including doubled haploids, and genomics-assisted breeding, enable a breeding cycle time of 4 years. Large-scale on-farm testing (>700 farms) captured gender-disaggregated yields and farmer preferences. Between 2022-2024, partners in nine countries released 72 hybrids, all selected for climate resilience and nitrgen use efficicny. Eight were selected for higher provitamin A content. Fall-army worm resistance allows farmers to reduce pesticide use.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/35?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(37, 'Bean breeding networks in Africa accelerate genetic gains for biofortified varieties including bush and climbing beans, meeting farmer and seed company demands in diverse markets', 'Common beans are an important staple food for more than 200 million Africans, providing an affordable source of proteins, complex carbohydrates and valuable micronutrients. They improve soil fertility and are a significant source of income. In collaboration with the Pan Africa Bean Research Alliance (PABRA) and farmers, CIAT and national bean breeding efforts seek to develop improved and marketable bush and climbing bean varieties. The program builds on past progress on progress in resistance to bean diseases and tolerance to drought. Current emphasis is on gender intentional traits, biofortification for zinc and iron, and reducing cooking time. Breeding is a cyclical process that adjusts to feedback from farmers, seed companies and end-users. Breeding cycle time is 24 months. Between 2022 and 2024, partners in 20 countries tested and registered 79 bean varieties (25 in 2024) that were collaboratively developed between local breeding programs and CIAT.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/37?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(369, 'Aligning standardized breeding pipeline processes and stage gates in Genetic Innovation with skill-sets of diverse teams required to execute vanguard breeding programs', 'In 2022, common stage gates spanning the whole breeding pipeline and sub-pipeline segments of collaborative breeding networks were defined. They provide a framework to define the connection between teams along the germplasm development process in a systematic manner for more effective handover between breeding teams, including those in partner organizations. The stage gating is the basis for more clearly communicating individual team focus, success, and defining professional development strategies. During 2024 high-level skill sets were defined that drive the product development process in different stages. Insights help in identifying potential gaps, duplications, and reorganization opportunities, given that similar skill sets are repeated across centers, crops and locations. Participants in a crop lead meeting highlighted the need and intent to better leverage capabilities across centers, based on such understanding. Skill sets may also drive gender-differential preferences for professional roles. The approach supports the design of more inclusive career pathways.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/369?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(46, 'Sweetpotato breeding network in Eastern Africa accelerates genetic gains for market-demanded, resilient, high provitamin A varieties, generating new income opportunities for women farmers', 'Sweetpotato breeding seeks to enable 15 million resource‐poor households in Africa and Asia to improve their diets and raise incomes. The Southern Africa collaborative breeding network accelerates the breeding of orange-flesh and white sweetpotato varieties for improved yield, nutritional value, virus and weevil resistance, and earlier maturity. Since 2022, demand- and gender- responsive target product profiles (TPPs) draw on the input of user studies (Ethiopia, Uganda, Tanzania), and product design and advancement meetings. As a result, end use traits are now being measured in both early- and late-stage testing to promote greater adoption and address gender-differentiated perspectives. Market Segments were better defined and now target 1.14 million hectares. Modified breeding schemes, novel designs, larger-scale testing and genotype-by-environment analyses have increased selection intensity and genetic gains. On-farm trialing in Uganda using TRICOT has included 450 trials, assessing farmers ranking. Between 2022-2024, partners in Burundi and Uganda registered four white-fleshed varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/46?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(45, 'Sweetpotato breeding network in Southern Africa accelerates genetic gains for market-demanded, resilient, high provitamin A varieties, generating new income opportunities for women farmers', 'The Southern Africa collaborative sweetpotato breeding network accelerates and enhances the breeding of orange-flesh and white sweetpotato varieties for improved yield, virus and weevil resistance, and end use traits, to reduce malnutrition and raise incomes. Since 2022, demand- and gender- responsive target product profiles (TPPs) draw on the input of user studies (Malawi, Mozambique), and product design and advancement meetings. As a result, TPPs were updated for sensorial traits that lead to greater adoption and consider gender-differentiated perspectives. Market Segments were better defined and now target 660,000 hectares. Modified breeding schemes, novel designs, larger-scale testing and genotype-by-environment analyses have increased selection intensity and genetic gains. On-farm trialing has grown to 64 sites across Mozambique, Malawi and Zambia, assessing farmers ranking and preferences and, in some instances, cooking qualities. Mozambique registered three biofortified varieties in 2022; Zambia promotes eight clones in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/45?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(367, 'Aligning CGIAR breeding processes to a common, high level organizational structure create clarity of roles and contributions among the individual initiatives and teams to the overall breeding success', 'Modern breeding organizations drive efficiency gain, opportunity identification, and operational effectiveness through the coordinated engagement of specialized teams and processes. In interaction with the other Genetic Innovation initiatives, this innovation builds upon the extended stage plan established and validated in 2022 and 2023. It comprises ten distinct stages from product concept and market segmentation to product launch. It includes an extended suppliers-inputs-process-outputs-customers (SIPOC) model that provides a high-level overview of the processes, defines the inputs needed, the supplier(s) of each input, the key processes at each stage, the outputs of the processes, and the customers of each output. The approach was socialized and promoted across CGIAR breeding teams and partners. Deep-dive sessions in 2024 show that 53% fully and 41% partially implemented harmonized stage gate terminology. The terminology is also used in interactions with partners. This development entails a significant step change for consistently aligning CGIAR breeding processes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/367?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(350, 'The Breeding Portal captures and makes CGIAR breeding strategies transparent across CGIAR-NARES networks; and makes the data available to the public through the Global Market Intelligence Platform (GloMIP)', 'The Breeding Portal is an authoritative platform that captures CGIAR-NARES collaborative breeding strategies and makes the data public through the Global Market Intelligence Platform (GloMIP). Users need have distinct levels of permissions to create, edit, or modify data, or change dataset status from Proposed to Active to Archived. The Active Data set describes current CGIAR-funded breeding activities. Data sets include: Traits used, Target Product Profile pursued, Breeding Pipelines employed, Varieties released, Market Segments targeted, and (since 2024) Partnerships and level of collaboration established. Data in this system drive Impact Area characterization for the portfolio. During 2024, additional interoperability was established with the Breeding Scheme Manager, the Enterprise Breeding System and GloMIP to enable analytical capabilities for Key Performance Indicators identified by a cross-organizational team of scientists. At this stage, 333 users have access to the Breeding Portal. Its evolution is driven by users'' needs, and complementary to existing platforms.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/350?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(344, 'Successful approaches to developing farmer-and market-relevant variety identification schemes increase the emphasis on both precision-phenotyping essential traits and using farmer-representative conditions early in the breeding process', 'Considerable efforts are being made by crops to make variety identification schemes more representative of the target population of environments (TPE) and farmers'' conditions. Between 2022 and 2024, breeding programs made substantive efforts in aligning breeding schemes with the target product profiles (TPP). 58% of breeding programs reported the extent they are able to measure TPP traits during the different testing stages. The majority can measure around 75% of the essential traits during early testing, allowing higher selection pressures and faster progress. Efforts are on-going to increase this proportion. 89% of the teams have a check strategy in place and 93% made efforts to analyze genotype-by-environment interactions, mostly at Late Testing, to inform their testing strategy. Inclusion of on-farm sites has increased; 32% use it during early testing. These results indicate that breeding programs make significant efforts in making selection environments more representative of the target environments for new varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/344?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(342, 'CGIAR center experiences with implementing faster and more accurate breeding cycles outline opportunities for more rapidly improving crops most important to farmers in Africa, Asia and Latin America', 'Innovations under this result include implementing genome-assisted rapid-cycle recurrent selection schemes, more accurate and earlier selection of crossing parents, and rapid advancement approaches, to accelerate the rate of genetic gain in major food crops. End-of-initiative deep-dive sessions with CGIAR breeding teams captured 2022-2024 progress. Currently, cycle time across crops has further reduced to 41 months, going as low as 24 months, with further scope for improvement. Fifty-eight percent of programs use rapid-cycle genomic selection in at least one pipeline, while 32% are establishing genomic selection approaches. This represents significant progress from 2022 and 2023 when only 35% and 45% implemented genomic selection, and cycle times were longer. Use of genomic selection implies and drives that programs also adopt more sophisticated and capable data management systems. Successful models need to be scaled across more breeding pipelines and parameters for estimating breeding value need continuous improvement.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/342?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(188, 'Tools and approaches to help breeders to optimize breeding schemes aligned with the desirable characteristics of farmer and market demanded crop varieties', 'As breeders more accurately define Target Product Profiles (TPP), associated breeding schemes need to be revisited so the right emphasis is placed on traits that are to be improved or kept at a competitive level. During 2024, the Breeding Scheme Manager was linked to the Breeding Portal and deployed with improvements to cater for the wider range of breeding approaches in the portfolio. At the end of 2024, 40 users were registered and at different stages of documenting their breeding schemes. In collaboration with quantitative geneticists, 58% of breeding programs have made changes in breeding schemes in at least one pipeline based on a breeding program simulation tool (AlphaSimR). In addition, all breeding programs have taken measures to improve heritability, selection intensity, genetic variance and/or genetic correlations with the target environment and capture essential traits earlier during selection. The data shows a progressive optimization of CGIAR breeding approaches.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/188?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(346, 'A new approach aligns genetic gain assessments of CGIAR breeding pipelines across crops and traits, providing a tool for monitoring the impact of breeding pipeline optimization efforts', 'The innovation seeks to provide an annual update on genetic gains by crop across CGIAR breeding pipelines, using standardized methods.  In 2024, and in alignment with efforts of the ReFOCUS work package, genetic gain estimates were carried out by market segment. Realized genetic gain for yield was reported for 11 crops, 57 pipelines and 107 Market Segments. Realized genetic gains were statistically significant and positive for 62% (66 out of 107) of the studies. Genetic gain estimates of >1% annually were reported for 40% of the market segments. 43% were reported at Early Testing Stage; while 57% were reported using Late Testing Stage data. 58% fully and 32% partially used the recommended check strategy. Genetic gain estimates for 18% of market segments were reported using data from >10 environments per year. Progress can be seen from 2022 and 2023 reports, with more programs and pipelines having reliable genetic gains assessment.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/346?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3544, 'Accelerating Rice varietal turnover through fast-paced  production, supply and delivery models and partnerships implemented in Asia and Africa, for both early generation and quality seeds of newer products', 'Comprehensive partnerships were established across public, private and farmers to ensure seed supply chain for validated products are strengthened. To guarantee that high-quality seeds  are produced  from early-generation seeds and disseminated to end users (farmers and different delivery network partners), the partnerships concentrated on collaborating agencies that play roles in seed production and multiplication . The commercialisation and scaling of recently arrived products from breeding programs with methodical announcement, positioning, licensing, and multiplication arrangements were also accomplished through the establishment of various partnerships for hybrid crops (such as hybrid maize).  Numerous small-scale institutions (such as farmer-producer companies, cooperatives, and other small and medium-sized businesses) were also significantly empowered to become self-sufficient in owning and promoting seed enterprises and businesses through targeted technology introduction, planned training, and related small equipment support.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3544?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3531, ' On-farm testing (OFT) validation protocols for Rice  across priority market segments are streamlined with national partners in Asia, Africa and OFT result driven demand creation/ positioning happens through extension actors', 'To assess the true genetic gain potential, create evidence driven entry points for better demand creation , new products were compared to market-dominant and farmer-popular variants to trigger adoption and scaling decisions . In collaboration / led by various  national  partners, farmers were chosen, their locations were examined, social mobilisation was carried out, data was gathered, and reflection and analysis were conducted.  The data from this year would be comparable to that from the scaling attempts the following year. A total of 956 sites  farmer-led  were hosted for newer products spread across various market segments  inclusive of  rice, maize, and wheat (on average, 12 segments per major product under the cereal group), with about 35 market segments being targeted , led by institutions like IRRI, CIMMYT, Africa Rice , IITA, ICRISAT, ICARDA', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3531?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(9443, 'Progress on Aflasafe Research & Delivery, Incubation & Training (ARDIT) facility', 'IITA management approved and dedicated ex-aflasafe factory at IITA campus in Ibadan, to be re-designed and converted to aflasafe R4D, incubation and training facility (ARDIT). With PHI support, this will be a small scale manufacturing facility for research and production of small quantities of aflasafe for the countries without manufacturing plant and will also be used for training of manufacturing partners and joint research with public and private sector entrepreneurs on new manufacturing processes and new products, and for the industrial production of the active ingredient dry spores.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9443?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(2549, 'Global and National Agri-Food Market and Policy Analysis Models to support decision-makers in assessing value chain trade-offs.', 'An integrated framework of global and country Agri-Food Market and Policy Analysis Models (AMPAM) is implemented to assess the potential for and trade-offs associated with scaling interventions and innovations that create jobs, boost incomes, and reduce environmental footprints along food value chains. The AMPAM modeling framework supports decision-makers in assessing the full potential, scalability, market opportunities, and priorities for trade-offs associated with incentive schemes, investments, and innovations. It builds on IFPRI''s recursive-dynamic, multi-region, multi-sector computable general equilibrium (MIRAGRODEP) global model and the Rural Investment and Policy Analysis (RIAPA) country models.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2549?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(38, 'Soybean breeding network for Africa accelerates genetic gains for more productive, rust-resistant varieties that are adapted to poor soil conditions, to provide smallholders with diversification opportunities', 'In collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, IITA soybean improvement targets increased and stable yields, resistance to soybean rust that can cause up to 60% yield loss, reduced pod shattering and tolerance to soil nutrient and pH deficiencies. Breeding pipelines are pursued: 1. Early and 2. Medium-to-late maturing soybean for the lowland savanna in West Africa, 3. Early and 4. Medium-to-late maturing soybean for the mid-altitude savanna in southern Africa. Annual genetic gain for grain yield from 2017 to 2023 were 2.40%, 2.14%, 2.34% and 3.24%, respectively. Breeding cycle time was reduced to 2.5 years. Collaboration with public and private sector partners improves the pace, scale, and output of varietal development and widens the genetic diversity available to African countries. Joint advancement meetings with female and male value chain stakeholders identify candidate varieties for on-farm testing. Between 2022-2024, partners in five countries registered 14 varieties for use.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/38?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5142, 'Novel stress tolerant (abiotic and biotic), highly productive and resource efficient forages to drive livestock production and productivity for market oriented small and medium, size farm operations.', 'Good quality feed year-round is limiting livestock productivity on-farm and resource use in farms of small to medium size. Feed production can amount to 50 to 70% of livestock production costs. Forages are a means to overcome the feed gap, and resilient forages responding to different abiotic and biotic stresses, including low soil fertility, drought / excess water, pest diseases, are essential, increasing livestock productivity by up to 15%. To scale such technologies, suitable entry points i.e., market-oriented livestock production particularly dairy are required. Enablers are animal generics and health technologies which capitalize on the improved feeding as well as capacity and dissemination channels such as cooperatives and functional seed supply systems. We are using a diversity of advanced forages which have a good availability of seed and/or planting material and demand pull through livestock production in Kenya; at the same time a pipeline for improved forages is being maintained.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5142?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4136, 'Real-time artificial intelligence driven diagnostic app that can be used to assess diets and nudge app users to make better-informed choices about healthy diets', 'FRANI is an artificial-intelligence (AI) app for mobile devices that diagnoses diet-related problems for adolescents. The tool provides tailored nutritional advice tailored, allowing users to make decisions to improve their diets. Moreover, the AI tool could provide governments and development partners with real-tie diagnostics to strengthen surveillance systems in health and nutrition. 

In Sri Lanka, recent surveys have shown that adolescents may face nutrition-related health problems such as anemia. There is also a growing prevalence of obesity. This research proposal describes the preliminary acceptability, usability, and possible likability of a novel AI-based mobile phone app prototype to be developed for image recognition of food, provide feedback and motivate users to make healthier food choices and improve the lives of millions of adolescents. The proposal was co-authored with IFPRI FRESH researchers and staff from the University of Wayamba, Sri Lanka - who will carry out the research study in-country.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4136?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(2425, 'Mechanized direct-seeding for sustainable, economically-viable and climate resilient rice production in the Mekong sub-region', 'Mechanized direct seeded rice (DSR) introduces innovations in both the design and sourcing of specialized seeding machines, as well as in developing business models to facilitate access to these machines. This method involves sowing rice seeds directly into the soil using mechanical seeders that precisely plant seeds in rows, covering them efficiently, whether in dry or wet conditions. The innovation lies in the mechanization process, which incorporates advanced seeding machinery designed or sourced specifically to optimize seed placement and soil contact, leading to reduced seed rates and decreased water requirements.

Additionally, this approach not only addresses the challenges of labor and cost reduction but also promotes water conservation and faster planting times. By integrating these technological and entrepreneurial innovations, mechanized DSR aims to overcome the limitations of traditional and broadcast seeding methods, such as low yields and high operational costs, while also contributing to environmental sustainability by reducing greenhouse gas emissions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2425?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(1163, 'Rice -Varietal  Delivery/ Partnership Models', 'Diverse innovative and inclusive public private partnership and delivery models tested,  assessed for their effectiveness and scaled focusing on accelerating varietal turnover. This includes community led cluster demonstrations, varietal cafeteria , dealer led demonstrations and participatory varietal selection, community led seed enterprise models, EGS linkage and multiplications models with private , community multipliers and NARES linkage', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1163?phase=1', 5, NULL),
(3100, 'E-learning course that introduces basic innovation and scaling concepts, and applies these concepts to a real case study. Designed for students, scientists, development experts, and research/ innovation managers', 'Achieving impact at scale is one of the greatest challenges facing the research and development community, and ‘scaling of innovation’ is increasingly popular in the world of public research and development. However, innovation and scaling often mean different things for stakeholders representing research, development organizations, private sector and government organizations. To support organizations, project teams and their partners in developing a common understanding of innovation and scaling in the context of international development, an online course was developed. 

The course covers 6 Modules that answer questions such as:
1.	What do we mean with innovation?
2.	How do innovations scale as part of packages?
3.	How do we ensure (gender-)responsible scaling?
4.	How do we measure the Scaling Readiness of innovations?
5.	How to develop evidence-based scaling strategies with partners?
6.	Why invest in innovation portfolio management?

The course is available in English, French and Spanish and can be accessed through: https://innovationandscaling.thinkific.com/.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3100?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(889, 'National science-policy dialogue platform as official space for multi-stakeholder dialogue and a key institutional mechanism for mainstreaming adaptation of agriculture and food systems to climate change in Mali', 'Created with CCAFS West Africa support in 2012, the national science-policy dialogue platform in Mali (C-CASA Mali) has promoted consultation between researchers-public authorities-NGOs-farmers'' organizations-grassroots actors, thus have been crucial to improve interactions between policy makers and researchers. Member-institutions adherence and participation to the platform''s vision and activities are key factors determining the platform''s ability to play its part in climate governance in Mali. The platform''s institutionalization and the stakeholders'' commitment have enabled the platform to play a key role in the identification and prioritization of climate-smart agriculture practices and investment portfolios. The knowledge generated by the platform through its research made it possible to identify constraints and challenges, to inform and guide adaptation planning and governance in Malian agriculture/food systems. The establishment of platforms at district level is necessary to consider the concerns of grassroots communities and a better harmonization in the implementation of climate change adaptation programs and policies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/889?phase=1', 8, NULL),
(10493, 'Digital climate and agro-advisory platform (with risk financing mechanim)', 'Aclimate agro-meteorological platform phase rolled out with weather forecasts and historical climate information. This tool will support government and private sector actors with anticipating shocks and its impacts on agriculture yields to allow for agriculture risk management, in support of financial and programatic decision making for the agriculture sector, along with the capability for early warning, for specific value chains.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10493?phase=3', 4, NULL),
(775, 'The sub-setting tool provides Genebank managers and users (breeders, scientists, students, NGOs) a web-tool to subset (select) genebank accessions available from genebanks based on environmental parameters', 'Breeders are forced to seek the variation they require from genetic resource collections conserved in genebanks. The challenge when searching specific traits from genebanks is that resources to search for a given trait are often such that only a relatively small subset of the available genebank can be evaluated. Thus, to be effective, a strategy is required to identify the smallest possible subset of germplasm to evaluate whilst increasing the chances of capturing the sought-after variation. Genesys currently holds more than 4 million genebank accessions from 462 institutes and is accessed by users to order germplasm. From these, around 768,576 accessions are held at CGIAR genebanks. Identifying the correct set of accessions to respond to these requests adequately can be time-consuming.The subsetting tool allows to develop and implement data mining techniques for subsetting germplasm accessions, thus helping address germplasm requests more effectively and efficiently. The sub-setting is based on indicators for historical climate information, soil information and specific per crop regarding stresses. This web tool allows users to compose the needed subsets and order them directly from genebanks in Genesys. In the tool, a user identifies a series of potential subsets using data and analytics and then selects the subset of accessions that best suits their interest. For example, amongst four potential subsets, they may select the subset with accessions grown in the driest places.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/775?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1811, 'Development of digital groundwater monitoring dashboard to enhance sustainable and equitable ground water use in collaboration with Nepal''s Groundwater Resources Development Board', 'This website is a product of the Cereal Systems Initiative for South Asia (CSISA)-Resilience project in partnership with Nepal’s Groundwater Resources Development Board and aims to contribute to Nepal’s capacity to build a digital groundwater monitoring system through a small pilot project in the USAID Feed the Future Zone of Nepal’s Tarai region. The ongoing expansion of groundwater resource development will undoubtedly provide a great contribution to Nepal’s irrigation sector and food security. Along this way, a digital groundwater monitoring system will be critical to help the country guide the development and management of the resource by providing decision-makers and communities with adequate spatial evidence and information about the opportunities and risks for sustainable and equitable groundwater use.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1811?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5015, 'Rice Crop Manager Advisory Service (Climate+) platform to generate and provide farmers with Agro-advisories to address dynamic weather conditions and potential climate-related risks through printouts and/or SMS', 'The Rice Crop Manager Advisory Service (RCMAS) Climate+ generates climate-adjusted nutrient and crop recommendations to address dynamic weather conditions and potential climate-related risks during the cropping season. It is a link between the RCMAS platform, https://rcm.da.gov.ph/, and the Agro-climatic Advisory Portal (ACAP), https://amia-cis.github.io via applications programming interface (API). The RCMAS uses the 6-month seasonal and 10-day weather forecasts from the ACAP to formulate agro-advisories aiming to reduce crop loss due to climate risks such as floods and drought. RCMAS Climate+ upgrades the existing RCMAS platform with pre-season and mid-season climate-related recommendations, such as adjustments to the date of sowing, fertilizer application timing in response to floods or droughts during the crop growth period and (2) real-time or near-real-time climate information dissemination to farmers, which could enable better decision-making under dynamic climate conditions. The agro-advisories are provided to farmers through the one-page printout and/or SMS.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5015?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(886, 'WikiPapa Platform: a decision support tool for monitoring the conservation status of potato landraces in space and time in diversity hotspots', 'The WikiPapa platform was created in 2021 to help the global community, it is an integrated system that involves farmers (guardians of the potato), consumers, students, teachers, and researchers. The WikiPapa environment connects people with the biodiversity of native potatoes through technological tools, combined with smartphones, as well as opening the world of science to students and young people to achieve learning in the exploration, observation, and discovery of the Biodiversity of native potatoes. WikiPapa’s mission is to support the in situ conservation of the biodiversity of native Andean potato varieties through citizen science, utilizing VarScout tools. This interactive platform showcases a collection of over 950 varieties from Peru, Chile, Bolivia, and Colombia and has gathered around 2,000 observations from citizen scientists and contributions from 946 farmers, representing a total of 1,481 unique varieties.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/886?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(255, 'Technology and business model for mechanized rice straw management in Vietnam and Cambodia', 'Technology and business model for mechanized rice straw collection and management for low emission and circular economy in rice production were developed by the International Rice Research Institute in collaboration with Nong Lam University and Philippine Rice Research Institute for farmer cooperatives and local service providers. The business model includes the key information on its establishment, operation, marketing, and sustainable promotion such as business model canvas, finance, feedstock potential, markets, energy efficiency, and greenhouse gas emission balance. 

Now, rice straw management is recognized by the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development (MARD) of Vietnam as one of the innovations that is critical for the circular and low emission agriculture in Mekong River Delta. It''s implementation has expanded to Cambodia to establish experimental sites and engagement activities with local stakeholders were conducted.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/255?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(824, 'Reaching over 50,000 farmers, the NextGen agroadvisory has achieved wheat grain yield of 38% by smallholder farmers in Ethiopia', 'With fertilizer application rates in Africa from the current 20 kg ha−1 to the 50 kg ha−1 target agreed by the Abuja Declaration, food production would have grown by 209% in the last ten years instead of just 24%. The NextGen agroadvisory system was developed by partners in Ethiopia to guide optimal application of organic and inorganic inputs considering specific field, household, and climate conditions. Validation and piloting with wheat farmers show that the advisory: (a) brings yield advantage of up to 24% compared to national and local recommendations; (b) has wheat yield gains of up to 38% compared to farmers’ practices; (c) enables smallholder farmers enjoy partial profit increase by up to $580 ha-1 season-1, and (d) significantly increased nitrogen and water use efficiencies by up to 30%-36% compared to national blanket recommendations. The advisory has attracted wider attention with a potential for “Pan-African Agroadvisory Decision Support System”.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/824?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(953, 'Scaling Readiness: an evidence-based approach to co-design, implement and monitor scaling strategies for innovation teams and innovation portfolio managers.', 'Scaling Readiness offers an evidence-based innovation management and intervention support system designed for research and impact managers to strategize and implement their innovation design, development, dissemination and use support activities. It combines an innovation systems perspective for impact and a rigorous evidence synthesis method, using metrics developed initially by NASA and further developed by the European Commission. It links current agricultural and food innovation systems with potential impact at scale and sheds light on which innovations, investments and partnerships should be prioritized by projects or organizations to achieve impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/953?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4995, 'Women''s and Men''s Political Participation Training Curriculum', 'Women’s and Men’s Political Participation Trainings are curricula co-designed by ActionAid Nigeria and CGIAR researchers to strengthen women’s sense of collective efficacy and leadership skills, and to increase men’s support for women’s voice and agency. Women''s exclusion from civic spaces often leads to policies that inadequately address issues vital to their well-being, such as provision of public services (e.g., healthcare, education, agricultural extension, etc) and infrastructure. Women''s political participation remains low for several reasons, including lack of resources, time and information; social norms that discourage women''s involvement in politics; and psychological barriers that make women doubtful of their ability to influence change. The women''s curriculum is designed to counteract the psychological and social barriers to women''s political participation. Meanwhile, the men''s curriculum is designed to enhance the support that women receive from their husbands to participate politically by training men on the benefits of women''s political participation for their families and communities, as well as how to operationalize this support for women’s benefit.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4995?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6577, 'Community seed production and scaling of cereals, food legumes and potato innovations for  mixed farming system of  North Shoa, Ethiopia', 'The highlands of North Shoa in Amhara region  are dominated by cereals (wheat, barley and tef) followed by food legumes. The productivity of cereals and food legumes is due to biotic and abiotic factors as well as insufficient input supplies like certified seeds, planting materials, inoculants and other inputs. Because of diseases and insect pests, acid acidity and water logging problems, farmers are reducing areas of  food legumes and growing more cereals leading to low crop diversity that can reduce soil fertility, incomes, food diversity and increase disease threats. Many cereals, food legume  and potato varieties are released by many centers and did not reach farmers due to limited demonstration and shortage of quality early generation seeds and planting materials. Poor access to quality seeds contributes to low crop productivity and limited technology scaling in north Shoa.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6577?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(8998, 'Participatory variety selection and early generation seed production of cereals and legumes', 'The highlands of North Shoa in Amhara region are dominated by cereals  followed by food legumes. The productivity of cereals and food legumes is low due to biotic and abiotic factors as well as insufficient input supplies like certified seed, inoculants and other inputs. Because of diseases and insect pests, acidity and water logging problems, farmers are reducing areas of food legumes and growing more cereals leading to low crop diversity, reduced soil fertility, incomes and food diversity. Several cereals, food legume and potato  varieties are released by many centers did not reach farmers due to limited demonstration to create awareness and demand and shortage of quality early generation seed. Moreover, popular varieties certified seed production is hampered due to shortage of breeder and pre-basic seeds of new and popular varieties from research centers. The poor access to quality seed contributes to low crop productivity and crop diversity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8998?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9001, 'Multiplication of early generation planting materials and demonstration of vegetatively propagated crops for mixed farming system in North Shoa, Ethiopia', 'The highlands of North Shoa in Amhara region are dominated by cereals (wheat, barley and tef) followed by food legumes. The productivity of cereals and food legumes is due to biotic and abiotic factors as well as insufficient input supplies like planting materials and other inputs. Farmers are trying to add vegetatively propagated horticultural crops (tubers, bulbs and fruit trees) into the framing systems. Horticultural crops are very important to diversify diet, incomes and reduce risks of cereal and legume crops. There are many temperate fruits (apple, peach, pear, plum etc), potato and garlic varieties developed by the research system but most of them are not demonstrated to the farming communities. Horticultural crops are becoming important due to increased small scale irrigation in the highlands as opposed to cereal and legume crops and promotion and awareness programs on nutrition.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9001?phase=3', 9, NULL),
(4877, 'Business incubation and mentorship for empowering pig agripreneurs and strenghthening business linkages with customers and business development services in Uganda', 'In Uganda, pig agripreneurs especially women,  have limited networks to enable optimal business operations. Besides, they lack business skills to effectively engage with clients. The incubation and mentorship program is customer focused and strengthens network with financial services and linkages with pig farmers and other service providers', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4877?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(777, 'Agrobiodiversity Solution Hotspot Tool for local stakeholders to address water, energy, food and environment (WEFE) challenges', 'The agrobiodiversity solution hotspot tool is improving understanding of relationships across the food system in India and helping identify priority interventions to restore and enhance ecosystem functions and services for sustainable social and economic benefits. Location-specific threat datasets have been combined with diversity datasets from the Agrobiodiversity Index to create a threat hotspot and agrobiodiversity solution tool for local stakeholders to address water, energy, food and environment (WEFE) challenges. Initially focusing on the Ganges Basin in India, the project has utilized a collaborative approach, co-developing and co-creating with local stakeholders and government partners to develop and refine the tool. This has led to a practical downscaled Agrobiodiversity Index approach that can be used by local stakeholders to address WEFE challenges. Using a locally relevant methodology, the project has prioritized locations to enhance agrobiodiversity, improving agricultural system sustainability and resilience in India. Intensive collaboration took place in India with local and national research and government partners. Due to its methodology, this innovation can be transferred to other geographies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/777?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(868, 'Knowledge Platform for Inclusive and Sustainable Food Markets and Value Chains (KISM)', 'Knowledge Platform for Inclusive and Sustainable Food Markets and Value Chains (KISM) is a platform to help farmer organizations, food businesses, governments, and practitioners make better-informed investment and policy decisions on inclusive and sustainable food value chains. The platform consolidates available evidence of existing experiences with product and process innovations within food value chains regarding their impact in terms of inclusiveness (employment and income generation, value-added sharing, poverty reduction, food security) and sustainability (emissions, supply chain resilience). 
Users can access the knowledge platform through a dedicated website. Available evidence on the effectiveness of (bundled) value chain innovations is presented by using the logic of the Initiative''s theory of change (ToC). Users can further access various tools for value chain analysis, including the Tools4VCs set developed previously by CGIAR''s research program on Policies, Institutions, and Markets (PIM). Furthermore, through the platform users can connect with other communities of practice (CoP), including the "Living Income CoP". KISM has further been designed to undertake e-disussions and serve as a hub for policy and stakeholder dialogues undertaken by the Initiative.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/868?phase=1', 3, NULL),
(797, 'Mixed cultivation or ley-cropping of forage legumes with maize to achieve high yields of grain, green fodder and residue to feed livestock', 'This innovation seeks to increase production of energy and protein-dense livestock feed products / ingredients from maize-based farming systems in sub-humid agro-climatic regions of southern Africa. This will be achieved through sustainable intensification practices that involve adoption of improved forage legumes varieties by smallholder farmers.  Specifically the innovation will  provide site-specific advice to farmers and agricultural extension staff on climate smart, annual (or weakly biennial) forage legumes varieties of Lablab purpureus (Lablab), Mucuna pruriens (Velvet beans) and Vigna anguiculata (Cowpeas) that can be intercropped or grown in rotation with maize to produce forage and  grain products or by-products, which will be used as ingredients in manufacture of commercial and home-mixed feed rations for beef, dairy and small-stock production. Forage-based feeds are more accessible and cost-effective to smallholder farmers.  As such, they would be suitable replacements for the largely expensive commercial supplements supplied by private sector companies, which have generally been poorly adopted by the smallholder farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/797?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(3841, 'Efficacy of soft chemistry pesticides, botanical oils and entomopathogens evaluated against cassava whiteflies', 'Cutting dipping in Mandiplus and application of neem oils significantly reduced adult whitefly numbers 45 – 74% compared to control. Mandiplus alone was the most effective at 74% followed by Mandiplus + neem oil (61%), TopNeem Ultra oil (50%) and crude neem oil in ethanol (45%). Nymphs were also significantly reduced by 49 – 70% in treatments compared to control. Mandiplus + neem had (70%), Mandiplus alone (68%), TopNeem Ultra (56%) and crude neem in ethanol (49%). The root yield in kg/plant was significantly higher in Mandiplus and Mandiplus + neem treatments by 78 – 86% compared to control. TopNeem Ultra and neem in ethanol had yield increases of 18 – 19% though these were not significantly different from the control.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3841?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7497, 'Introduction and promotion of Berken plow for better in situ water conservation and enhancing resilience to climate change impacts', 'Berken Plow was innovated by Dr. Melesse Temesgen and tested by IWMI and partners for its economic; environmental and social variability and  under uncontrolled conditions by IWMI and Farm Africa. It was implemented on more than 150 farmers by IWMI and Farm Africa to reduce farmers’ workload and cut into the depth of soil to allow infiltration of rainwater. The infiltration of water helps to reduce soil erosion and enhances the retention of moisture in the soil. 150 Berken Plows were distributed to 150 farmers in different intervention kebeles in the Central Rift Valley (CRV).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7497?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(7542, 'Introduction and promotion of vermicompost for better yield and ecofriendly production in the Central Rift Valley of Ethiopia', 'The introduction and promotion of vermicompost in the Central Rift Valley (CRV) intervention area to address the impacts of challenges of soil organic matter depletion and soil nutrient mining. This innovation can be an important input to sustainable intensification innovation bundles.  The vermicompost technology has not been widely adopted in the CRV of Ethiopia although the area is characterized by low soil fertility, high application of chemical fertilizer, and pollution of water bodies such as Ziway, Langano, Abiyata, and Shala lakes. In CRV, 268 farmers were involved in the preparation and application of vermicompost including means to earn money. Vermicompost can be made using different organic materials and adding the worms into it to do the job. Currently, in some parts of the country, it is an important organic fertilizer production process but the scientific underpinning and business model development need some focus.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7542?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(1922, 'Longa: An Artificial Intelligence-based audio analytics tool for identifying farmers'' need and targeting digital extension services', 'The increasing capabilities of Artificial Intelligence (AI)-augmented data analytics present significant opportunities for agricultural extension organizations operating in the Global South. In this work, we developed a proof-of-concept audio analytics tool to demonstrate the possibility of automating the process of translating and analyzing farmers'' voice message data collected by Farm Radio Rural radio programs. This innovation, Tambaya ("Ask" in Hausa) draws on several cutting-edge AI tools, in particular, the use of unsupervised learning to generate language models, to expand the number of languages available to the user. In the context of NGOs working with African (Tanzania, Uganda, Kenya and Nigeria) radio stations, Tambaya will allow them to quickly analyze farmers'' voice messages, understand farmers'' needs, and draw insights to deliver targeted extension services.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1922?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(6293, 'Mbili Mbili strip cropping systems for crop and forage diversification, enhanced productivity, nutrition, soil health, and climate resilience in Malawi', 'The innovation co-validates cereal-multipurpose legume and forage strip-cropping system with different spatial configurations on effects they pose on productivity, feed quality, nutrition, resilience to climate change and overall economics of cereals and multi-purpose legumes and forage intercrops', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6293?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(11962, 'Principal Component Analysis methodology used by governments to identify critical locations for sustainable groundwater monitoring', 'Principal Component Analysis (PCA) approach is an innovation that helps to identify and pinpoint critical locations for monitoring groundwater resources. The approach utilizes advanced statistical techniques by integrating various environmental parameters such as, land use, hydrology, climate, and soil attributes to gauge the susceptibility of regions to groundwater quality and quantity. IWMI piloted this innovation together with the Government of Punjab, Pakistan, where it is empowering stakeholders to make collaborative decisions to prioritize areas for targeted groundwater interventions. This includes where to install groundwater monitoring devices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/11962?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(11963, 'Groundwater management information system supporting sustainable water resource decisions and policies to address long-term groundwater challenges', 'This innovation is a web-based data-driven tool that will help policy makers and implementers to manage, analyze, and report on groundwater data. This data will be crucial for making decisions about the long-term health of the state''s groundwater resources. The tool will track factors like groundwater availability, quality, withdrawal, licensing, and more. This innovation is currently piloted in Punjab, Pakistan to support the Punjab Water Services Regulatory Authority, established under the Punjab Water Act 2019. It will be further upscaled to the country level in order to support implementation of National and provincial water policies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/11963?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(3982, 'Risk-contingent credit product, aimed at increasing farmers access to production credit and protecting financial institutions against the risks of credit default', 'Risk-contingent credit (RCC) is a linked financial product that embeds within its structure an insurance protection which, when triggered, offsets loan payments due to the lender. RCC mitigates drought-related agricultural risks and provides access to credit for smallholder farmers, requiring minimal collateral. Building on existing partnerships and field collaboration, the current project intends to (1) improve the RCC product to minimize basis risk in product design by incorporating key environmental variables from state-of-the-art remote sensors, along with measurements from weather stations; (2) integrate a gender dimension into RCC analysis by including intra-household and cluster-level randomized controlled trials (RCTs) to reach, benefit, and empower women farmers; (3) develop formal relations with an increasing number of RCC service providers for piloting RCC in Kenya, Zambia, and Ethiopia; and (4) build capacity on risk management through finance and agronomy trainings for farmers in Kenya and Ethiopia, and development of outreach and guidance materials.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3982?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3359, 'System dynamics model developed to improve coordination between climate action and peacebuilding in support of social cohesion and sustainable natural resource management', 'Combining systems thinking with co-benefits theory, a multi-scalar coordination model was developed to map potential synergies among actors implementing programs on various identified entry points along multiple dimensions of the humanitarian aid, development and peacebuilding nexus for preventing and recovering from environmental crises and conflict. Entry points for collaboration between the climate and peacebuilding sectors include climate and conflict risk assessments, the management of land and water resources, ecosystem restoration, nature-based climate adaptation, climate and conflict smart agriculture, natural resources governance, sustainable market development, and policy coherence between climate action and economic development models. Such coordination has the potential to bring social, economic, and environmental co-benefits, such as social cohesion and livelihood creation, while contributing to broader resilience and peace.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3359?phase=4', 1, NULL),
(3798, 'The Rice Observatory: An information system that provides indicators related to the productive context of rice in Latin America', 'The Rice Observatory is an initiative led by the Laboratory of Applied Economics of the Alliance of Bioversity International and the international Center for Tropical Agriculture (CIAT) , With support from the Latin American Fund for Irrigated Rice (FLAR), To establish an information system that provides indicators related to the productive context of rice in Latin America. It includes data related to the context of rice in each country, information on the production system, information on trade and competitiveness, consumption data rice, policies and incentives in the industry, inventory of technologies related to rice, recent news on rice, inventory of quality requirements of rice cultivation and indicators related to the agronomic managemente of rice in the region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3798?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6223, 'Climate and nutri-smart agricultural bundles for vulnerable and host populations in Nigeria to improve nutrition and livelihoods', 'This innovation provides a bundle of climate and nutri-smart inputs to vulnerable and host populations in a fragile setting, with the intention of improving the nutritional and resilience outcomes of the recipients. The bundle includes biofortified seed, fertilizer, pesticide, and insurance. Selected farmers will receive a voucher for 50% of the value of bundle. The goal of this project is to provide a sustainable way for vulnerable people to sustainably improve their nutrition and livelihoods.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6223?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1935, 'Digital Traceability: A biofortified rice traceability system for seed businesses in Indonesia', 'Seed, grain, and food businesses in Indonesia rarely use any form of traceability, with some limited use of paper-based records or digitized business management systems. This makes protecting the value of biofortified zinc rice difficult to protect throughout the supply chain. This innovation aims to bring together private-sector businesses that offer digital traceability systems to seed businesses that supply zinc-fortified rice in Indonesia. In 2023, we collaborated with a local innovation partner to design and develop a digital tool to monitor full supply chain activities of zinc wheat in two regions: Jakarta and Yogyakarta. In 2024, users will also participate in the research pilot. The final tool will be presented to users in 10 districts with proportion of the users selected for questionnaires on likeability and usability of the tool.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1935?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1037, 'A global platform for multiple users including the UN and other development agencies for sourcing, consolidating, and sharing information on rangelands status, change, influencing forces, and restoration opportunities.', 'The global rangelands data platform is being established in response to a gap in rangelands data identified by the UN Environment Assembly through Resolution L17 Innovations in Sustainable Rangelands and Pastoralism 2019 and the complimentary report: A Case of Benign Neglect. Knowledge Gaps About Sustainability in Pastoralism and Rangelands. With funding from the CGIAR Big Data Platform and through a consultative process the outline of the platform was conceptualised. A global rangelands atlas was launched as a first step. It is anticipated that the global rangelands data platform will be launched in 2026. It will bring together big data on rangelands, source data including exploring crowd-sourcing and be an interactive platform for producing reports and trends for multiple users including UN Agencies monitoring rangeland and development agencies and actors developing interventions for rangelands, researchers and academics, and ultimately pastoralists themselves.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1037?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(1783, ' Groundwater governance toolbox: Guidance on how to identify, adapt, and combine tools to improve groundwater governance in a participatory and locally-appropriate manner for government agencies and communities', 'Groundwater is especially challenging resources to govern, because it is mobile and difficult to observe. Externally imposed rules often fail to work, but examples of community governance of groundwater are also scarce. This toolbox offers examples of innovative procedures, policy instruments, and institutional tools. Stakeholders engaged in enhancing groundwater governance can utilize these examples to comprehend their issues and opportunities. Additionally, they can deliberate on and potentially reach consensus on norms or rules aimed at preventing aquifer depletion. Furthermore, the toolbox facilitates the creation of shared gains that enhance water productivity. Institutional tools for groundwater governance could help deal with complex nexus linkages such as transitions to solar-powered pumping, aquifer recharge and storage to buffer against drought, and regenerating ecosystems. This groundwater governance toolbox offers government agencies and communities resources for thinking about the variety of instruments available and how they might be chosen, combined, and adapted to create customized toolkits to solve problems and achieve gains in specific contexts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1783?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4908, 'Collective action for smallholder sesame farmers to lower transaction costs in marketing', 'Smallholder sesame producers and local traders usually act individually. Therefore, they usually struggle with high transaction costs and market harassment by intermediaries and other agents along the value chain. Small groups will be formed voluntarily by participating farmers and traders to make marketing decisions more efficient.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4908?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(60, 'Forage breeding partnerships between eastern and southern Africa, and Latin America accelerate genetic gains for forage varieties that contribute to the productivity and environmental sustainability of crop-livestock systems', 'Forage quantity and quality constrain smallholder livestock production in Sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America, the Caribbean, and Tropical Asia. Improved forages can enhance farm productivity and contribute to the environmental sustainability of crop-livestock systems. Forage breeding seeks to identify niches for forages in a participatory manner, exchanging more productive and resilient farmer-demanded forages between regions. The CIAT breeding program works with four breeding pipelines involving interspecific Urochloa, Urochloa and Megathyrsus types for livestock grazing, and/or cut and carry.  Between 2022-2024, market segments and Target Product Profiles were updated, training populations for genomic selection established, phenotyping accuracy improved, cycle time in Urochloa reduced to 3 years, genotype-by-environment analyses executed, NARS partners trained in using Near-Infrared Spectroscopy (NIRS), and public and private sector partners more strongly involved in station and on-farm evaluations. Partners in six countries registered eight new varieties, all of them deriving from pipelines improved for drought and acid soil tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/60?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1024, 'Smart Fertilizer Recommendation Tool: A Smartphone and SMS-Based Solution for Extension Agents and Lead Farmers in Sorghum, Teff, and Wheat Cropping Systems in Ethiopia', 'A user-centered smartphone and SMS-based landscape segmented (hillslope, midslope, foot slope) fertilizer recommendation tool for extension agents and lead farmers that enables better fertilizer targeting and optimized application to improve productivity, nutrient use efficiency and reduce costs of wheat, Teff, and sorghum farmers in Ethiopia. The advisory tool will run on a prediction engine that uses data analysis techniques to generate fertilizer recommendations that will be of value at the local and national levels where its advisories will be a reference point for the national soil health investment and the national soil strategy.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1024?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1189, 'RiceAdvice Lite - Digital Agronomy Advisory Tool for Site-Specific Fertilizer and Crop Management in Economic Community Of West African States countries', 'RiceAdvice Lite is a decision support tool designed to provide smallholder farmers, Atafi lead farmers, and extension agents with expert guidance on fertilizer and weed management, specifically tailored to enhance rice cultivation. Another component of the innovation is the development of a digital data collection tool to support the Sustainable Rice Platform (SRP) Standard and Performance Indicators. This aspect of the project aims to automate the assessment of farmer compliance with SRP standards, streamlining data processing, analytics (incorporating machine learning and yield decomposition analysis among other Key Performance Indicators), and visualization for external stakeholders. The innovation is  focusing on delivering these sophisticated advisory and analytical services to directly benefit rice producers and contribute to sustainable agriculture practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1189?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(43, 'Rice breeding networks in South Asia accelerate genetic gains for updated, diverse end-user requirements, in varieties enabling fast crop rotations or suited for irrigated and (semi-)deep-water systems', 'In collaboration with public and private partners and farmers in South Asia, rice breeding sustainably enhances yields; resilience to pests and diseases; tolerance to drought, salinity, while improving grain and nutritional qualities. Increasingly defined product concepts and larger-scale on-farm trialing, executed in collaboration with national research systems, result in varieties better aligned with the demands of farmers, consumers, and other end-users while pointing to further improvement needs. Nine breeding pipelines develop early maturing, stress-tolerant varieties for direct seeding, and medium and late maturing rice for irrigated, deep, and semi-deep-water systems, and saline areas. Genomic selection and rapid generation reduced breeding cycle time to 3 years. A recently developed SpeedFlower protocol will further reduce cycle times. Large-scale on-farm evaluation in Bangladesh and India created gender-disaggregated farmer feedback and identified suitable lines for different market segments. Between 2022-2024, partners in Bangladesh, India and Nepal registered 77 varieties, improved for climate resilience.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/43?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(31, 'Durum wheat breeding network accelerates genetic gains for more productive, resilient varieties that meet end-user requirements for farmers in Africa, the Middle East, and Asia', 'As demand for pasta and semolina products grows across Africa, the Middle East, and Asia, ICARDA durum breeding is addressing the climatic challenges, pests, and diseases that hinder domestic production. The resulting varieties deliver drought and heat tolerance, stable production across seasons, resistances to major biotic stresses, and meet end-users requirements. Together with public and private sector partners and farmers, six breeding pipelines target the global drylands: 1. South Asia drylands - limited irrigation, 2. West/Central Asia drylands - rainfed, 3. North Africa drylands - rainfed, 4. East Africa drylands - rainfed, 5. High-cold drylands - rainfed, 6. West Africa savannas - irrigated. The current breeding cycle time is 2.5 years, using speed breeding, genomic selection, and exploiting rare alleles from genebank accessions.  Between 2022 and 2024, partners in 8 countries registered 16 varieties (seven in 2024). Five derived from pipelines that select for drought and heat tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/31?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1030, 'Water Productivity Atlas that enables planners and managers to identify solutions to improve water productivity', 'Water productivity constitutes one of the key ways to expand Water Energy Food Ecosystems (WEFE) benefits in water scarce contexts, yet water productivity is variable and in cases low. This innovation is focused on development of a Decision Support System (DSS) that enables planners and managers to identify solutions (e.g., resources allocations or crop mixes) that improve productivity. The DSS is a computer-based platform reveals the productivity associated with alternative investment choices, which can support investment decisions that optimize water productivity and WEFE benefits in different geographies. Key users of the DSS platform are water planners and managers, as well as those in related sectors (i.e. Energy, Food and Ecosystems).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1030?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(30, 'Bread wheat breeding network accelerates genetic gains for high-yielding, climate-resilient varieties that meet consumer preferences in semi-arid/arid regions in Africa, Central and West Asia; Partner release eight varieties', 'As demand for wheat grows rapidly across Africa, the Middle East, and Asia, ICARDA is addressing the growing climatic challenges, pests, and diseases that hinder its domestic production by generating an effective wheat-breeding strategy. The resulting wheat varieties deliver high yield potential, resistance/tolerance to abiotic and biotic stresses, and acceptable end-use qualities. ICARDA''s bread wheat breeding program develops new candidate varieties in collaboration with public and private sector partners and farmers, through two breeding pipelines targeted at 1. Rainfed spring bread wheat for Central and West Asia, and North, Eastern, Southern and West Africa dry lands, and 2. Heat tolerant spring bread wheat for Eastern, Southern, West and North Africa, and Central and West Asia regions. The current breeding cycle time is 3 years. Between 2022 and 2024, partners in released eight varieties, deriving from pipelines that select for heat and drought tolerance. Biofortification for zinc and iron is ongoing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/30?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3419, 'MiniSASS v2: An Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based water quality monitoring system', 'The miniSASS (Stream Assessment Scoring System) was developed as a citizen science tool for monitoring the health of river systems and reflecting the water quality by assessing macroinvertebrates communities. The miniSASS samples the macroinvertebrate community in a river reach and compares the community present to the expected community under ideal natural conditions. The information garnered during a survey relies heavily on the accurate identification of macroinvertebrates by low-skilled citizen scientists. This leaves a potential for errors in identification which may impact the accuracy of results and, ultimately, of the river health assessment. In response, we initiated the development of the new version of miniSASS, the MiniSASS v2, a smartphone application with built-in artificial intelligence and machine-learning algorithms for the automatic, real-time identification of macroinvertebrates.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3419?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1296, 'The GANSO (GANaderia SOtenible or Sustainable Livestock) guarantee is a voluntary evaluation tool for livestock producers to improve livestock production systems to sustainable landscapes.', 'The ''GANSO Guarantee'' is a voluntary evaluation tool for livestock producers. It was developed by the GANSO (GANadería SOstenible / Sustainable Livestock) organization, co-founded by the Alliance BI-CIAT, created to improve the livestock production systems in the Colombian Eastern Plains, from extensive degraded pastures and soils to productive and sustainable landscapes.  The guarantee has grown and developed as a set of 5 pillars (environment, people, animal welfare, management & meat quality) and 52 practices are independently assessed and categorized in order of their compliance level. This gives the farmers incentives to sell beef at a premium price, supported by the consumers'' willingness to pay for environmental-friendly products. These products are currently available in one the largest retail store in Colombia, with 182 stores in 6 cities. Starting in Meta department, now the tool is being implemented in other departments of the Orinoquia region.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1296?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4972, 'Urochloa hybrids, improved forage grasses (resistant to (a)biotic stress and with improved productivity), mostly for cattle farmers', 'CIAT started an interspecific breeding program with Urochloa brizantha (CIAT-6294 cv. Marandú), Urochloa decumbens (CIAT-0606, cv. Basilisk), and Urochloa ruziziensis (BR4X-44-2) in 1987 (Rao et al. 1998; Miles et al. 1995), and since then delivered numerous Urochloa hybrid lines to the private forage seed sector for further selection and commercialization. This has led to the release of the first Urochloa hybrid ever in 2001 in Mexico by Grupo Papalotla and to several subsequent hybrid releases thereafter. Today, 6 different Urochloa hybrids are being commercialized in around 70 countries globally, mainly among cattle farmers, leading to increased animal productivity, livelihood benefits, and positive environmental externalities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4972?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(6299, 'Multi-stakeholder knowledge sharing platform on Mixed Farming Systems in Mid-hills of Nepal', 'The Multi-Stakeholder Agriculture Knowledge Hub (MSAKH) is an innovative platform that fosters collaboration among local and municipal stakeholders to address agricultural challenges, share experiences, and promote sustainable mixed farming systems. Its primary goals include empowering farmers—particularly women, Dalits, and marginalized groups—by improving their access to resources and advocating for policy changes. The key objectives of the platform are to enhance inclusive participation, facilitate knowledge sharing, strengthen advocacy, build capacity, support monitoring and coordination, and scale innovative agricultural practices. In March 2024, four MSAKHs were established—one each in Wards 6 and 7 of Halesi Tuwachung Municipality and Wards 12 and 14 of Gurbhakot Municipality. These hubs were formed in the presence of the research team, social mobilizers, municipal staff, and ward chairpersons. Each MSAKH comprises diverse stakeholders, including farmers, agro-entrepreneurs, local elected representatives and officials, financial institutions, community-based organizations (CBOs), and the private sector.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6299?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6292, 'Efficient Water Use Practices for Agriculture: Micro-Irrigation Systems', 'Khotang and Surkhet lie in the mid-hills of Nepal and are drought-prone areas. Insufficient water availability in both areas has impacted agricultural productivity. During the participatory innovation selection process for various components of MFS, farmers ranked micro-irrigation as their priority innovation to increase vegetable productivity. Hence, interested farmers were given training on micro-irrigation practices and other efficient water-use practices for agriculture. 76 drip irrigation kits and 114 sprinkler heads have been distributed to the farmers for the pilot study. Farmers are supported by irrigation tools based on inclusive criteria to ensure that women and marginalized farmers are not left behind.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6292?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(6801, 'Improving water use efficiency through micro irrigation practices', 'Khotang and Surkhet lie in the mid-hills of Nepal and are drought-prone areas. Insufficient water availability in both areas has impacted agricultural productivity. During the participatory innovation selection process for various components of MFS, farmers ranked micro-irrigation as their priority innovation to increase vegetable productivity. Hence, interested farmers were given training on micro-irrigation practices, along with efficient water use practices including conservation agriculture, mulching, and multi-use water systems. 76 drip irrigation kits and 114 sprinkler heads have been distributed to the farmers for the pilot study. Farmers are selected based on inclusive criteria to ensure that women and marginalized farmers are not left behind.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6801?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(6810, 'Inclusive multi-stakeholder knowledge sharing platform on mixed farming system for farmers, and relevant stakeholders in Halesi Tuwachung Municipality, Khotang district and Gurbhakot Municipality, Surkhet district to support farmers.', 'The innovation aims to establish an inclusive multi-stakeholder knowledge sharing platform on Mixed Farming Systems (MFS) in the research area. The platform will serve as a collaborative space for diverse groups of farmers, researchers, extension workers, local policymakers, private sectors and other relevant stakeholders. The innovation intends to provide a platform to  exchange knowledge on mixed farming system and indigenous agriculture practices, discuss challenges faced by diverse groups of farmers and stakeholders, collectively identify solutions for improved farm productivity, water resource management, income generation, market opportunities and other related issues. The platform will also provide opportunities to farmers to extend linkage/network and collaborate with diverse stakeholders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6810?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(4625, 'Processed (shredded) rice straw for improved dairy buffalo milk productivity, for use by buffalo farmers in Nepal', 'Processed enriched straw is a new processing technique to improve low quality rice straw, which forms 70% of the daily diet of large ruminants in Nepal. In this process the straw is shredded using a shredding machine and fortified with 8% molasses, 1% urea and 1% mineral mixture. It is then compressed and bagged as mash or block. Feed industries procure rice straw from farmers in the harvest season, which will then be processed and sold to dairy keepers through their cooperatives in the lean season when there is acute nutrient stress for dairy animals.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4625?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1105, 'A digital decision support tool to help livestock farmers to produce improved animal diet on farm by balancing nutrients, processing roughages and mixing feed ingredients in proper proportion in Nepal.', 'The tool is a mobile based application that can be used by extension agents and farmers to balance their animals’ diet by matching nutrients required by the animal (for body mainetance, production and reproduction) and their availability in the feed offered. The application selects the cheapest locally available feeds to bridge the nutritional gap.  The suggestions are given in the form of an advice memo, which includes guidelines for efficient use of different feeds. Additional information such as the nutrient gap, cost of purchased feeds and additional income before and after balancing the diet are also given.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1105?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(1116, 'National food systems greenhouse gas emission profiles to help decision makers plan for food systems emissions reduction strategies', 'There is a lack of data on food systems greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions at the food system level. The country profiles, which will take the form of reports and briefs, will synthesize GHG emission-  and climate change-related data at the national level, provide an analysis of key drivers of and contributors to GHG emissions in food systems and explore the potential of alternative mitigation actions and associated trade-offs, taking into account environmental boundaries and transformational needs. Iterative versions of these profiles will include new data on emissions generated by the Initiative and deepen the food systems approach in order to break down entrenched sectoral categories and existing adaptation and mitigation silos while providing a means to integrate and assess emissions from multiple sources along the food value chain from production to consumption. In 2022 progress was made on draft profiles for Bangladesh, China, Colombia, Kenya and Vietnam.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1116?phase=4', 0, NULL),
(7993, 'EncontrAR Platform: a online knowledge management system of nature-positive solutions for climate adaptation in the Andes', 'A web platform that seeks to connect the supply and demand of knowledge about Andean family agriculture in a context of climate change. Its users will be able to find experiences and good practices of Andean family agriculture from Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador and Peru, as well as contribute new (nature-positive) experiences with key data from its evidence-based implementation in the mountainous territory.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7993?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(39, 'Partner-led rice breeding network in Latin America exchanges experiences and develops more productive varieties for farmer evaluation in Latin America and Africa', 'International Center for Tropical Agriculture-(CIAT) rice breeding in Latin America takes place together with Latin American Fund for Irrigated Rice-(FLAR), a partnership with 36 public and private sector partners in 17 countries, which influences two-thirds of the 5.7 million hectares sown to paddy rice in Latin America. Breeding seeks to make direct-seeded rice varieties more productive, resilient, competitive and healthy. CIAT and national partners use different breeding strategies, accompanied by selection in hot spots for main diseases, leading to regular variety releases. Change ambitions are shared with those of the other CGIAR-NARES-SME networks: pipelines focused on specific environments and markets; better product profiling; integration between discovery, development, and delivery; and cutting-edge breeding strategies and methods to accelerate genetic gains. Breeding cycle times are 32-62 months. Resulting breeding materials are routinely shared with partners in Africa. Between 2022-2024, partners in 13 countries registered 16 varieties selected for late-stage drought tolerance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/39?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3314, 'Connecting Tumaini, an artificial intelligence powered smartphone app, with occurrence maps for banana disease monitoring.', 'The Tumaini app is an AI-powered banana disease detection tool that can identify symptoms on any part of the crop, including the leaf, pseudostem, fruit, bunch, or entire plant. The Tumaini app helps agricultural extension workers and banana farmers to correctly identify banana diseases and improve disease surveillance and management. Once a pest or disease location is identified and can be reported using the GPS uploading option and then it can be displayed in the global map database. Once a pest or disease affecting a crop is identified, quick and targeted action can reduce the extent of outbreaks and potentially save entire crops. Tumaini is available in English, Spanish, French, Tamil, and Swahili.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3314?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(1038, 'Participatory land use planning across administrative borders in pastoral areas for protecting shared grazing lands and other resources.', 'Participatory land use planning (PLUP) for pastoral areas overcomes many of the challenges of conventional land use planning including creating hard boundaries around villages or other administrative units, breaking up and limiting or preventing access to shared grazing lands and water points. PLUP for pastoral areas aims to keep rangelands and particularly grazing lands intact working across administrative boundaries through joint PLUP agreements strengthening reciprocal relations, collective tenure and good governance and resolving and preventing conflicts between land users. Pastoral communities traditionally marginalized from village PLUP process benefit particularly from the approach due to it being more inclusive of all land users in the planning and decision-making processes. The PLUP process includes the collection of data for assessing land potential most relevant for pastoral systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1038?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5847, 'Marker-assisted introgression of fecundity mutation to support community-based breeding programs of sheep in low input systems', 'Fecundity gene introgression has become an economical method to improve sheep prolificacy in developing countries. The innovation aims to increase the number of effective carriers of the mutation by the introgression of fecundity mutation into non-carrier conventional flocks based on a marker-assisted breeding program. The genotyping was carried out to follow up on the segregation of prolificacy mutation. The conventional, non-prolific ewes, were inseminated by carrier rams and yielded 100% carriers females at heterozygous state. These females were inseminated with conventional rams and produced carriers’ lambs with 66.7% males and 33.3% females. The prolific males are to be kept in breeding centers to disseminate the fecundity mutation in commercial flocks. The innovation was developed and tested in Tunisia and within Sustainable Animal Productivity (SAPLING) countries. It has the potential to be applied in Ethiopia, particularly in Bonga sheep, where a mutation linked to higher prolificacy has already been identified.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5847?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1261, 'South Asia Drought Monitoring System (SADMS) platform supports national and local authorities for drought monitoring and management.', 'South Asia Drought Monitoring System (SADMS) is a powerful platform that helps farmers, authorities, and extension workers to monitor and effectively manage droughts. It combines real-time weather updates and satellite data to provide (1) forecast droughts and monitor their severity at district-level, (2) trigger contingency plans when drought strikes, protecting smallholder farmers, and (3) analyze national and regional data from satellites and other sources. SADMS was developed successfully in South Asia and was now transferred to Africa. It inspired similar platforms in Zambia and Pakistan, proving its adaptability and effectiveness. SADMS is a data-driven approach empowers communities, safeguards vulnerable populations, and builds regional resilience against a shared climatic challenge. SADMS stands as a testament to innovation in the face of environmental adversity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1261?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(807, 'Providing off-grid solar irrigation technologies and services to low-income farmers through inclusive and sustainable end-user finance mechanisms.', 'Addressing physical and economic water scarcity through the provision of off-grid solar pumps to smallholder farmers in key-value chains and supporting adaption through sustainable finance solutions in Zambia. In many off-grid areas, petrol and diesel pumps are commonly used for irrigation. However, they are expensive to run and contribute to environmental pollution. With the abundance of sunshine in Sub Sahara Africa, solar pumps offer small farmers a promising alternative – if they can afford the initial investment. Financial mechanisms like pay-as-you-go models are linked to asset sales to decrease the investment threshhold for smallhold farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/807?phase=1', 6, NULL),
(1006, 'The Women''s Empowerment in Livestock Index(WELI) for measuring women''s empowerment in systems where livestock is important for livelihood', 'The WELI facilitates assessments of the impact of livestock interventions on the empowerment of women involved in the sector. By getting better at understanding what interventions work best at providing empowering opportunities, we can refine these interventions and in the long term, provide better empowering opportunities for women involved in livestock. Providing women farmers with opportunities for empowerment through livestock is important to both enhance household livelihoods and nutrition while also promoting progress on gender equity. In 2015, the International Livestock Research Institute (ILRI), in collaboration with Emory University developed the WELI tool based on the Women’s Empowerment in Agriculture Index (WEAI) developed earlier by IFPRI. The WELI measures women’s empowerment in livestock and crop agriculture and focuses specifically on key areas of livestock production, such as animal health, breeding and feeding; and on use of livestock products, such as animal-source-food processing and marketing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1006?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9256, 'National and provincial Indigenous foods platform for strengthening island food systems with Indigenous food and improving nutrition in Solomon Islands', 'Solomon Islands remains a highly agricultural society with high rates of consumption of traditional subsistence garden produce and aquatic foods. Imported foods have a role to play, but only if they are viewed as complementary to the local foods produced from gardens and seas rather than being a major component of the diet. Imagining food futures based on strengths in Indigenous food systems is important to change the course of island food systems in transition. Strengthening Indigenous food systems in Solomon Islands has been a priority by WorldFish and KastomGaden Association. A tokStori (indigenous forum format) brought together rural actors and key stakeholders to talk about traditional farming and aquatic foods under a nutrition-sensitive approach and to listen to ideas about what the Solomon Islands food systems future could look like. It has created a social movement platform for strengthening island food systems with Indigenous foods. 
Still relevant in 2024.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9256?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(6807, 'Acid soils dashboard makes available data to researchers and extensionists on lime requirements, yield response to lime, and profitability of lime for the main crop types cultivated in sub-Saharan Africa.', 'This dashboard provides state of the art data for ex-ante assessment of the profitability of liming on sub-Saharan Africa''s croplands. It provides state-of-the-art data and information on acid soil management for the major cereal, legume, root and tuber, and commodity crops in sub-Saharan Africa. It contains information related to soil pH, lime rates, profitability of liming and technical and scientific resources on soil acidity and acid soil management. The dashboard provides information to researchers, extensionists and the public who are interested to learn about acid soils in Sub-Saharan Africa.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6807?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(3248, 'Apical Rooted Cuttings - cost-effective method for potato seed production in Subsaharan Africa', 'An advancement in 2024 was a study to understand the challenges in production and supply of early generation seed by evaluating the efficiency and cost-effectiveness of three rapid multiplication techniques: rooted apical cuttings (RAC), aeroponics and sand-hydroponics. Results show significant differences in multiplication rates among cultivars across the three techniques and with no major interaction observed between cultivar maturity type and the multiplication technique used. None of the variations of the multiplication rates among cultivars resulted in a preference of another multiplication technology because of the cultivar. Analysis of the cost of set-up and operation of the multiplication facilities shows aeroponics as the most expensive technique to implement, followed by ARC and sand hydroponics.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3248?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(7569, 'Basin digger under minimum soil disturbance for smallholder Conservation Agriculture (Pfumvudza) in Southern Africa', 'In Zimbabwe, three million smallholder farmers are currently practicing Conservation Agriculture (CA) under the Climate-Proofed Presidential Input Scheme (Pfumvudza/Intwasa). It is also popular practice in Zambia and Malawi. The crops covered are maize, sorghum and legumes. Under this practice, that follows the principles of CA, farmers dig planting basins, apply lime, manure, fertilizer, and seeds in the basin. Such practice has resulted in yield boost due to the benefits of precision input use in addition to CA. However, it is limited to small plots due to drudgery that limits its wider adoption outside of the small plots. It is done mainly by women who are sometimes accompanied by children.  CIMMYT has developed an innovative low-cost mechanized solution called “Basin Digger” that can be used to save time and reduce drudgery, saving up to 85% labour compared to the current practice of manual digging using hand hoes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7569?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(5176, 'Unlocking indigenous plants as innovative solutions for restoring agro-ecological zones under changing climate in the dry areas', 'In response to  rangeland degradation, conventional practices involving exotic species like Acacia saligna and Atriplex nummularia have exacerbated ecological imbalances by endangering local flora and fauna and diminishing biodiversity. While developing the Tunisian National Strategy for Advancing Feed and Forage Resources in Tunisia, ICARDA proposed innovative solutions centered on the reintroduction of indigenous species. These were carefully selected to thrive in specific agroecological zones addressing key constraints, and which will be implemented by the Office of Livestock and Pastures and other national institutions. Drivers of loss of indigenous species planting include limited seed availability, lack of awareness, and entrenched institutional behavior. Therefore, our strategies involve genetic source scanning to match indigenous species with environmental conditions, raising awareness about indigenous species'' benefits, and efforts to change institutional behavior. Additionally, we are exploring ways to enhance seed collection and multiplication. This approach revitalizes degraded rangelands, contributing significantly to climate change mitigation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5176?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5256, 'Restoring rangelands: the art of flexible livestock grazing to restore soil health, ecosystem function and ecosystem services', 'Nomadic livestock systems have evolved over millennia in response to seasonal rainfall patterns. In Tunisia, where approximately one-third of the land is semi-arid, arid, or desert, degradation poses a significant challenge. The Gdel resting technique, a low-cost intervention, has been part of the national strategy for rangeland improvement since 1990, and is implemented by developing agencies for rangeland improvement. The pastoralists whose animals would normally graze in these areas are supported through the provision of feed supplements for 3 consecutive years. However, the implementation had its shortfalls. In 2018, unexpectedly high rainfall led to abundant vegetation growth, but the rigid implementation of the resting technique hindered pastoralists from capitalizing on the surplus biomass. In response, ICARDA developed flexible or opportunistic grazing criteria and trained partners to adapt to climate variability. This innovative approach allows pastoralists to optimize resource utilization during favorable years and addressing the shortcomings of previous implementations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5256?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4622, 'Pig weigh band for use by pig farmers and aggregators in Uganda, to enhance transparency in trade', 'In Uganda smallholder pig systems, the pig keepers usually do not have access to weigh-scale to obtain accurate pig weights. Knowledge of weight is important for both pig management decisions (such as determining drug dosage) and for determining sale price (which is based on estimated liveweight or carcass weight derived from this). To overcome this, a pig weigh-band was developed. A weigh-band is similar to a tape-measure, however it shows weight estimates of the pigs based on their heart-girth. The equation to predict weight from heart-girth was developed from a study on 764 pigs of different ages, sex, and breed types, from 157 smallholder pig keeping households in Central and Western Uganda (see Marshall et al., 2023). The weigh-bands and an associated training manual have now been produced, and are undergoing a pilot with women and men pig keepers in Uganda.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4622?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8922, 'Biopesticide-based Integrated Pest Management (IPM) of maize and cowpea insect pests', 'Five neem-based formulations were developed to promote the Biopesticide-based Integrated Pest Management (IPM) of cowpea (Maruca vitratta, Aphis craccivora, Megalurothrips sjostedti, and Clavigralla tomentosicollis) and maize pests, including African armyworm and the invasive fall armyworm (Spodoptera frugiperda). The formulations include (i) Neem oil Concentrate, (ii) Neem grain cake, (iii) Neem oil concentrate fortified with Eucalyptus leaf powder, (iv) Neem oil concentrate fortified with Hyptis leaf powder, and (v) Neem oil concentrate fortified with Lantana leaf powder. Procedures were established for manufacturing concentrates including packing and storage, and working solutions for field application using knap snack sprayers. The formulations were validated under researcher-managed trials and compared with synthetic pesticide applications. Training was organized for farmers and extension officials on manufacturing and applications of biopesticides. It also included the determination of pest incidence and decisions on IPM application. These formulations are ready for scaling, which will be pursued as the next steps.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8922?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(1300, 'e-Agrology: A High-Frequency Monitoring System Enhancing Sustainable Agriculture and Livestock Production for Farmers in the Global South', 'e-Agrology is a high-frequency monitoring system for agricultural production in the global South. This open-source solution for web and mobile includes a data collection module adapted for any crop and production system (perennials, seasonal, intercrops); a transformation module for automated data cleaning and standardization; and a visualization module for dashboard creation and data download. It allows monitoring of production systems, including detailed data on planting dates, pest and disease management, agronomic practices, costs, yield, input prices, commercialization, and post-harvest. The system has supported CGIAR private sector partners in measuring their sustainability within agri-food systems; also, we have generated site-specific recommendations for farmers based on optimal planting dates, nutrient management, best practices, and yield limiting factors per agroecology. By December 2022, more than 250,000 farmers are registered with 350,000 agronomic seasonal logbooks. Sustainability indicators have been measured for including water-use efficiency, nutrient-use efficiency, CO2 emissions, profitability, yield, and others.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1300?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4185, 'Rikuy-Agrobio: a toolbox for in-situ agrobiodiversity monitoring', 'Rikuy-Agrobio is a toobox based website that contains different methodologies to monitor agrobiodiversity. The tools contained in Rikuy-Agrobio allow agrobiodiversity monitoring from genes to landscape. This tool is promoted and enriched by the Andean Initiative and Nature +.  Exercises of use with farmers are already planned to monitor agrobiodiversity in hotspots of diversity. The following description sheets will be found in Rikuy-Agrobio:
1. Rapid Agrobiodiversity assessment:https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127623
2. Trials in farmers'' fields and morphological descriptors: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127635
3. Photographic documentation: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127631
4. Qualitative red listing: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127638
5. Project-based learning: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127699
6. Quantitative red listing: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127700
7. Genotyping: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127701
8. Baseline catalogues: https://hdl.handle.net/10568/127702 
9. Participatory mapping - pGIS
10. Citizen science with children and youth', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4185?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(36, 'Maize breeding networks involving 60+ partners in South Asia accelerate genetic gains for hybrids that remain productive under drought, heat and excessive moisture, benefitting farmers in stress-prone environments', 'In collaboration with the International Maize Improvement Consortium in South Asia, encompassing 60 public and private sector organizations, and farmers, maize breeding efforts aim at developing climate-resilient hybrids, with resistance to key diseases and other farmer-preferred traits, for the region''s smallholders that grow rainfed maize in three types of highly stress-prone environments: 1. Rainfed ecologies with an annual rainfall of 600-800mm, encompassing about 2.85 million hectares; 2. Ecologies with highly erratic rainfall exposing maize to bouts of excessive moisture and periodic drought, encompassing 4.3 million hectares, and 3. Heat-stressed ecologies, encompassing 1.7 million hectares. On-farm testing assess performance of candidate varieties with over 75 farmers in India, Bangladesh and Nepal. Cutting-edge tools and technologies, including doubled haploids, and genomics-assisted breeding enable a breeding cycle time of just over 4 years. Between 2022-2024 partners in Bangladesh, India, Nepal, and Sri Lanka registered ten climate resilient maize hybrids.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/36?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(2765, 'AgriFoodTech and ClimateTech innovation challenges to scout and accelerate science-based technologies, in the early stage, that are accessible to all and can generate impact for sustainable agriculture and climate action', 'The Agritech Innovation Challenges scout and accelerate agri-food tech solutions for sustainable, efficient, and resilient agrifood systems, and to ignite entrepreneurial fervor and scientific innovativeness among talents with ambition to tackle complex agricultural challenges. As an ecosystem enabler, the CGIAR Accelerate for Impact Platform (A4IP) leverages CGIAR’s scientific expertise, foot-on-the-ground, and legacy in research and innovation to co-design, accelerate, and de-risk the development and deployment of science-driven agri-tech and climate-tech solutions for sustainable agriculture and climate action.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2765?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(8453, 'Scaling Fund: Unlocking the power of innovation scaling for transformative impact in the agrifood systems', 'Scaling funds provide essential financial support needed to take promising innovations to the next level. The Fund aims to support bridging the gap between initial funding and the critical expansion phase, facilitating growth, market penetration, and sustainability. Scaling Funds are instrumental in propelling early-stage ventures towards success, enabling them to reach broader markets, serve more customers, and drive impactful change, ultimately Fuelling progress in various sectors and driving economic growth. 

As such, the CGIAR Scaling Fund plays a pivotal role in driving innovation, creating opportunities, and bolstering economic development in today’s dynamic and ever-evolving agri-food landscape. The Fund serves as a platform for CGIAR and its partners to collaboratively develop, negotiate, and finance scaling of innovations for transformative impact in the agri-food sector. This is done through funding, capacity building and continuous coaching of teams through their scaling trajectory.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8453?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3012, 'New varieties promoted using TPP for the market segment helps in positioning the variety in the market-place for better adoption which addresses pain points of growers in the market segment', 'List of varieties to be replaced were shared by National Partners. IRRI, shared potential candidates that match the varieties and are improved for the deficiencies existing in the old varieties. These lines were tested vigorously and after head-to-head comparison, selected lines were released as varieties through the national systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3012?phase=1', 6, NULL),
(3016, 'Replacement and positioning of rice varieties using target product profile in Sub-Saharan Africa', 'Together with the NARES, a clear recommendation for varieties to be ''replaced'' and ''Product Positioning'' for the varieties was prepared and to be promoted in Sub-Saharan Africa. During the 2021-2022 season in Kenya, a total of twenty-four tricot trials were established and managed in eleven rice growing hubs (schemes) distributed in seven counties. During the 2021-22 season in Tanzania, a total of thirteen tricot trials were established in thirteen rice-growing hubs (schemes) distributed in ten districts. During the 2021-22 main (wet) season in Mozambique,  a total of fourteen tricot trials were established in ten rice growing hubs (schemes) distributed in five districts. In the off-season, seven released varieties were used for the establishment of eleven tricot trials in Mozambique.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3016?phase=1', 6, NULL),
(4902, 'Clustering smallholder shrimp farming for traceability and sustainability certification in Bangladesh', 'Clustering shrimp farming for traceability and certification strengthens backward linkages with farmers via aggregation, disintermediation, technology transfer, and enabling certification. 
In ACI (Advanced Chemical Industries) Agrolink, clustering shrimp farming organizes groups of 20-25 farmers with contiguous ponds into a cluster with direct shrimp procurement to process by the company. The clustering shrimp farming is bundled with providing Input credit to shrimp farmers who buy ACI Animal Health’s feed, insurance, and formalization of contracts with clusters to support the buyback of shrimp. 
In BSFF (Bangladesh Shrimp and Fish Foundation), shrimp farmers commit to monoculture and are provided SPF-PL (Specific Pathogen Free Post-Larvae), cluster-level credit from designated banks, and a buyback arranged with specific processing firms. Farmers are persuaded to deepen their ponds to reduce water temperature variability that can trigger disease outbreaks, and facilitate increased stocking densities as a precondition to participate.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4902?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(864, 'Foresight Monitoring Tool for Adaptive Management and Learning along the Theory of Change for Foresight Initiative Staff', 'Foresight Monitoring Tool for Adaptive Management and Learning was designed to support planning, reporting, managing and learning  along the Foresight Initiative Theory of Change (ToC) populated by a number of knowledge products, capacity sharing activities and innovations generated by the synergistic collaboration of a multidisciplinary  team of scientists from 11 entities and external partners.
Further development will focus on progress towards Outcomes and Outcome Indicators;  incorporating budget planning and expenses per output;  network maps with partners and key stakeholders  and human resources allocated to each output/outcome. The Tool aims to support the achievement of results, feedback for management decisions and improved performance towards impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/864?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(4872, 'Interlinking extension and coffee markets through a novel and transparent standard for coffee sales to improve smallholder coffee farm productivity, coffee quality, and price transmission', 'Interlinking extension and coffee markets through a novel and transparent standard for coffee sales to improve farm productivity, coffee quality, and price transmission. An app is used to communicate the quality test results to farmers. Integrating technical assistance with coffee sales by quality and weight is new to Honduras''s competitive and fragmented coffee supply chains. The innovation bundle would be demanded, monitored by exporters, and implemented by intermediaries. The traceability scheme will also facilitate compliance with the (EU) regulations on zero deforestation and child-labor-free agricultural supply chains.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4872?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1005, 'Living Labs for People as innovation and knowledge co-production space for sustainable and low-emission food system transformation', 'Living Labs for People are a space for food system stakeholders to co-design, test, demonstrate, and advance socio-technical innovations and associated modes of governance for sustainable food system transformation that delivers mitigation benefits. The Living Lab for People conceptual framework provides guiding principles for the design and implementation of Living Labs across the initiative''s target countries and its intended users are stakeholders (farmers, farmer organizations, development organizations, local governments, etc.) implementing Living Labs for People.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1005?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(863, 'GeoAgro-MiSR: A comprehensive digital solution that enables Egyptian small holder farmers achieve climate smartness, sustainable intensification and economic optimization under climate change and economic volatility', 'The GeoAgro-MiSR system represents a powerful digital transformation that serves the national extension system, farmers, youth, and women, providing them with site-specific agronomic recommendations generated from farm analytics. EiA envisions a bottom-up and top-down synergy approach to agricultural transformation by leveraging digital tools and engaging stakeholders at different scales. At the grassroots level, the GeoAgro-MiSR smartphone application offers tailor-made agronomic advisories and services to smallholder farmers. It also facilitates citizen science by allowing users to contribute data to a centralized database. The GeoAgro-MiSR system comprises a centralized server hosted at ICARDA and two user interfaces: the GeoAgro-MiSR app and the GeoAgro-MiSR administrative dashboard. Communication between users and experts is via real-time interaction, including the "ask an expert" module and P2P chatting. The system also provides weather forecasts, remote sensing analytics for biophysical variability, and crop-specific agronomic practices.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/863?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(955, 'Online Economics Modeling Course for Policy Analysts', 'This effort seeks to leverage significant advances in distance learning technologies and techniques to deliver a step change in the capabilities of countries to utilize simulation models in pursuit of their development goals, notably food security in a context of climate change. It has been designed with a focus on scaling economic simulation modeling capacities for meeting the climate challenge in low- and middle-income countries, targeting four sets of key institutions: central decision-making units within governments; research institutes with formal linkages to policymaking; central banks; and large research networks. Central to this innovation is an online economic modeling program. Alongside in-person trainings, communities of practices, and a website portal, the program targets different audiences: people who i) want to know what insights can be gained and generated using economic modeling tools b) run the models for decision making, and iii)  design scenarios or models of their own.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/955?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3397, 'Framework developed to prioritize innovations and technologies with potential to reduce food systems greenhouse gas emissions', 'This framework helps to assess the potential of innovations and technologies to contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions from food systems. It considers (1) the technological,  institutional/political, social and biophysical context; (2) the local or country-specific barriers  to adoption; (3) value chain-specific barriers to scalability; (4) context-specific carbon benefits, sustainable development co-benefits and undesired social and biophysical effects that may result from broad adoption once barriers are overcome. The framework also helps in identifying scaling bottlenecks and designing a cost-effective scaling strategy for the prioritized innovations/strategies.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3397?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(952, 'Incorporation of organic growth additives to enhance in vitro propagation of selected shrub/tree species for large scale restoration and for supporting local communities dependent on agrosilvopastoral systems in building resilience.', 'Climate change and anthropogenic activities have negatively impacted agro-silvo-pastoral ecosystems across the planet. The global agenda to achieve ecosystem restoration targets has resulted in an increased demand for plant materials that current production systems are unable to satisfy. Under open field cultivation, several species show a low rate of seed germination or need special climatic conditions to germinate or take a long time to establish. Consequently, there is an urgency to preserve and propagate selected genotypes by improving efficient mass propagation techniques. It is necessary to resort to innovation tools, such as in vitro tissue culture technology. Plant tissue culture is used for producing high-quality, disease-free, and true-to-type plants at a fast rate. In vitro propagation can help meet the increasing demand for planting materials aimed at large scale restoration and afforestation programs. Incorporating organic growth additives can stimulate tissue growth and increase the number of shoots, leaves, and roots.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/952?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(1102, 'Sustainable rangeland management toolkit with a holistic and multidisciplinary approach for addressing the biophysical and sociocultural aspects under changing climate and land use leading towards more resilient pastoral systems', 'The sustainable rangeland management (SRM) toolkit advocates for a holistic and participatory multi-stakeholder approach that emphasizes the important role played by governance of rangelands using bottom-up landscape restoration. It emphasizes the need to ensure that decisions are based on both indigenous knowledge and modern science to address site-specific challenges. The goal of the toolkit is to assist and prepare practitioners working closely with local communities perform proper diagnostic, plan, and implement specific interventions and continuously monitor and evaluate rehabilitation or restoration efforts. Users have access to a collection of validated SRM practices aimed at achieving a neutral level of degradation (LDN) and offering a strong potential to rehabilitate and restore degraded rangeland ecosystems. The case studies demonstrate step by step how the toolkit is used to identify practical solutions that are integrated and socially accepted leading towards more resilient livestock and pastoral systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1102?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3018, 'Seasonal-spatial integrated model developed to improve the economic-environmental performance of crop production in countries characterized by seasonal and spatial variations in crop mix and cropping practices', 'The modeling framework consists of three models: the inVEST spatially explicit nutrient delivery ratio model, used to assess the effect of cropping intensity and fertilizer application rate on the disservice of nutrient runoff; an econometric land use model, to examine how cultivation area responds to the price change of each of the two main crop groups (rice and non-rice crops) for each of the three cropping seasons; and a nonlinear programming model for minimizing total nitrogen loss in the country from soil across the annual three crop growing seasons. With proper adaptations, the modeling method can be used to study trade-offs between food and nutrition security and the provision of other ecosystem services such as carbon sequestration.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3018?phase=1', 1, NULL),
(4828, 'Pig multistakeholder platforms (MSPs) for enhancing value chain actor and stakeholder interactions for shared learning and enhancing visibility of the pig sector', 'Pig value chain actors and stakeholders lack platforms to foster interactions and leverage a collective voice to address value chain constraints and influence or contribute to policies that impact the sector. Multistakeholder platforms provide an entry point for various stakeholder groups to engage with pig value chain players e.g. private-public sector partnerships, funding agencies, civil society, policy makers, and development partners. The multistakeholder platforms create networks and avenues for learning, knowledge sharing and empowerment of actors in the pig value chain. The MSPs were formed in 2016 and are still useful avenues for sharing learning on SAPLING innovations and enhancing visibility and voice of pig value chain actors and stakeholders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4828?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(1250, 'Uncertainty calculator for national government livestock greenhouse gas emission inventories', 'The International Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) methodological guidance recommends countries report the uncertainty of their greenhouse gas inventories. However, uncertainty is practically never reported due to a lack of knowledge of the appropriate methods available. In collaboration with demand and scaling partner, the New Zeland Agricultural Greenhouse Gas Research Center (NZAGRIC), in support of the Greenhouse Gas Research Alliance Livestock Research Group, the Livestock and Climate Initiative is designing, piloting, and scaling a tool to automate uncertainty calculations based on user-supplied input data. This greenhouse gas uncertainty calculator is programmed in R and will be built into an interactive app (i.e., Shiny).  The tool is intended to be used by national government employees who prepare and have responsibility for submitting greenhouse gas inventories.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1250?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(4141, 'Farmer-Interface-Application (FIA) field scouting tool for real-time farmer access to decision-making on IPM of multiple pests/crops', 'The Farmer Interface App FIA is a digital advisory running on Android. It integrates a scouting algorithm guiding the farmer with simple symbols on how to move randomly in the field, check several crop plants, inspect them for pests (or damage symptoms), and press the right symbol to record the presence/absence of the pest. The App updated with the phenology data collected at eight constant temperatures using maize and onion as host plants, and models describing the life history of FAW were developed. These include the development time model, the development rate model, the mortality model, female fecundity, and adult (male and female) senescence. These models were therefore validated by comparing laboratory life table data with data collected at ambient temperature. FIA and VIPS were integrated by extending the FIA-cloud service to communicate with the VIPS platform.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4141?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(7980, 'Demand-driven assistance provision as part of agribusiness acceleration programs for high-impact sustainable agribusinesses to successfully start and thrive through a conducive policy and regulatory enabling environment', 'A conducive enabling environment for agribusinesses is fundamental for African economies to grow rapidly. The unit for enabling environment technical assistance supports agribusinesses identified by agribusinesses acceleration programs to practice inclusive and sustainable agriculture, investment, value chain development, and trade, with a high impact potential: benefitting and creating employment for the disadvantaged and marginalized groups, including women, youth, and migrants, and fostering climate-resilient agriculture, climate adaptation, and sustainable natural resource management. This unit assists agribusinesses in three general clusters: (1) mapping, (2) networking, and (3) advocacy. The first two are “low-hanging fruits,” providing agribusinesses with research-based evidence on the policy, market, and regulatory environments to increase awareness and connect them with public and private actors for market expansion and overcoming bureaucratic bottlenecks. The advocacy cluster, a “high-hanging fruit,” seeks to drive regulatory change through collaboration with national governments, a long-term process, and links businesses with industry associations toward this goal.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7980?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4821, 'The Compound Risk and Crisis Assessment framework for instability and displacement organisations', 'Analytical framework which provides development and displacement organizations with relevant and tailored information that is critical for planning resilience-building under climate change in fragile and conflict affected settings.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4821?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(789, 'Conservation agriculture is a cropping systems based on 3 interlinked principles: minimum soil disturbance, crop residue retention and crop diversification through rotation and intercropping.', 'Conservation Agriculture (CA) is based on three key principles: minimum soil disturbance, provision of permanent soil cover and the use of rotations or associations amongst other good agriculture practices. These CA principles are applicable to a wide range of crop production systems from low-yielding, rainfed conditions to high-yielding, irrigated conditions. However, the techniques used may vary from place to place depending on farm power, system management conditions and farmer circumstances. Recent work conducted in Zambia showed that CA-based systems are capable of increasing economic and nutritional returns. The social and economic benefits gained from combining production and protecting the environment under CA are greater than those from production alone. CA in maize-legume systems addresses major production challenges in farming areas of Eastern and Southern Africa where both cereals and legumes are commonly grown.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/789?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4738, 'Cross-border water risk framework to promote water security in emergency operations in Fragile and Conflict Affected Settings (FCASs)', 'Cross-border water management risk framework - to be incorporated into Environmental and Social Impact Assessments (ESIAs) for emergency operations (can be used by local/national authorities and service providers and development and humanitarian actors to guide water programs and investments).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4738?phase=3', 0, NULL),
(4765, 'Early warning, Action,  Finance platform (AWARE) in Fragile and Conflict Affected Communities', 'Data-driven anticipatory action to mitigate the impacts of refugees and Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) in host communities'' food, land and water system in a changing climate.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4765?phase=3', 0, NULL),
(5351, 'MDII: A multidimensional index designed to promote ethical and socially inclusive digital innovation processes within food, land, and water systems', 'Digital innovations are increasingly being utilized in food, land, and water systems. It''s crucial that these innovations are inclusive and cause no harm, especially for less developed countries where many people rely on food systems for their livelihoods. However, there is a lack of standard metrics and guidelines for measuring and promoting digital inclusivity. To address this gap, the Multidimensional Digital Inclusivity Index (MDII) has been developed. The MDII offers 22 indicators of digital inclusivity across five dimensions. It can be used to assess and promote the level of digital inclusivity of an innovation, an innovation bundle, an organization, or a country. This index aims to make digital innovations accessible, usable, beneficial, participatory, and safe for socially and economically marginalized groups in food systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5351?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(781, 'Green manure/cover crops (GMCC) for improved livestock feeding, are plants that are grown in cropping systems not primarily for their grain but to provide groundcover, feed, and to fertilize the soil', 'Feeding green manure/cover crops (GMCCs) to livestock is an innovative way of improving livestock productivity while attaining other benefits. GMCCs are plants that are grown in cropping systems not primarily for their grain but to provide groundcover, feed, and fertilize the soil. In most cases green manures are legumes that fix nitrogen from the air and, once killed by rolling, spraying, or uprooting, provide large amounts of biomass. This biomass can be used to feed livestock amongst other uses. GMCCs may be grown either as rotational crops or as intercrops with the main crop. When they are introduced as intercrops, they are commonly killed before introducing the next main crop. GMCCs have different growth habits and hence give different benefits depending on their characteristics. They also may serve different purposes in the farming systems depending on their local context.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/781?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(8087, 'Brachiaria grass hedgerows in maize-forage legume rotation systems for improved crop and livestock productivity in conservation agriculture systems', 'Forage legumes such as velvet bean, lablab, and cowpea are usually of a vigorous growth habit and their integration as rotational crops in maize systems reduces the competition between the maize and the legumes. In such systems, more biomass is produced such that a part of it can be fed to livestock while another part is retained as mulch although in most cases it is not adequate. However, such diets are usually high in protein with the need to balance them off with high-energy grasses for a more balanced diet for animals. The introduction of Brachiaria grass (Brachiaria ruziziensis) as a hedgerow in the maize-forage legume rotation system is a possible solution. The maize and the legume are grown in two separate fields and rotated annually. In each phase of the rotation, Brachiaria is grown between the first two and the last two crop rows as hedgerows.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8087?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4830, 'Analysis framework for Ecosystem of Food, Land, Water Systems (FLWS) actors in Fragile and Conflict-Affected Settings (FCAS) for greater intervention efficiency', 'Framework for mapping out different actors involved in stimulating and growing Food, Land, Water Systems (FLWS) innovations in Fragile and Conflict-Affected Settings (FCAS), assessing the role of different actors and explore how FLWS innovators contribute to increasing resilience in food production, land management, water resources, and migration, as well as anticipatory action for crisis management, identifying existing activities that specialise in supporting FLWS innovations in FCAS, extracting learnings, and exploring opportunities and strategies for supporting and stimulating sustainable development, peace-building, and crisis response efforts.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4830?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(794, 'Strip cropping is an improved intercropping strategy for smallholder farmers that capitalize on a better plant arrangement. Two types of strip cropping are common: Double-row strip cropping and four row strip cropping.', 'Intercropping is a system based on producing two or more crops within the same space. However, such a system is characterized by competition between crops. To curb competition, strip cropping, an innovative way of growing crops using spatial arrangements that reduce competition can be used. Two forms of this system (two-row and four-row) are based on the reduction in the spacing of maize rows to open space for legumes to grow. Under two-row strip cropping, maize is planted into rip-lines opened in double rows (50cm apart) and a gap of 130cm is left in between the maize double rows where legumes will be planted. For four-row strip cropping four rows of maize alternate with four rows of a legume but the inter-row spacing of maize is reduced from the usual 90 cm to 45 cm. This narrowing and reduction in size increases the growing space for four rows of legumes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/794?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(795, 'In maize-legume intercropping, two or more crops are grown simultaneously by smallholders in the same cropping season sharing the same piece of land. This practice reduces the risk of total crop failure and improves nutritional yields.', 'Maize monocropping usually leads to soil nutrient mining, the prevalence of pests and diseases, and undiversified household nutrition. Diversification through either intercropping or crop rotation is a method that can be used to reverse these negatives. However, crop rotations are usually not feasible for farmers with small landholding sizes. For such farmers, intercropping is a viable solution. Intercropping is the practice of growing two or more crops in the same area, at the same time. In general, intercropping is better suited to farmers with small landholdings whereas crop rotation is more suitable for farmers with larger landholdings. However, other factors such as household needs and farm economics determine the choice of these two practices. The land equivalent ratio (LER) is often used to measure the benefits of intercropping compared to planting a single crop (sole cropping).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/795?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(783, 'Maize and legumes rotate annually to reduce the effects of pest and disease  build-up and improve soil fertility and nutrition. Cereal-legume rotations are  agriculture practices which are common and targeted to all farmers globally.', 'Maize-legume rotations in Conservation Agriculture systems are an innovative method of reducing the build-up of pests and diseases and improve soil fertility and nutrition. Cereal-legume rotations are agriculture practices which are common and targeted to all farmers globally. Maize-legume rotations are traditonally practiced in areas where pest pressure is high and where soil fertility is limiting. Instead of growing just one crop (such as maize) in monoculture, the cereals are rotated with leguminous (cowpea, groundnuts, soybeans, common beans) or non leguminous crops (sunflower, cotton etc.) on an annual basis. As a good agriculture practice, this is essential for all farming, particularly in Conservation Agriculture systems.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/783?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3852, 'Integrated management of maize lethal necrosis (MLN), including native genetic resistance and biopesticides, for supporting smallholder farmers', 'Management of maize lethal necrosis (MLN) by the smallholders needs an integrated approach, as the disease is caused by a combination of viruses, especially Maize Chlorotic Mottle Virus (MCMV) and Sugarcane Mosaic Virus (SCMV). An experiment was undertaken in 2022 at the MLN Screening Facility at Naivasha, Kenya, under controlled conditions, using biopesticides for the control of thrips and aphids that act as vectors for MLN-causing viruses, in combination with MLN-resistant hybrids. Preliminary results showed significant effect of biopesticides in combination with native genetic resistance to MLN. These results need to be further validated in farmers'' fields.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3852?phase=3', 1, NULL),
(3183, 'Innovation Portfolio Management to intentionally manage science, innovation and scaling investments along an impact pathway', 'Most projects, programs or organizations do not just manage one, but multiple innovations. Innovation portfolio management refers to managing a portfolio of innovations, innovation projects or interventions aimed at delivering (positive) societal impact. The main objective is to ensure that the innovation portfolio of a project, program, country or entire organization is healthy and delivers against outcomes and impact. It can support portfolio managers in strategic decision-making, prioritization (e.g. as part of stage-gating), risk management and resource mobilization around gaps in the portfolio. It can also support strategic communication about the innovation portfolio with external audiences such as funders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3183?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5374, 'Orange Fleshed Sweetpotato (OFSP) Puree for nutrition  and livelihoods improvement through its commercialization and job creation to women and youths', 'Orange-Fleshed Sweetpotato (OFSP) puree is a semi-processed product made by steaming and mashing OFSP roots. It is highly valued for its nutritional content, particularly its rich supply of beta-carotene, a precursor of Vitamin A, which addresses Vitamin A deficiencies in populations at risk. It is used as an ingredient in various food preparations. OFSP puree is versatile and can be used as an ingredient in various food products, i.e. in Baking: bread, buns, mandazi, cakes, baby foods. OFSP puree derived porridge is used in the households and school feeding program.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5374?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4823, 'Climate, Peace and Migration Partnership (CPM-Partnership)', 'Partnership programme with humanitarian agencies and research institutes in the Global South with two objectives of a) making CGIAR analysis operationally relevant and b) supporting local research and evidence-generation. It will contribute to the localisation agenda of the humanitarian and development communities, promoting national leadership, citizen accountability and more direct funding to local actors. To be developed through 4 pillars: 1) SECONDMENTS of CGIAR staff to humanitarian, peacebuilding and development partners, providing expertise and technical support. 2) CONNECTING researchers and practitioner/policy experts and supporting global and regional networks of scientists and universities to promote quality research. 3) LOCALIZING support to researchers and science institutions to strengthen research capacity and accelerate peer-reviewed publication and use of science in and from the Global South. 4) DOCUMENTING the process and modalities of the Partnership, including failures and successes, to enable learning and establish a framework and evidence-base for future investments.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4823?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4834, 'Monitoring migration and displacement narratives in digital platforms to support decision-making', 'Publicly available dashboard that monitors digital platforms (e.g. social media) for institutional and citizen-level narratives about migration, mobility and displacement. The main aims are to map the policy landscape around migration and to uncover migrant communities’ concerns and drivers to decision-making. Analysis is focused on natural language processing, visual analysis and social network analysis.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4834?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(4829, 'Stability-and-Peace Accelerator for Food-Land and Water Systems Actors in Fragile and Conflict Affected Settings', 'Science-driven enterprise acceleration programme that identifies and enables the scaling of high impact innovations through demand-driven capacity building in fragile contexts and produces investment opportunities for public/private finance actors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4829?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(8103, 'Identifying mechanisation niches for improving irrigation-as-a-service business model', 'Stable Foods offers irrigation as service based on the biophysical and socio-ecoenomic setup of the farmer. The technological interventions are not one size fits all, instead they are geared towards developing interventions that match the end user. For example, a ground truthing exercise identifies the terrain, water source, the  farmers prevailing conditions such as affordability, land to labor ratio, irrigation potential, and distance to markets. Thereafter, the information is aggregated to develop a mechanization niche that describes the level of intensification, labor issues and market demands. This approach goes beyond conventional irrigation design/development approach by capturing the whole irrigation development value chain.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8103?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(4820, 'The CGIAR Climate Security Observatory - Displacement module', 'The Climate Security Observatory (CSO) is an online decision-making support tool providing robust, localized, and policy-relevant evidence on	how	exactly climate security risks	may	emerge across different geographic contexts. It aims to answer several	research	questions on how the  climate security nexus operates at the subnational level. To achieve this , the CSO performs a series of mixed-methods analyses (data-driven literature review, pathway analysis, social media issue mapping, governance coherence analysis, network analysis, spatial analysis, econometric analysis), before synthesizing outputs into an open-access online dashboard. Within the CSO, a module for decision making on displacement-related is being developed.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4820?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4764, 'Host Community Vulnerability Assessment Framework', 'The framework, tested with development and humanitarian actors, will guide food, land and water vulnerability analysis in host communities to guide anticipatory action initiatives - including inclusive early action plans for emergency response, and long-term climate risk prevention investments (climate adaptation investments).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4764?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(4835, 'The gender transformative qualitative field work methodology for fragile contexts to support research on interventions', 'The gender-responsive and transformative qualitative fieldwork methodology spells out how the Climate Security Team’s research considers gender equity and/ equality to ensure compliance with the Gender Equity and Social Inclusion (GESI) principles. It seeks to promote gender-sensitive, responsive and transformative research that considers and assesses how particular intersections of identities and social positions impact access to rights and opportunities and how policies, programmes, services and laws affect people in different, context-specific or even unexpected ways. Understanding various intersecting systems of inclusion/exclusion and advantage/ disadvantage allows a better appreciation of the lived experiences of different groups of women and men and how all these compound to shape gender inequality embedded in gender norms and power relations within communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4835?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(790, 'Regional and national policy dialogues to scale support to the enabling environment for sustainable crop diversification and trade in East and Southern Africa (ESA)', 'The policy support program provides governments in East and Southern Africa (ESA) with context-specific recommendations to help improve their enabling environment for climate-resilient agricultural transformation. Specific interests are in sustainable crop intensification and diversification and agribusiness and value chain development, with attention to gender and youth empowerment. The program comprises two pillars: 1) national policy dialogues, which bring together governments, agribusinesses, donors, researchers, and civil society organizations to explore feasible policy and investment interventions, and 2) the ESA Policy Hub, which scales the key takeaways from dialogues to the regional level to tap opportunities arising from trade and regional integration of national economies. Moreover, the program consists of two main innovation bundles: One, the main bundle, is “long-term technical assistance to governments,” garnering results from crosscutting scientific research and national policy dialogues and taking into account climate shocks and stresses to inform policy-making. Two, a “demand-driven technical facility” is available to national actors (government agencies, farmers, and small- and medium-scale enterprises) and connects strategically selected enterprises with a high-impact potential to agribusiness accelerator programs. The program fosters strategic alliances between various innovation partners and works closely with governments to ensure mainstreaming of recommendations into policymaking and implementation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/790?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(309, 'Ukama Ustawi Scaling Hub : A platform for enabling the scaling of agricultural innovations', 'The Scaling Hub aspires to support scientists, private and public sectors, and development partners in Eastern and Southern Africa who are designing, funding and/or delivering agricultural innovations for the transformation of food, land, and water systems. Its mission is to contribute to sustainable impacts by providing a range of research, capacity development on responsible scaling, and other engagements to help increase the use of proven agricultural innovations. More specifically, the hub will (i) incubate proven agricultural innovations and accelerate their adoption by end-users through facilitation of public and private partnerships; (ii) Enhance capacities within CGIAR and with strategic partners to enable responsible and inclusive scaling of agricultural innovations; (iii) Generate and share practical and scientific knowledge on the scaling of agricultural innovations; and (iv) Provide data-driven insights on CGIAR innovations in ESA and make it easier for partners to identify collaboration opportunities with CGIAR in East and Southern Africa. (v) Provide practical tools and frameworks on how to implement responsible scaling for transformative impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/309?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4901, 'Women’s Empowerment in Agrifood Governance (WEAGov) Assessment Framework on National Agrifood Policymaking', 'The Women''s Empowerment in Agrifood Governance (WEAGov) is a diagnostic tool developed by IFPRI to help evaluate the extent to which women are empowered across the policy cycle related to agrifood systems. WEAGov provides a way for national policymakers, civil society organizations, and researchers to identify gaps and opportunities to raise women’s voice and agency at different stages of the policy cycle, and provides a basis for monitoring progress in women’s empowerment in agrifood systems governance over time.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4901?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8663, 'A policy support toolkit for informing Agriculture Diversification in Eastern and Southern Africa', 'This activity provides support for policy design, implementation and appraisal in the area of agriculture diversification in the Eastern and Southern African region with a specific focus on the Ukama Ustawi focus countries. The toolkit will have two components for a start namely; a) an up-to-date database of indicators relevant to diversification, and b) a policy oriented report. The policy support tool, thus, seeks to avail timely and comprehensive information on the key indicators of diversification, its benefits and challenges. These indicators will include inventories of key value chains in the region and for each country, highlighting the current and past levels of productivity, shares of intra-and extra-regional trade and opportunities enhancing, and challenges limiting sustainable diversification and value chain development. The  toolkit also tracks and highlights the status of socio-economic characteristics of the population in the Eastern and Southern Africa, and their interface with agriculture outcomes. The data generated will also be availed in interactive maps to permit quick analysis and presentation of information  for decision making. The report will present key recommendations for policy makers and practitioners to ensure regional and country resilience, and food security  for all, through sustainable agriculture diversification.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8663?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(5321, 'Guidance on social protection design for implementers and donors to build synergies between climate adaptation, poverty reduction, and gender equality', 'This innovation provides concrete actionable guidance, with examples, on promising evidence-informed approaches to design social protection in a way that achieves synergistic benefits across climate adaptation, poverty reduction, and gender equality. The innovation brings together evidence reviews, programmatic document reviews, case studies, and stakeholder consultation to develop this guidance, for implementers and donors across each of these three objectives to make effective use of limited financing.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5321?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(3074, 'Methodology for socially inclusive scaling of climate technologies', 'This output converts basic research on social dimensions of climate technologies into practical tools and methodologies to improve social inclusion in scaling climate interventions. Gender is a key social dimension, but the final tool will also address other factors such as age, wealth and education.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3074?phase=3', 3, NULL),
(4818, 'Toolkit for enhancing women''s empowerment in anticipatory action programming before crises', 'This toolkit provides a framework for analyzing the gender dimensions of anticipatory action programming by considering the extent to which women are reached by, benefit from, and are empowered by policies and programming intended to build individuals'' resilience in advance of shocks and crises.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4818?phase=4', 2, NULL),
(7975, 'Ukama Ustawi''s Learning Alliance: An innovative approach for sustainable knowledge management, access, and dissemination between the CGIAR, National Agricultural Research Systems (NARS) and farmers in East and Southern Africa', 'Ukama Ustawi''s Learning Alliance stands as an innovative response, addressing persistent knowledge management, access, and dissemination challenges in East and Southern Africa''s agricultural landscape. This transformative innovation serves as a model for implementing the CGIAR-CAADP XP4 Knowledge Management Framework by systematically bridging the gap between CGIAR, National Research Systems, and farmers. Through actively sharing knowledge from UU to regional and national platforms, the Learning Alliance makes research outputs easily accessible to a diverse audience, including farmers, extension officers, researchers, and policymakers.
Operating through a multifaceted approach, the Alliance packages Ukama Ustawi’s knowledge outputs into user-friendly formats, strengthening farmer-led networks with webinars and field trips and leveraging digital technologies for real-time access This forward-thinking approach positions the Learning Alliance as a transformative force, ensuring impactful practices reach those who need them most. Supporting technology dissemination, co-designing mechanisms that capture regional priorities, contributing to institutional frameworks, strengthening capacities, and aligning with CGIAR-CAADPXP4 framework, the Alliance plays a catalyst role for resilient and sustainable agricultural development across East and Southern Africa.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7975?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7570, 'Virtual Reality Field Trips to showcase field work across the East and Southern Africa region', 'Virtual Field Trips (VFTs) integrated with Virtual Reality (VR) technology enable users to visit a site without physically travelling there. Real-world environments are captured and can be experienced by various stakeholders such as researchers, partners and donors. VFTs feature additional resources such as interviews, links to publications, demonstration videos and data in an immersive and interactive format with a 360 degree field of view. VFTs are accessible on smartphones/tablets, desktop/laptops and VR headsets.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7570?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4833, 'Water security strengthening through disaster risk management in displacement-affected and host communities', 'Ethiopia hosts over 916,000 refugees and asylum seekers , in addition to over four million IDPs . In many situations, this increases pressure on natural resources due to additional demands for food, water, and other natural resources. IWMI will use its expertise and knowledge assets to address water security challenges of host communities and develop context-specific solutions. The research on the vulnerabilities in this context has informed a package of nature-based solutions options and interventions together with potential areas for implementation. This is coupled with guidance for governance and actions prior to the implementation of identified nature-based solutions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4833?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1031, 'Climate-credit risk scoring system for farming systems to guide adaptation finance', 'A novel data-driven, climate-credit risk scoring tool has been developed for dairy farmers, integrating a climate module into the traditional credit assessment. This tool empowers lenders to assess climate- related risks accurately, making more informed, precise, and fair lending decisions. Climate-credit risk scoring allows farmers to secure loans and establish credit histories. It also helps in identifying climate risk drivers, and adaptation pathways for risk-prone farming systems, promoting financial inclusion for vulnerable communities. The tool combines data and science and has been developed based on the inputs of diverse livestock, crop and climate scientists, enabling a holistic and comprehensive tool development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1031?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(5917, 'Model for establishing and strengthening women and men farmer groups in Northwest Vietnam for increased linkages to input and output markets and more profitable livestock-keeping (cattle and pig) enterprises', 'Farmer groups are core for improved farmers'' access to input and output markets, as well as being a forum for capacity development and co-create of solutions to issues pertaining to pig and cattle keeping. However, the existing farmer groups engaged in cattle and pig production in the Mai Son district are not functioning well (regarding economic and financial sustainability, service offers, market connection, and trading capacity). To maximize the roles of farmer groups in strengthening the pig and cattle value chains, we plan to (i) develop and implement strategic plans for upgrading the functioning, social and gender inclusion, and capacities of the existing farmer groups and (ii) use that lesson to support the establishment and efficient operation of new farmer groups.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5917?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(9373, 'Small-scale feed processing and marketing by youth and women groups in Ethiopia', 'Feed resources are scarce and expensive in the Initiative intervention area of Bonga and Doyogena in Ethiopia. Two youth and women groups in these regions have been supported through research and development activities in the last number of years to improve their small ruminant production. The lack of nutritious and inexpensive suitable feed resources has now solved through the introduction of a complete feed processing unit composed of a grinder, conveyor belt and feed mixer. Each farmer group has received a feed processing unit which is produced by a local manufacturer. International Center for Agricultural Research in the Dry Areas and its partners have developed suitable feed ratios taking into account the availability of local ingredients. The co-designed business plans show the potential of this local feed production. This innovation will improve access to inexpensive quality feed and generate income for groups through feed commercialization and feed grinding services.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9373?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8741, 'A methodological approach to the assessment of feminization of agricultural labour through demographic and health survey data', 'Feminisation of agriculture’ is generally utilized to indicate an expansion of women’s engagement in agricultural production, as labourers or decision-makers. Feminization of agriculture is often reported as a global trend. While literature on feminization of agriculture (FoA) has seen a steady rise in the last decade, there is little consensus on defining feminization of agriculture and consistent approaches that provide comparative quantitative data on FoA are lacking. This innovation is a methodological approach to assessing the extent of FoA, deployed on DHS data from South and South-East Asia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8741?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(8734, 'Social behaviour change communication (SBCC) approach, through social media and other approaches, for gender-transformative change including more equitable engagement in, and benefit from, agribusiness for women and youth in Tanzania', 'Gender norms in Tanzania discourage women from engaging in business, including livestock business, particularly when the business becomes lucrative. To address these discriminating norms, we team up with an award winning social media company for behavioural change (Shujaaz) and implemented various campaigns, targeting the youth in particular, through social media and a bundle of other communication approaches. The innovation has now been expanded to Vietnam which faces similar challenges. In Vietnam we are working with the Vietnam Women Academy to implement the approach for the gender transformative work through other communication approaches e.g WhatApp, Poems, Competitions on the best key messages, Women and Farmers unions, farmer WhatsApp groups.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8734?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2714, 'Farmer participatory validation and adaptation trials for selected biopesticides against the diamondback moth on cabbage', 'The diamondback moth, Plutella xylostella (Lepidoptera: Plutellidae) is the major insect pest attacking cabbage crops in West Africa. As commonly practiced control measures, farmers very often resort to spray chemical insecticides at high dose and application frequency. Over the years, this indiscriminate use of chemical pesticides has led to the resurgence of pesticide resistance in the pest, hence demanding more frequent applications with all the unintended harmful effects of human and environmental health. In the attempt to break the vicious circle of pesticide resistance, bio-pesticides such as the entomopathogenic fungus Beauveria bassiana have been identified as very efficient alternative control options in managing the pest and hence are the target of our intervention.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2714?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(2692, 'Inoculative releases of the predatory mite Phytoseiulus longipes against the tomato mite Tetranychus evansi in Ghana', 'The tomato spider mite Tetranychus evansi (Acari: Tetranychidae) is one of the key pests causing economic losses to tomato crops and other nightshades such as African eggplant leaf. Farmers resort to often inappropriate application of chemical pesticides, with important unintended effects of human and environmental health. Our innovation provides them with a non-chemical pest control alternative which is safe and sustainable, based on the introduction and releases of the specific predatory mite Phytoseiulus longipes (Acari: Phytoseiidae).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2692?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4628, 'Buffalo fertility management package (including ear sensor, vaginal cytology, one-shot hormone treatment, Ultra Sound pregnancy diagnosis detector) for use by field veterinarians and technicians to improve buffalo reproduction in Nepal.', 'Buffaloes are seasonal breeders and to address low conception rates fertility camps are organized where a set of integrated interventions consisting of clinical examination and hormonal therapy, fecal examination and anthelmintic control, body condition score estimation and mineral supplementation, heat detection using Foldscope and use of conception enhancer before artificial insemination or natural service are provided.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4628?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(8646, 'Penside rapid diagnostics for African swine fever for use by veterinarians and veterinary technicans in Low and Middle Income Countries.', 'Diagnostic of African swine fever(ASF) includes from clinical diagnostics to identification of the agent to serological tests, according to the World Organisation of Animal Health. Clinical diagnostics is often challenging since ASF shares common clinical signs with other pig diseases in its acute form; and for the chronic forms subtle clinical signs can be overlook. Laboratory diagnostics are more accurate, including the identification of the viral particle or the presence of antibodies against the virus. However, all these laboratory test pose the same problem, they are difficult to implement in the field where infrastructures and human expertise is limited. A new generation of rapid lateral flow assays for the ASF diagnostic have been developed by various companies for both viral particle detection and anti-ASF Virus antibodies detection. We evaluated different LFA with interest for the African market. A report with the findings and a proposed way forward will be provided.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8646?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(4658, 'School-based model poultry farms for school-feeding programs to increase consumption of animal source foods (eggs) in primary schools.', 'Malnutrition is one the important cause of morbibity and mortality in the world and highest malnutrion levels are still found in Africa. School-age children are at high risk of nutrition deficiency. Here we develop and test a scalable model for integrating poultry product consumption (eggs) in school feeding programs in Ethiopia, Kenya, and Tanzania. By feeding an egg/day/child through a multi-sectorial collaboration approach we will improve school-age children’s nutrition outcomes. The effect of one egg/child/day will be assessed.  The project will establish a poultry farm in each of the selected schools compounds that will hold about 1200 layers and part of the eggs produced from this farm will be supplied to the existing school feeding program, while the remaining will be sold to the local community to cover production costs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4658?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(4644, 'PigSmart digital extension platform for enhancing farmers knowledge in climate smart best practices in herd health, genetics, feeding and manure management.', 'PigSmart platform delivers digital extension messages to pig farmers. It covers an integrated content that is delivered to pig farmers based on the stage of growth of the pigs. The messages include best practices in feeding, herd health management, breeding and genetics, manure management and heat stress management. The process of integrating and adapting the content followed key steps: (i) curation of research outputs on feeds, health, genetics, and heat stress, (ii) drawing messages from the research outputs, (iii) validating messages from the researchers and government extension  to ensure integrity of content, (iv) integrating messages premised on the different stages of pig growth, and (v) developing audio skits from integrated content in Luganda. A brochure on the integrated content was developed and disseminated to pig farmers in addition to the audio skits. The messages can be used by extension agents to train farmers on best practices in pig production.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4644?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(4854, 'Government, Research and Development partner collaboration framework for increased investments to scale proven innovations and enhance the business environment for agri-preneuers in Tanzania', 'Private sector engagement in livestock value chains in Tanzania needs strengthening  and therefore, there is need for advocacy to increase investments. This innovation is to enhance collaboration between key government, research and development actors in Tanzania, including the International Livestock Research Institute. It targets private sector investments for scaling of proven Sustainable Animal Productivity (SAPLING) Initiative innovations involving women and youth. It aims to ensure a more productive, competitive and sustainable livestock sector in Tanzania. Working with relevant government agencies, the Tanzania Ministry of Livestock and Fisheries (MLF) will promote the delivery and use of innovation packages best suited for development of the livestock sector in Tanzania. It will strive to achieve impact through public-private partnerships (PPPs), networks, and complementary institutions across livestock research and development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4854?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(4626, 'Improved Brachiaria and Guinea grass cultivars, suited to multiple locations in Nepal, for improved agronomic performance, animal nutrition and dairy buffalo performance', 'Seven improved cultivars of Brachiaria and Guinea grasses along with local forages are being evaluated in multiple locations in Nepal involving farmers and other stakeholders, and best bet cultivars will be scaled for commercial fodder production. Evaluation of the forage cultivars is ongoing on station and performance of forage species/cultivars will be assessed by the farmers and other stakeholders visiting the experimental plots at different stages. The preferred cultivars will be identified for on-farm evaluation as outreach research activities. Use of the improved cultivars will lead to improved diets for dairy buffalo leading to improved productivity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4626?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(12383, 'Robust management practice (''ram effect'') for enhanced reproductive performance of sheep for farmers in dryland areas.', 'The innovation describes how sheep use a unique amalgam of behavioral, physiological and genetic mechanisms to preserve fertility under a looming scenario of warmer climate. Management of reproduction that relies on naturally based solutions is extremely important to counter the negative perception around hormone-based interventions. In Mediterranean latitudes and wider regions of non-tropical drylands, sheep do not normally ovulate during spring but exposure to a ram can induce oestrus and ovulation. This innovation promotes the use of the response of maiden Barbarine ewes in drylands to the ‘ram effect’ during springtime for improved fertility.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12383?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(12186, 'Integrated nutrition education package to smallholder chicken keepers for increased consumption of animal source food (ASF) and improved nutritional status in Ethiopia, Kenya and Tanzania', 'The integrated nutrition education package is a nutrition-sensitive intervention that aims to enhance the consumption of chicken meat, eggs and other nutrient-dense foods and improve the nutritional status of smallholder farmers, most notably under-five children, lactating mothers and pregnant women. This activity will be embedded in the existing on-farm chicken performance testing program and it will be provided to smallholder farmers using digital and paper-based nutrition education tools. The package will have different contents suitable for smallholder farmers. Baseline and end-line data will be collected to explore the impact of the interventions.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12186?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5280, 'Evidence based recommendations for policy makers and investors for a supportive regulatory framework for service providers including formalization of forage seed supplies in Tanzania.', 'The use of improved forage seed in Tanzania remains a major challenge for developing of a strong and steady animal feed resource base and access to quality forages backed by a forage sector  and supply chain that assures availability of quality seeds/planting materials. This innovation aims to strangthen the regulatory framework to promote steady supply of certified forage seeds in Tanzania. To do this we are exploring use of trade Block approach i.e., East Africa where Tanzania and Kenyas are members.Two Brachiaira hybrids  (Cobra & Cayman) data had been procured from Kenya Plant Health Inspectorate Service and presented to Tanzania Official Seed Certification Institute (TOSCI) entity in Tanzania and will only need one season of growth to register them in Tanzania. If the route works, it will be shorter than the conventional National Performance Trials. Data available about the two varieties in Tanzania has also been presented to TOSCI.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5280?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(12182, 'Mobile application for chicken breeding data management to ease data entry and flock managment by chicken breed improvement programs', 'This a real time data entry visualisation and data management App for production traits from chicken breeding programs. The App provides easy entry and generation of quick outputs required for decision making and further advanced data analysis.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/12182?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5848, 'Improved access to certified sires from the Community-Based Breeding Program by smallholder sheep and goat farmers in Ethiopia and Mali', 'This innovation describes the implementation of the sires’ certification in the framework of Community-Based Breeding Programs of small ruminants in Ethiopia. The sire certification system is described and areas that need to be strengthened are suggested. Currently, the existing certification scheme is based on the genetic worth (Estimated breeding value-EBV) of the sire and other technical requirements for certification including physical examination, semen assessment, mating ability and vaccination against prevalent infectious diseases. The innovation describes the institutional set up whereby the innovations is owned by the national system.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5848?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4645, 'Community-based young village livestock promoters to act as knowledge brokers and business agents for integrated delivery of technology, inputs and services to dairy buffalo producers in Nepal', 'Youth with basic qualification in livestock production are recruited by upstream (agro-dealers, technology providers, etc.) and downstream (milk aggregators, cooperatives) actors to train farmers and catalyze demand for technologies and services. Village livestock promoters play a critical linkage role between smallholder farmers and inputs/service providers and bundle advisory services, thus enhancing access to and uptake of appropriate technologies/inputs/services by smallholder farmers. Village livestock promoters apply entrepreneurial skills to earn sustainable income as they perform their agency roles', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4645?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(3302, 'Development of a non-invasive seed health testing protocols: standardization of Videometer multispectral Imaging for seed characterization', 'This research aimed to determine if there are distinct features between virus-contaminated and normal seeds that could be used as a proxy marker for detecting virus-contaminated seed lots. Experiments were performed using a seed-transmissible virus species, bean common mosaic virus (BCMV), in 12 cowpea genotypes. The harvested seeds were sorted using the Videometer that provides precise measurements of seed color, shape, and size, including (i) normal size and seed coat color, (ii) normal size with seed coat discoloration, (iii) small size with no discoloration, and (iv) small size with discoloration. According to the categorization of seed features, 68.3% of BCMV seed transmission occurred through normal seeds. In contrast, 17.5%, 3.2%, and 11.1% of seed transmission rates were attributed to normal-sized discolored seeds, small-sized seeds without discoloration, and small-sized discolored seeds, respectively. Work is ongoing to validate more data parameters generated using Videometer to differentiate contaminated seeds.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3302?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(4993, 'Improved chickens, animal services and markets for women farmers, delivered by women veterinarians in Tanzania', 'A package combining gender accommodative and transformative approaches to deliver improved chickens, animal services and markets to women farmers by women veterinarians. Gender accommodative approaches were adopted to enhance the business and chicken management skills of the women vets. Gender transformative approaches (in this case social media) were adopted to address gender norms that make women’s involvement in chicken business socially inappropriate. Restrictive gender norms are considered the root cause of gender-based disadvantage; unlike gender accommodative approaches, gender transformative approaches aim to change such restrictive norms for lasting social change.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4993?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1106, 'Farmer-to-Farmer Scaling Networks for scaling climate adaptation technologies', 'The Farmer-to-Farmer Scaling Networks is a farmer-led innovation connecting farmers to each other for information exchange and capacity building, and ultimately the scaling of climate adaptation technologies and innovations that have been developed by the farmers themselves. The pioneers of adaptation are farmers who have developed their adaptation practices with their own endogenous innovations beyond piloting stage. We support them in developing a farm-level adapted scaling approach complementary to existing institutions, involving pioneers themselves as the main actors in the scaling process. Together with the pioneers we study their practices, and then progressively include more farmers, farmer groups, cooperatives in a knowledge exchange through participatory field days where pioneers share and discuss the findings of this data collection on their practices. Gradually, we develop a Farmer2Farmer knowledge network that connects pioneers and their learning groups with research, extension, development organisations, and private sector actors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1106?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(8777, 'ILCYM Online: A web platform for insect life-table modeling', 'Insect Life Cycle Modeling software (ILCYM – version 4.0) introduces a new execution platform accessible from any internet-connected mobile device with a web browser. The software now operates from a server, allowing it to function for multiple users simultaneously, eliminating the need for installation on a desktop computer or laptop. This update not only enhances accessibility but also ensures faster and more flexible processing to predict climate change scenarios on insect life cycle and phenology models.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8777?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3241, 'Measuring demand for climate-resilient maize varieties in Nigeria', 'A willingness to pay (WTP) auction experiment was conducted to measure farmers demand for climate-resilient improved maize varieties in Nigeria. The maize seed auction experiments were conducted with about 1,100 maize farmers across two States of Nigeria. To examine how gender roles in seed purchasing and planting decision may shape demand for improved maize varieties, the WTP experiments were conducted with both male and female farmers. Additional seed and fertilizer bunding and deferred payment treatments were also included to investigate how demand for high-quality seed can be enhanced through a bundle of product/services that agrodealers may use to improve their value proposition to farmers. Results from this study are expected to better inform policymakers about inclusive seed marketing strategies to accelerate varietal turnover and deepen demand for quality seeds in Nigeria.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3241?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(1020, 'Understanding climate change impacts and adaptation practices for cocoa management in West Africa', 'Empowering Smallholder Producers for Perennials in West Africa for Climate Resilience. This innovation integrates three tools—Aclimatar, Cacao Diversity, and Croppie—to address challenges faced by smallholder farmers in Ghana and Côte d''Ivoire.

Aclimatar and Cacao Diversity provide site-specific climate risk assessments and tailored adaptation strategies. Through an intuitive map interface, users access critical climate data, crop-specific impacts, and prioritized measures to promote resilience. The Cacao Diversity Tool supports cacao farm sustainability by predicting climate change impacts, recommending propagation materials, and assessing cadmium levels in soils and cacao beans.

Croppie is an AI-assisted yield estimation tool that simplifies certification by replacing costly, time-consuming manual methods. It reduces costs by 50% and prevents uncertified produce leakage during onboarding without harvest records, enhancing efficiency and inclusion of smallholders.

Together, these tools equip farmers with actionable insights, strengthen local projects, and foster sustainable practices, ensuring resilience and climate adaptation in smallholder communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1020?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(7874, 'Obsrv.AI: An Artificial Intelligence-based chatbot for location and crop-specific agricultural extension organizations in India', 'The Observ.AI chatbot (https://agri.obsrv.ai), a collaborative pilot initiative with ILRI and Sanketika, is designed specifically for farmers. This project highlights how a Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI) approach can generate tailored agro-advisories, taking into account location and crop specifics based on scientific knowledge. In addition to the chatbot, a dashboard has been developed for agricultural extension organizations. This dashboard enhances the decision-making capabilities and farmer assistance of agricultural extension services by providing sophisticated, data-driven, location-specific climatic information.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7874?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(8658, 'Improved agri-input and production line methods for diversified production and enterprises', 'Directly sourcing from smallholder farmers can significantly improve their livelihoods and productivity. Input credit systems have also been proven to effectively enhance productivity and livelihoods of smallholders, reduce rural poverty, and promote sustainable agricultural practices. These initiatives bridge seed supply gaps, and establish risk management strategies, collaboration with seed enterprises, and in-house seed production units are some options that can be used. To support this, it''s essential to establish training guidelines for agronomic and post-harvest skills, fertilizer recommendations, and seed quality support for producer cooperatives, especially for women, along with financial literacy and agribusiness skills training. Financing is also crucial to enhance the commercialization of high-demand diversified produce and products for farmers, and training and working capital to grow and establish new crops are essential. To overcome supply chain constraints and increase efficiency, digital registration and payment processes for producers are being explored.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8658?phase=3', 7, NULL),
(784, 'Mbili Mbili is an innovative cropping system to ensure crop diversification and resilience in smallholder farming systems', 'Mbili Mbili intercropping system has been promoted as a crop diversification and intensification approach. Mbili-Mbili exploits plant spatial configurations to increase light penetration to the legumes, otherwise often shaded by cereals. The main aim is to increase legume productivity, while maintaining the same productivity of the cereal and taking advantage and complementarity of the different growth habits and durations of the combined crops. Mbili Mbili enables a farmer to grow both legumes and cereals on the same land and thus harvest at different times. In event of a loss, one crop serves as an insurance. This makes the system climate smart. Mbili Mbili system has been reported to have a higher returns (upto 37% more) compared to the standard farmer intercropping practices. The technology has been tested, and widely promoted in maize growing areas in Kenya and other countries in east and southern Africa.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/784?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(33, 'Maize breeding networks involving 30+ partners in Latin America use cutting-edge tools to accelerate genetic gains to develop and deploy multi-stress resilient, nutritious hybrids/varieties, addressing smallholders and local market requirements', 'In collaboration with partners of the International Maize Improvement Consortium for Latin America, encompassing 30 public and private sector organizations, maize breeding efforts targeted at Latin America and the Caribbean aim to develop high-yielding, multi-stress tolerant, and easy-to-produce hybrids and open-pollinated varieties for regions, addressing the requirements of smallholder farmers and consumers. The resulting varieties carry multiple stress tolerance, including drought tolerance and tar spot complex resistance, and enhanced nutritional quality (Provitamin A).  Cutting-edge tools and technologies, including doubled haploids and genomics-assisted breeding enable a breeding cycle time of 4-5 years. Between 2022-2024, partners in 10 countries released 57 varieties, 23 of them in 2024. All were selected for drought tolerance and resistance to locally important diseases in addition to being more productive.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/33?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4631, 'Forage seed business models through policy influence and forage registration to enhance adoption of elite forages by forage seed businesses and livestock producers.', 'Quality forages increase livestock productivity while taking care of the environment. Elite forages from selection and breeding require registration in countries to allow private sector uptake for business while improving access to the seeds by livestock producers. Fast tracking forage registration with the regulation entities in the respective countries including sharing policy briefs that highlight areas that require to be addressed is needed in order to support forage seed businesses and use. In addition, we must facilitate use through raising awareness with the livestock producers. We plan to scale across Sustainable Animal Productivity (SAPLING) and other countries registration and highlighting policy issues, where appropriate.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4631?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(1268, 'Improved soybean digitally mediated agronomic advisory to improve yields, soil health and incomes in the Chinyanja Triangle(Malawi/Mozambique/Zambia)', 'The innovation, a digital Agronomy Advisory tool in its prototype phase, delivers site-specific guidance on soybean farming to smallholder farmers. It advises on the best planting times, varieties, fertilization strategies, and crop establishment methods. Integrated with Solidaridad''s digital advisories, this tool targets yield optimization, increased profitability, and sustainable farming in the Chinyanja Triangle (Malawi, Mozambique, Zambia).

Smallholder soybean farmers are the main users, with Solidaridad overseeing the tool''s implementation. The content, developed by agronomists and agricultural experts, is research-based and customized for the region''s specific needs. This digital solution aims to overcome soybean production hurdles, offering farmers practical, technology-driven support to enhance their agricultural productivity.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1268?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(798, 'Addressing drought and invasive pests and diseases through multiple stress tolerant soybean varieties', 'Through breeding demand driven farmer preferred climate smart varieties as well as making early generation seed (EGS) accessible, we (i) support NARS, SME seed companies, and community based out-grower partners to accelerate variety release and upscaling of seed production, (ii) develop and test innovative, evidence-based tools and strategies to improve women and youth entrepreneurs’ access to climate smart seed, accelerate varietal turnover; and expand seed marketing to women, youth and men farmers who are not reached by current seed value chains.; (iii) Promote the use of good agronomic practices (e.g. minimum tillage, rotations, mulching, etc) to enhance crop productivity; and (iv) improve access to technical information through training of extension staff and making seed production protocols available to users (farmers, extension and seed companies).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/798?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(7421, 'Sustainable Solar Irrigation navigating the Water-Energy-Food-Environmental (WEFE) Nexus', 'Solar-powered irrigation is rapidly increasing in Africa and South Asia. This innovation addresses the urgent need for sustainable solar irrigation recognizing its intricate connections within the Water-Energy-Food-Environment (WEFE) nexus. It brings together a set of innovative solutions and approaches to the design, operation and implementation of solar irrigation that maximizes positive and minimizes negative outcomes. Components include (i) tools for optimal water pumping to prevent groundwater depletion, (ii) new approaches for evidence-based decision-making for assessing the financial feasibility of solar irrigation, (ii) capacity building programs for enhancing the understanding of the WEFE environment in which solar irrigation takes place, (iv) water-efficient irrigation practices (v) gender-responsive finance and business models; (vi) and evidence-based policy guidance promoting sustainable solar irrigation for multiple outcomes, including job creation, gender equality, climate action and improved nutrition.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7421?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(791, 'Development and Scaling of Bean-Based Composite Flour Formulations for Enhanced Community Nutrition and Small-Scale Industries: Nutrition, Gender and Climate-smart Technologies to Enhance Income Generation and Social Impact', 'This innovation converts beans and other nutrient-dense foods into composite flours, addressing major challenges in Africa''s food systems. Protein deficiencies affect 26% of African''s population, while micronutrient deficiencies like iron and zinc are widespread, with sub-Saharan Africa having the highest rates of anaemia (46%-71%) and vitamin A deficiency (48%) in children under-five. These deficiencies contribute to stunted growth, weakened immunity, and poverty. Traditional bean processing is also labour-intensive and inefficient, limiting the appeal of beans. This innovation improves nutrient absorption by reducing anti-nutritional factors like phytic acid and lectins through techniques such as soaking, malting, roasting and pressure cooking. Instant and regular bean composite flour products also offer extended shelf life, reducing food waste and improving food security. The technology supports small and medium enterprises, fosters economic growth, and strengthens supply chains while improving nutrition across families and communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/791?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9913, 'Community-designed One Health Units as a model for integrated human, animal and environment health service delivery to pastoralists in the Horn of Africa', 'The One Health Unit (OHU) serves as a collaboration and coordination platform for frontline service providers and community-based actors to deliver integrated services to improve the health of humans, animals and the environment. In mobile OHUs, frontline service providers move along pastoralist/livestock routes, according to a monthly schedule, changing sites every day and responding to the needs of the population in a certain site/location. In static OHUs, frontline service providers work side by side in a common facility (e.g., human/animal health post, water point or other village gathering site). The OHU is generally staffed by government employees from different sectors who are released from their regular duties to deliver integrated services through the OHU. The services are targeted towards pastoralist and agropastoralist communities, their livestock, and the rangelands which they access, with a particular focus on vulnerable and hard-to-reach communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9913?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(786, 'Mechanization starter pack for smallholder farmers in Southern Africa', 'Smallholder agricultural mechanization is the call of the day for Africa to improve land and labour productivity, reduce production cost and drudgery, and make agriculture attractive to youth. Successful mechanization in most of African countries relies on selection, validation, piloting, and scaling of appropriate inexpensive machinery solutions adapted to farm size and financial ability of smallholder farmers. Over the past decade, CIMMYT has tested, validated, and piloted small scale machinery and business models suitable for southern African farmers. Based on the experiences and successful piloting, a ‘mechanization starter pack’ has been developed and is recommended for scaling through a business model called the ‘Service Provider Model’. The ‘mechanization starter pack’ includes a two-wheel tractor (2WT), a 2WT operated ripper, a 2WT operated trailer, an engine operated multi-crop thresher, peanut sheller, peanut roaster, peanut butter machine and chopper grinder depending on the farming context of the country. As per the model, a service provider (SP) owns and maintains the machines and operate them on a fee-for-service basis throughout the year benefiting many farmers. The service fee is usually much cheaper for end users (farmers) compared to the cost of traditional practices and creates a win-win situation that makes the business viable for both SP and farmers. The machines chosen for the starter pack allows the SP to generate income throughout the year by providing ploughing, ripping, and threshing/shelling (depending on time of the year), and transportation (year-round) services. Currently there are more than 300 SPs are operating their businesses in Zimbabwe, Malawi and Zambia benefiting thousands of farmers.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/786?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(5373, 'Scaling with households and groups: socially inclusive approaches supporting equitable scaling of locally led adaptation in Kenya''s dairy sector', 'Climate adaptation processes can be uneven and reinforce inequitable power relations and outcomes. In dairy households of Nandi and Bomet counties in Kenya, despite women’s significant labor contributions, gender norms limit women’s access to resources and decision-making agency. Rather than top-down, one-size-fits-all solutions, adaptation must prioritize flexible, locally led approaches tailored to diverse capacities of households and groups with differential access to resources and opportunities. Our innovation combines two inclusive approaches, scaling with households and champions in groups to enhance women''s capacities to learn, lead and scale adaptation. The first approach works with households as part of the Pioneer Positive Deviance (P-PD) approach to scale farmer-led solutions developed by “adaptation pioneer households” (APHs). The second approach works with local champions and grassroots women-led groups to support women''s peer learning in collectives and strengthen their leadership capacities. Synergies between these two piloted approaches support equitable scaling of locally led adaptation.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5373?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(2104, 'SPaRC model: Grid-connecting solar pumps and buy-back of surplus solar electricity to incentivize efficient energy and water use in Southern Asia', 'The SPaRC model deploys a ''nexus approach'' to promoting solar irrigation in groundwater-stressed regions and offers several co-benefits including an additional, counter-climatic income source for farmers, reduction in perverse farm power subsidies, incentive for efficient energy and groundwater use, and reduced GHG emissions from irrigation. In SPaRC, farmers are given financial and institutional assistance to put up solar irrigation pumps that are connected to the grid, with a guaranteed buy-back of any surplus solar electricity. This decentralized grid-connected solar pump approach enables farmers to become "prosumers" of solar energy, increasing their income and resilience by adding solar energy as a "crop." It replaces the need of diesel or electricity in irrigation, helping to reduce GHG emissions. And finally, it also encourages farmers not to use all of their energy for groundwater pumping. This is particularly relevant in groundwater-depleted regions of South Asia, where free or highly subsidized electricity has led to excessive pumping of groundwater. This model creates an opportunity cost of using energy in irrigation, if not sold to the grid. Therefore, with an attractive buyback rate, policymakers can incentivize more efficient use of water and energy.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2104?phase=1', 6, NULL),
(2117, 'Decision Support Tool for Right Sizing of Solar-powered Irrigation Pumps in Africa and South Asia', 'Improvements in technology, reduction in costs and significant policy support has driven expansion of solar irrigation in Asia and Africa. In India - which is the early leader in solar irrigation adoption, the number of solar pumps is estimated to have grown from a handful in 2010 to more than 350,000 by 2020. The Government of India has announced an ambitious campaign to support the deployment of more than 3.5 million solar pumps over the coming years. Given the significant public and private investments in solar pumps, it is critical that these pumps be ''right-sized'' - over-sizing can lead to wasteful expenditure and poor utilization while under-sizing can seriously limit the pumps'' benefits. With support from GIZ, IWMI partnered with ICAR and BISA to develop the Beta version of the SIP Sizing Tool for MNRE, Government of India. The tool estimates irrigation and pumping requirement, analyzes future scenarios and recommends the right size for installation of a solar pump. 
In 2023, IWMI and GIZ expanded their collaboration in promoting this innovation in Nepal. An Innovation Package and Scaling Readiness (IPSR) workshops was conducted in Kathmandu to identify challenges towards solar irrigation in Nepal and co-create solutions for promoting the Decision Support Tool for Right Sizing of Solar-powered Irrigation Pumps. In 2024, the project team has developed a Beta version of an excel based ‘Solar Water Pump Planning Tool for Nepal’.  The tool is officially launched for use by the AEPC and Provincial governments in Nepal. The simple and user-friendly tool will help local government and other stakeholders to quickly estimate the system costs for budget planning. The team will continue its work with further engagement for scaling the tool in India, Nepal and elsewhere.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/2117?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(793, 'Methodology to scale research innovations through the help of private companies in the extended agricultural sector (Small and medium-sized enterprises, startups, associations, corporates) in developing markets.', 'The science-driven accelerator program for climate-smart agribusinesses focuses on scaling climate-smart agricultural innovations and strengthening the commercial capacity of private sector entities in the agricultural ecosystem. Designed with insights from private sector accelerators, the program supports companies with the potential to achieve both impact and commercial success. Operating in Zambia, it aims to expand the reach of climate-smart innovations and enhance the business capabilities of participating agribusinesses.
 
The program concludes with a demo day, connecting agribusinesses to public, concessional, and private investors, as well as potential market partners, facilitating access to finance and market linkages. After two accelerator cohorts and a large-scale bootcamp, the importance of expanding the scientific network across underrepresented countries like Zambia, Zimbabwe, Uganda, and Malawi became clear. Additionally, fostering ongoing networking among founders with complementary value propositions has become a priority, enabling sustained collaboration and shared growth across programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/793?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(42, 'Rice breeding networks in Southeast Asia accelerate genetic gains for climate resilient varieties suitable for direct seeding, reducing greenhouse gas emissions and helping to address labor shortages', 'Rice production in Southeast Asia comprises almost 30% of the World’s rice harvest and produces significant greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions. Floods and droughts affect rice production adversely. Urbanization often means less labor for agriculture and changing demands on diets. Rice breeding seeks to reduce GHG emissions through resilient varieties suitable for direct seeding. These varieties help save water and reduce labor requirements due to their better suitability to mechanized crop establishment. Gendered trait preferences are considered in defining target product profiles. Multi-sectoral consortia for public and private sector partners - the Hybrid Rice Development Consortium (HRDC) and the Direct Seeded Rice Consortium (DSRC) - play important roles in pursuing breeding priorities. Current cycle time is 36 months. Genomic selection is executed in all pipelines. Introgression of high value traits is agressively being pursued.  Between 2022-2024, partners in Myanmar and the Philippines registered 17 varieties, all with climate resilience traits.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/42?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(3522, 'PROBFLO v2: A user-friendly environmental flow assessment tool for evaluating socio-ecological consequences of basin-level water management scenarios for transboundary natural resource management authorities', 'The original PROBFLO was developed as a holistic environmental flow (e-flow) assessment tool. PROBFLO evaluates the socio-ecological consequences of various water resource scenarios and generates e-flow requirements on a regional spatial scale. It follows the ecological risk assessment exposure and effects approach, with multiple stressors, habitats, and ranked ecological impact relationships. Due to the complexity, PROBFLO is currently only used by skilled scientists. As a response to the demand partner in the Limpopo River basin, we are developing a new version of PROBFLO in collaboration with the World Modeler program. The new version will be equipped with a user-friendly interface and streamlined workflow to make the tool more accessible to stakeholders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3522?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(6718, 'GROWSMART: Farmer advisories to optimize soybean yields in the Chinyanja Triangle for an El Niño year', 'GROWSMART is a package of data science-driven advisories, developed using crop models and machine learning techniques based on historical weather and soybean yield data from short, medium and long duration varieties in the Chinyanja Triangle (consisting of regions of Zambia, Malawi, and Mozambique). Actionable farmer management options were developed in English and Chinyanja based on this analysis and shared with national governmental and non-governmental partners via GROWSMART.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6718?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(5040, 'Toolkit for conceptualizing and measuring effective voice and agency of women in climate-related governance at the individual and group levels', 'This CGIAR innovation provides a comprehensive framework and set of tools for conceptualizing and measuring women''s empowerment in governance, focusing on individual and collective agency beyond the household. Grounded in Kabeer’s empowerment framework, it highlights the interplay of resources, agency, and achievements at both individual and group levels. The toolkit operationalizes key concepts such as collective efficacy, action, and achievements, integrating tangible and intangible resources like social capital. Case studies from India, Guatemala, Uganda, and Nigeria illustrate its application, providing nuanced insights into how group dynamics, organizational structures, and decision-making processes shape empowerment outcomes. The innovation bridges gaps in understanding collective agency, offering participatory and mixed-method tools for analyzing women''s roles in climate-related governance and other domains. By advancing measurement and application, this toolkit empowers practitioners, researchers, and policymakers to foster gender-equitable governance systems that enhance women''s voice, agency, and community impact.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5040?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(4273, 'Validation trials for a hand-held decision support system for late blight management in Kenya', 'This report presents the findings from the validation of a Decision Support Tool (DST) developed to optimize late blight management in potato farming. The DST, available as physical discs or an interactive mobile application, integrates host resistance, environmental conditions, and management practices to provide fungicide spray recommendations. Color-coded discs (red, yellow, and green) display spray guidelines that adjust based on potato variety and weather conditions, as informed by research or expert input. The objective of this study was to evaluate the technical efficiency of the tool, assess its efficacy in reducing chemical inputs, and identify potential areas for improvement. The evaluation was conducted on a sample of 61 farms from Kenya''s mid- and high-altitude agroecologies, which represent the primary potato-growing regions in the country.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4273?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(7543, 'Exclosures for landscape restoration in Ethiopia contribute to boost agricultural productivity', 'The overall area covered by exclosures is currently increasing by 2%/year and could reach 5-7 million ha by the early 2030s. Similar rehabilitation of degraded rangelands has been fostered by establishing exclosures in different parts of Africa and Asia. This trend in exclosure expansion is attributable to their many benefits: restoring degraded landscapes, increasing carbon sequestration, and improving other ecosystem services. This can provide opportunities for livelihood diversification, and thus enhancement, decrease soil erosion and seed loss in farmlands located downslope of exclosures, thereby helping boost agricultural productivity over the medium to long term.The major agroecological transition fostered by setting up exclosures in degraded ecosystems is the shift from a free grazing system (i.e. natural) to a cut-and-carry system (i.e. knowledge/labour intensive agroecological system). The initiatives are built on previous IWMI activities. In the project areas, we developed a participatory freshwater conservation plan where exclosure is an important intervention. We will pilot and train partners, and show business models.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7543?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(1593, 'High-frequency diet quality data collection and mapping', 'The innovation is end-to-end integrated system that combines citizen engagement for data curation with modern agile analytics to provide real-time and actionable insights about dietary patterns and food system dynamics. The system is made up of three components: citizen-powered high-frequency data collection, user-centered dashboard analytics, and demand-driven reflexive modeling of intervention options. The innovation stands out by leveraging crowdsourced data collection methods that are both cost-efficient and easily deployable, enabling more frequent and accurate insights compared to traditional in-person methods on diets, markets, and transport infrastructure. It aims to serve as scalable platform to engage, inform, and empower individuals and decision-makers; support data-driven and informed decision-making; and provide robust data to inform policies and interventions, direct capital investments to improve food system resilience, and increase preparedness/planning for potential and direct risk. It is already under development with diet quality, food flows, and transport network being mapped in Rwanda and Kenya.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1593?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(10492, 'A rapid and scalable rice blast diagnostic tool - Timely disease identification point-of-care  system (TIDIS) to support national institutions in monitoring and rapidly diagnosing rice crop diseases.', 'The synergy of pathogen diagnostics, epidemiology, and advanced digital disease detection systems holds immense potential for elevating continuous monitoring, rapid diagnosis, and real-time tracking of crop diseases in resource-constrained southern regions. AfricaRice and the John Innes Centre (JIC) collaborate on the Plant Health Initiative, developing TIDIS—a portable, scalable point-of-care management system for disease identification. TIDIS employs sequencing and analysis of blast-infected rice leaf samples, enabling virulence genomic profiling and polymorphic gene definition through bioinformatics and Wet-lab PCR pooling techniques. Currently, TIDIS implementation highlights the diversity of Ugandan isolates compared to those from Cote d’Ivoire, prompting further sample collection for result validation in hotspot areas. Ongoing analysis of newly sequenced datasets aims to enhance tree informativeness and pooling efficiency, marking significant progress in combating crop diseases.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10492?phase=3', 2, NULL),
(8170, 'Analysis Tool for Economics of Land Degradation (ATELD) to quantify and monetize ecosystem services lost due to inaction, gained/prevented due to action, and returns on investments to combat land degradation', 'The Analysis tool for Economics of Land Degradation (ATELD) is an innovation that involves three separate but complementary modules each with specific function. The first module enables researchers and experts to estimate the quantities and monetary values of ecosystem services (ESS) lost due to inaction to combat land degradation (LD). If a package of suitable policy, institutional, and technological innovations for combating LD have been identified, the second module enables to estimate the benefits (in terms of the quantities and monetary values of ESS that can be prevented from being lost) due to the interventions. The third module estimates the cost of implementing the package of innovations,  total discounted benefits of action over the entire planning period, and ultimately provides estimates of the returns on investment that are provided in the form of benefit:cost ratios – i.e., the monetary values of the returns on every dollar invested in combating LD.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8170?phase=4', 5, NULL),
(10129, 'Facilitating Scale-Up of Orange-Fleshed Sweet Potato (OFSP) Flour and Puree Processing Through Stakeholder Partnerships', 'Production of Orange-Fleshed Sweet Potato (OFSP) Flour  involving both cooperatives and individual processors. This involves incentivizing more processors to invest in OFSP processing infrastructure, including offering tax incentives, to stimulate increased participation in flour processing. The primary goal is to streamline coordination among key stakeholders, with the Root and Tuber Crops Development Trust (RTCDT) taking the lead in orchestrating the diverse entities involved in processing activities. The main targets of this effort are scaling partners, particularly processors engaged in OFSP product processing in Malawi .', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/10129?phase=3', 5, NULL),
(7453, 'Benefits and costs of gully rehabilitation measures from the perspective of local communities in southern Ethiopia', 'The work is done to investigate the effectiveness of low-cost gully rehabilitation measures to reduce the upward retreat of gully heads and soil loss, and explore how farmers and communities view gully interventions and whether demonstrating farm field experiments in context changes their knowledge and perceived capacity to act.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7453?phase=3', 8, NULL),
(7387, 'The Women''s Empowerment in Energy Index (WEEI) supports governments and other next users to reduce inequities in rural energy access', 'The Women''s Empowerment in Energy Index (WEEI) was developed to help governments, investors and practitioners understand sources of and dynamics in gendered disempowerment linked to energy sources and technologies in rural areas. It identifies sources of gendered disempowerment in rural energy access and use and helps identify interventions to close the gendered gap in rural energy access and use. It has been tested in an uncontrolled environment in Nepal and India and partially (i.e. with women only) in Pakistan.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/7387?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(416, 'Improved community-led water governance approach through sluice gate irrigation system for rice farmers in coastal zones of Bangladesh', 'Engaging with communities to employ more pragmatic land-use planning and scheduling of sluice gate operations has shown the potential to intensify and diversify cropping systems, and increasing production by 2-3 fold. This was achieved using improved rice varieties and non-rice crops, enabled by strategic management of the sluice gates to drain the lands as needed during the wet season and to facilitate timely establishment of non-rice crops after rice harvest. 

The sluice gate operation is being introduced and tested in multiple sites to facilitate adoption of improved cropping system (year-round), and terminal drainage to facilitate mechanized paddy harvest and early establishment of rabi crops in the coastal polder zone. Several trainings are also being provided to ensure synchronized operation of sluice gates of intended users such as water management groups and rice farmers for improved productivity and intensified production systems in the polders.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/416?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(415, 'Diversified and intensified rice-based cropping systems in the southern coastal zone of Bangladesh', 'Diversifying and intensifying the rice-based cropping systems in the southern coastal zone of Bangladesh is a priority to meet the food, feed, and fiber demand of an increasing population and changing climate. RICE is working in the region to catalyze an enabling environment for the early establishment of dry season crops by introducing rice varieties with a shorter growth duration than traditional rice in wet season, and empowering water management organizations for improving water governance.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/415?phase=1', 5, NULL),
(999, 'Epidemiology and Control of Peste des Petits Ruminants Study Design and Toolbox: A suite of tools for stakeholders toward understanding epidemiology and socio-economic impact of peste des petits ruminants (PPR)', 'To fully understand the epidemiology and socio-economic impact of Peste des Petits Ruminants (PPR) to overcome the challenges faced by stakeholders in the control of the disease, an interdisciplinary approach that brings together social and biosciences should be followed. A collaborative effort was made to harmonize data collection across countries and partners. As a result, a study design and toolkit consisting of different methods and types of data collection tools was developed. Each tool addresses a specific set of data needs and when combined they will provide in-depth understanding of the PPR situation of the country under research, which will help to identify entry points for evidence-based control of PPR.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/999?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(661, 'Foresight and Rapid Response Modeling System (FARRMS) is an on-demand modeling tool to analyze impact of global crises to advise donor government in responses.', 'Governments and donors often request modeling analysis to help understand the immediate consequences of, for example, global crises or local shocks, such as droughts/floods, and inform the design of policy and investment responses. The Foresight and Rapid Response Modeling System (FARRMS) streamlines our global and national data and modeling systems so that we can more rapidly respond to these requests and increase the likelihood that policy actions are evidence-based. FARRMS was conceived of during our response to COVID and was substantially developed in response to the fallout from the Russia-Ukraine War. FARRMS was used in mid-2022 to analyze impacts of the global crises on a large number of developing countries, and to advise donor governments in their responses. The country studies were conducted together with local partners, including country-based CGIAR researchers. The country studies were updated/extended in late-2022 to analyze a global economic slowdown during 2023.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/661?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1098, 'Mechanized direct seeding for improved farming efficiency and reduced carbon footprint in rice production in Vietnam', 'This innovation includes a technical package of mechanized wet-direct seeding (mDSR) tailored to improved agronomy, climate adaptation and mitigation in rice production. mDSR is developed in association with supporting technologies such as precision land leveling, water management, fertilizer and weed management, and integrated with digital agriculture for optimized crop management, harvest and postharvest management.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1098?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(1489, 'Biomass Climate Adaptation Index (CAI): A satellite-based near real-time climate adaptation monitoring system', 'The lack of systematic tools for measuring climate change adaptation limits progress assessment toward the goals of the Paris Agreement. To address this gap, we introduce the Biomass Climate Adaptation Index (Biomass CAI) as a novel approach to measure agricultural climate adaptation progress at various scales. This method models time series of Vegetation Index (VI) for seasonal cropland, including both smallholders and larger industrial crops, and accounting for external factors like weather and past VI trends. Predictions of greenness, incorporating current weather impact and calibrated over a two-decade baseline, are compared with satellite-measured values using a synthetic counterfactual approach. Deviations suggest improvements or deterioration in agricultural status, informing adaptation progress and intervention needs. Although implemented in Sub-Saharan Africa, in an area ranging from Senegal to Ethiopia, it lacks thorough validation beyond small-scale studies. The Biomass CAI focuses on decision-support to maximize impact of adaptation investments and interventions in agricultural lands.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2023, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1489?phase=3', 6, NULL),
(4638, 'High-quality Peste des Petits Ruminants (PPR) thermotolerant vaccine to reduce small ruminant mortality', 'Since 2016, the International Livestock Research Institute has partnered with Hester Biosciences Ltd of India, a private vaccine production company, to support the Central Veterinary Laboratory of Mali in optimizing and producing thermotolerant vaccine called “ILRI thermotolerant Peste des Petits Ruminants vaccine produced by the Thermovac process” developed by Internationa Livestock Research Institute and referred here as “ILRI protocol”. The proposed vaccine has proved considerably stable at ambient temperatures up to seven days, which has tremendous advantage in hot countries and in remote areas. The project has gone beyond technology development to identify and test components of new institutional model for delivering effective Peste des Petits Ruminants control services to small ruminant producers in Mali', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4638?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(454, 'Social behavior change communication for community health workers and men and women caregivers to improve nutrition knowledge and address norms for inclusion of livestock-derived foods in diets in East Africa', 'The innovation comprises approaches and gender sensitive delivery models for influencing behaviour on diets that include livestock derived foods and food safety practices through social behaviour change communication. It also includes approaches and methodolgies for addressing gender norms and socio-cultural barriers to animal source foods consumption.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/454?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(1026, 'Integrating Water Storage Diagnostic to diversify and expand water storage options to enhance Water Energy Food Environment Benefits for water planners and basin managers.', 'The water storage diagnostic is an approach to integrating assessments of different storage types, such as aquifers, soil moisture, and dams. The diagnostic framework supports planning and management decisions across these water storage types by identifying and comparing their relative opportunities and risks. Methods for evaluating individual storage options include recession flow analysis and hydrogeological modelling (for aquifers); analysis of earth observation data (for dams); and analysis of in-situ and remotely sensed or modelled data (for soil moisture). Planning and management across (rather than within) storage types enable the identification of complementary roles for alternative storage options and the design of a diversified path to storage development, which brings a suite of benefits including greater WEFE benefits to water-using communities. The results of diagnostics have now been used by River Basin Organizations and basin managers', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1026?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(8954, 'Faster growing genetically improved catla (Generation 1) produced in Bangladesh', 'A total of 303 Generation 1 (G1) catla families produced in 2021, 2022 and 2023 were maintained in 2024.  Fish produced in 2023 were assessed at harvest age and candidate parents selected from this cohort.  Parents from the 2021 cohort (3 years old) were mated to produce the first G2 families in 2024.  
Based on the genetic quality of the parents of Generation 1, a genetic gain of approximately 10% is anticipated.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/8954?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(776, 'Innovative Software Tools for Estimating Environmental Flow Requirements Based on Monthly River Flow Time Series', 'The Environmental Flow Estimation Tools are a set of user-friendly (online and offline) software tools enabling desktop estimation of quick,  coarse-scale environmental flow requirements of rivers. The input data required are time series data of monthly “natural” river flows, either measured or estimated. The tools are meant to be used by water planners and managers in making initial planning-level estimates of environmental flows, which may then be followed by more comprehensive assessments using further data related to aquatic ecology, moving beyond river flow data. Five tools are currently available, two covering major rivers and the other three specifically for estimating environmental flow requirements in the Ganges Basin, Sri Lanka and Nepal. The tools are also useful for building capacity on environmental flow estimation, including for students and practitioners seeking to gain an initial understanding of the concept of environmental flows.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/776?phase=4', 6, NULL),
(44, 'Sweetpotato breeding networks in Asia & Latin America accelerate genetic gains for diverse market segments; shorter season varieties, varieties with low sweetness or purple flesh are in demand', 'The CIP sweetpotato breeding program in Peru is serving mostly Asia and Latin America with breeding pipelines for  1. “orange flesh sweet potato (OFSP) for wide adaptation and earliness” suitable for 90 days crop seasons which are in demand in wheat/rice rotation systems, 2. “OFSP with no/low sweetness after cooking” suitable for 120 days crop seasons, and 3. purple-flesh sweetpotatoes for 120 days crop seasons. Genomic tools accelerate the development of more productive, biofortified, consumer-preferred varieties with higher foliage yield. Breeding cycle time is 30 months. Dissemination to local partners is through true seed from elite-crosses, and via in-vitro plantlets of advanced clones. On-farm testing in Peru has been completed, identifying two clones for release. Close to 93,000 true seeds, each a unique genotype, were distributed to partners in Bangladesh, Vietnam, China, with further requests from Mozambique, India, Uganda and Vietnam. Ethiopia released a biofortified variety from the Peru-based program.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/44?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(41, 'Rice breeding network in Eastern and Southern Africa accelerates genetic gains for productive, resilient, market-demanded varieties fitting local growing conditions, to help increase domestic production', 'Rice breeding in East and Southern Africa aims to curb the region’s reliance on rice imports and contribute to the Africa-wide rice development strategy. Collaborative breeding efforts between IRRI, AfricaRice and national partners develop climate-resilient, market-demanded varieties that enhance productivity and reduce the production gap. Modern breeding approaches facilitate the faster development of tailored rice varieties for intensive cropping systems. They leverage IRRI''s breeding investments in Asia that develop early and medium maturing, stress-tolerant varieties (i) for direct seeding, and (ii) for irrigated, deep and semi-deep-water systems. Cycle time is 36 months. Increasingly defined product concepts and on-farm trialing result in varieties better aligned with farmer and end-user demands while pointing to further improvement needs. Sites in Mozambique, Kenya and Tanzania participate in early testing, while late-stage testing takes place in more countries.  Between 2022-2024, partners in four countries registered 20 varieties, all of them improved for climate resilience traits.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/41?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(305, 'Potato breeding network in Latin America accelerates genetic gains for biofortified, disease- and heat-tolerant varieties, also creating income and nutrition benefits for farmers in Africa and Asia', 'CIP potato breeding harnesses the latest scientific knowledge and tools to develop better varieties. Even though targeted and first tested regionally, the Latin American potato breeding pipelines have significant benefits globally as they pursue: 1. Late blight, heat tolerant potato varieties to enable farmers to cope with climate change. 2. Biofortified potatoes with elevated levels of iron and zinc, and disease and heat resilience traits to reduce malnutrition. Breeding cycle time is 3 years. Selection is for marketable tuber yield and late blight resistance. Biofortified potatoes crosses were made in 2012 between a Peruvian landrace and elite lines with high yield and resistance to prevalent diseases. Twelve clones were selected in collaboration with Peruvian partners and 1600 farmers. Between 2022-2024, partners in China, NIgeria and Peru released 11 varieties, two of them with 37% more iron and 35% more Zinc, contributing to reducing malnutrition.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/305?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(48, 'A public-partnership accelerates genetic gains for early-maturing highly marketable potatoes that keep up production in spite of the increasing number of biotic and abiotic stresses encountered by farmers in Asia', 'Potato is challenged by several biotic stresses including late blight, lowland tropics viruses, bacterial wilt, and nematodes along with abiotic stresses such as heat and drought in South and South-East Asia. CIP initiated a public-private partnership in 2016, in collaboration with partners in Asia and HZPC, a leading private potato seed company, to develop and commercialize early maturing climate-resilient potato varieties for different agro-ecologies and market segments in Asia and Africa. Breeding cycle time is 3.5 years. The tropically adapted advanced clones with late blight and virus resistances were developed using shuttle breeding between highlands and lowland agro-ecologies in Vietnam and the use of molecular markers. The advanced clones are being tested in on-farm trials across major potato-growing areas in lowland and highland agro-ecologies of Vietnam. Between 2022-2024, partners in five countries. registered 53 varieties, 23 in 2024. Nine varieties derived from pipelines improved for heat and drought.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/48?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(4819, 'Internal Migration Propensity Index (i-MPI) tool to predict domestic migrants for better targeting of policy and development interventions', 'An internal Migration Propensity Index (i-MPI) was developed in 2023 for Guatemala and Honduras, aimed at predicting permanent domestic migration at the household level, following the methodology of the e-MPI (to predict cross-border migration) and adapting it to challenges specific to internal migration. The index relies on a concise set of simple, non invasive questions that are easy to collect and that combined best predict the probability that at least one individual from a household permanently migrates to a different community within the same country over the next 12-24 months. During 2024, the tool was validated completely out of sample (in a new panel household sample) and found to be both predictive of migration and outperform other indicators potentially related to migration. Such a tool can be used for both monitoring and targeting purposes to improve livelihoods at the local level and avoid the need to migrate.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4819?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(3418, 'Voluntary food safety grading of vendors in traditional markets', 'Food safety practices in traditional markets are often very poor, due to lack of infrastructure, low knowledge levels, and the absence of incentives. This innovation addresses these gaps by providing food safety training to vendors and offering an opportunity for enrollment in a voluntary food safety grading program. Consumers will be made aware of the program through posters advertising the grading program and signs indicating the food safety grade of participating vendors.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/3418?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(778, 'Multistakeholder Platforms are networks designed to implement program interventions by bringing together critical actors to improve business ecosystems and incentivise actors in bean corridors', 'Multistakeholder platforms were established organized around lead firms as major buyers of beans, linked to bean producers, and supported by other service providers such as finance, credit, information, research and extension, and policy support. PABRA and NARS provided initial facilitation for the establishment of the platforms. An assessment was conducted based on 227 women and 53 youth who actively participated in the bean business platforms across Rwanda, Cameroon, Madagascar, Tanzania, and Burundi between 2017 and 2023. Among the women and youth who participated, an average of 81% indicated that they were satisfied with the business platform indicator. The satisfaction levels were measured by the sampled individuals using a scale of one to five, with five being the highest, and were then converted to a percentage. In 2024, demonstration of the role of multistakeholder platforms in accelerating new variety dissemination and demonstrated the use of improved and certified seeds in Malawi.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/778?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(1100, 'Multidsciplinary working group (MWG) as a dedicated approach for building farmers'' resilience through improved local climate information production, dissemination and update in Senegal', 'The multidisciplinary working group (MWG) serves as a pivotal and inclusive platform dedicated to advancing the production of climate services, their interpretation into actionable decisions, and widespread dissemination. This is an innovative approach to establsihing multi-stakeholder platforms to enhance the uptake of weather and climate information services. The national MWG comprises key departments and institutions, fostering collaboration and comprehensive representation. At the local level, 32 MWGs, led by district Prefects, address region-specific challenges through decentralized structures to ensure that MWGs are well-connected with local realities and can effectively address region-specific challenges. Regular collaboration is maintained through tri-monthly meetings where members from both national and local MWGs convene. These meetings cover a spectrum of pertinent topics, including meteorology, hydrology, agriculture, livestock, environment, and market dynamics. The collective insights garnered from these discussions are distilled into recommendations, consolidated in a bulletin. To ensure widespread awareness, these recommendations are disseminated through community radios.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1100?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(806, 'Conjunctive use of groundwater and surface water with solar irrigation pumping to enhance irrigation water availability and productivity, food and water security for smallholder farmers in dry areas', 'The innovation uses the site suitability mapping for implementing the Aquifer Storage and Recovery (ASR) method called Bhungroo Irrigation Technology (BIT) and solar irrigation pumping to smallholder irrigation schemes that use groundwater and surface water in the Inkomanti-Usuthu Catchment Management Area in South Africa. The ASR /BIT uses one borehole to recharge the aquifer during the wet season and abstract water from the same borehole during the dry season. This resulted in increased irrigation water availability and reduced energy use from grid electricity and fuel pumps, thereby increasing farm profit. Energy use for irrigation contributed 50-70% of the total farming expenses. Successful implementation of the BIT and solar irrigation pumping at a site requires the delineation of suitable areas based on scientific, natural parameters and expert judgment.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/806?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4977, 'Digital information systems with weather and marketing information to enhance market access for smallholder sesame farmers in Ethiopia', 'A digital information system that enables the efficient collection, processing, and distribution of information on weather and sesame marking. The weather data will be about temperature and precipitation. The marketing data will focus on input and output prices, sesame supply, quantity demanded, and new regulations.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4977?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5037, 'Extension and sensitization campaign for smallholder dairy farmers that explains various strategies for increasing the compositional quality of milk, such as choice of animal breed, pasture management, and supplementary feeding.', 'Extension and sensitization campaigns targeting smallholder dairy farmers generally focus on milk sanitation, highlighting the importance of washing udders and using clean and appropriate receptacles such as steel buckets and milk churns. Processors indicate that milk sanitation is fine, but the quality of milk in terms of composition (butter fat content and solid non-fats such as proteins, carbohydrates, and minerals) often needs improvement. We pilot a short, engaging video that explains various strategies that smallholder dairy farmers can use to increase the compositional quality of milk, such as choice of animal breed, pasture management, and supplementary feeding. By better aligning the quality demands of processors with the supply of raw milk, processors can produce more at a lower cost, and these rents can then be redistributed throughout the value chain.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5037?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5032, 'Installing simple quality monitoring devices paired with a data-collection application at milk collection centers in Uganda to differentiate milk qualities and encourage smallholder dairy farmers to supply better quality milk.', 'The proposed innovation involves making quality visible at milk aggregation points by installing simple quality monitoring equipment. Installing milk analyzers at milk collection centers will enable quick and easy testing of milk delivered by smallholder farmers on compositional quality of the milk, such as butter fat and Solid Non-Fat (protein, carbohydrates, and minerals). The milk analyzer will be paired with a custom application that runs on a tablet computer that can be used to keep track of milk samples. This will enable milk collection centers to increase the quality of their aggregate milk and negotiate a quality premium to processors demanding high-quality milk. (Part of) this premium can be passed on to farmers to encourage them to supply better-quality milk.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5032?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4870, 'Knowledge Platform to facilitate evidence-based decision-making of governments, businesses, farmers, and practitioners toward more Inclusive and Sustainable Food Markets and Value Chains (KISM).', 'Knowledge Platform to facilitate evidence-based decision-making of governments, businesses, farmers, and practitioners toward more Inclusive and Sustainable Food Markets and Value Chains (KISM). The platform consolidates evidence of existing experiences with product and process innovations within food value chains regarding their inclusiveness (employment and income generation, value-added sharing, poverty reduction, food security) and sustainability (emissions, supply chain resilience).', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4870?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5013, 'Solar dryer services for smallholder fruit and vegetable farmers to enable hygienic, quality-preserving conservation of perishable farm products', 'The solar dryer is a greenhouse-like structure: a metal or wood frame with plastic foil or acrylic glass that hosts well-ventilated tables or racks where sliced farm produce may be spread in thin layers. Compared to the traditional drying method of spreading sliced produce on the ground under the sun, the solar dryer allows a more hygienic, quality-preserving conservation of perishable farm produce like fruits and vegetables.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5013?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5002, 'A refrigeration-equipped truck and logistical support system for fruit and vegetable farmers and retailers to transport and preserve their perishable produce stored in standardized plastic crates', 'A refrigeration-equipped truck transports fruits and vegetables in standardized plastic crates. The efficient operation of cool transportation also needs a logistical support system. Cool transportation can preserve the quality of fruits and vegetables when transporting them in distance from production sites, typically in the northern regions, to large demand centers in the southern regions. Post-harvest losses in quality and quantity are reduced to a minimum to benefit producers and consumers, often spatially distanced. Preserving product quality is expected to increase incentives to enhance product quality.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5002?phase=4', 7, NULL),
(5005, 'Fully solar-powered walk-in cold stations for the 24/7 storage and preservation of fruits and vegetables, located at markets and used by vendors or retailers to store their perishable products.', 'Full solar-powered walk-in cold stations for the 24/7 storage and preservation of fruits and vegetables, located at markets, ideal for vendors/retailers to store their produce. The cold storage prevents post-harvest losses, i.e. prolonging the shelf-life, nutritional and economic value of the perishable produce.  The cold station is run as a business, with the storage service being priced per space and time of storage. The facility is guarded 24/7 and can be accessed during work hours.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5005?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5003, 'Improve cowpea variety for grain and quality fodder production in northern Ghana', 'Cowpea is a among key legume crops grown in northern Ghana and it plays an important role in integrating crop-livestock farming system through provision of protein food for human consumption, fodder for livestock feed and biological nitrogen fixing for soil fertility improvement. Despite the contribution of cowpea fodder to livestock feed, national breeding program of cowpea in Ghana does not consider fodder quality as part of criteria for the release of improved cowpea varieties. Hence this innovation assesses the fodder quality in addition to grain production of improved cowpea varieties to determine the suitability of the improved cowpea varieties for crop-livestock farming system in northern Ghana.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5003?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(5632, 'Improve soybean variety and soil amendment for quality grain and  fodder production in northern Ghana', 'Soybean is an important legume and cash crop in Ghana. The grains are good source of protein  food for household consumption, it  used to make cooking oil as well as feed in the poultry industry. The fodder is also a good source of feed for livestock and it  also improves soil fertility through biological nitrogen fixation. However, low soil fertility is a major constrain for soybean production in Ghana and the lack of consideration of fodder quality in the selection criteria of improve varieties by national breeding program affects its use in crop-livestock farming system. This innovation assess the effect of soil amendment and improve soybean varieties on quality of grain and fodder production in crop-livestock farming systems in northern Ghana.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5632?phase=4', 3, NULL),
(6296, 'Mechanizing maize seed and basal NPK fertilizer application to improve grain yield, nutrient use efficiency and biomass availability for livestock feed', 'This innovation builds on  Africa RISING technology which evaluated the effect of time of NPK fertilizer application for maize production in northern Ghana and reported an increase in grain yield (22-59%), agronomic nitrogen use efficiency (26-80%), return on investment (1-9 Fold), reduction in ammonia flux emission (1-49%) with application of NPK fertilizer to maize at planting relative to the conversional practice of applying NPK fertilizer to maize at 2 weeks after planting (Irmgard 2021; unpublished data). Adoption study of the innovation by farmers showed that about 40-98% of them are not applying the innovation. Inadequate availability of labor was cited as key barrier to scaling of innovation. The objective of this innovation is to address the labor challenges associated with the planting of maize and application of NPK fertilizer on the same day using sample planters. The hand push planter sow’s maize seed and apply fertilizer in two different holes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6296?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(5830, 'Digital Genetic Data Base for Small Ruminants in Ethiopia (DTREO) - a digital database revolutionizing small ruminant genetics through a digital platform targeting small ruminant producers and breeders', 'This innovation involves the establishment of the DTREO - Digital Genetic Data Base for Small Ruminants in Ethiopia, which has now expanded to other countries (East Africa and, Middle East and Northern Africa countries). The DTREO database is designed to be flexible and allows for the capture of reproduction details and performance data using smartphones or tablets operated by enumerators. It has the capability to function offline, enabling data to be saved locally and then synchronized with the cloud when connectivity is available. The data stored in the cloud is subsequently analyzed to rank animals based on their Estimated Breeding Value (EBV), and the feedback is shared with the community. Furthermore, the DTREO database serves to generate input data for genetic analysis. Presently, the DTREO database contains information on 134 thousand animals from 69 Community-based Breeding Program villages.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5830?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(9546, 'Climate Security Sensitivity Tool for practitioners and policymakers to assess the peace potential of climate adaptation interventions', 'The Climate Security Sensitivity Tool is a 2-step user friendly tool to assess the peace potential of climate adaptation interventions. Peacekeeping and climate adaptation are rarely integrated in adaptation initiatives. The Climate Security Sensitivity Tool tries to close this gap by assessing whether climate action programs and their implementation are appropriate for the context in which they are carried out and recommending solutions to improve their appropriateness. It is aimed at practitioners, policymakers, and international organizations interested in diagnostic research for peace-responsive climate action in agricultural and rural development.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/9546?phase=4', 4, NULL),
(805, 'Shamba Shape Up: Using digital channels to deliver bundled agriculture, climate, extension, and financial information services to farmers via mass media', 'Shamba shape is a farm makeover show which reaches over 8 million viewers in Kenya, and is now scalling up its reach to a show in Zambia and we would like to propose its scale up also to Uganda. The show will bring in new elements, providing seasonal forecasting to farmers with agro-advisory, reporting on FEWS-net early warning and agro-meteorological bulletins for access by smallholder farmers, through TV, Radio and the IShamba mobile app. The T.V show has reached 2.3 million people in Zambia.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/805?phase=4', 9, NULL),
(5278, 'From fleece to screens: Revolutionizing sheep and goat marketing in the digital age by linking small ruminants producers to markets', 'The International Center for Agricultural Research in the Dry Areas (ICARDA) and the Ethiopian national research systems have succeeded to work together to introduce community-based breeding programs and improved fattening technologies that are suitable to various production systems. At the same time, significant commercial and market-oriented patterns and practices are now emerging as result of the support. There exists some scientific evidence for the demand for livestock market facilities being developed and make them accessible, sustainable and guide a national effort. Along with these, ICARDA is collaborating with a private livestock marketing enterprise (Yazerber) to organize and launch a trustful and contented market linkages between sheep and goats producers and domestic consumers in and around Addis Ababa city. This activity is expected to increase sales of animals from target areas through increased market access and improves efficiency in well-organized livestock markets. Buyers have the option of buying their animals digitally.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/5278?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(1249, 'Fostering the scaling of mechanised conservation agriculture by small farmers in Morocco (MOROCCO-Conversation Agriculture Use Case)', 'Conservation Agriculture (CA) optimizes agro-ecosystem management to enhance productivity, profitability, and food security for smallholder farmers, while conserving and improving the environment. This innovation comprises two main components:

MVP 1: Integrates mechanized conservation agriculture with precise agronomy tailored to Morocco''s diverse soil and climate conditions, focusing on crop diversification and effective weed control, to be implemented by smallholder farmers.

MVP 3: A collaborative effort to scale up mechanized CA with the involvement of various sector stakeholders, specifically designed for smallholder farmers. This component acts as a strategic roadmap for national governments, donors, financiers, extension services, and NGOs to efficiently spread CA technologies among smallholder farming communities.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/1249?phase=4', 8, NULL),
(4212, 'Study protocols for country-specific analysis of climate data combined with existing data on women’s well-being from randomized impact evaluations of social protection programs', 'This innovation is a novel approach to measuring impacts of social protection programs, in terms of their roles in supporting gendered responses to climate change in low- and middle-income countries. A multi-disciplinary team of researchers have designed a protocol that includes combining data from an experimental impact evaluation of a social protection program with detailed weather data from satellites to investigate how the social protection programs shape gender dimensions of outcomes from extreme weather events. The data for this research is drawn from country-specific sources on weather events and nationally or locally implemented social protection programs.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4212?phase=1', 2, NULL),
(4208, 'Protocol for improved understanding of how integrating men’s engagement on involvement in chores and childcare activities into a social protection graduation model program can affect men’s behaviors and gender attitudes', 'A protocol for improved understanding of how integrating men’s engagement on involvement in household chores and childcare activities into a social protection graduation model program can affect men’s behaviors and gender attitudes. As learning partners for the Strengthening PSNP for Institutions and Resilience (SPIR) program that provides gender-sensitive livelihood and nutrition programming in a graduation model to beneficiary households in Ethiopia’s Productive Safety Net Program (PSNP), IFPRI researchers are collaborating with implementation partners from World Vision, CARE and the Organization for Rehabilitation and Development in Amhara (ORDA) to design and implement an experimental impact evaluation to test the effectiveness of a men’s engagement training to encourage men to reconsider their roles in support of household chores and childcare activities. The study uses reporting from both men and their female partners on men’s behaviors on these tasks and examines whether changes in men’s behaviors are accompanied with changes in gender attitudes.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2022, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/4208?phase=1', 0, NULL),
(6983, 'Generate evidence around the effectiveness of the ultra-poor graduation model for an urban displaced population in Baidoa, Somalia', 'Mixed-method impact evaluation testing the innovation, and generating broader learning for stakeholders around the effects of UPG in a humanitarian setting.  The innovation is a layered intervention including short-term multipurpose cash payments, livelihoods restoration and recovery (via asset transfers or vocational trainings), formation of household savings groups, and household-level coaching.', NULL, 'Innovation development', 2024, 'https://reporting.cgiar.org/reports/result-details/6983?phase=4', 7, NULL);
COMMIT;